{"histologic_feature": "Histologic feature refers to a specific characteristic or attribute observed in tissue samples under a microscope. These features are crucial for diagnosing and understanding various medical conditions.|Here is another description: Histologic features are the microscopic characteristics observed in tissue samples, which are crucial for diagnosing and understanding various diseases. These features include aspects like cell structure, tissue organization, and the presence of specific markers.|Here is another description: Histologic feature pertains to the specific characteristics or attributes observed in tissue samples under a microscope. These features are crucial for diagnosing and understanding various medical conditions.|Here is another description: Histologic feature refers to the specific characteristics or attributes observed in tissue samples under a microscope. These features are crucial for diagnosing various medical conditions and are subject to interpretation by pathologists.|Here is another description: Histologic features are specific characteristics observed in tissue samples under a microscope, such as the degree of steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis. These features are critical in diagnosing and staging various liver conditions, including MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"histologic_feature__compared_in__aim_mash_versus_mean_pathologist_versus_mean_pairwise_pathologist": "Compared in indicates a process where different methods or observers are evaluated against each other to assess their performance or agreement in identifying or measuring histologic features."},
{"aim_mash_versus_mean_pathologist_versus_mean_pairwise_pathologist": "AIM-MASH versus Mean pathologist versus Mean pairwise pathologist represents a comparison between an automated image analysis method (AIM-MASH) and the average performance of individual pathologists, as well as the average performance when pathologists work in pairs, in terms of their ability to identify and agree on histologic features."},
{"inter_reader_concordance": "Inter-reader concordance refers to the level of agreement between different readers or observers when evaluating the same set of data or features. In the context of histologic features, it measures how consistently pathologists interpret and classify these features."},
{"inter_reader_concordance__measured_in__histologic_feature": "The predicate 'measured in' indicates the context or domain within which the subject is evaluated or assessed. Here, it specifies that inter-reader concordance is being assessed specifically within the domain of histologic features."},
{"consensus": "Consensus refers to the agreement reached among pathologists when evaluating histologic features. It represents a collective decision or opinion formed after discussion and consideration of various viewpoints.|Here is another description: Consensus in this context refers to the agreed-upon gradings and assessments of histologic features in MASH biopsies by a panel of expert pathologists. This collective judgment serves as a benchmark against which the performance of individual expert readers is measured, providing a standard for evaluating their diagnostic accuracy and reliability.|Here is another description: The consensus refers to the agreement reached among a panel of experts, in this case, three expert pathologists and a machine learning model, regarding the grading of histologic features in MASH biopsies.|Here is another description: Consensus in this context refers to the agreed-upon grades or stages determined by a panel of expert pathologists, excluding the third pathologist whose performance is being evaluated. This consensus serves as a benchmark for assessing the accuracy and reliability of individual pathologists' evaluations."},
{"consensus__achieved_in__histologic_feature": "Achieved in indicates the context or setting where consensus was reached. In this case, it specifies that consensus was obtained during the evaluation of histologic features by pathologists."},
