{"the_rightmost_column_as_a_comparison_with_recently_published_results_from_the_pathology_committee_of_the_mash_crn": "The rightmost column as a comparison with recently published results from the pathology committee of the MASH CRN refers to a specific location within a dataset or report where \u03ba statistics are presented. This column is used to compare the current results with those from a recent publication by the pathology committee of the MASH CRN, providing a benchmark for evaluating the performance or agreement rates."},
{"\u03ba_statistics__are_from__kleiner_and_colleagues_13": "The phrase \"are from\" indicates the source or origin of the \u03ba statistics. In this context, it specifies that the \u03ba statistics used for comparison are derived from a specific study conducted by Kleiner and colleagues."},
{"kleiner_and_colleagues_13": "Kleiner and colleagues [13] represent the authors of a referenced study that provided \u03ba statistics for comparison. Their work is cited to benchmark the results of the current study against previously published data from the pathology committee of the MASH CRN."},
{"gnn": "GNN, or Graph Neural Network, is a machine learning model designed to process and analyze data in graph format, particularly for interpreting medical images and predicting histological grades and stages."},
{"gnn__predicts__mash_crn_steatosis_grade": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates the action performed by the GNN. It signifies that the GNN is capable of forecasting or estimating certain medical grades and stages based on the input it receives from histological images."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade": "MASH CRN steatosis grade is a medical grading system used to assess the severity of steatosis, which is the accumulation of fat in liver cells. The GNN is trained to predict this grade from H&E-stained whole slide images (WSIs), mapping continuous predicted scores to specific grades."},
{"gnn__predicts__lobular_inflammation_grade": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates the action performed by the GNN. It signifies that the GNN is capable of forecasting or estimating a specific outcome based on the input data it processes. In this scenario, the GNN is predicting the lobular inflammation grade from the analyzed images."},
{"lobular_inflammation_grade": "Lobular inflammation grade is a medical assessment that quantifies the degree of inflammation present in the lobules of liver tissue. This grade is an important indicator of liver health and is part of the MASH CRN (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis Clinical Research Network) grading system used to evaluate liver histopathology in medical images."},
{"gnn__predicts__hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates the action performed by the GNN. It signifies that the GNN is capable of forecasting or estimating a specific medical condition or grade based on the input it receives from the images."},
{"hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "Hepatocellular ballooning grade is a medical term used to describe the degree of swelling in liver cells, which can be an indicator of liver disease severity. In this context, it is one of the outcomes that the GNN is trained to predict from the histological images."},
{"gnn__predicts__fibrosis_stage": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates that the GNN is used to forecast or estimate a specific outcome based on the input data. Here, it means the GNN is capable of determining the severity or stage of a medical condition by analyzing stained whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"fibrosis_stage": "\"Fibrosis stage\" refers to the degree of scarring (fibrosis) in liver tissue, which is a critical indicator of the severity of liver diseases such as Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). The fibrosis stage is assessed through histological examination of MT-stained WSIs, where different stages indicate varying levels of tissue damage and scarring."},
{"h_e_stained_wsis": "H&E-stained WSIs refer to whole slide images that have been stained using the Hematoxylin and Eosin method, a common staining technique in histopathology that highlights cellular and tissue structures for detailed examination."},
{"h_e_stained_wsis__used_for__predicting_mash_crn_steatosis_grade_lobular_inflammation_grade_and_hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "The phrase \"used for\" indicates the purpose or application of the subject in a specific context, in this case, the utilization of H&E-stained WSIs in medical diagnostics to assess various liver conditions."},
{"predicting_mash_crn_steatosis_grade_lobular_inflammation_grade_and_hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "Predicting MASH CRN steatosis grade, lobular inflammation grade, and hepatocellular ballooning grade involves using diagnostic tools or methods to estimate the severity of liver conditions such as fat accumulation, inflammation, and cell damage, which are critical for assessing liver health and disease progression."},
{"mt_stained_wsis": "MT-stained WSIs refer to whole slide images that have been stained using a Masson's Trichrome (MT) technique, which is commonly used to highlight fibrous tissue in histological samples."},
{"mt_stained_wsis__used_for__predicting_fibrosis_stage": "The phrase \"used for\" indicates the purpose or application of the MT-stained WSIs in a specific context, which in this case is the prediction of fibrosis stage."},
{"predicting_fibrosis_stage": "Predicting fibrosis stage involves using the information from MT-stained WSIs to determine the extent or severity of fibrosis, which is the thickening and scarring of connective tissue, usually as a result of injury."},
{"gnn_predicted_scores": "GNN-predicted scores are continuous values generated by a Graph Neural Network (GNN) to predict various grades and stages of liver conditions such as MASH CRN steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis."},
{"gnn_predicted_scores__mapped_to__bins": "The process of mapping involves assigning GNN-predicted scores to specific categories or ranges that correspond to established medical grades or stages, making the predictions interpretable and applicable in a clinical context."},
{"bins": "Bins represent predefined ranges or categories into which GNN-predicted scores are mapped. Each bin corresponds to a specific grade or stage of a liver condition, such as MASH CRN steatosis grade 0, 1, 2, or 3.|Here is another description: Bins in this context refer to the discrete categories or intervals into which continuous scores are mapped for interpretability. These bins are used to represent specific grades or stages of medical conditions."},
{"bins__equivalent_to__one_grade_or_stage": "The predicate \"equivalent to\" signifies that each bin is directly correlated with a specific grade or stage. This means that the range of scores within a bin corresponds to a particular level of a medical condition."},
{"one_grade_or_stage": "One grade or stage represents the specific level of a medical condition, such as steatosis grade, lobular inflammation grade, hepatocellular ballooning grade, or fibrosis stage. Each grade or stage is a distinct category used to describe the severity or extent of the condition."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_0": "MASH CRN steatosis grade 0 is the lowest level of steatosis in the MASH CRN grading system, indicating minimal or no fat accumulation in liver cells."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_0__continuous_range__0_1": "The continuous range represents a numerical scale used to quantify the severity of steatosis, with specific intervals assigned to each grade to facilitate precise grading."},
{"0_1": "The object \"0\u20131\" defines the specific numerical interval assigned to MASH CRN steatosis grade 0, indicating the range of values that correspond to this grade on the continuous scale."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_1": "MASH CRN steatosis grade 1 is a specific level of fat accumulation in the liver as defined by the MASH CRN grading system, indicating a moderate degree of steatosis."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_1__continuous_range__1_2": "The continuous range represents a numerical scale used to quantify the severity of liver steatosis, where specific ranges are assigned to different grades to provide a more detailed assessment of the condition."},
{"1_2": "The object \"1\u20132\" denotes the specific numerical range on the continuous scale that corresponds to MASH CRN steatosis grade 1, indicating the severity level of fat accumulation in the liver within this grade."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_2": "MASH CRN steatosis grade 2 is a specific level of severity in the assessment of hepatic steatosis, a condition characterized by the accumulation of fat in liver cells. This grade is part of a standardized system used to quantify the extent of steatosis in liver tissue."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_2__continuous_range__2_3": "The predicate \"continuous range\" refers to a numerical scale used to quantify the severity of MASH CRN steatosis grades. This scale allows for a more precise and detailed assessment of the condition, providing a continuous measure that can be mapped to discrete grades."},
{"2_3": "The continuous range of 2\u20133 represents a specific segment on the numerical scale used to assess MASH CRN steatosis grade 2. This range indicates the severity level of steatosis within the context of the grading system, where higher numbers correspond to more severe conditions."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_3": "MASH CRN steatosis grade 3 is a specific category within the MASH CRN grading system used to assess the severity of steatosis, or fat accumulation in liver cells, based on histological examination of liver tissue."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade_3__continuous_range__3_4": "The continuous range represents a numerical scale used to quantify the severity of steatosis within the MASH CRN grading system, allowing for a more precise and detailed assessment of liver condition."},
{"3_4": "The range 3\u20134 indicates the specific numerical values assigned to MASH CRN steatosis grade 3 within the continuous grading scale, reflecting a moderate to severe level of steatosis in the liver tissue."},
{"aim_mash_algorithm_scoring": "The AIM-MASH algorithm scoring is a method used for evaluating histologic features in medical images, specifically within the context of model performance testing for enrollment criteria and endpoints."},
{"aim_mash_algorithm_scoring__was__perfectly_repeatable": "The predicate \"was\" indicates a state of being or condition that the subject, AIM-MASH algorithm scoring, exhibited during the initial model performance testing."},
{"perfectly_repeatable": "\"Perfectly repeatable\" describes the condition of the AIM-MASH algorithm scoring, meaning that the results it produced were consistent and identical across multiple independent reads of the same whole slide images (WSI), achieving a 100% agreement rate."},
{"model_versus_model_agreement_rate": "The model versus model agreement rate refers to the percentage of agreement between multiple independent runs or instances of the AIM-MASH algorithm when scoring the same whole slide images (WSIs) for histologic features."},
{"model_versus_model_agreement_rate__was__100": "The predicate \"was\" indicates a state of being or a condition that existed at a specific point in time, in this case, the agreement rate between model runs."},
{"100": "The object \"100%\" represents the complete agreement rate achieved by the AIM-MASH algorithm when comparing ten independent reads per WSI, indicating perfect repeatability in the model's performance."},
{"intra_pathologist_agreement_using_conventional_approaches": "The intra-pathologist agreement using conventional approaches refers to the consistency in diagnoses or assessments made by the same pathologist when reviewing histologic features multiple times. This metric is crucial for evaluating the reliability of manual pathology assessments."},
{"intra_pathologist_agreement_using_conventional_approaches__ranged_from__37_to_74": "The phrase \"ranged from\" indicates a variability or spectrum within which the intra-pathologist agreement values fall. It suggests that the agreement rates are not uniform but vary within a certain range."},
{"37_to_74": "The object \"37% to 74%\" represents the range of intra-pathologist agreement rates observed using conventional approaches. This range highlights the variability in consistency among pathologists when assessing histologic features repeatedly."},
{"aim_mash_performance_accuracy": "The AIM-MASH performance accuracy refers to the effectiveness and precision of the AIM-MASH algorithm in scoring histologic features compared to a pathologist-based consensus."},
{"aim_mash_performance_accuracy__was_assessed_using__a_mixed_leave_one_out_mloo_approach": "The assessment of AIM-MASH performance accuracy using a mixed leave-one-out (MLOO) approach indicates the method employed to evaluate how well the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring predictions align with the consensus of pathologists."},
{"a_mixed_leave_one_out_mloo_approach": "A mixed leave-one-out (MLOO) approach is a validation technique where each sample is left out once during the model training process to ensure that the model's performance is tested on unseen data, providing a robust measure of its accuracy and generalizability."},
{"model_versus_consensus_agreement_rates": "The subject refers to the agreement rates between the model and a consensus in assessing histologic features. This comparison is crucial for evaluating the model's performance in a clinical context, where consensus among pathologists is often used as a gold standard."},
{"model_versus_consensus_agreement_rates__fell_within_the_range_of__previously_reported_rates_of_inter_pathologist_agreement": "The predicate indicates that the model versus consensus agreement rates are being compared to a specific range. This comparison helps to contextualize the model's performance within the broader landscape of inter-pathologist agreement, providing a benchmark for assessing its reliability and accuracy."},
{"previously_reported_rates_of_inter_pathologist_agreement": "The object describes the previously reported rates of inter-pathologist agreement. These rates serve as a reference point for evaluating the model's performance, highlighting the variability and challenges inherent in histologic feature assessment by human pathologists."},
{"agreement_rate_for_steatosis": "The agreement rate for steatosis refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of steatosis in liver tissue images."},
{"agreement_rate_for_steatosis__was__\u03ba_0_74_95_ci_0_71_0_77": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the measured or observed value of the agreement rate for steatosis in the context of evaluating the AIM-MASH algorithm's performance."},
{"\u03ba_0_74_95_ci_0_71_0_77": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.74, 95% CI 0.71\u20130.77\" represents the kappa statistic value of 0.74 with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.71 to 0.77, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for steatosis."},
{"agreement_rate_for_ballooning": "The agreement rate for ballooning refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of ballooning in liver tissue."},
{"agreement_rate_for_ballooning__was__\u03ba_0_70_95_ci_0_66_0_73": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the measured or observed value of the agreement rate for ballooning, reflecting the performance of the AIM-MASH algorithm in comparison to the consensus of pathologists."},
{"\u03ba_0_70_95_ci_0_66_0_73": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.70, 95% CI 0.66\u20130.73\" represents the kappa statistic value and its 95% confidence interval, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of ballooning."},
{"agreement_rate_for_lobular_inflammation": "The agreement rate for lobular inflammation refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of lobular inflammation in liver tissue samples."},
{"agreement_rate_for_lobular_inflammation__was__\u03ba_0_67_95_ci_0_64_0_71": "The predicate \"was\" indicates that the agreement rate for lobular inflammation has been measured and recorded as a specific value, reflecting the performance of the AIM-MASH algorithm in comparison to the consensus of pathologists."},
{"\u03ba_0_67_95_ci_0_64_0_71": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.67, 95% CI 0.64\u20130.71\" represents the kappa statistic value and its 95% confidence interval, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for lobular inflammation. A kappa value of 0.67 suggests a substantial level of agreement, while the confidence interval provides a range within which the true agreement rate is likely to fall."},
{"agreement_rate_for_fibrosis": "The agreement rate for fibrosis refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of fibrosis in liver tissue samples."},
{"agreement_rate_for_fibrosis__was__\u03ba_0_62_95_ci_0_58_0_65": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the measured value or status of the agreement rate for fibrosis, reflecting the performance of the AIM-MASH algorithm in comparison to the consensus of pathologists."},
{"\u03ba_0_62_95_ci_0_58_0_65": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.62, 95% CI 0.58\u20130.65\" represents the kappa statistic value and its 95% confidence interval, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for fibrosis, indicating a substantial level of agreement."},
{"agreement_between_the_model_and_consensus": "The agreement between the model and consensus refers to the level of concordance observed when comparing the scoring predictions of the AIM-MASH algorithm with a consensus reached by pathologists for the four cardinal histologic features.|Here is another description: The agreement between the model and consensus refers to the level of concordance observed when comparing the scoring predictions made by the AIM-MASH algorithm with a consensus score determined by pathologists for histologic features."},
{"agreement_between_the_model_and_consensus__was_greater_than__agreement_for_any_individual_pathologist_against_the_other_three_reads": "The phrase \"was greater than\" indicates a comparative relationship, showing that the level of agreement between the model and the consensus exceeded another specified measure of agreement."},
{"agreement_for_any_individual_pathologist_against_the_other_three_reads": "The agreement for any individual pathologist against the other three reads represents the level of concordance when comparing the scoring of a single pathologist with the combined scoring of the other three pathologists for the same histologic features."},
{"agreement_between_the_model_and_consensus__was_greater_than__any_mean_pairwise_pathologist_agreement": "The phrase \"was greater than\" indicates a comparative relationship, showing that the agreement level between the model and the consensus exceeded another specified measure of agreement."},
{"any_mean_pairwise_pathologist_agreement": "Any mean pairwise pathologist agreement refers to the average level of agreement observed when comparing the scores given by individual pathologists to each other for the same histologic features."},
{"fda__has_proposed__criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment": "The predicate \"has proposed\" indicates that the FDA has put forward or suggested a set of criteria or guidelines for a specific purpose, in this case, for MASH clinical trial enrollment."},
{"criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment": "The criteria for MASH clinical trial enrollment are specific guidelines or requirements proposed by the FDA to determine the eligibility of patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis for participation in clinical trials aimed at studying and treating MASH."},
{"aim_mash__was_deployed_on__wsis_from_two_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trials": "The deployment of AIM-MASH involved applying the model to whole slide images (WSIs) to assess histologic criteria and determine patient eligibility for clinical trials."},
{"wsis_from_two_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trials": "WSIs from two completed phase 2b MASH clinical trials represent the dataset on which AIM-MASH was deployed. These images were used to generate histologic scores and compare AI-derived predictions with pathologist scores."},
{"aim_mash__to_generate__scores_based_on_histologic_criteria": "The predicate \"to generate\" in this context refers to the action performed by AIM-MASH, which involves producing scores based on specific histologic criteria. This action is crucial for identifying patients eligible for clinical trial enrollment."},
{"scores_based_on_histologic_criteria": "Scores based on histologic criteria are the output generated by AIM-MASH. These scores are used to evaluate the histologic condition of patients, specifically to distinguish between MASH \u2265 4 and MASH < 4, which are critical for determining trial enrollment eligibility."},
{"aim_mash__to_identify__patients_eligible_for_enrollment": "The predicate \"to identify\" in this context refers to the action performed by AIM-MASH to determine which patients meet the criteria for enrollment in MASH clinical trials based on histologic assessments."},
{"patients_eligible_for_enrollment": "Patients eligible for enrollment are those who meet the specific histologic criteria set by the FDA for participation in MASH clinical trials, as determined by AIM-MASH's evaluation of Whole Slide Images (WSIs)."},
{"ai_derived_predictions": "AI-derived predictions refer to the scores and assessments generated by an artificial intelligence model, specifically AIM-MASH, based on histologic criteria from whole slide images (WSIs) of patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis.|Here is another description: AI-derived predictions refer to the assessments and gradings made by an artificial intelligence model, specifically in the context of analyzing histologic features related to MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) within a clinical trial. These predictions are generated by the model after analyzing whole slide images (WSI) of liver biopsies."},
{"ai_derived_predictions__were_compared_with__central_pathologist_cp_scores": "The phrase \"were compared with\" indicates an evaluation process where the AI-derived predictions are assessed against a standard or another set of scores to determine their accuracy and clinical relevance."},
{"central_pathologist_cp_scores": "Central pathologist (CP) scores are the assessments and scores provided by a central pathologist, who is an expert in MASH histology, for the same cohorts of patients in phase 2b MASH clinical trials. These scores serve as a benchmark for comparing the AI-derived predictions."},
{"model_derived_histologic_predictions": "Model-derived histologic predictions refer to the assessments generated by an AI model, specifically AIM-MASH, which evaluates histologic criteria from whole slide images (WSIs) to determine patient eligibility for clinical trials.|Here is another description: Model-derived histologic predictions refer to the assessments generated by an AI model, specifically AIM-MASH, which evaluates histologic criteria from whole slide images (WSIs) to determine patient eligibility for clinical trials."},
{"model_derived_histologic_predictions__were_used_to_calculate__mash_crn_scores": "The phrase \"were used to calculate\" indicates that the model-derived histologic predictions served as the basis for computing specific scores, in this case, the MASH CRN scores, which are essential for evaluating patient eligibility in clinical trials."},
{"mash_crn_scores": "MASH CRN scores are quantitative assessments used in the context of clinical trials for noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. These scores help distinguish between patients with a MAS (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis Activity Score) of 4 or higher and those with a MAS below 4, which is a critical criterion for trial enrollment."},
{"model_derived_histologic_predictions__to_distinguish__mas_4_from_mas_4": "The predicate \"to distinguish\" in this context signifies the AI model's capability to differentiate between two categories based on histologic criteria, specifically MAS scores, which are critical for determining patient enrollment in clinical trials."},
{"mas_4_from_mas_4": "MAS \u2265 4 from MAS < 4 refers to the Model Activity Score (MAS) used in clinical trials for noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. MAS \u2265 4 indicates a higher level of disease activity, while MAS < 4 indicates a lower level, and distinguishing between these is crucial for trial enrollment criteria."},
{"aim_mash_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement": "The AIM-MASH versus consensus percentage agreement refers to the level of agreement between the AI-based model AIM-MASH and the consensus scores provided by pathologists in evaluating histologic criteria for MASH clinical trial enrollment."},
{"aim_mash_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement__was__0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_85": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the measured value of the AIM-MASH versus consensus percentage agreement in the context of evaluating AI-based histologic predictions against pathologist consensus scores."},
{"0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_85": "The object \"0.82 (95% CI 0.79\u20130.85)\" represents the percentage agreement value between AIM-MASH and the consensus scores, with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.79 to 0.85, indicating a high level of consistency between the AI model and pathologist consensus."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement": "The average pathologist versus consensus percentage agreement refers to the level of agreement between the assessments made by an average pathologist and a consensus of multiple pathologists in evaluating histologic criteria for MASH clinical trial enrollment."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement__was__0_81_95_ci_0_78_0_83": "The predicate \"was\" indicates that the average pathologist versus consensus percentage agreement had a specific value, which is quantified in the object of the triple."},
{"0_81_95_ci_0_78_0_83": "The object \"0.81 (95% CI 0.78\u20130.83)\" represents the percentage agreement between the average pathologist and the consensus of pathologists, with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.78 to 0.83."},
{"fibrosis_stages_1_3_f1_f3_versus_f4": "Fibrosis stages 1\u20133 (F1\u2013F3) versus F4 refers to a comparison between the earlier stages of liver fibrosis (F1 to F3) and the more advanced stage (F4), which is often associated with cirrhosis."},
{"fibrosis_stages_1_3_f1_f3_versus_f4__model_versus_consensus_agreement__0_97_95_ci_0_95_0_98": "The model versus consensus agreement indicates a comparison between the agreement level of a predictive model and the agreement level reached by a consensus of experts in diagnosing or assessing the severity of liver fibrosis."},
{"0_97_95_ci_0_95_0_98": "0.97 (95% CI 0.95\u20130.98) represents a high level of agreement, with a confidence interval that suggests a very narrow range of possible values, indicating strong reliability and consistency in the model's predictions compared to expert consensus."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement": "The average pathologist versus consensus agreement refers to the level of agreement between the average assessment made by pathologists and a consensus agreement on a particular medical condition or diagnosis."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement__agreement__0_96_95_ci_0_95_0_97": "The predicate \"agreement\" in this context signifies the degree of concordance or similarity between the average pathologist's assessment and the consensus agreement on the staging of fibrosis."},
{"0_96_95_ci_0_95_0_97": "The object \"0.96 (95% CI 0.95\u20130.97)\" represents the quantitative measure of agreement, indicating a high level of concordance with a confidence interval that provides a range within which the true agreement is likely to fall."},
{"fda__has_proposed__criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_endpoint_assessment": "The predicate \"has proposed\" indicates that the FDA has put forward or suggested certain criteria or guidelines for a specific purpose, in this case, for the assessment of clinical trial endpoints related to MASH."},
{"criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_endpoint_assessment": "The criteria for MASH clinical trial endpoint assessment refer to the specific standards or guidelines proposed by the FDA to evaluate the outcomes of clinical trials focused on MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis), particularly concerning the resolution of MASH and improvement in fibrosis."},
{"recommended_mash_trial_endpoints": "Recommended MASH trial endpoints refer to the criteria proposed by the FDA for assessing the outcomes of clinical trials focused on noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. These endpoints are crucial for evaluating the efficacy of treatments in such trials."},
{"recommended_mash_trial_endpoints__include__evidence_of_efficacy_using_a_histologic_endpoint_of_mash_resolution_or_fibrosis_improvement": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the recommended MASH trial endpoints encompass or consist of specific criteria. In this context, it indicates that the endpoints proposed by the FDA for MASH clinical trials are comprised of certain measures of efficacy."},
{"evidence_of_efficacy_using_a_histologic_endpoint_of_mash_resolution_or_fibrosis_improvement": "Evidence of efficacy using a histologic endpoint of MASH resolution or fibrosis improvement refers to the specific criteria included in the recommended MASH trial endpoints. This object describes the desired outcomes in clinical trials, which are the resolution of MASH or improvement in fibrosis, as assessed through histologic methods."},
{"aim_mash_predictions__were_used_to__determine_component_scores_and_evaluate_composite_endpoints_in_an_exploratory_retrospective_analysis": "The phrase \"were used to\" indicates the application or utilization of AIM-MASH predictions in a specific process or analysis, highlighting their role in providing data or insights."},
{"determine_component_scores_and_evaluate_composite_endpoints_in_an_exploratory_retrospective_analysis": "Determining component scores and evaluating composite endpoints in an exploratory retrospective analysis involves analyzing historical data to assess various factors and outcomes related to MASH clinical trial endpoints, using AIM-MASH predictions as a tool for this evaluation."},
{"aim_mash_derived_histologic_changes": "AIM-MASH-derived histologic changes refer to the alterations in tissue structure and cellular composition identified by the AIM-MASH model in patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. These changes are critical for evaluating the efficacy of treatments in clinical trials."},
{"aim_mash_derived_histologic_changes__were_compared_with__a_consensus_determination_of_the_endpoints_by_three_expert_pathologists": "The phrase \"were compared with\" indicates an analytical process where the histologic changes identified by AIM-MASH were assessed against another standard. This comparison aims to validate the accuracy and reliability of AIM-MASH predictions in determining clinical trial endpoints."},
{"a_consensus_determination_of_the_endpoints_by_three_expert_pathologists": "A consensus determination of the endpoints by three expert pathologists represents the gold standard against which AIM-MASH predictions are compared. This consensus involves the collective judgment of experienced professionals who evaluate histologic changes to determine the efficacy of treatments for MASH and fibrosis."},
{"aim_mash_based_grading_and_staging": "AIM-MASH-based grading and staging refers to the evaluation method using the AIM-MASH system for assessing histologic changes in patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. This system is designed to predict and score histologic changes from baseline in clinical trials."},
{"aim_mash_based_grading_and_staging__were_comparable_to__those_of_mean_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus": "The phrase \"were comparable to\" indicates that the results or performance of AIM-MASH-based grading and staging were similar in effectiveness or accuracy to another method, in this case, the consensus determination by expert pathologists."},
{"those_of_mean_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus": "\"Those of mean individual pathologist versus consensus\" describes the comparison benchmark, which is the average agreement rate between individual pathologists' assessments and a consensus decision made by a group of expert pathologists. This benchmark is used to evaluate the performance of the AIM-MASH system."},
{"fibrosis_improvement_without_worsening_of_mash": "Fibrosis improvement without worsening of MASH refers to a clinical scenario where there is an enhancement in the fibrotic condition of the liver without any deterioration in the metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH) condition. This is a critical endpoint in evaluating the efficacy of treatments for noncirrhotic MASH patients."},
{"fibrosis_improvement_without_worsening_of_mash__percentage_agreement_rates_were__0_80_95_ci_0_76_0_84_and_95_ci_0_77_0_83_respectively": "The phrase \"percentage agreement rates were\" indicates the level of concordance or agreement between different assessments or evaluations. In this context, it refers to the comparison of agreement rates between AIM-MASH predictions and consensus determinations by expert pathologists regarding the histologic endpoints."},
{"0_80_95_ci_0_76_0_84_and_95_ci_0_77_0_83_respectively": "The object \"0.80 (95% CI 0.76\u20130.84 and 95% CI 0.77\u20130.83, respectively)\" represents the percentage agreement rates for fibrosis improvement without worsening of MASH. These rates are given with their respective 95% confidence intervals, indicating the reliability and precision of the agreement between AIM-MASH predictions and consensus pathologist evaluations."},
{"mash_resolution_without_worsening_of_fibrosis": "MASH resolution without worsening of fibrosis refers to a clinical trial endpoint where patients with noncirrhotic MASH show resolution of the disease without any deterioration in liver fibrosis. This is a critical endpoint for evaluating the efficacy of treatments in MASH clinical trials.|Here is another description: MASH resolution without worsening of fibrosis refers to a clinical trial endpoint where patients with noncirrhotic MASH show complete resolution of the disease without any progression in liver fibrosis. This is a critical measure of efficacy in MASH clinical trials, particularly in phase 3 trials where both MASH resolution and fibrosis improvement are assessed."},
{"mash_resolution_without_worsening_of_fibrosis__model_versus_consensus_agreement_was__0_86_95_ci_0_82_0_89": "The predicate \"model versus consensus agreement was\" indicates a comparison between the agreement rates of an AI-based model (AIM-MASH) and a consensus determination by expert pathologists. This comparison assesses how closely the model's predictions align with the expert consensus on histologic changes."},
{"0_86_95_ci_0_82_0_89": "The object \"0.86 (95% CI 0.82\u20130.89)\" represents the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH model and the consensus of expert pathologists for the endpoint of MASH resolution without worsening of fibrosis. This value, with a 95% confidence interval, quantifies the level of concordance between the model's predictions and the expert consensus."},
{"mash_resolution_without_worsening_of_fibrosis__pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_was__0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_86": "The predicate \"pathologist versus consensus agreement was\" indicates the level of agreement between individual pathologists' assessments and a consensus determination made by a group of expert pathologists. This metric is used to evaluate the reliability and consistency of histologic endpoint assessments in clinical trials."},
{"0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_86": "The object \"0.82 (95% CI 0.79\u20130.86)\" represents the agreement rate between individual pathologists and the consensus determination for the endpoint of MASH resolution without worsening of fibrosis. This value, with a 95% confidence interval, indicates a high level of agreement, suggesting that individual pathologists' assessments are largely consistent with the consensus view."},
{"2_point_reduction_in_mas": "A \u22652-point reduction in MAS refers to a significant decrease in the Model for Assessment of Steatohepatitis score, indicating an improvement in the severity of the disease.|Here is another description: A \u22652-point reduction in MAS refers to a significant decrease in the Metabolic Activity Score, which is a measure used in the assessment of histologic changes in patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. This reduction is an important endpoint in clinical trials evaluating the efficacy of treatments for MASH."},
{"2_point_reduction_in_mas__model_versus_consensus_agreement_was__0_79_95_ci_0_74_0_83": "The predicate \"model versus consensus agreement was\" indicates the level of agreement between the AI-based model's assessment and the consensus determination by expert pathologists regarding the histologic changes in MASH."},
{"0_79_95_ci_0_74_0_83": "The object \"0.79 (95% CI 0.74\u20130.83)\" represents the agreement rate between the AI-based model and the consensus of expert pathologists, with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.74 to 0.83, indicating a high level of concordance in assessing a \u22652-point reduction in MAS."},
{"2_point_reduction_in_mas__pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_was__0_77_95_ci_0_74_0_81": "The predicate \"pathologist versus consensus agreement was\" indicates the level of agreement between the assessment made by an individual pathologist and the consensus determination made by a group of expert pathologists. This metric is used to evaluate the reliability and consistency of histologic endpoint assessments in clinical trials."},
{"0_77_95_ci_0_74_0_81": "The object \"0.77 (95% CI 0.74\u20130.81)\" represents the agreement rate between the assessments made by individual pathologists and the consensus determination by expert pathologists for a \u22652-point reduction in MAS. This rate, with a 95% confidence interval, indicates the degree of consistency and reliability in the histologic endpoint assessment."},
{"aim_mash__ability_to_measure__histologic_response_to_a_therapeutic": "The ability to measure refers to AIM-MASH's capability to evaluate and quantify the histologic changes in response to therapeutic interventions. This involves comparing model predictions with clinical pathology (CP) measurements to determine the effectiveness of treatments."},
{"histologic_response_to_a_therapeutic": "Histologic response to a therapeutic pertains to the changes observed in tissue samples, such as improvements in fibrosis stages or resolution of MASH, following the administration of a drug or combination of drugs. This response is crucial for determining the efficacy of treatments in clinical trials."},
{"retrospective_analysis": "A retrospective analysis is a type of study that looks back at data that has already been collected to evaluate the effectiveness or outcomes of a treatment or intervention. In this context, it involves reviewing past data from a clinical trial to assess drug efficacy."},
{"retrospective_analysis__performed_in__atlas_phase_2b_clinical_trial": "The phrase \"performed in\" indicates that the retrospective analysis was carried out using data specifically from the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial. This means the analysis was based on the results and observations gathered during this particular trial."},
{"atlas_phase_2b_clinical_trial": "The ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial is a study designed to evaluate the efficacy of two drugs, cilofexor and firsocostat, both as monotherapies and in combination, in patients with advanced fibrosis. The trial aimed to measure improvements in histology and other clinical endpoints.|Here is another description: The ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial is a study designed to assess the efficacy of certain drugs in treating patients with advanced fibrosis. It specifically focuses on evaluating the effectiveness of these drugs in improving histologic outcomes in patients with F3\u2013F4 fibrosis."},
{"atlas_phase_2b_clinical_trial__evaluated_the_efficacy_of__cilofexor_cilo_and_firsocostat_fir": "The predicate \"evaluated the efficacy of\" refers to the process by which the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial assessed the effectiveness and therapeutic benefits of specific drugs. This evaluation aimed to determine how well the drugs performed in improving patient outcomes as measured by histologic response."},
{"cilofexor_cilo_and_firsocostat_fir": "Cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR) are two drugs that were evaluated in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial. These drugs were tested both as monotherapies and in combination to see their efficacy in treating advanced fibrosis in patients.|Here is another description: Cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR) are two drugs that were tested in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial for their efficacy in treating patients with advanced fibrosis."},
{"cilofexor_cilo_and_firsocostat_fir__evaluated_as__monotherapies_and_in_combination_cilo_fir": "The predicate \"evaluated as\" indicates that the efficacy of cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR) was assessed in the clinical trial."},
{"monotherapies_and_in_combination_cilo_fir": "Monotherapies and in combination (CILO + FIR) refer to the different treatment regimens tested in the trial, where the drugs were administered either individually or together."},
{"patients": "The patients in this context are participants in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial, specifically those who were administered either cilofexor, firsocostat, or a combination of both as part of the study to evaluate drug efficacy.|Here is another description: The patients in this context are individuals with advanced MASH fibrosis who participated in a clinical trial. They underwent baseline and week 48 biopsies as part of the trial's evaluation process."},
{"patients__had__advanced_f3_f4_fibrosis": "The predicate \"had\" indicates the presence or condition of advanced fibrosis in the patients, which was a criterion for their inclusion in the clinical trial and a factor in assessing the efficacy of the treatments."},
{"advanced_f3_f4_fibrosis": "Advanced (F3\u2013F4) fibrosis refers to severe scarring of the liver, categorized as stages F3 and F4 on the fibrosis scale. This condition was present in the patients participating in the ATLAS trial, and its presence was a key factor in evaluating the effectiveness of the drugs being tested."},
{"no_treatment_arm": "The \"no treatment arm\" refers to a control group in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial that did not receive any of the tested drugs (cilofexor, firsocostat, or their combination) and served as a baseline for comparing the efficacy of the treatments."},
{"no_treatment_arm__achieved__statistical_significance_for_the_primary_endpoint": "The predicate \"achieved\" indicates that the control group in the clinical trial reached a certain level of outcome or result in the study. In this context, it is used to describe whether the control group met the required statistical criteria for evaluating the primary endpoint."},
{"statistical_significance_for_the_primary_endpoint": "\"Statistical significance for the primary endpoint\" refers to the predetermined level of statistical evidence needed to conclude that the observed effect in the clinical trial is not due to random chance. In this case, the control group did not reach this level of significance for the primary endpoint, meaning the results were not strong enough to confidently attribute any observed effects to the treatments being tested."},
{"cohort_that_received_the_combination_of_cilo_fir": "The cohort refers to a group of patients who participated in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial and were administered a combination of cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR). These patients were specifically selected for having advanced fibrosis (F3\u2013F4) related to MASH."},
{"cohort_that_received_the_combination_of_cilo_fir__showed__the_greatest_improvement_in_histology_relative_to_placebo": "The predicate \"showed\" indicates that the cohort demonstrated a measurable outcome in response to the treatment. In this context, it signifies that the cohort exhibited a specific result following the administration of the CILO + FIR combination therapy."},
{"the_greatest_improvement_in_histology_relative_to_placebo": "The greatest improvement in histology relative to placebo refers to the most significant positive change observed in the tissue structure and cellular composition of the liver, as assessed through histologic evaluation. This improvement was more pronounced in the CILO + FIR treatment group compared to the placebo group in the clinical trial."},
{"aim_mash_models": "AIM-MASH models are AI-based systems designed to assess treatment response in clinical trials for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). These models are specifically developed to measure histologic changes in response to therapeutic interventions.|Here is another description: AIM-MASH models are advanced computational models designed to assess and predict the efficacy of drug treatments for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) fibrosis based on imaging data.|Here is another description: AIM-MASH models are advanced computational tools designed to assess the efficacy of treatments for metabolic associated steatohepatitis (MASH) by analyzing whole slide images (WSIs) from patient biopsies."},
{"aim_mash_models__deployed_on__digitized_wsis_n_99_from_baseline_and_week_48_biopsies": "The predicate \"deployed on\" indicates the action of utilizing or applying the AIM-MASH models to a specific dataset. In this context, it means that the models were used to analyze and generate predictions based on the provided dataset of digitized whole slide images."},
{"digitized_wsis_n_99_from_baseline_and_week_48_biopsies": "Digitized WSIs (whole slide images) from baseline and week 48 biopsies represent the dataset on which the AIM-MASH models were applied. These images, totaling 99 in number, were used to evaluate the histologic response to treatment in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial."},
{"model_predictions": "Model predictions refer to the outcomes generated by the AIM-MASH models when applied to digitized whole slide images (WSIs) from clinical trial biopsies. These predictions are used to assess treatment response in patients.|Here is another description: Model predictions refer to the outcomes generated by the AIM-MASH models when applied to digitized whole slide images (WSIs) from baseline and week 48 biopsies in the ATLAS clinical trial. These predictions are aimed at assessing histologic response to therapeutic interventions."},
{"model_predictions__generated_for__ordinal_mash_crn_grades_stages": "The phrase \"generated for\" indicates that the model predictions were specifically created to evaluate and provide data on the ordinal MASH CRN grades/stages, which are critical metrics in assessing the histologic response to treatment in clinical trials."},
{"ordinal_mash_crn_grades_stages": "Ordinal MASH CRN grades/stages are standardized measurements used to classify the severity of metabolic associated steatohepatitis (MASH) and the stage of liver fibrosis. These grades/stages are essential for determining treatment efficacy and patient outcomes in clinical trials."},
{"model_predictions__compared_with__cp_measurements_of_grades_stages_for_the_trial_s_primary_and_two_exploratory_endpoints": "The phrase \"compared with\" indicates a direct evaluation or analysis of the model predictions against another set of measurements. In this context, it signifies that the model predictions are being assessed in relation to the CP measurements to determine their accuracy and effectiveness in detecting treatment response."},
{"cp_measurements_of_grades_stages_for_the_trial_s_primary_and_two_exploratory_endpoints": "CP measurements of grades/stages for the trial\u2019s primary and two exploratory endpoints refer to the conventional pathologist (CP) assessments of histologic changes in liver biopsies. These measurements are used as a benchmark to evaluate the performance of the AIM-MASH model predictions in the ATLAS clinical trial."},
{"aim_mash__detected__a_greater_proportion_of_treatment_responders_in_the_cilo_fir_group_for_all_three_endpoints_measured_compared_with_the_cp": "The predicate \"detected\" refers to the action performed by AIM-MASH in identifying and quantifying treatment responders based on histologic improvements in patients. This involves comparing model predictions with clinical pathology (CP) measurements."},
{"a_greater_proportion_of_treatment_responders_in_the_cilo_fir_group_for_all_three_endpoints_measured_compared_with_the_cp": "The object describes the outcome where AIM-MASH identified a higher proportion of treatment responders in the group receiving the combination of cilofexor and firsocostat (CILO + FIR) across three measured endpoints compared to the clinical pathology (CP) method. This includes improvements in fibrosis stages and MASH resolution without worsening."},
{"1_stage_fibrosis_improvement_without_mash_worsening": "The subject refers to a specific outcome in a clinical trial, where there is an improvement in fibrosis by at least one stage without any worsening of Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). This outcome is critical in evaluating the efficacy of treatments aimed at improving liver health in patients with advanced fibrosis."},
{"1_stage_fibrosis_improvement_without_mash_worsening__percentage__27_versus_19": "The predicate indicates the percentage of patients who achieved the specified outcome in the clinical trial. It quantifies the proportion of participants who experienced at least one stage of fibrosis improvement without MASH worsening, providing a measure of treatment effectiveness."},
{"27_versus_19": "The object represents the percentage values of 27% and 19%, which compare the effectiveness of two different evaluation methods in detecting treatment response. Specifically, 27% is the percentage of responders identified by AIM-MASH, while 19% is the percentage identified by the conventional pathologist (CP) method."},
{"mash_resolution_without_fibrosis_worsening": "MASH resolution without fibrosis worsening refers to the scenario where patients with Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH) experience a complete resolution of the disease without any worsening of their liver fibrosis. This is a significant outcome in clinical trials evaluating the efficacy of treatments for MASH, as it indicates not only the elimination of the disease but also the maintenance or improvement of liver health."},
{"mash_resolution_without_fibrosis_worsening__percentage__24_versus": "The predicate \"percentage\" in this context refers to the proportion or rate at which a specific outcome occurs within a given population or sample. In clinical trials, percentages are often used to express the efficacy of a treatment, such as the percentage of patients who achieve a certain outcome like MASH resolution without fibrosis worsening."},
{"24_versus": "The object \"24% versus\" indicates a comparison of percentages between two groups, likely a treatment group and a control or placebo group in a clinical trial. The \"24%\" represents the percentage of patients in the treatment group who achieved MASH resolution without fibrosis worsening, while the \"versus\" suggests that this percentage is being compared to another percentage, presumably from the control or placebo group."},
