{"model_derived_scores": "Model-derived scores refer to the numerical values generated by an artificial intelligence model, specifically AIM-MASH, to assess and grade medical conditions based on biopsy data."},
{"model_derived_scores__distinguished__fibrosis_stages_f1_f3_versus_f4_and_mas_4": "The predicate 'distinguished' indicates that the model-derived scores were able to differentiate or identify distinct categories or stages of a medical condition, in this case, fibrosis and MAS grades."},
{"fibrosis_stages_f1_f3_versus_f4_and_mas_4": "Fibrosis stages F1\u2013F3 versus F4 and MAS \u2265 4 represent specific categories of liver condition severity. Fibrosis stages F1\u2013F3 indicate earlier or less severe stages of liver fibrosis, while F4 indicates a more advanced stage. MAS \u2265 4 signifies a higher grade of steatohepatitis activity, with each component grade being at least 1, compared to MAS < 4 which indicates lower activity."},
{"aim_mash_agreement": "The AIM-MASH agreement refers to the level of concordance or agreement achieved by the AIM-MASH system in grading or staging medical conditions, specifically in the context of clinical trials for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis).|Here is another description: The AIM-MASH agreement refers to the level of concordance or agreement between the AI model's assessments and the consensus grading/staging of liver biopsies in clinical trials. It is used to evaluate the model's performance in distinguishing different stages of liver fibrosis and MASH severity."},
{"aim_mash_agreement__was_comparable_to__consensus_and_each_pathologist": "The phrase \"was comparable to\" indicates that the level of agreement or performance of the AIM-MASH system is being compared to another standard or benchmark, in this case, the consensus and individual pathologists."},
{"consensus_and_each_pathologist": "The object \"consensus and each pathologist\" refers to the collective agreement reached by a group of experts (consensus) and the individual assessments made by each pathologist, serving as benchmarks for evaluating the performance of the AIM-MASH system."},
{"bar_plots": "Bar plots are graphical representations used to display and compare numerical data across different categories. In the context of clinical trials, they are employed to visually represent the outcomes or scores derived from various assessments."},
{"bar_plots__represent__the_point_estimate_of_each_enrollment_criteria_endpoint": "The predicate \"represent\" indicates that the bar plots serve as a visual depiction or illustration of the point estimates for each enrollment criteria endpoint. This means that the bar plots are used to show the specific values or outcomes associated with the criteria used for trial enrollment."},
{"the_point_estimate_of_each_enrollment_criteria_endpoint": "The point estimate of each enrollment criteria endpoint refers to the specific value or measurement that represents the outcome of the enrollment criteria in a clinical trial. These point estimates are crucial for evaluating whether patients meet the necessary criteria for enrollment and for assessing the performance of diagnostic models like AIM-MASH."},
{"whiskers": "Whiskers in this context refer to the visual elements in a bar plot that indicate the range of the 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for the data being represented.|Here is another description: Whiskers in this context refer to the lines extending from the ends of the box in a box plot, which are used to represent the variability outside the upper and lower quartiles."},
{"whiskers__represent__the_95_cis_estimated_using_10_000_bootstrap_samples": "The predicate'represent' indicates that the whiskers are used to visually depict or symbolize the 95% CIs in the bar plots, providing a graphical representation of the variability and uncertainty in the data."},
{"the_95_cis_estimated_using_10_000_bootstrap_samples": "The 95% CIs estimated using 10,000 bootstrap samples are statistical measures that provide a range within which the true value of a parameter is expected to lie with 95% confidence. These CIs are calculated through a resampling technique called bootstrapping, which involves repeatedly sampling the data with replacement to estimate the distribution of the statistic."},
{"aim_mash_agreement__was_comparable_to__that_of_an_average_pathologist": "The predicate \"was comparable to\" indicates that the AIM-MASH agreement's performance in grading/staging liver biopsies was on par with the performance of an average pathologist. This suggests that the AI model's assessments were as reliable and accurate as those made by a typical human pathologist."},
{"that_of_an_average_pathologist": "The object \"that of an average pathologist\" refers to the level of agreement or concordance typically achieved by a human pathologist when grading/staging liver biopsies. It serves as a benchmark for evaluating the performance of the AIM-MASH agreement, indicating that the AI model's assessments were as good as, or equivalent to, those made by a typical human pathologist."},
{"assessment": "The assessment refers to the evaluation process conducted using the AIM-MASH model to determine agreement with consensus on efficacy endpoints in a clinical trial.|Here is another description: The assessment refers to the evaluation process using the continuous scoring system developed to detect histologic changes within the range of an ordinal bin. This system is designed to facilitate interpretation and navigation between ordinal and continuous scoring systems."},
{"assessment__was_performed_on__an_external_held_out_validation_dataset_from_a_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"was performed on\" indicates that the assessment was carried out using a specific dataset to evaluate the model's performance in grading and staging."},
{"an_external_held_out_validation_dataset_from_a_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The object, an external held-out validation dataset from a phase 2b MASH clinical trial, consists of biopsies from patients meeting specific endpoints, used to validate the AIM-MASH model's efficacy in a real-world clinical setting."},
{"biopsies": "Biopsies refer to the tissue samples obtained from patients in clinical trials, specifically from the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trials, used to assess various medical conditions and endpoints."},
{"biopsies__were_of_patients_meeting__the_following_endpoints_fibrosis_improvement_without_mash_worsening_n_279_mash_resolution_without_fibrosis_worsening_n_279_and_mas_reduction_2_n_326": "The phrase \"were of patients meeting\" indicates that the biopsies were taken from patients who satisfied specific clinical criteria or endpoints in the trials, allowing for the evaluation of treatment efficacy and disease progression."},
{"the_following_endpoints_fibrosis_improvement_without_mash_worsening_n_279_mash_resolution_without_fibrosis_worsening_n_279_and_mas_reduction_2_n_326": "The following endpoints refer to specific clinical outcomes measured in the trials: fibrosis improvement without worsening of Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH), MASH resolution without fibrosis worsening, and a reduction in the Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis Activity Score (MAS) of at least 2 points. These endpoints are critical for evaluating the effectiveness of treatments in clinical trials."},
{"5": "The subject \"5%\" refers to a specific percentage value that is part of a statistical comparison or analysis, likely indicating a threshold or a particular point of interest in the data being discussed.|Here is another description: The subject, 5%, represents a specific percentage value that is part of a statistical or analytical result, likely indicating a proportion or rate within a study or experiment.|Here is another description: The object '5' represents the specific affiliation number assigned to Arun J. Sanyal, which likely corresponds to a particular institution or group within the collaborative project."},
{"5__2_point_reduction_in_mas__60_versus_35": "The predicate \"\u22652-point reduction in MAS\" describes a condition or outcome where there is a reduction of at least 2 points in a measurement scale referred to as MAS (which could stand for a specific assessment scale in the context of the study or analysis)."},
{"60_versus_35": "The object \"60% versus 35%\" represents a comparative statistical result, showing two different percentages that are being compared. This likely indicates the proportion of subjects or patients achieving the condition described by the predicate in two different groups or under different conditions."},
