{"bl_cfib_3_6": "BL cFib < 3.6 refers to a baseline measurement of circulating fibrinogen levels that are less than 3.6 g/L."},
{"bl_cfib_3_6__log_rank_p__0_0001": "The log-rank P value is a statistical measure used to compare the survival distributions of two or more groups. It assesses whether there is a significant difference in survival times between the groups being compared."},
{"0_0001": "A log-rank P value of < 0.0001 indicates a highly statistically significant difference in survival distributions between the groups being compared, suggesting that the difference is unlikely to have occurred by chance."},
{"bl_cfib_4_6": "BL cFib < 4.6 refers to a condition where the baseline circulating fibrinogen level is less than 4.6 grams per liter. This is a specific threshold used to categorize patients based on their fibrinogen levels at the start of a study or treatment."},
{"bl_cfib_4_6__log_rank_p__0_03": "The log-rank _P_ is a statistical test used to compare the survival distributions of two or more groups. In this context, it is used to determine if there is a significant difference in survival or event rates between patients with BL cFib < 4.6 and those with BL cFib \u2265 4.6."},
{"0_03": "The value 0.03 represents the p-value obtained from the log-rank test. A p-value of 0.03 indicates that there is a statistically significant difference between the groups being compared, with a 3% chance that the observed difference is due to random variation rather than a true effect."},
