{"ai_based_continuous_scores": "AI-based continuous scores refer to the continuous numerical values generated by an artificial intelligence system to assess and quantify certain medical conditions or parameters, in this case, MASH CRN scores."},
{"ai_based_continuous_scores__correlation_with__mean_scores_across_three_pathologists_from_emminence": "The correlation with indicates a statistical relationship or association between the AI-based continuous scores and another set of scores, specifically the mean scores across three pathologists from EMMINENCE."},
{"mean_scores_across_three_pathologists_from_emminence": "Mean scores across three pathologists from EMMINENCE represent the average scores given by three different pathologists who evaluated the same medical condition or parameter using their expertise and judgment."},
{"results": "The results refer to the outcomes or findings presented in the analytic performance test set, specifically focusing on the correlation of AI-based continuous scores with mean scores from pathologists."},
{"results__shown_for__both_ai_derived_ordinal_bins_blue_and_pathologist_derived_ordinal_bins_gray": "The predicate \"shown for\" indicates that the results are being presented or displayed in relation to both AI-derived and pathologist-derived ordinal bins. It signifies that the results are applicable or relevant to these two categories."},
{"both_ai_derived_ordinal_bins_blue_and_pathologist_derived_ordinal_bins_gray": "The object consists of two elements: AI-derived ordinal bins (blue) and pathologist-derived ordinal bins (gray). These bins represent categorized data derived from AI algorithms and human pathologists, respectively, and are visually distinguished by color in the context provided."},
{"plotted_values": "The plotted values refer to the data points shown in the graph or figure, which represent the results of a statistical analysis comparing AI-based continuous scores with mean scores from pathologists."},
{"plotted_values__derived_from__kendall_s_tau_\u03c4_rank_correlation_analysis": "The phrase \"derived from\" indicates that the plotted values are the outcome or result of a specific statistical method or analysis. In this context, it means that the values shown in the plot are the results obtained after applying Kendall\u2019s tau (\u03c4) rank correlation analysis to the data."},
{"kendall_s_tau_\u03c4_rank_correlation_analysis": "Kendall\u2019s tau (\u03c4) rank correlation analysis is a non-parametric statistical method used to measure the correlation between two ranked variables. It assesses the similarity of the orderings of the data when ranked by each of the quantities. In this context, it was used to analyze the correlation between AI-based continuous scores and mean scores from pathologists."},
{"fdr_correction_of_p_values": "FDR correction of P values refers to the adjustment of P values to control the false discovery rate, which is the expected proportion of false discoveries amongst the total discoveries."},
{"fdr_correction_of_p_values__performed_using__the_benjamini_hochberg_procedure": "Performed using indicates the method or procedure applied to carry out a specific task or analysis, in this case, the adjustment of P values."},
{"the_benjamini_hochberg_procedure": "The Benjamini\u2013Hochberg procedure is a widely used method for controlling the false discovery rate in multiple hypothesis testing. It adjusts the P values to account for the number of tests performed, thereby reducing the likelihood of false positives."},
{"filled_circles": "Filled circles in the context refer to specific data points on a graph or chart that are used to represent certain values or outcomes. These circles are typically used in scientific figures to denote particular results or measurements."},
{"filled_circles__indicate__statistical_significance_fdr_corrected_p_0_05": "The predicate \"indicate\" in this context means that the filled circles on the graph serve as a visual cue or marker to show that a certain condition or threshold has been met. In this case, they signify that the data points have achieved statistical significance after applying the False Discovery Rate (FDR) correction."},
{"statistical_significance_fdr_corrected_p_0_05": "Statistical significance, FDR-corrected P < 0.05, refers to a common threshold used in scientific research to determine whether the observed results are likely not due to random chance. An FDR-corrected P value less than 0.05 means that the probability of the results occurring by chance is less than 5%, after adjusting for multiple comparisons using the Benjamini\u2013Hochberg procedure."},
{"cfib_versus_cpa_measurements": "The subject refers to the comparison of cFib (continuous fibrosis scores) and CPA (proportionate area fibrosis measurements) in the context of a clinical trial. This comparison is aimed at evaluating the effectiveness of a treatment versus a placebo."},
{"cfib_versus_cpa_measurements__in__primary_endpoint_responders_in_the_atlas_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"in\" signifies the inclusion or examination of cFib and CPA measurements within a specific group of patients. In this case, it indicates that these measurements are being analyzed among those who responded to the primary endpoint of the ATLAS clinical trial."},
{"primary_endpoint_responders_in_the_atlas_clinical_trial": "The object describes the group of patients known as \"primary endpoint responders in the ATLAS clinical trial.\" These are individuals who showed a positive response to the primary endpoint of the trial, which is a key measure of the treatment's effectiveness. The analysis of cFib and CPA measurements within this group helps to assess the impact of the treatment on fibrosis levels."},
{"cfib_and_cpa": "cFib and CPA are metrics used to measure the extent of fibrosis in liver tissue. cFib refers to continuous fibrosis scores, which provide a quantitative measure of fibrosis, while CPA (continuous proportionate area) measures the proportionate area of fibrosis in the liver."},
{"cfib_and_cpa__compared_between__patients_receiving_treatment_and_placebo_using_two_sided_mann_whitney_u_tests": "The comparison between cFib and CPA measurements was conducted using two-sided Mann\u2013Whitney U tests. This statistical test was employed to determine if there were significant differences in fibrosis scores between patients receiving treatment and those receiving a placebo."},
{"patients_receiving_treatment_and_placebo_using_two_sided_mann_whitney_u_tests": "Patients receiving treatment refers to individuals in the ATLAS clinical trial who were administered a specific therapy aimed at reducing liver fibrosis. Placebo patients, on the other hand, received a non-active treatment as a control group to compare the efficacy of the actual treatment."},
{"continuous_fibrosis_scores": "Continuous fibrosis scores refer to the AI-based measurements of liver fibrosis severity, which are derived from the analysis of liver tissue images. These scores provide a quantitative assessment of the extent of fibrosis, which is crucial for evaluating the progression of liver diseases such as MASH (Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"continuous_fibrosis_scores__significantly_reduced_in__treated_patients_n_17_versus_placebo_patients_n_6_mann_whitney_u_20_0_p_0_02": "Significantly reduced in indicates a statistical comparison where the continuous fibrosis scores in one group are notably lower than in another group. This reduction is determined to be statistically significant based on the Mann\u2013Whitney U test, suggesting that the treatment has a meaningful impact on reducing fibrosis scores."},
{"treated_patients_n_17_versus_placebo_patients_n_6_mann_whitney_u_20_0_p_0_02": "Treated patients (n = 17) versus placebo patients (n = 6; Mann\u2013Whitney U = 20.0, P = 0.02) refers to the two groups being compared in the study. The treated group consists of 17 patients who received a specific treatment, while the placebo group includes 6 patients who received a placebo. The Mann\u2013Whitney U test result (U = 20.0, P = 0.02) indicates that the difference in continuous fibrosis scores between these two groups is statistically significant."},
{"proportionate_area_fibrosis_measurements": "Proportionate area fibrosis measurements refer to the quantitative assessment of the extent of fibrosis in a given area, typically used in medical imaging and pathology to evaluate the severity of fibrotic changes in tissues."},
{"proportionate_area_fibrosis_measurements__not_significantly_reduced__mann_whitney_u_39_0_p_0_21": "The predicate \"not significantly reduced\" indicates that the proportionate area fibrosis measurements did not show a statistically significant decrease when compared between two groups, as determined by a specific statistical test."},
{"mann_whitney_u_39_0_p_0_21": "The object \"Mann\u2013Whitney U = 39.0, P = 0.21\" represents the results of a Mann\u2013Whitney U test, a nonparametric test used to compare differences between two independent groups. Here, the U value of 39.0 and a P-value of 0.21 suggest that the difference in proportionate area fibrosis measurements between the groups is not statistically significant."},
{"cfib_and_cpa_values": "cFib and CPA values refer to continuous fibrosis scores and proportionate area fibrosis measurements, which are used to assess the extent of fibrosis in patients."},
{"cfib_and_cpa_values__for_patients_classified_as__nonresponders_n_76_in_the_treatment_n_45_or_placebo_n_31_group": "The phrase \"for patients classified as\" indicates a categorization or grouping of patients based on their response to a treatment or placebo, specifically within the context of a clinical trial."},
{"nonresponders_n_76_in_the_treatment_n_45_or_placebo_n_31_group": "Nonresponders (n = 76) in the treatment (n = 45) or placebo (n = 31) group refers to a subset of patients in a clinical trial who did not show a positive response to either the treatment or the placebo, with specific numbers indicating the count of patients in each category."},
{"boxes": "Boxes in the context refer to the visual elements in a box plot that are used to represent statistical data. They are graphical representations that help in understanding the distribution of data points."},
{"boxes__represent__the_25th_percentile": "The predicate'represent' indicates that the boxes in the box plot are used to symbolize or stand in for specific statistical values within the data set, in this case, the 25th percentile."},
{"the_25th_percentile": "The 25th percentile, also known as the first quartile, is a value below which 25% of the data points fall. In the context of a box plot, it is one of the key statistics that helps in understanding the spread and central tendency of the data."},
{"whiskers__extend_to__points_that_lie_within_1_5_fold_of_the_inter_quartile_range_of_the_25th_and_75th_percentiles": "The predicate \"extend to\" describes the action or relationship where the whiskers reach out to specific data points within a defined statistical range."},
{"points_that_lie_within_1_5_fold_of_the_inter_quartile_range_of_the_25th_and_75th_percentiles": "The points that lie within 1.5-fold of the inter-quartile range of the 25th and 75th percentiles are the specific data points to which the whiskers extend. These points are determined by calculating 1.5 times the inter-quartile range (the difference between the 75th and 25th percentiles) and then identifying the data points that fall within this calculated range."},
{"stratification": "Stratification refers to the process of dividing patients into different groups based on specific criteria. In this context, it involves categorizing patients with BL F3 or F4 fibrosis from the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts.|Here is another description: Stratification refers to the process of categorizing patients based on specific criteria, in this case, their progression rates in clinical trials.|Here is another description: Stratification refers to the process of dividing patients into different groups based on specific criteria. In this context, it involves categorizing patients with BL F3 or F4 fibrosis from the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts into rapid and slow progressors."},
{"stratification__of__patients_with_bl_f3_or_f4_fibrosis_from_stellar_3_and_stellar_4_trial_cohorts": "The predicate \"of\" in this context indicates the relationship between the process of stratification and the specific group of patients it is applied to. It signifies that the stratification is performed on or pertains to the mentioned patient cohort."},
{"patients_with_bl_f3_or_f4_fibrosis_from_stellar_3_and_stellar_4_trial_cohorts": "Patients with BL F3 or F4 fibrosis from the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts are the specific group being stratified. These patients have been diagnosed with either F3 or F4 fibrosis at baseline and are part of the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 clinical trials."},
{"stratification__into__rapid_red_and_slow_orange_progressors": "The predicate \"into\" signifies the action of dividing or categorizing the subject (patients) based on their progression rates into distinct groups."},
{"rapid_red_and_slow_orange_progressors": "Rapid (red) and slow (orange) progressors are the two distinct groups into which patients are categorized based on their progression rates, with rapid progressors showing quicker disease progression and slow progressors showing slower progression."},
{"stratification__based_on__continuous_score_cutoffs_of_3_6_and_4_6_respectively": "The predicate \"based on\" indicates the criteria or method used for the stratification. Here, it specifies that the division of patients into rapid and slow progressors is determined by certain continuous score cutoffs."},
{"continuous_score_cutoffs_of_3_6_and_4_6_respectively": "The continuous score cutoffs of 3.6 and 4.6 are the specific values used to stratify patients. These cutoffs are applied to categorize patients into rapid (red) and slow (orange) progressors, respectively, based on their scores."},
{"kaplan_meier_and_cox_proportional_hazards_regression_analyses": "Kaplan\u2013Meier and Cox proportional hazards regression analyses are statistical methods used to analyze survival data and the time to occurrence of events. These analyses help in understanding the impact of different factors on the progression of diseases."},
{"kaplan_meier_and_cox_proportional_hazards_regression_analyses__are_shown__for_f3_and_f4_fibrosis": "The phrase \"are shown\" indicates that the results or outcomes of the Kaplan\u2013Meier and Cox proportional hazards regression analyses have been presented or displayed, likely in a graphical or tabular format within the context provided."},
{"for_f3_and_f4_fibrosis": "F3 and F4 fibrosis refer to different stages of liver fibrosis, a condition where excessive connective tissue builds up in the liver. F3 indicates severe fibrosis, while F4 indicates cirrhosis, the most severe stage of liver fibrosis. The analyses are specifically conducted to evaluate these stages."},
{"f3_fibrosis": "F3 fibrosis refers to a stage of liver fibrosis where there is significant scarring but the liver structure is still preserved. It is a critical stage in the progression of liver disease, often assessed in clinical trials to evaluate the efficacy of treatments.|Here is another description: F3 fibrosis refers to a stage of liver fibrosis where there is significant scarring but the liver structure is still largely preserved. It is a critical stage in the progression of liver disease, often assessed in clinical trials to evaluate the efficacy of treatments."},
{"f3_fibrosis__log_rank_statistic__31_0": "The log-rank statistic is a statistical method used to compare the survival distributions of two samples. In this context, it is used to assess the difference in progression rates between patients with different fibrosis stages in clinical trials."},
{"31_0": "The value 31.0 represents the log-rank statistic calculated for patients with F3 fibrosis in the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts. This statistic indicates a significant difference in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors within this fibrosis stage."},
{"f3_fibrosis__p_value__2_6_10_8": "The P-value is a statistical measure that helps determine the significance of results in a hypothesis test. In this context, it indicates the probability that the observed differences in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors with F3 fibrosis are due to random chance."},
{"2_6_10_8": "The P-value of 2.6 \u00d7 10 [\u22128] is an extremely low value, suggesting that the differences in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors with F3 fibrosis are statistically significant and not likely due to random chance. This strong statistical significance supports the validity of the observed results."},
{"f4_fibrosis": "F4 fibrosis refers to a severe stage of liver fibrosis, indicating significant scarring of the liver tissue. In the context of the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trials, patients with F4 fibrosis were categorized based on their progression rates.|Here is another description: F4 fibrosis refers to a stage of liver fibrosis where significant scarring has occurred, impacting liver function and potentially leading to cirrhosis. In the context of the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trials, F4 fibrosis is used to stratify patients based on their progression rates."},
{"f4_fibrosis__log_rank_statistic__4_8": "The log-rank statistic is a statistical measure used in survival analysis to compare the survival distributions of two or more groups. In this context, it is used to evaluate the difference in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors among patients with F4 fibrosis."},
{"4_8": "The log-rank statistic value of 4.8 indicates the result of the statistical test comparing the progression rates of patients with F4 fibrosis in the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trials. A log-rank statistic of 4.8, with a p-value of 0.028, suggests a significant difference in progression rates between the groups."},
{"f4_fibrosis__p_value__0_028": "The P-value is a statistical measure that helps determine the significance of results in a hypothesis test. In this context, it indicates the probability that the observed differences in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors with F4 fibrosis occurred by random chance."},
{"0_028": "The P-value of 0.028 signifies a statistically significant result, suggesting that the difference in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors with F4 fibrosis is unlikely to have occurred by random chance, with a significance level typically set at 0.05."},
{"rounded_cutoffs": "Rounded cutoffs refer to specific, adjusted threshold values that were selected for use in the analysis of patient data in the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts."},
