{"maximize_hazards": "Maximizing hazards in this context means selecting cutoff values that would result in the most significant differentiation in hazard ratios between different patient groups, thereby improving the predictive accuracy of the analysis."},
{"discriminatory_accuracy": "Discriminatory accuracy refers to the ability of a model or scoring system to correctly distinguish between different categories or outcomes. In the context of medical research, it is crucial for evaluating the effectiveness of diagnostic tools.|Here is another description: Discriminatory accuracy refers to the effectiveness of a model or method in distinguishing between different outcomes or classes. In this context, it is specifically about the ability of AI-derived continuous scores to differentiate patient outcomes."},
{"discriminatory_accuracy__of__ai_derived_continuous_scores_versus_ordinal_scores": "The phrase \"of\" in this context signifies the relationship between the discriminatory accuracy and the type of scores being compared\u2014AI-derived continuous scores versus ordinal scores. It indicates that the discriminatory accuracy is being assessed in terms of these two different scoring methods."},
{"ai_derived_continuous_scores_versus_ordinal_scores": "AI-derived continuous scores versus ordinal scores represent two different methods of scoring used in medical diagnostics. Continuous scores provide a range of values, offering more granularity, while ordinal scores categorize data into distinct, ordered groups. The comparison aims to determine which scoring method offers better discriminatory accuracy in predicting medical outcomes."},
{"discriminatory_accuracy__to_predict__progression_to_cirrhosis_left_and_lre_right_in_stellar_3_and_stellar_4_trial_cohorts": "The predicate \"to predict\" indicates the action or purpose of using discriminatory accuracy. It involves forecasting or estimating the likelihood of certain medical outcomes based on the AI-derived scores."},
{"progression_to_cirrhosis_left_and_lre_right_in_stellar_3_and_stellar_4_trial_cohorts": "Progression to cirrhosis and liver-related events (LRE) are critical outcomes in the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts. These outcomes represent advanced stages of liver disease, where cirrhosis is the severe scarring of the liver and LREs include events like liver failure or complications from cirrhosis."},
{"continuous_auc": "Continuous AUC refers to the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve derived from continuous scores, which is a measure of the accuracy of a diagnostic test or model in distinguishing between two groups.|Here is another description: Continuous AUC refers to the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve derived from continuous scores, which is a measure of the accuracy of a test in distinguishing between two groups."},
{"continuous_auc__was_significantly_greater_for__progression_to_cirrhosis_0_66_95_ci_0_60_0_71_versus_0_59_95_ci_0_55_0_60": "The phrase \"was significantly greater for\" indicates a comparative analysis where the continuous AUC showed a statistically significant improvement over another metric, in this case, ordinal scores, in predicting specific outcomes."},
{"progression_to_cirrhosis_0_66_95_ci_0_60_0_71_versus_0_59_95_ci_0_55_0_60": "The object describes the specific outcome and the comparative metrics: progression to cirrhosis with a continuous AUC of 0.66 (95% CI 0.60\u20130.71) versus an ordinal score AUC of 0.59 (95% CI 0.55\u20130.60). This comparison highlights the superior discriminatory accuracy of the continuous scores over the ordinal scores in predicting the progression to cirrhosis."},
{"continuous_auc__was_significantly_greater_for__progression_to_lre_0_61_95_ci_0_51_0_71_versus_0_54_95_ci_0_47_59": "The phrase \"was significantly greater for\" indicates a comparison where the continuous AUC showed a statistically significant higher value in one scenario over another, specifically in predicting progression to LRE."},
{"progression_to_lre_0_61_95_ci_0_51_0_71_versus_0_54_95_ci_0_47_59": "The object describes the specific values of the continuous AUC for progression to LRE, with a value of 0.61 and a 95% confidence interval of 0.51\u20130.71, compared to another value of 0.54 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.47\u20130.59."},
