{"ai_based_computational_pathology_models_and_platforms": "AI-based computational pathology models and platforms are advanced technological solutions designed to enhance the functionality and accuracy of pathology-related tasks. These models and platforms leverage artificial intelligence to analyze and interpret complex medical data, aiding in the diagnosis and treatment of diseases."},
{"ai_based_computational_pathology_models_and_platforms__were_developed_using__good_clinical_practice_good_clinical_laboratory_practice_principles": "The phrase \"were developed using\" indicates the process through which the AI-based computational pathology models and platforms were created. It highlights the methodologies and standards that were adhered to during their development."},
{"good_clinical_practice_good_clinical_laboratory_practice_principles": "Good Clinical Practice (GCP) and Good Clinical Laboratory Practice (GCLP) principles are internationally recognized ethical and scientific quality standards that ensure the design, conduct, performance, monitoring, auditing, recording, analyses, and reporting of clinical trials are conducted in a manner that ensures the safety, rights, and well-being of trial subjects, as well as the credibility of clinical data.|Here is another description: Good Clinical Practice/Good Clinical Laboratory Practice principles are a set of international standards designed to ensure the ethical and scientific conduct of clinical trials and laboratory practices. They aim to protect the rights, safety, and well-being of trial participants and ensure the credibility of clinical trial data."},
{"good_clinical_practice_good_clinical_laboratory_practice_principles__include__controlled_process_and_testing_documentation": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that Good Clinical Practice/Good Clinical Laboratory Practice principles encompass or contain specific elements or components as part of their framework. In this context, it indicates that these principles incorporate certain practices or requirements."},
{"controlled_process_and_testing_documentation": "Controlled process and testing documentation refer to the systematic and regulated procedures and records maintained during the development and operation of AI-based computational pathology models and platforms. This documentation ensures that all processes are meticulously tracked, verified, and compliant with established standards, thereby enhancing the reliability and integrity of the models."},
{"this_study": "This study refers to a research project involving the analysis of anonymized liver tissue samples and digitized images from patients with MASH who participated in various randomized controlled trials.|Here is another description: This study refers to a research project or investigation that has been conducted, the details of which are outlined in the context provided. It is an academic or scientific endeavor that has received financial support."},
{"this_study__was_conducted_in_accordance_with__the_declaration_of_helsinki_and_good_clinical_practice_guidelines": "The phrase \"was conducted in accordance with\" indicates that the study adhered to specific ethical and procedural standards during its execution."},
{"the_declaration_of_helsinki_and_good_clinical_practice_guidelines": "The Declaration of Helsinki and Good Clinical Practice guidelines are international ethical standards and principles that ensure the safety, dignity, and well-being of human subjects involved in clinical research."},
{"anonymized_liver_tissue_samples_and_digitized_wsis": "Anonymized liver tissue samples and digitized whole slide images (WSIs) of H&E- and trichrome-stained liver biopsies were collected for research purposes, ensuring patient confidentiality."},
{"anonymized_liver_tissue_samples_and_digitized_wsis__were_obtained_from__adult_patients_with_mash": "The phrase \"were obtained from\" indicates the source or origin of the anonymized liver tissue samples and digitized WSIs, highlighting the process through which these materials were acquired for the study."},
{"adult_patients_with_mash": "Adult patients with metabolic associated steatohepatitis (MASH) who had participated in various randomized controlled trials of MASH therapeutics, providing informed consent for future research and tissue histology.|Here is another description: Adult patients with MASH are individuals diagnosed with Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis, a condition characterized by liver inflammation and fat accumulation, often linked to metabolic disorders."},
{"adult_patients_with_mash__had_participated_in__complete_randomized_controlled_trials_of_mash_therapeutics": "The predicate \"had participated in\" indicates that the adult patients with MASH were involved as participants in specific medical studies designed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of treatments for their condition."},
{"complete_randomized_controlled_trials_of_mash_therapeutics": "Complete randomized controlled trials of MASH therapeutics refer to comprehensive, scientifically rigorous studies where participants are randomly assigned to different treatment groups to test the effectiveness of various therapies for MASH."},
{"approval": "Approval refers to the authorization granted by central institutional review boards for the conduct of the study, ensuring compliance with ethical standards and patient consent protocols."},
{"approval__was_previously_described_in_references__15_21_24_25": "The predicate \"was previously described in references\" indicates that detailed information about the approval process, including the specific references where it is documented, has been provided in earlier sections of the document."},
{"15_21_24_25": "The object \"15\u201321, 24, 25\" refers to the specific references within the document where detailed descriptions of the approval by central institutional review boards can be found. These references correspond to various clinical trials and their associated documentation."},
{"all_patients": "All patients refers to the participants involved in the various randomized controlled trials for MASH therapeutics, whose anonymized liver tissue samples and digitized WSIs were utilized in the study."},
{"all_patients__had_provided__informed_consent_for_future_research_and_tissue_histology": "Had provided indicates that prior to the commencement of the study, the patients completed the necessary procedures to give their consent, ensuring that their participation in future research and the use of their tissue for histology was ethically and legally sanctioned."},
{"informed_consent_for_future_research_and_tissue_histology": "Informed consent for future research and tissue histology is the formal agreement given by patients, allowing their tissue samples to be used for ongoing and future scientific studies and histological examinations, ensuring compliance with ethical standards and patient rights."},
{"ml_model_development": "ML model development refers to the process of creating machine learning models designed to segment and grade histologic features of MASH, using datasets from various clinical trials."},
{"ml_model_development__uses__8_747_h_e_and_7_660_mt_wsis": "The predicate \"uses\" indicates the utilization of specific datasets in the development of machine learning models. In this context, it highlights the resources employed to train and validate the models."},
{"8_747_h_e_and_7_660_mt_wsis": "The object, 8,747 H&E and 7,660 MT WSIs, represents the whole slide images (WSIs) stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) and Masson's Trichrome (MT) used in the training of machine learning models. These images were collected from six completed phase 2b and phase 3 MASH clinical trials."},
{"ml_models": "ML models refer to machine learning models developed for medical image analysis, specifically designed to process and interpret whole slide images (WSIs) of liver biopsies.|Here is another description: ML models refer to machine learning algorithms designed for specific tasks in image analysis, particularly in medical imaging where they are used to interpret and analyze complex visual data."},
{"ml_models__are_for__segmenting_and_grading_staging_mash_histologic_features": "The predicate \"are for\" indicates the purpose or function of the ML models, which in this context is to perform specific tasks related to medical image analysis."},
{"segmenting_and_grading_staging_mash_histologic_features": "Segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features involve the automated identification, delineation, and assessment of histopathological characteristics associated with Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH) in liver biopsy images, such as steatosis, lobular inflammation, and fibrosis."},
{"samples": "Samples refer to the biological specimens, specifically liver biopsy whole slide images (WSIs), that were gathered as part of various clinical trials focused on metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH).|Here is another description: Samples refer to the tissue specimens collected from patients as part of various clinical trials focused on MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). These samples were processed according to the specific protocols of their respective trials.|Here is another description: Samples refer to the collected and processed specimens, specifically H&E and MT liver biopsy WSIs, used in the machine learning model development and external test sets for segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features."},
{"samples__were_collected_from__six_completed_phase_2b_and_phase_3_mash_clinical_trials": "The phrase \"were collected from\" indicates the origin or source of the samples, specifying the clinical trials from which these samples were obtained."},
{"samples__were_scanned_on__leica_aperio_at2_or_scanscope_v1_scanners": "The phrase \"were scanned on\" indicates the process by which the collected samples were digitized. This scanning is a critical step in converting physical tissue samples into digital whole slide images (WSIs) that can be analyzed by machine learning models."},
{"leica_aperio_at2_or_scanscope_v1_scanners": "Leica Aperio AT2 and Scanscope V1 scanners are high-resolution digital pathology scanners used to capture images of tissue samples at magnifications of \u00d720 or \u00d740. These scanners are essential tools in digitizing histology slides for further analysis and machine learning applications."},
{"samples__were_scanned_at__20_or_40_magnification": "The predicate \"were scanned at\" indicates the action performed on the samples, which involved using specific equipment to digitize the specimens at certain magnifications for further analysis and model training."},
{"20_or_40_magnification": "\u00d720 or \u00d740 magnification refers to the levels of enlargement at which the samples were scanned using Leica Aperio AT2 or Scanscope V1 scanners. These magnifications allowed for detailed visualization and analysis of the histologic features in the liver biopsy WSIs."},
{"model_performance_assessments": "Model performance assessments refer to the evaluations conducted to determine the accuracy and reliability of machine learning models in segmenting and grading/staging histologic features of MASH. These assessments are crucial for verifying that the models can consistently and correctly interpret medical images."},
{"model_performance_assessments__were_conducted_in__external_held_out_validation_dataset": "The phrase \"were conducted in\" indicates the location or dataset where the model performance assessments took place. This predicate signifies that the evaluations were performed using a specific set of data to ensure the models' performance is tested under conditions that mimic real-world applications."},
{"external_held_out_validation_dataset": "The external, held-out validation dataset is a separate set of whole slide images (WSIs) that were not used during the model training phase. This dataset serves as a critical tool for validating the model's performance in an unbiased manner, ensuring that the model can generalize well to new, unseen data."},
{"validation_dataset": "The validation dataset is a collection of whole slide images (WSIs) used to assess the performance and accuracy of machine learning models developed for segmenting and grading/staging histologic features of MASH. It serves as an external, held-out test set to ensure the models' performance is evaluated fairly, as it was not used during the model development phase.|Here is another description: The validation dataset is a collection of whole slide images (WSIs) from a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial, used for model performance accuracy analysis and repeatability assessments. It includes baseline and end-of-treatment biopsies reviewed by clinical pathologists."},
{"validation_dataset__comprises__wsis_of_baseline_and_end_of_treatment_biopsies_from_a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"comprises\" indicates that the validation dataset is made up of or includes specific elements. In this context, it signifies that the validation dataset consists of whole slide images from a particular source, which is essential for evaluating the machine learning models' performance."},
{"wsis_of_baseline_and_end_of_treatment_biopsies_from_a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The object \"WSIs of baseline and end-of-treatment biopsies from a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial\" refers to the whole slide images that were collected at the beginning and end of the treatment period in a phase 2b clinical trial focused on MASH. These images are crucial for assessing changes in histologic features over the course of the treatment and for validating the machine learning models' ability to accurately segment and grade these features."},
{"validation_dataset__was_not_used_for__model_development": "The predicate \"was not used for\" indicates that the validation dataset was intentionally excluded from the process of developing the machine learning models. This exclusion ensures that the dataset serves as an unbiased external test set for evaluating model performance."},
{"model_development": "Model development refers to the process of training and refining machine learning algorithms using a designated training dataset. In this context, the models were developed using a separate set of H&E and MT WSIs from various MASH clinical trials, distinct from the validation dataset.|Here is another description: Model development refers to the phase in which the machine learning model is trained and refined using high-quality annotations to improve its performance and accuracy in tasks such as image recognition or classification."},
{"clinical_utility_of_model_derived_features": "The clinical utility of model-derived features refers to the effectiveness and practical value of features generated by machine learning models in the context of medical diagnosis and treatment. These features are designed to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of diagnosing and assessing medical conditions, particularly in the context of MASH (Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis) histology."},
{"clinical_utility_of_model_derived_features__was_assessed_by__generated_ordinal_and_continuous_ml_features_in_wsis_from_four_completed_mash_clinical_trials": "The phrase \"was assessed by\" indicates the method or process used to evaluate the clinical utility of the model-derived features. In this context, it means that the effectiveness and value of these features were measured and analyzed using specific datasets and methodologies to determine their impact on medical decision-making and patient outcomes."},
{"generated_ordinal_and_continuous_ml_features_in_wsis_from_four_completed_mash_clinical_trials": "Generated ordinal and continuous machine learning (ML) features in whole slide images (WSIs) from four completed MASH clinical trials represent the specific data and outcomes used to assess the clinical utility of the model-derived features. These features were created from digital images of liver biopsies, which were analyzed to provide quantitative and qualitative information about the histologic characteristics of MASH in patients enrolled in these trials."},
{"board_certified_pathologists": "Board-certified pathologists are medical professionals who have completed specialized training and certification in the field of pathology, which involves the study of diseases and their causes through the examination of tissues, organs, and bodily fluids.|Here is another description: Board-certified pathologists are medical professionals who have completed specialized training and certification in pathology, the study of diseases. These experts have specific experience in evaluating MASH histology, which involves the microscopic examination of liver tissue affected by Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis."},
{"board_certified_pathologists__have__experience_in_evaluating_mash_histology": "The predicate \"have\" in this context indicates possession or the acquisition of a specific skill or expertise. It signifies that the subject, board-certified pathologists, possess a particular capability or knowledge base."},
{"experience_in_evaluating_mash_histology": "Experience in evaluating MASH histology refers to the practical knowledge and skill that pathologists have gained in analyzing the microscopic features of liver tissue affected by Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH), a condition characterized by liver inflammation and damage associated with metabolic disorders.|Here is another description: Experience in evaluating MASH histology refers to the specialized knowledge and skill pathologists have acquired in assessing liver tissue affected by Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). This expertise is critical for accurately diagnosing and grading the severity of the condition based on histological features observed in tissue samples."},
{"board_certified_pathologists__assisted_in__the_development_of_the_present_mash_ai": "The phrase \"assisted in\" indicates that the board-certified pathologists provided support and expertise during the creation process. Their involvement was crucial in ensuring the accuracy and reliability of the AI system by leveraging their specialized knowledge in MASH histology."},
{"the_development_of_the_present_mash_ai": "The present MASH AI is an artificial intelligence system designed to aid in the diagnosis and assessment of Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis. This AI tool likely utilizes machine learning algorithms to analyze medical images and data, providing healthcare professionals with valuable insights and support in managing MASH cases."},
{"pathologists__provided__hand_drawn_annotations_of_key_histologic_features": "The act of providing involves the contribution or supply of necessary data or information. In this scenario, pathologists are contributing hand-drawn annotations which are essential for training image segmentation models and AI scoring models."},
{"hand_drawn_annotations_of_key_histologic_features": "Hand-drawn annotations of key histologic features refer to the detailed markings and notes made by pathologists on medical images to highlight important characteristics and structures within the tissue. These annotations serve as critical training data for machine learning algorithms designed to recognize and quantify histologic patterns."},
{"hand_drawn_annotations": "Hand-drawn annotations refer to the manual markings and labels created by pathologists on histologic images to highlight key features. These annotations are essential for providing detailed and precise information about the tissue structures and pathological features present in the images."},
{"hand_drawn_annotations__for__training_image_segmentation_models": "The predicate \"for\" in this context signifies the purpose or intended use of the hand-drawn annotations. Specifically, it indicates that these annotations are created with the goal of being utilized in the process of training image segmentation models."},
{"training_image_segmentation_models": "Training image segmentation models involves using annotated data to teach algorithms how to accurately segment and classify different regions within histologic images. These models are designed to automatically identify and delineate various tissue types and pathological features, aiding in the analysis and diagnosis of medical images."},
{"pathologists__provided__slide_level_mash_crn_steatosis_grades_ballooning_grades_lobular_inflammation_grades_and_fibrosis_stages": "The predicate \"provided\" indicates the action taken by the pathologists in contributing essential data. This involves supplying specific grades and stages related to liver histology, which are critical for training and validating AI scoring models designed to assess the severity of liver conditions."},
{"slide_level_mash_crn_steatosis_grades_ballooning_grades_lobular_inflammation_grades_and_fibrosis_stages": "Slide-level MASH CRN steatosis grades, ballooning grades, lobular inflammation grades, and fibrosis stages are detailed assessments of liver tissue characteristics. These grades and stages are part of the MASH CRN scoring system, which pathologists use to evaluate the severity of liver diseases such as steatohepatitis, providing crucial data for AI model training."},
{"slide_level_mash_crn_grades_stages__for__training_the_ai_scoring_models": "The predicate \"for\" in this context signifies the purpose or application of the slide-level MASH CRN grades/stages. Specifically, it indicates that these grades/stages are utilized as a foundational dataset to train and develop AI scoring models, enhancing their accuracy and reliability in diagnosing and staging liver diseases."},
{"training_the_ai_scoring_models": "Training the AI scoring models involves using the slide-level MASH CRN grades/stages provided by pathologists to teach algorithms how to recognize and quantify histologic features in liver disease. This process aims to create robust AI models capable of assisting pathologists in making more consistent and accurate diagnoses."},
{"pathologists__required_to_pass__a_proficiency_examination": "The requirement to pass a proficiency examination indicates a necessary evaluation process that pathologists must undergo. This process ensures that they have the requisite skills and knowledge to accurately provide annotations and gradings for the AI models, maintaining the integrity and reliability of the model development process."},
{"a_proficiency_examination": "A proficiency examination in this context is a test designed to assess the competence of pathologists in providing accurate MASH CRN grades and stages for medical images. It involves comparing their gradings with a consensus median provided by expert pathologists, ensuring that only qualified individuals contribute to the model development."},
{"proficiency_examination": "The proficiency examination is a test required for pathologists to pass in order to provide slide-level MASH CRN grades and stages for model development. It ensures that the pathologists have the necessary skills and knowledge to accurately assess MASH cases."},
{"proficiency_examination__included__providing_mash_crn_grades_stages_for_20_mash_cases": "The predicate \"included\" signifies that the proficiency examination contained or comprised the task of providing MASH CRN grades and stages for 20 MASH cases as part of its assessment criteria."},
{"providing_mash_crn_grades_stages_for_20_mash_cases": "Providing MASH CRN grades/stages for 20 MASH cases is a specific task within the proficiency examination. It involves pathologists assessing and grading the severity of various histologic features in 20 cases of MASH, which is used to evaluate their competency and agreement with a consensus median provided by expert pathologists."},
{"scores": "The scores refer to the MASH CRN grades/stages provided by pathologists for model development, which include steatosis grades, ballooning grades, lobular inflammation grades, and fibrosis stages."},
{"scores__compared_with__a_consensus_median_provided_by_three_mash_crn_pathologists": "The comparison involves evaluating the agreement between the scores provided by individual pathologists and a consensus median, which is a measure of central tendency derived from the scores of three MASH CRN pathologists."},
{"a_consensus_median_provided_by_three_mash_crn_pathologists": "The consensus median provided by three MASH CRN pathologists represents a collective assessment of the MASH CRN grades/stages, serving as a benchmark for evaluating the performance of individual pathologists in the proficiency examination."},
{"agreement_statistics": "Agreement statistics refer to the measures of how closely the MASH CRN grades/stages provided by individual pathologists align with a consensus median established by three MASH CRN pathologists. These statistics are crucial for evaluating the consistency and reliability of pathologists' assessments.|Here is another description: Agreement statistics refer to the measures of how closely the pathologists' grading of MASH cases aligns with a consensus median provided by three MASH CRN pathologists. These statistics are crucial for evaluating the reliability and consistency of the pathologists' assessments."},
{"agreement_statistics__reviewed_by__a_pathai_pathologist_with_expertise_in_mash": "The phrase'reviewed by' indicates that the agreement statistics underwent an evaluation process. This process involved an expert assessment to determine the suitability of the pathologists' grading for assisting in the development of AI scoring models."},
{"a_pathai_pathologist_with_expertise_in_mash": "A PathAI pathologist with expertise in MASH is a specialist who has in-depth knowledge and experience in the histopathological evaluation of MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). This expert is responsible for reviewing agreement statistics to ensure the quality and accuracy of the data used in model development."},
{"agreement_statistics__leveraged_to__select_pathologists_for_assisting_in_model_development": "The phrase 'leveraged to' indicates that the agreement statistics were utilized as a tool or criterion in the decision-making process. In this context, it means that the agreement statistics were used to inform and guide the selection of pathologists."},
{"select_pathologists_for_assisting_in_model_development": "Selecting pathologists for assisting in model development involves choosing individuals based on their performance and agreement with established standards. These pathologists are then tasked with providing annotations and grades that are essential for training and refining the AI models used in histologic analysis."},
{"59_pathologists": "A group of 59 pathologists who were involved in the process of developing and training medical image segmentation models and AI scoring models for histologic feature analysis."},
{"59_pathologists__provided__feature_annotations_for_model_training": "The action taken by the 59 pathologists, which involved supplying necessary data in the form of annotations to facilitate the training of models designed to recognize and segment key histologic features in medical images."},
{"feature_annotations_for_model_training": "The specific data contributions made by the 59 pathologists, which included detailed annotations of histologic features that were essential for the training of image segmentation models, enhancing the models' ability to accurately identify and delineate these features in medical images."},
{"five_pathologists": "Five pathologists who were selected after passing a proficiency examination involving grading 20 MASH cases. Their scores were benchmarked against a consensus median provided by three MASH CRN pathologists."},
{"five_pathologists__provided__slide_level_mash_crn_grades_stages": "The action of supplying or furnishing something needed or requested. In this context, it refers to the pathologists offering their professional assessments and data for the development of AI scoring models."},
{"pathologists__provided__pixel_level_annotations_on_wsis": "The predicate 'provided' indicates the action taken by the pathologists, which involves supplying or furnishing something necessary or requested. Here, it refers to the act of pathologists supplying detailed, pixel-level annotations on WSIs, which are essential for training machine learning models in medical image analysis."},
{"pixel_level_annotations_on_wsis": "Pixel-level annotations on WSIs refer to the detailed markings made by pathologists on digital images of tissue slides at the level of individual pixels. These annotations highlight specific features, substances, or areas of interest within the images, such as those relevant to MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis), artifacts, or background elements. These annotations are crucial for the development and training of machine learning models designed to detect and quantify histologic features in medical images."},
{"pathologists__used__a_proprietary_digital_wsi_viewer_interface": "The predicate \"used\" indicates the action performed by the subject (pathologists) involving the object (a proprietary digital WSI viewer interface). In this scenario, it describes how pathologists employed a specific tool to carry out their task of annotating tissue features on WSIs."},
{"a_proprietary_digital_wsi_viewer_interface": "A proprietary digital WSI viewer interface is a specialized software tool designed for viewing and interacting with whole slide images (WSIs). In this context, it is used by pathologists to annotate tissue features at a pixel level, facilitating the collection of data for machine learning model training."},
{"pathologists__were_instructed_to__draw_or_annotate_over_the_h_e_and_mt_wsis": "The instruction given to pathologists involves a specific task: to draw or annotate over the histological whole slide images (WSIs). This task is crucial for collecting examples of substances relevant to medical conditions like MASH, as well as identifying artifacts and background elements within the images."},
{"draw_or_annotate_over_the_h_e_and_mt_wsis": "The object of the instruction involves the H&E (Hematoxylin and Eosin) and MT (Masson's Trichrome) WSIs, which are types of stained tissue slides used in pathology to visualize different tissue components and structures. The annotations over these slides are intended to highlight specific features and substances relevant to the study of MASH and other histologic conditions."},
{"pathologists__collected_examples_of__substances_relevant_to_mash_artifact_and_background": "The predicate \"collected examples of\" indicates the action performed by the pathologists, which involves gathering and marking instances of specific substances and elements within the WSIs to aid in the training of machine learning models."},
{"substances_relevant_to_mash_artifact_and_background": "The objects collected by the pathologists include substances relevant to MASH (a medical condition), as well as examples of artifact and background present in the WSIs. These annotations are crucial for training machine learning models to accurately detect and quantify features related to MASH histology and to differentiate between foreground and background elements."},
{"instructions": "The instructions refer to the guidelines and directives given to pathologists for annotating tissue features on whole slide images (WSIs). These instructions are crucial for ensuring consistency and accuracy in the annotations, which are used to train machine learning models."},
{"instructions__are_included_in__supplementary_table_4_refs_33_36": "The phrase 'are included in' indicates that the instructions are part of a larger document or dataset. This inclusion ensures that the guidelines are readily accessible to those who need to reference them while performing annotations or reviewing the annotated data."},
{"supplementary_table_4_refs_33_36": "Supplementary Table 4 (refs. 33\u201336) is a document that contains detailed instructions provided to pathologists for annotating specific histologic substances on H&E and MT WSIs. This table is referenced in the main text and includes citations to additional resources (refs. 33\u201336) that may provide further context or details about the annotations."},
{"total_feature_annotations": "The total feature annotations refer to the comprehensive set of pixel-level annotations provided by pathologists on whole slide images (WSIs) using a proprietary digital viewer interface. These annotations were created to collect numerous examples of substances relevant to MASH, as well as examples of artifacts and background."},
{"total_feature_annotations__were__103_579": "The predicate \"were\" indicates the existence or the completion of the total feature annotations. It signifies that the annotations have been provided and are available for use in training machine learning models."},
{"103_579": "The object \"103,579\" represents the total number of feature annotations that were collected. These annotations were used to train machine learning models for detecting and quantifying features relevant to image/tissue artifact, foreground versus background separation, and MASH histology."},
{"feature_annotations": "Feature annotations refer to the pixel-level markings provided by pathologists on whole slide images (WSIs) to identify and delineate various histologic substances and artifacts relevant to MASH. These annotations serve as critical data points for machine learning applications."},
{"feature_annotations__were_collected_to__train_the_ml_models": "The phrase \"were collected to\" indicates the purpose behind gathering the feature annotations. It signifies that the annotations were amassed with the specific intention of utilizing them for a subsequent process, in this case, to facilitate the training of machine learning models."},
{"train_the_ml_models": "Training machine learning (ML) models involves using a dataset to adjust the model's parameters, allowing it to learn patterns and make predictions or decisions. In this context, the ML models are being trained to detect and quantify features relevant to image/tissue artifact, foreground versus background separation, and MASH histology."},
{"ml_models__were_trained_to__detect_and_quantify_features_relevant_to_image_tissue_artifact_foreground_versus_background_separation_and_mash_histology": "The phrase \"were trained to\" indicates the process by which machine learning models are developed. It involves feeding the models large amounts of annotated data so they can learn to recognize patterns and make decisions based on that data."},
{"detect_and_quantify_features_relevant_to_image_tissue_artifact_foreground_versus_background_separation_and_mash_histology": "The object describes the specific tasks the ML models were trained to perform, which include detecting and quantifying features related to image or tissue artifacts, distinguishing between foreground and background elements, and analyzing MASH histology, a method used to assess liver tissue for signs of disease."},
{"pathologists__evaluated__histologic_features": "The action of evaluating involves a thorough examination and assessment of specific characteristics or features. In this case, the pathologists are tasked with assessing particular aspects of tissue samples to determine the presence and extent of liver disease."},
{"mas_and_crn_fibrosis_staging_rubrics": "The MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics are standardized systems used by pathologists to evaluate and score the severity of liver fibrosis in histological samples. These rubrics provide a structured approach to assessing the extent of fibrosis, which is crucial for diagnosing and managing liver diseases."},
{"mas_and_crn_fibrosis_staging_rubrics__developed_by__kleiner_et_al": "The predicate 'developed by' indicates the origin or authorship of the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics. It signifies that these rubrics were created and formalized by a specific group of researchers or experts in the field of hepatology and pathology."},
{"kleiner_et_al": "Kleiner et al. refers to a group of researchers, led by Dr. Kleiner, who are recognized for their contributions to the development of standardized methods for assessing liver fibrosis. Their work has been influential in establishing guidelines and rubrics that are widely used in clinical and research settings."},
{"cases": "The cases refer to the histologic features evaluated by pathologists according to the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics developed by Kleiner et al."},
{"cases__reviewed_and_scored_using__wsi_viewer": "The predicate'reviewed and scored using' indicates the process by which the cases were examined and assessed. This involves a detailed evaluation of the histologic features, followed by scoring based on the established rubrics."},
{"wsi_viewer": "The WSI viewer is a tool used by pathologists to review and score the cases. It allows for the detailed examination of histologic features according to the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics."},
{"model_development_dataset": "The model development dataset is a collection of data used specifically for the purpose of developing a predictive model. This dataset includes various Whole Slide Images (WSIs) from patients, which are essential for training and evaluating the model."},
{"model_development_dataset__was_split_into__training_70_validation_15_and_held_out_test_15_sets": "The predicate \"was split into\" indicates the process by which the model development dataset was divided into different subsets. This division is crucial for ensuring that the model is trained, validated, and tested on separate data to avoid overfitting and to accurately assess its performance."},
{"training_70_validation_15_and_held_out_test_15_sets": "The object \"training (~70%), validation (~15%) and held-out test (\u223c15%) sets\" refers to the three distinct subsets into which the model development dataset was divided. The training set, comprising approximately 70% of the data, is used to train the model. The validation set, around 15%, is used to tune model parameters and prevent overfitting. The held-out test set, also about 15%, is used to evaluate the final performance of the model on unseen data."},
{"dataset": "The dataset refers to the model development dataset that was divided into training, validation, and held-out test sets. This dataset is crucial for developing and validating machine learning models."},
{"dataset__was_split_at__the_patient_level": "The predicate \"was split at\" indicates the method or level at which the dataset was divided. This is an important detail as it affects how the data is distributed across different sets and can impact the model's performance and generalizability."},
{"the_patient_level": "The patient level signifies that the dataset was divided in such a way that all whole slide images (WSIs) from the same patient were allocated to the same development set. This ensures that there is no data leakage between the training, validation, and test sets, maintaining the integrity of the model evaluation process."},
{"all_wsis_from_the_same_patient": "All WSIs (Whole Slide Images) from the same patient refer to the complete set of digital pathology images captured from tissue samples of a single individual, which are used in medical analysis and diagnosis."},
{"all_wsis_from_the_same_patient__were_allocated_to__the_same_development_set": "The phrase \"were allocated to\" indicates the process of assigning or designating all WSIs from the same patient to a specific category or group during the dataset splitting phase of model development."},
{"the_same_development_set": "The same development set refers to a predefined subset of the overall dataset, specifically designated for either training, validation, or testing purposes in the model development process, ensuring that all data from the same patient are kept together within this set."},
{"sets": "The subject refers to the training, validation, and held-out test sets derived from the model development dataset. These sets are divided at the patient level, ensuring that all whole slide images (WSIs) from the same patient are allocated to the same development set."},
{"sets__were_balanced_for__key_mash_disease_severity_metrics": "The predicate indicates that the sets were carefully balanced to ensure equitable representation of various metrics. This balancing was done to the greatest extent possible, despite challenges posed by clinical trial enrollment criteria."},
{"key_mash_disease_severity_metrics": "The object describes the key MASH disease severity metrics for which the sets were balanced. These metrics include MASH CRN steatosis grade, ballooning grade, lobular inflammation grade, and fibrosis stage, which are critical for assessing the severity of MASH disease."},
{"balancing_step": "The balancing step refers to the process of ensuring that the model development dataset is evenly distributed across key MASH disease severity metrics, such as steatosis grade, ballooning grade, lobular inflammation grade, and fibrosis stage."},
{"balancing_step__was_occasionally__challenging": "The phrase \"was occasionally\" indicates that the balancing step did not always proceed smoothly and faced difficulties at times during the dataset splitting process."},
{"challenging": "The term \"challenging\" describes the difficulties encountered during the balancing step, which were partly due to the MASH clinical trial enrollment criteria that restricted the patient population to specific ranges of disease severity."},
{"challenge": "The challenge refers to the difficulty encountered during the balancing step of splitting the model development dataset into training, validation, and held-out test sets. This step aimed to ensure that the sets were balanced for key MASH disease severity metrics."},
{"challenge__was_due_to__mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria": "The phrase \"was due to\" indicates the cause of the challenge. In this context, it explains that the difficulty in balancing the dataset was a direct result of specific restrictions imposed on the patient population."},
{"mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria": "The MASH clinical trial enrollment criteria refer to the specific requirements that patients had to meet to be included in the clinical trial. These criteria restricted the patient population to those within certain ranges of the MASH disease severity spectrum, thereby complicating the balancing of the dataset."},
{"enrollment_criteria__restricted__the_patient_population_to_those_fitting_within_specific_ranges_of_the_disease_severity_spectrum": "The predicate \"restricted\" indicates that the enrollment criteria have placed limitations or boundaries on the patient population that can participate in the study. This restriction is intended to focus the study on a particular subset of patients who meet certain predefined conditions."},
{"the_patient_population_to_those_fitting_within_specific_ranges_of_the_disease_severity_spectrum": "The patient population to those fitting within specific ranges of the disease severity spectrum refers to the subset of patients who have been selected based on their disease severity levels. This selection ensures that only patients within certain severity ranges are included in the study, which helps in maintaining the study's focus and relevance."},
{"held_out_test_set": "The held-out test set is a subset of the model development dataset, specifically allocated to evaluate the final performance of the model. It comprises approximately 15% of the total dataset and is kept separate from the training and validation sets to prevent any data leakage."},
{"held_out_test_set__contains__a_dataset_from_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"contains\" signifies that the held-out test set includes or holds within it a specific dataset. This inclusion is crucial for assessing the model\u2019s performance on new, unseen data, ensuring that the evaluation is robust and unbiased."},
{"a_dataset_from_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The object, a dataset from an independent clinical trial, refers to a collection of data gathered from a clinical trial that is separate from the main dataset used for model development. This dataset is used in the held-out test set to validate the model\u2019s performance on completely new and independent patient data."},
{"algorithm_performance": "Algorithm performance refers to the effectiveness and efficiency of a model or algorithm in processing and analyzing data, particularly in the context of medical imaging and diagnosis. In this scenario, it involves the model's ability to accurately predict MASH disease severity metrics using a balanced dataset."},
{"algorithm_performance__is_meeting__acceptance_criteria_on_a_completely_held_out_patient_cohort_in_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"is meeting\" indicates that the algorithm performance is being evaluated against specific standards or criteria. In this context, it means that the model's predictions are being assessed to ensure they align with the expected outcomes and acceptance criteria established for the held-out patient cohort."},
{"acceptance_criteria_on_a_completely_held_out_patient_cohort_in_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The acceptance criteria on a completely held-out patient cohort in an independent clinical trial represent the standards by which the algorithm\u2019s performance is judged. This involves ensuring that the model can generalize well to new, unseen data from an independent clinical trial, thereby confirming its reliability and validity in a real-world setting."},
{"test_data_leakage": "Test data leakage refers to the scenario where information from the test set inadvertently influences the model during training, leading to overly optimistic performance estimates. In the context of model development for medical algorithms, avoiding test data leakage is crucial to ensure the model's generalizability and reliability on unseen data."},
{"test_data_leakage__is_being_avoided__by_using_an_independent_clinical_trial_dataset": "The phrase \"is being avoided\" indicates a deliberate action taken to prevent test data leakage. In the process of developing and validating medical algorithms, researchers and developers implement various strategies to ensure that the test set remains completely independent from the training and validation sets. This avoidance is essential for obtaining an accurate assessment of the algorithm's performance on new, unseen data."},
{"by_using_an_independent_clinical_trial_dataset": "Using an independent clinical trial dataset as the held-out test set ensures that the algorithm's performance is evaluated on a completely separate patient cohort. This dataset is distinct from the data used during the training and validation phases, thereby providing a robust and unbiased assessment of the algorithm's capabilities. It helps in confirming that the model can generalize well to new, previously unseen cases."},
{"ai_mash_algorithms": "The AI MASH algorithms are advanced computational methods designed to analyze and interpret complex medical imaging data, specifically within the context of tissue compartment segmentation."},
{"ai_mash_algorithms__were_trained_using__three_categories_of_tissue_compartment_segmentation_models": "The phrase \"were trained using\" indicates the process by which the AI MASH algorithms acquired their operational capabilities. This training involved exposure to and learning from predefined models to enhance their accuracy and efficiency in segmenting tissue compartments."},
{"three_categories_of_tissue_compartment_segmentation_models": "The three categories of tissue compartment segmentation models represent distinct approaches or frameworks used to train the AI MASH algorithms. These models are detailed in Supplementary Tables 6\u20139, providing comprehensive insights into their objectives, purposes, inputs, outputs, and training parameters."},
{"summaries_of_each_model": "The summaries of each model refer to concise descriptions or overviews of the different tissue compartment segmentation models used in the AI MASH algorithms. These summaries provide an understanding of each model's objectives and functionalities."},
{"summaries_of_each_model__are_included_in__supplementary_table_6": "The phrase \"are included in\" indicates that the summaries of each model are part of a larger document or table. This inclusion allows readers to access detailed information about the models in a structured and organized manner."},
{"supplementary_table_6": "Supplementary Table 6 is a table that contains the summaries of each model used in the AI MASH algorithms. This table serves as a reference for readers to quickly understand the objectives and purposes of each model without having to delve into the detailed descriptions provided in other supplementary tables."},
{"detailed_descriptions_of_each_model_s_purpose_input_and_output_as_well_as_training_parameters": "Detailed descriptions of each model\u2019s purpose, input and output, as well as training parameters, refer to comprehensive information about the various AI MASH algorithms used for tissue compartment segmentation. This includes specifics on what each model is designed to achieve, the type of data it processes, the results it produces, and the settings used during its training phase."},
{"detailed_descriptions_of_each_model_s_purpose_input_and_output_as_well_as_training_parameters__can_be_found_in__supplementary_tables_7_9": "The phrase 'can be found in' indicates the location where detailed information about the models is stored or documented. It suggests that the comprehensive descriptions are not presented in the main text but are instead located in a specific section or appendix of the document."},
{"supplementary_tables_7_9": "Supplementary Tables 7\u20139 are additional tables included in the document that provide further details and data not covered in the main text. These tables specifically contain detailed descriptions of each model\u2019s purpose, input and output, as well as training parameters for the AI MASH algorithms."},
{"cnns": "CNNs, or Convolutional Neural Networks, are a class of deep learning models used in the context of tissue compartment segmentation. They are employed to analyze and segment different types of tissue within whole slide images (WSIs) with high precision.|Here is another description: CNNs, or Convolutional Neural Networks, are a class of deep learning models particularly effective in image recognition and processing tasks. In the context of medical imaging, CNNs are utilized for their ability to automatically and accurately identify and classify various structures within images."},
{"cnns__allowed__massively_parallel_patch_wise_inference": "The predicate \"allowed\" signifies that the use of cloud-computing infrastructure enabled or made possible a specific capability or process for the CNNs. In this context, it refers to the facilitation of a technical operation that enhances the efficiency and thoroughness of the CNNs' performance."},
{"massively_parallel_patch_wise_inference": "Massively parallel patch-wise inference is a computational technique that involves processing multiple small sections (patches) of an image simultaneously across many computing units. This method allows for efficient and comprehensive analysis of every tissue-containing region within a whole slide image, achieving a spatial precision of 4\u20138 pixels."},
{"patch_wise_inference": "Patch-wise inference refers to a method where a convolutional neural network (CNN) processes small sections or patches of an image individually. This approach allows for detailed analysis and is particularly useful in handling large images by breaking them down into manageable parts.|Here is another description: Patch-wise inference refers to the method used in convolutional neural networks (CNNs) where the input image is divided into smaller patches or sections. Each patch is then processed individually by the network, allowing for detailed and localized analysis of the image data."},
{"patch_wise_inference__was_performed_on__every_tissue_containing_region_of_a_wsi": "The phrase \"was performed on\" indicates that the action of patch-wise inference was applied or executed specifically targeting a certain area or set of data. In this context, it signifies that the patch-wise inference method was utilized as a technique on a particular type of image data."},
{"every_tissue_containing_region_of_a_wsi": "Every tissue-containing region of a WSI (Whole Slide Image) refers to all areas within a digital pathology image that contain tissue. These regions are critical for analysis as they hold the biological information necessary for diagnosis and research. The WSI represents a digitized version of a glass slide used in pathology, allowing for detailed examination and computational analysis."},
{"patch_wise_inference__had_a_spatial_precision_of__4_8_pixels": "The phrase \"had a spatial precision of\" indicates the level of accuracy or resolution at which the patch-wise inference method operates. It specifies the smallest unit or detail that the method can discern within the image, reflecting the fineness of the analysis performed."},
{"4_8_pixels": "A spatial precision of 4\u20138 pixels means that the patch-wise inference method can distinguish details within the image down to the level of individual pixels, with a range of 4 to 8 pixels. This level of precision allows for highly detailed and accurate segmentation and analysis of tissue compartments in whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_differentiate__evaluable_liver_tissue_from_wsi_background": "The phrase \"was trained to differentiate\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process to learn how to distinguish between different types of image content. This training enables the CNN to accurately identify and separate specific elements within the images it analyzes."},
{"evaluable_liver_tissue_from_wsi_background": "\"Evaluable liver tissue from WSI background\" refers to the CNN's ability to distinguish between actual liver tissue that can be assessed for medical purposes and the background of Whole Slide Images (WSI). This differentiation is crucial for accurate medical diagnosis and analysis."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_differentiate__evaluable_tissue_from_artifacts_introduced_via_tissue_preparation_or_slide_scanning": "The predicate \"was trained to differentiate\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process to learn how to distinguish between different elements within the medical images. This training enables the CNN to accurately identify and separate evaluable tissue from other elements that may be present in the images."},
{"evaluable_tissue_from_artifacts_introduced_via_tissue_preparation_or_slide_scanning": "The object \"evaluable tissue from artifacts introduced via tissue preparation or slide scanning\" refers to the specific categories the CNN was trained to differentiate. Evaluable tissue is the actual liver tissue that can be assessed for diagnostic purposes. Artifacts include any distortions or irregularities introduced during the tissue preparation process (like tissue folds) or during the slide scanning process (like out-of-focus regions), which are not part of the actual tissue but can interfere with accurate analysis."},
{"cnn__was_developed_for__both_h_e_and_mt_stains": "\"Was developed for\" indicates the purpose or specific application for which the CNN was created. In this case, it highlights that the CNN was specifically designed and trained to address the challenges associated with differentiating various elements in medical images stained with H&E and MT."},
{"both_h_e_and_mt_stains": "Both H&E and MT stains refer to two common staining techniques used in histology to enhance the visibility of different tissue components under a microscope. H&E stands for Hematoxylin and Eosin, which stains nuclei blue and cytoplasm pink, while MT stands for Masson's Trichrome, which is used to stain collagen fibers blue and muscle fibers red, aiding in the differentiation of tissue structures."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_segment__cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features": "The phrase \"was trained to segment\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process to learn how to identify and delineate specific features within H&E stained histological images. Segmentation in this context means the CNN was taught to distinguish and mark different regions or features within the images based on their visual characteristics."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features": "Cardinal MASH H&E histologic features refer to key microscopic characteristics observed in liver tissue stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E), which are critical for diagnosing and assessing the severity of liver conditions such as Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). These features include macrovesicular steatosis, hepatocellular ballooning, and lobular inflammation."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features__include__macrovesicular_steatosis": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, cardinal MASH H&E histologic features, comprises or encompasses certain elements. In this context, it indicates that macrovesicular steatosis is one of the features that are part of the cardinal MASH H&E histologic features."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features__include__hepatocellular_ballooning": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, cardinal MASH H&E histologic features, comprises or encompasses specific elements or characteristics, such as hepatocellular ballooning, among others."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features__include__lobular_inflammation": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, cardinal MASH H&E histologic features, comprises or contains specific elements. In this context, it indicates that lobular inflammation is one of the features that are part of the cardinal MASH H&E histologic features."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_segment__other_relevant_features": "\"Was trained to segment\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process where it learned to identify and delineate specific regions or features within histopathological images. This segmentation process is crucial for accurately recognizing and categorizing different tissue structures and pathological features."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__portal_inflammation": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various specific histological characteristics, among which portal inflammation is one."},
{"portal_inflammation": "Portal inflammation is a histological feature characterized by the presence of inflammatory cells within the portal tracts of the liver, which can be an indicator of various liver conditions and is one of the features segmented by the CNN in H&E WSIs."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__microvesicular_steatosis": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various histological characteristics. This relationship indicates that microvesicular steatosis is one of the features within this broader category."},
{"microvesicular_steatosis": "Microvesicular steatosis is a histological feature characterized by the presence of small fat droplets within hepatocytes. Unlike macrovesicular steatosis, where larger fat droplets displace the nucleus, microvesicular steatosis involves numerous small fat droplets that do not displace the nucleus, often associated with specific metabolic or toxic conditions."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__interface_hepatitis": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various histological characteristics, among which interface hepatitis is specifically mentioned as one of the elements."},
{"interface_hepatitis": "Interface hepatitis is a histological feature characterized by inflammation at the interface between the portal tract and the lobule in liver tissue, which is one of the additional features considered relevant for the segmentation model in analyzing H&E stained WSIs."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__normal_hepatocytes": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various specific histological characteristics. It indicates a relationship where the subject encompasses the object as part of its components."},
{"normal_hepatocytes": "Normal hepatocytes are liver cells that do not exhibit signs of steatosis (fat accumulation) or ballooning (swelling due to injury). They represent the standard, healthy state of liver cells in histological examinations."},
{"cnns__were_trained_to_segment__large_intrahepatic_septal_and_subcapsular_regions_pathologic_fibrosis_bile_ducts_and_blood_vessels": "The predicate \"were trained to segment\" indicates that the CNNs underwent a training process specifically designed to enable them to distinguish and delineate different components within medical images. This training involves learning from annotated examples to recognize and segment specific features or regions."},
{"large_intrahepatic_septal_and_subcapsular_regions_pathologic_fibrosis_bile_ducts_and_blood_vessels": "The objects of segmentation in this context include \"large intrahepatic septal and subcapsular regions, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts and blood vessels.\" These are specific anatomical and pathological features within liver tissue images that the CNNs are trained to identify and segment. Accurate segmentation of these features is crucial for diagnosing and understanding liver conditions."},
{"segmentation_models": "Segmentation models refer to machine learning models, specifically convolutional neural networks (CNNs), designed to segment and classify different regions within medical images, such as whole slide images (WSIs). These models are trained to identify and delineate specific structures or pathologies within the images."},
{"segmentation_models__were_trained_utilizing__an_iterative_model_development_process": "The phrase \"were trained utilizing\" indicates the process through which the segmentation models acquired their capabilities. It suggests that the models underwent a training phase where they learned from annotated data to recognize and segment various features within the images."},
{"an_iterative_model_development_process": "An iterative model development process is a methodical approach to training machine learning models. It involves multiple cycles of annotation, model training, evaluation, and refinement based on feedback from expert pathologists. This process ensures that the models improve over time and achieve high performance in segmenting and classifying medical images accurately."},
{"training_set_of_wsis": "The training set of Whole Slide Images (WSIs) consists of digital pathology images used for training machine learning models. These images are critical for developing algorithms that can segment and analyze different tissue structures and pathologies."},
{"training_set_of_wsis__was_shared_with__a_select_team_of_pathologists": "The predicate \"was shared with\" indicates the action of distributing the training set of WSIs to a specific group. This sharing is essential for collaborative annotation and model development, ensuring that expert input is incorporated into the machine learning process."},
{"a_select_team_of_pathologists": "A select team of pathologists with expertise in the assessment of MASH histology were chosen to receive the training set of WSIs. These pathologists are responsible for providing accurate annotations that will be used to train and validate segmentation models, ensuring the models' effectiveness and reliability."},
{"pathologists__were_instructed_to_annotate_over__h_e_and_mt_wsis": "The phrase \"were instructed to annotate over\" indicates that the pathologists were given specific directions to mark or label certain features or areas within the images they were examining. This process is essential for training machine learning models by providing them with accurate and relevant data."},
{"h_e_and_mt_wsis": "H&E (Hematoxylin and Eosin) and MT (Masson's Trichrome) WSIs (Whole Slide Images) are types of stained tissue slides used in pathology. H&E staining is a common method that highlights cellular structures, while MT staining is specifically used to identify fibrous tissue, which is important in assessing conditions like fibrosis."},
{"primary_annotations": "Primary annotations refer to the initial set of annotations made by a select team of pathologists with expertise in MASH histology. These annotations were made on whole slide images (WSIs) stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) and Masson's trichrome (MT), focusing on various histologic features such as fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"primary_annotations__were_reviewed_by__internal_pathologists": "The predicate \"were reviewed by\" indicates a process where the primary annotations underwent a quality control step. This review was essential to ensure the accuracy and appropriateness of the annotations, removing any that were based on misunderstandings or were otherwise inappropriate."},
{"internal_pathologists": "Internal pathologists are medical professionals within the organization who specialize in the study of diseases through the examination of tissues and cells. In this context, they were responsible for reviewing the primary annotations to maintain the integrity and quality of the data used for training segmentation models.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are a specialized team within an organization who possess expertise in the assessment of histology, particularly in the context of medical conditions like MASH. They are responsible for reviewing and validating annotations made by other pathologists to ensure accuracy and adherence to instructions.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are medical professionals within an organization who specialize in the diagnosis of diseases by examining tissues and cells under a microscope. They play a crucial role in ensuring the accuracy and reliability of histopathological assessments.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are a specialized team of medical professionals within an organization who possess expertise in the assessment of histology, particularly in the context of MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) histology. They are responsible for reviewing and annotating whole slide images (WSIs) to ensure the accuracy and quality of the annotations used in training machine learning models.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are medical professionals within an organization who specialize in the diagnosis of diseases by examining tissues and cells under a microscope. They play a crucial role in ensuring the accuracy and reliability of histopathological assessments."},
{"internal_pathologists__removed_annotations_from__pathologists_who_misunderstood_instructions_or_provided_inappropriate_annotations": "The action of removing annotations from pathologists signifies a quality control measure. Internal pathologists review the work of their peers to identify and exclude annotations that do not meet the required standards, either due to misunderstanding of instructions or inappropriate application."},
{"pathologists_who_misunderstood_instructions_or_provided_inappropriate_annotations": "Pathologists who misunderstood instructions or provided inappropriate annotations are those whose work did not align with the expected standards or guidelines. Their annotations were deemed unsuitable for use in training segmentation models, necessitating their removal to maintain the integrity and accuracy of the model training process."},
{"final_subset_of_primary_annotations": "The final subset of primary annotations refers to a refined collection of initial annotations made by pathologists on whole slide images (WSIs). These annotations were reviewed and filtered to remove any inappropriate or incorrect markings, ensuring that only high-quality, relevant data was used for further processes."},
{"final_subset_of_primary_annotations__was_used_to_train__the_first_iteration_of_all_three_segmentation_models": "The phrase \"was used to train\" indicates that the final subset of primary annotations served as the foundational dataset for the initial training phase of machine learning models. This process involved feeding the annotations into the models to teach them how to recognize and segment various histologic features accurately."},
{"the_first_iteration_of_all_three_segmentation_models": "The first iteration of all three segmentation models represents the initial versions of machine learning models designed to segment different histologic features within whole slide images. These models were trained using the final subset of primary annotations to learn how to accurately identify and delineate regions such as nonpathologic fibrosis, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"internal_pathologists__reviewed__model_derived_segmentation_overlays": "The predicate \"reviewed\" indicates that the internal pathologists examined and assessed the model-derived segmentation overlays to evaluate their accuracy and identify any areas where the model may have failed to correctly segment the histologic features."},
{"model_derived_segmentation_overlays": "Model-derived segmentation overlays are visual representations generated by convolutional neural network (CNN) models that highlight specific regions within whole slide images (WSIs), such as areas of fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels. These overlays are used to assess the performance of the segmentation models and identify areas for improvement."},
{"internal_pathologists__identified__areas_of_model_failure": "The predicate \"identified\" in this context refers to the action taken by internal pathologists where they examined the segmentation overlays generated by convolutional neural network (CNN) models. Through this review, they pinpointed specific regions within the images where the model's performance was subpar, thus highlighting areas requiring improvement."},
{"areas_of_model_failure": "Areas of model failure refer to specific regions within the whole slide images (WSIs) where the convolutional neural network (CNN) models did not perform adequately. These areas were identified by internal pathologists during their review of the model-derived segmentation overlays, indicating where the model struggled to accurately segment features such as fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"internal_pathologists__requested__correction_annotations_for_substances_for_which_the_model_was_performing_poorly": "The predicate \"requested\" indicates that the internal pathologists made a formal or explicit demand for something. In this context, it signifies that the pathologists identified a need and asked for specific actions to be taken to address it."},
{"correction_annotations_for_substances_for_which_the_model_was_performing_poorly": "Correction annotations for substances refer to additional, refined markings or labels provided by expert pathologists to improve the accuracy of a model's performance. These annotations are specifically targeted at areas where the model is underperforming, aiming to enhance its ability to correctly identify and segment histologic features in medical images."},
{"trained_cnn_models": "Trained CNN models refer to convolutional neural networks that have undergone a training process using pathologist annotations. These models are designed to segment various regions within whole slide images (WSIs), including nonpathologic fibrosis, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"trained_cnn_models__were_deployed_on__the_validation_set_of_images": "The predicate \"were deployed on\" indicates that the trained CNN models were applied to a specific dataset to evaluate their performance. This deployment is a critical step in the model development process, allowing for quantitative assessment against collected annotations."},
{"the_validation_set_of_images": "The validation set of images is a subset of the dataset reserved for evaluating the performance of the trained CNN models. This set includes whole slide images (WSIs) that were not used during the training phase, providing an unbiased assessment of the model's ability to segment histologic features accurately."},
{"correction_annotations": "Correction annotations refer to the additional annotations gathered to improve the performance of segmentation models. These annotations are specifically aimed at addressing areas where the initial model performance was lacking, providing more accurate examples of histologic features."},
{"correction_annotations__were_collected_from__expert_pathologists": "The phrase \"were collected from\" indicates the source or origin of the correction annotations. In this context, it signifies that the necessary additional annotations were obtained from a specific group of professionals to enhance the model's accuracy and performance."},
{"model_training": "Model training refers to the process of training convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to segment specific regions in whole slide images (WSIs), such as nonpathologic fibrosis, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels. This training is part of an iterative development process aimed at improving the accuracy and reliability of the segmentation models.|Here is another description: Model training is the process by which a machine learning model learns from data to make predictions or decisions. In this context, it refers to the phase where the model is being taught using the high-quality annotations to improve its performance in tasks such as image recognition or classification.|Here is another description: Model training refers to the process of using data to teach a machine learning model to make predictions or decisions. In this context, the model was trained on a dataset that did not include images from the specified clinical trial, ensuring an unbiased evaluation of the model's performance."},
{"model_training__was_monitored_and_hyperparameters_were_adjusted_based_on__the_model_s_performance_on_pathologist_annotations_from_the_held_out_validation_set": "The predicate \"was monitored, and hyperparameters were adjusted based on\" indicates an ongoing process where the performance of the model training was continuously observed. Adjustments to hyperparameters were made in response to the model's performance metrics derived from evaluations against expert pathologist annotations."},
{"the_model_s_performance_on_pathologist_annotations_from_the_held_out_validation_set": "The object \"the model\u2019s performance on pathologist annotations from the held-out validation set\" refers to the evaluation metrics and qualitative assessments provided by expert pathologists. These annotations serve as a benchmark to measure the effectiveness of the model training, guiding necessary adjustments to achieve optimal performance."},
{"artifact_h_e_tissue_and_mt_tissue_cnns": "The subject refers to convolutional neural networks (CNNs) specifically designed for analyzing H&E (Hematoxylin and Eosin) stained tissue and MT (Masson's Trichrome) stained tissue. These CNNs are specialized tools used in medical imaging to segment and analyze different tissue types and structures within whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"artifact_h_e_tissue_and_mt_tissue_cnns__were_trained_using__pathologist_annotations_comprising_8_12_blocks_of_compound_layers_with_a_topology_inspired_by_residual_networks_and_inception_networks_with_a_softmax_loss": "The predicate indicates the process by which the CNNs were developed and refined. It involves training these networks using a specific set of annotations provided by pathologists. This training process is iterative and involves multiple stages of annotation review, model training, and performance evaluation to ensure the CNNs accurately segment and identify various tissue features."},
{"pathologist_annotations_comprising_8_12_blocks_of_compound_layers_with_a_topology_inspired_by_residual_networks_and_inception_networks_with_a_softmax_loss": "The object describes the detailed annotations used to train the CNNs. These annotations, provided by expert pathologists, consist of 8\u201312 blocks of compound layers. The network topology is inspired by residual networks and inception networks, employing a softmax loss function to classify different tissue features. This setup ensures the CNNs can effectively learn and generalize from the complex and varied data provided by the pathologists."},
{"cnn_segmentation_models": "CNN segmentation models refer to convolutional neural network models designed for the task of segmenting images, specifically in this context, whole slide images (WSIs) of liver tissue stained with Masson's trichrome (MT) to differentiate between various tissue components such as fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"cnn_segmentation_models__used_a_pipeline_of__image_augmentations_during_training": "The phrase \"used a pipeline of\" indicates that the CNN segmentation models incorporated a series of processes or techniques in their training regimen. This pipeline is aimed at enhancing the model's ability to generalize and perform accurately across different clinical and research contexts."},
{"image_augmentations_during_training": "Image augmentations during training refer to a set of techniques applied to the input images to artificially expand the training dataset and improve the model's robustness. These augmentations include random cropping, rotation, color perturbations, and noise addition, among others, which help the model learn to recognize patterns under various transformations and conditions."},
{"cnn_models_learning": "The subject refers to the learning process of Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) models, specifically in the context of segmenting medical images such as Whole Slide Images (WSIs) for histopathological analysis."},
{"cnn_models_learning__was_augmented_using__distributionally_robust_optimization_to_achieve_model_generalization_across_multiple_clinical_and_research_contexts_and_augmentations": "The predicate describes the method by which the CNN models' learning process was enhanced. It indicates that the models' learning was improved through a specific technique aimed at making the models more generalizable and robust across various clinical and research scenarios."},
{"distributionally_robust_optimization_to_achieve_model_generalization_across_multiple_clinical_and_research_contexts_and_augmentations": "The object describes the technique used to augment the CNN models' learning. Distributionally robust optimization is a method employed to ensure that the models can generalize well across different contexts and augmentations, making them more reliable and effective in varied clinical and research applications."},
{"augmentations": "Augmentations refer to a set of techniques applied to training data to improve the performance and generalization of machine learning models, particularly in image processing tasks. These techniques modify the input data in various ways to create a more robust and versatile training dataset."},
{"augmentations__included__random_crops_random_rotation_color_perturbations_and_random_noise_addition": "The predicate \"included\" signifies that the specified augmentations were part of the training process for the CNN segmentation models. These augmentations were incorporated to enhance the model's ability to generalize across different clinical and research contexts by providing a diverse set of training examples."},
{"random_crops_random_rotation_color_perturbations_and_random_noise_addition": "The object entities\u2014random crops, random rotation, color perturbations, and random noise addition\u2014are specific types of augmentations applied to the input images during training. Random crops involve selecting a subsection of the image, random rotation changes the orientation of the image, color perturbations alter the hue, saturation, and brightness, and random noise addition introduces Gaussian or binary-uniform noise to the image. These augmentations help the model learn to recognize patterns and features under various conditions, improving its robustness and performance."},
{"input_and_feature_level_mix_up": "Input- and feature-level mix-up refers to a technique used in machine learning, particularly in the training of convolutional neural networks (CNNs), where data augmentation strategies are applied at both the input level and within the feature space of the model. This method involves combining examples in a way that creates new training samples, which helps in generalizing the model better."},
{"input_and_feature_level_mix_up__was_employed_as__a_regularization_technique_to_further_increase_model_robustness": "The phrase \"was employed as\" indicates that input- and feature-level mix-up was utilized or put into service for a specific purpose within the context of training CNNs. It suggests that this technique was chosen among possibly many others to enhance certain characteristics of the model during its development."},
{"a_regularization_technique_to_further_increase_model_robustness": "A regularization technique to further increase model robustness is a method used in machine learning to prevent overfitting and improve the generalization of a model. Regularization adds additional information to the model to constrain its complexity, which helps it perform better on unseen data by reducing variance and helping to avoid overfitting."},
{"images__were__zero_mean_normalized_after_application_of_augmentations": "The predicate \"were\" indicates that a certain process or operation was applied to the subject (images). In this case, it signifies that the images underwent a specific treatment or transformation as part of the model training pipeline."},
{"zero_mean_normalized_after_application_of_augmentations": "Zero-mean normalization after application of augmentations refers to a preprocessing step where, following various image augmentations (such as random cropping, rotation, color perturbations, and noise addition), the images are normalized by reordering the color channels and subtracting a constant to center the data around zero. This step helps in standardizing the input data for the CNN models, enhancing their performance and generalization capabilities."},
{"cnn_model_predictions": "CNN model predictions refer to the outputs generated by a Convolutional Neural Network, which in this context are used to identify and quantify histologic features in medical images."},
{"cnn_model_predictions__were_used_in_combination_with__mash_crn_scores_from_eight_pathologists": "The phrase \"were used in combination with\" indicates that the CNN model predictions were integrated or merged with another set of data or scores to enhance the overall analysis or prediction process."},
{"mash_crn_scores_from_eight_pathologists": "MASH CRN scores from eight pathologists represent the assessments and gradings provided by a group of eight medical experts using the MASH Clinical Research Network scoring system, which is used to evaluate the severity of liver diseases such as steatosis, lobular inflammation, ballooning, and fibrosis."},
{"gnns__were_trained_to_predict__ordinal_mash_crn_grades_for_steatosis_lobular_inflammation_ballooning_and_fibrosis": "The predicate \"were trained to predict\" indicates that the GNNs underwent a training process where they learned to forecast specific outcomes based on input data. This training involved using CNN model predictions and MASH CRN scores from pathologists."},
{"ordinal_mash_crn_grades_for_steatosis_lobular_inflammation_ballooning_and_fibrosis": "The object \"ordinal MASH CRN grades for steatosis, lobular inflammation, ballooning and fibrosis\" refers to the specific health-related outcomes that the GNNs were trained to predict. These outcomes are part of the MASH CRN scoring system, which assesses various aspects of liver histology, including the severity of steatosis, lobular inflammation, ballooning, and fibrosis."},
{"gnn_methodology": "GNN methodology refers to the use of Graph Neural Networks, a type of machine learning model designed to work with data that can be represented in a graph structure. This methodology is particularly effective for analyzing complex, interconnected data such as human tissues.|Here is another description: GNN methodology refers to the use of Graph Neural Networks, a type of machine learning model designed to process data represented in a graph format. It is particularly effective in scenarios where relationships and structures between data points are crucial for analysis."},
{"gnn_methodology__was_leveraged_for__the_present_development_effort": "The phrase \"was leveraged for\" indicates that GNN methodology was utilized or applied as a tool or resource in the process of the present development effort. It suggests that the methodology played a crucial role in achieving the goals of the effort."},
{"the_present_development_effort": "The present development effort refers to the ongoing project or initiative aimed at creating or improving a system, product, or process. In this context, it involves the application of GNN methodology to analyze and predict ordinal MASH CRN grades for various histologic features in human tissues."},
{"gnn_methodology__is_well_suited_to__data_types_that_can_be_modeled_by_a_graph_structure": "The phrase \"is well suited to\" indicates that GNN methodology is particularly effective or appropriate for handling certain types of data. This suitability arises from the inherent design of GNNs to capture and analyze the complex relationships and structures present in graph-based data."},
{"data_types_that_can_be_modeled_by_a_graph_structure": "Data types that can be modeled by a graph structure include information that can be represented as nodes and edges, capturing relationships and dependencies between different elements. Examples include social networks, molecular structures, and, as mentioned in the context, human tissues organized into structural topologies like fibrosis architecture."},
{"human_tissues": "Human tissues refer to the various types of cells and structures that make up the human body, including organs, muscles, and connective tissues."},
{"human_tissues__are_organized_into__structural_topologies_including_fibrosis_architecture": "The phrase \"are organized into\" describes the structural arrangement or categorization of human tissues into specific patterns or configurations."},
{"structural_topologies_including_fibrosis_architecture": "Structural topologies, including fibrosis architecture, refer to the specific arrangements and patterns that human tissues can form, such as the abnormal tissue formation seen in fibrosis, a condition characterized by the excessive buildup of connective tissue."},
{"superpixels": "Superpixels are small, perceptually meaningful atomic image regions that are used to simplify image data by grouping pixels with similar characteristics. In the context of image processing and computer vision, superpixels help in reducing the complexity of the image by clustering similar pixels together."},
{"superpixels__construct__nodes_in_the_graph": "The predicate \"construct\" in this context refers to the process of building or forming the nodes in a graph using superpixels. This involves converting the clustered superpixels into discrete elements that can be represented as nodes within a graph structure."},
{"nodes_in_the_graph": "Nodes in the graph represent individual superpixel clusters that have been derived from the image data. Each node is characterized by various features such as spatial, topological, and logit-related attributes, which are used to describe the properties of the superpixel cluster it represents."},
{"hundreds_of_thousands_of_pixel_level_predictions": "Hundreds of thousands of pixel-level predictions refer to the detailed, granular data points generated by a convolutional neural network (CNN) when analyzing whole slide images (WSIs). Each prediction corresponds to a specific pixel within the image, indicating the presence or absence of certain histologic features."},
{"hundreds_of_thousands_of_pixel_level_predictions__reduce__thousands_of_superpixel_clusters": "The process of reducing involves consolidating and simplifying the vast amount of pixel-level data into a more manageable and interpretable form. This is achieved by clustering similar pixels together based on predefined features and their spatial relationships."},
{"thousands_of_superpixel_clusters": "Thousands of superpixel clusters are the result of the reduction process. Each cluster represents a group of pixels that share similar characteristics and are likely to belong to the same histologic feature. These clusters serve as nodes in a graph, facilitating further analysis and interpretation of the WSIs."},
{"wsi_regions_predicted_as_background_or_artifact": "WSI regions predicted as background or artifact refer to areas within whole slide images (WSIs) that have been identified by a predictive model as either irrelevant background or artifacts, rather than meaningful biological content."},
{"wsi_regions_predicted_as_background_or_artifact__exclude__during_clustering": "The predicate \"exclude\" in this context means that these identified WSI regions, deemed as background or artifact, are not considered or incorporated into the subsequent clustering process."},
{"during_clustering": "During clustering refers to the phase in the process where data points, in this case, superpixels from WSIs, are grouped together based on certain features or characteristics to form clusters. The exclusion of background or artifact regions ensures that only relevant biological data influences the formation of these clusters."},
{"directed_edges": "Directed edges in the context of graph construction refer to connections that have a specific direction, indicating a one-way relationship between nodes. These edges are essential in defining the structure and relationships within the graph."},
{"directed_edges__place__between_each_node_and_its_five_nearest_neighboring_nodes": "The predicate \"place\" in this context signifies the action of establishing or setting directed edges within the graph. It involves determining the specific connections between nodes based on certain criteria, such as proximity or relevance."},
{"between_each_node_and_its_five_nearest_neighboring_nodes": "The object \"between each node and its five nearest neighboring nodes\" describes the specific configuration of the directed edges. Each node in the graph is connected to its five closest neighboring nodes, creating a network of relationships based on spatial or feature-based proximity."},
{"k_nearest_neighbor_algorithm": "The k-nearest neighbor algorithm is a machine learning method used for classification and regression. In the context of the provided text, it is employed to identify the nearest neighboring nodes in a graph constructed from superpixel clusters."},
{"k_nearest_neighbor_algorithm__use__to_determine_nearest_neighbors": "The use of the k-nearest neighbor algorithm in this context involves applying the algorithm to determine the closest neighboring nodes for each node in the graph. This helps in establishing directed edges between nodes and their nearest neighbors."},
{"to_determine_nearest_neighbors": "The object \"to determine nearest neighbors\" refers to the goal of using the k-nearest neighbor algorithm to find the five closest neighboring nodes for each node in the graph. This process is crucial for constructing the graph's structure based on spatial and topological features."},
{"each_graph_node": "Each graph node in the context refers to individual elements within a constructed graph, specifically superpixel clusters derived from whole slide images (WSIs). These nodes are critical components in the graph-based representation used for analyzing and interpreting histologic features."},
{"each_graph_node__represent__by_three_classes_of_features": "The predicate'represent' indicates that each graph node is characterized or defined by a set of attributes or features. In this context, it means that the nodes are described using specific classes of features that encapsulate various properties relevant to the analysis."},
{"by_three_classes_of_features": "The object 'by three classes of features' refers to the specific types of features used to represent each graph node. These features are categorized into spatial features (mean and standard deviation of coordinates), topological features (area, perimeter, and convexity), and logit-related features (mean and standard deviation of logits for CNN-generated overlays). These classes of features are generated from previously trained convolutional neural network (CNN) predictions and are predefined as biologically relevant classes with known clinical significance."},
{"features": "The features in this context refer to the various attributes and characteristics extracted or derived from the data, specifically from the CNN predictions. These features are crucial for constructing the nodes in the graph and are categorized into spatial, topological, and logit-related features."},
{"features__generate__from_previously_trained_cnn_predictions": "The predicate \"generate\" indicates the process by which the features are produced. In this context, the features are generated from the predictions made by a previously trained Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). This generation process involves transforming raw data into meaningful attributes that can be used for further analysis and modeling."},
{"from_previously_trained_cnn_predictions": "The object \"from previously trained CNN predictions\" refers to the output of a Convolutional Neural Network that has been trained beforehand. These predictions serve as the basis for generating the features used in the graph construction. The CNN predictions are likely to include various histologic features relevant to the study, such as biological classes of known clinical relevance."},
{"cnn_predictions": "CNN predictions refer to the outputs generated by Convolutional Neural Networks, which are a class of deep learning algorithms designed to analyze visual imagery. In this context, CNN predictions are used to identify and classify various histologic features within whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"cnn_predictions__predefine__as_biological_classes_of_known_clinical_relevance": "The predicate \"predefine\" indicates that the CNN predictions are set or established in advance according to specific criteria. Here, it means that the CNN predictions are categorized into pre-established biological classes."},
{"as_biological_classes_of_known_clinical_relevance": "Biological classes of known clinical relevance are predefined categories that have established importance in clinical practice. These classes help in understanding and interpreting the histologic features identified by the CNN, ensuring that the predictions are meaningful and useful for clinical decision-making."},
{"spatial_features": "Spatial features refer to the characteristics that describe the arrangement and distribution of elements within a defined space. In the context of image analysis and machine learning, spatial features are critical for understanding the positional relationships between different components of an image."},
{"spatial_features__include__mean_and_standard_deviation_of_x_y_coordinates": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, spatial features, comprises or contains certain elements. In this case, it indicates that spatial features are made up of specific statistical measures related to coordinates."},
{"mean_and_standard_deviation_of_x_y_coordinates": "The mean and standard deviation of (x, y) coordinates are statistical measures that provide information about the central tendency and dispersion of the coordinates within a dataset. The mean represents the average position, while the standard deviation indicates the spread or variability of the coordinates around this average."},
{"topological_features": "Topological features refer to the geometric and structural properties of clusters within a dataset. In the context of graph-based models, these features help describe the shape and arrangement of nodes, which are essential for accurate classification and analysis."},
{"topological_features__include__area_perimeter_and_convexity_of_the_cluster": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, topological features, comprises certain elements. In this case, it indicates that topological features are made up of specific measurements related to the geometry of clusters."},
{"area_perimeter_and_convexity_of_the_cluster": "Area, perimeter, and convexity of the cluster are specific measurements that define the geometric properties of a cluster. The area represents the total space occupied by the cluster, the perimeter is the total length of the cluster's boundary, and convexity measures how much the cluster deviates from being a convex shape."},
{"logit_related_features": "Logit-related features are a set of characteristics derived from the outputs of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) that are used to represent the likelihood of different classes in the network's predictions. These features are crucial for understanding and interpreting the CNN's decision-making process."},
{"logit_related_features__include__mean_and_standard_deviation_of_logits_for_each_of_the_classes_of_cnn_generated_overlays": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, logit-related features, comprises certain elements. In this context, it indicates that the logit-related features are made up of specific statistical measures derived from the CNN's predictions."},
{"mean_and_standard_deviation_of_logits_for_each_of_the_classes_of_cnn_generated_overlays": "The mean and standard deviation of logits for each of the classes of CNN-generated overlays represent statistical measures that quantify the central tendency and variability, respectively, of the logit values produced by the CNN for each class. These measures help in assessing the confidence and spread of the CNN's predictions across different classes."},
{"scores_from_multiple_pathologists": "Scores from multiple pathologists refer to the individual assessments and ratings provided by different medical professionals who specialize in pathology. These scores are crucial in evaluating and diagnosing medical conditions based on histological features observed in tissue samples."},
{"scores_from_multiple_pathologists__use__independently_during_training": "The predicate \"use\" in this context signifies the application or incorporation of these individual pathologist scores into the training process of a machine learning model. Specifically, it indicates that the model leverages these scores as part of its learning mechanism to improve its accuracy and reliability in making predictions."},
{"independently_during_training": "\"Independently during training\" describes the manner in which the scores from multiple pathologists are utilized. This means that each pathologist's scores are considered separately and not aggregated or averaged, allowing the model to account for variability and potential biases in individual assessments. This approach helps in reducing the impact of scoring inconsistencies and enhances the model's ability to generalize across different pathologists' evaluations."},
{"consensus_scores": "Consensus scores refer to the aggregated evaluations from multiple pathologists, which are used to provide a more reliable and unbiased assessment of patient disease severity. These scores are derived from the independent evaluations of several pathologists, aiming to mitigate individual scoring variability and bias."},
{"consensus_scores__use__for_evaluating_model_performance_on_validation_data": "The use of consensus scores in this context signifies the application of these aggregated evaluations as a standard metric. This predicate highlights the process of employing multiple expert opinions to create a unified, more accurate measure for assessing model performance."},
{"for_evaluating_model_performance_on_validation_data": "Evaluating model performance on validation data involves assessing how well the model predicts outcomes based on a set of data that was not used during the training phase. This process ensures that the model's predictions are robust and generalizable to new, unseen data."},
{"leveraging_scores_from_multiple_pathologists": "Leveraging scores from multiple pathologists involves using the assessments and evaluations provided by several medical professionals specialized in pathology. This approach aims to enhance the accuracy and reliability of medical diagnoses by incorporating diverse expert opinions."},
{"leveraging_scores_from_multiple_pathologists__reduce__the_potential_impact_of_scoring_variability_and_bias_associated_with_a_single_reader": "The act of reducing in this context refers to minimizing or lessening the influence of certain factors. Specifically, it aims to decrease the effects of scoring variability and bias that can arise when diagnoses are made by a single reader."},
{"the_potential_impact_of_scoring_variability_and_bias_associated_with_a_single_reader": "The potential impact of scoring variability and bias associated with a single reader refers to the inconsistencies and subjective influences that can affect the accuracy of medical scoring when performed by one individual. This can include differences in interpretation, personal biases, and varying levels of experience or expertise."},
{"gnn_model": "The GNN model is a type of machine learning model that utilizes graph neural networks to process and analyze data represented in a graph structure. In this context, it is designed to handle complex, multi-dimensional data by integrating various features and relationships between nodes."},
{"gnn_model__specify__as_a_mixed_effects_model": "The predicate \"specify\" in this context refers to the process of defining or configuring the GNN model with certain characteristics or parameters. Specifically, it involves setting the model up to account for particular factors, such as biases from multiple pathologists, to improve its performance and reduce systemic errors."},
{"as_a_mixed_effects_model": "The object \"as a \u2018mixed effects\u2019 model\" describes the configuration of the GNN model to include both fixed and random effects. This setup allows the model to account for variability and bias introduced by different pathologists, enhancing its robustness and accuracy in predicting patient disease states."},
{"each_pathologist_s_policy": "Each pathologist\u2019s policy refers to the individual approach or method used by different pathologists in evaluating and scoring medical images, particularly in the context of assessing disease severity in whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"each_pathologist_s_policy__specify__in_the_model_by_a_set_of_bias_parameters_learned_during_training": "The predicate \"specify\" in this context means to define or detail the unique bias parameters associated with each pathologist\u2019s scoring tendencies within the Graph Neural Network (GNN) model. These parameters are tailored to account for the inherent variability and potential biases in how each pathologist assesses disease severity."},
{"in_the_model_by_a_set_of_bias_parameters_learned_during_training": "In the model by a set of bias parameters learned during training refers to the specific adjustments or corrections applied to the GNN model to accommodate the distinct scoring patterns of each pathologist. These bias parameters are derived from the training process, where the model learns to recognize and quantify the consistent deviations in scoring by different pathologists."},
{"bias_parameters": "Bias parameters are specific values learned during the training of a model to account for individual variations in scoring among different pathologists. These parameters help adjust the model's predictions to align more closely with the scoring tendencies of each pathologist."},
{"bias_parameters__discard__at_test_time": "The action of discarding refers to the process of removing or ignoring certain elements during a specific phase, in this case, at test time. This means that the bias parameters, which were used to adjust predictions during training, are not applied when the model is deployed for actual predictions."},
{"at_test_time": "At test time refers to the phase when the trained model is used to make predictions on new, unseen data. During this phase, the model relies solely on the unbiased estimate of the patient\u2019s disease state, without incorporating the bias parameters that were used during training."},
{"model": "The subject, 'Model', refers to a Graph Neural Network (GNN) designed to evaluate and score medical images, specifically Whole Slide Images (WSIs), based on histologic features and pathologist assessments.|Here is another description: The model in question is a Graph Neural Network (GNN) designed to evaluate and score medical images, specifically Whole Slide Images (WSIs), by leveraging predictions from a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and incorporating features relevant to clinical histology."},
{"model__train__on_all_unique_label_graph_pairs": "The predicate, 'train', describes the process of instructing and fine-tuning the GNN model using a dataset. This involves feeding the model with input data and adjusting its parameters to minimize errors in predictions, thereby improving its accuracy and reliability in assessing WSIs."},
{"on_all_unique_label_graph_pairs": "The object, 'on all unique label\u2013graph pairs', indicates the specific dataset used for training the GNN model. This dataset consists of unique combinations of labels (scores provided by pathologists) and graph representations of WSIs, where each graph is constructed from superpixels and their features, including spatial, topological, and logit-related attributes."},
{"label": "The label in this context refers to the classification or scoring assigned to a particular medical image or data point, specifically in the context of evaluating MASH histology."},
{"label__represent__by_a_score_and_a_variable_that_indicated_which_pathologist_in_the_training_set_generated_this_score": "The predicate'represent' indicates how the label is characterized or defined within the model. It specifies that the label is not a simple score but includes additional information about the source of the score."},
{"by_a_score_and_a_variable_that_indicated_which_pathologist_in_the_training_set_generated_this_score": "The object describes the detailed representation of the label, which includes a score and a variable that indicates which pathologist from the training set generated this score. This representation helps in accounting for individual pathologist biases and variabilities in scoring."},
{"model__select__the_specified_pathologist_bias_parameter_and_add_it_to_the_unbiased_estimate_of_the_patient_s_disease_state": "The predicate \"select\" in this context refers to the model's action of choosing a specific pathologist's bias parameter during the training phase. This selection is crucial for adjusting the model's predictions to account for individual pathologists' tendencies to either overestimate or underestimate disease severity."},
{"the_specified_pathologist_bias_parameter_and_add_it_to_the_unbiased_estimate_of_the_patient_s_disease_state": "The specified pathologist bias parameter represents an adjustment factor learned by the model to correct for the inherent biases of individual pathologists. This parameter is added to the unbiased estimate of the patient\u2019s disease state to produce a more accurate and personalized prediction, reflecting the specific scoring tendencies of the pathologist whose scores were used in training."},
{"biases__update__via_backpropagation_only_on_wsis_scored_by_the_corresponding_pathologists": "The predicate \"update\" in this context describes the process by which the GNN model refines the bias parameters associated with each pathologist. This updating is done during the training phase to better align the model's predictions with the scores provided by each pathologist."},
{"via_backpropagation_only_on_wsis_scored_by_the_corresponding_pathologists": "The object \"via backpropagation only on WSIs scored by the corresponding pathologists\" specifies the method and condition under which the bias parameters are updated. Backpropagation is a common machine learning technique used to adjust model parameters, and here it is applied selectively to Whole Slide Images (WSIs) that were scored by the specific pathologist associated with each bias parameter."},
{"gnns__deploy__using_only_the_unbiased_estimate": "The predicate \"deploy\" in this context refers to the action of utilizing or implementing the GNN model in a practical scenario. Specifically, it involves using the trained GNN model to generate predictions or scores for patient disease states based on the input data."},
{"using_only_the_unbiased_estimate": "\"Using only the unbiased estimate\" refers to the method by which the GNN model produces labels or scores during deployment. This means that the model relies solely on the unbiased estimate of the patient\u2019s disease state, discarding any bias parameters learned during training that were specific to individual pathologists. This approach aims to provide a more objective and consistent evaluation of the disease state across different pathologists."},
{"gnns_in_this_study": "GNNs in this study refer to Graph Neural Networks utilized for analyzing and interpreting medical images, specifically Whole Slide Images (WSIs), by constructing graphs from superpixel clusters and leveraging features derived from Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) predictions."},
{"gnns_in_this_study__train__using_mash_crn_scores_from_eight_pathologists": "The predicate \"train\" in this context signifies the process of instructing the GNNs to learn from the provided data, specifically using MASH CRN scores, to improve their ability to predict and evaluate histologic features and patient disease states accurately."},
{"using_mash_crn_scores_from_eight_pathologists": "The object \"using MASH CRN scores from eight pathologists\" describes the method and data source employed for training the GNNs. MASH CRN scores are clinical ratings provided by pathologists experienced in evaluating MASH histology, which are used to train the GNNs, helping them to learn and mitigate biases associated with individual pathologists' scoring tendencies."},
{"pathologists__have__experience_in_evaluating_mash_histology": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies possession or the acquisition of a particular skill or expertise. It indicates that the subject, pathologists, possess a specific capability or knowledge base relevant to their field."},
{"data": "Data in this context refers to the collection of information and features extracted from whole slide images (WSIs) using convolutional neural networks (CNNs). This data includes various classes of features such as biological, spatial, topological, and logit-related features, which are crucial for constructing graph nodes and edges."},
{"data__use__for_image_segmentation_model_training": "The use of data in this context involves employing the extracted features and information from WSIs to train an image segmentation model. This process includes constructing graph nodes from superpixels, defining edges between neighboring nodes, and utilizing CNN predictions to generate relevant histologic features."},
{"for_image_segmentation_model_training": "The image segmentation model training involves using the data, comprising CNN-generated features and predictions, to develop a model capable of segmenting and quantifying histologic features in WSIs. This training process leverages scores from multiple pathologists to reduce bias and improve model performance."},
{"gnn_nodes_and_edges__build__from_cnn_predictions_of_relevant_histologic_features": "The predicate \"build\" indicates the process of constructing or creating the GNN nodes and edges. This involves defining the nodes based on superpixel clusters and establishing connections (edges) between them using a nearest neighbor algorithm."},
{"from_cnn_predictions_of_relevant_histologic_features": "The object \"from CNN predictions of relevant histologic features\" describes the source data used to create the GNN nodes and edges. These predictions are generated by a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and include features such as spatial, topological, and logit-related attributes that are relevant to the histologic analysis."},
{"tiered_approach": "The tiered approach refers to a method where the graph neural network (GNN) nodes and edges are constructed from convolutional neural network (CNN) predictions of relevant histologic features. This method integrates multiple stages of model training to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of whole slide image (WSI) analysis."},
{"tiered_approach__improve__upon_previous_work": "The predicate \"improve\" indicates that the tiered approach enhances or makes better the outcomes or performance compared to a previous method. In this context, it suggests that the new method provides superior results or more effective processing of WSIs."},
{"upon_previous_work": "\"Upon previous work\" refers to the earlier methods or models that were used for slide-level scoring and histologic feature quantification. These previous works involved training models on scores from a single pathologist and using separate models for different tasks, which the new tiered approach seeks to enhance."},
{"previous_work": "Previous work refers to earlier research efforts conducted in the field of medical image analysis, specifically focusing on the development of models for evaluating histologic features and slide-level scoring in medical images."},
{"previous_work__train__separate_models_for_slide_level_scoring_and_histologic_feature_quantification": "The predicate \"train\" indicates the process of using data to teach or instruct a model, enabling it to learn patterns, make predictions, or perform specific tasks based on the provided examples."},
{"separate_models_for_slide_level_scoring_and_histologic_feature_quantification": "Separate models for slide-level scoring and histologic feature quantification represent distinct machine learning models developed in previous research. These models were designed to independently assess the overall severity or grade of a medical slide (slide-level scoring) and to quantify specific histologic features present within the slide, such as the presence and extent of certain cellular or tissue characteristics."},
{"ordinal_scores": "Ordinal scores refer to the numerical values assigned to different levels of severity or categories in a medical context, such as the degree of disease in a tissue sample. These scores are crucial for quantifying and standardizing the assessment of histologic features.|Here is another description: Ordinal scores refer to the grades or stages derived from the GNN model, which are used to assess the severity of histologic features like fibrosis in MAS and CRN.|Here is another description: Ordinal scores refer to the grades or stages derived from a Graph Neural Network (GNN) used in medical assessments, specifically for liver conditions like steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and CRN fibrosis."},
{"ordinal_scores__construct__directly_from_the_cnn_labeled_wsis": "The predicate 'construct' in this context means to build or create the ordinal scores. This involves processing and analyzing the data obtained from the CNN-labeled WSIs to generate meaningful and standardized scores that reflect the severity or category of the histologic features."},
{"directly_from_the_cnn_labeled_wsis": "CNN-labeled WSIs (Whole Slide Images) are digital images of tissue samples that have been analyzed and labeled by a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). These labels indicate the presence and extent of various histologic features, which are then used to directly construct ordinal scores that represent the severity or category of the disease."},
{"gnn_derived_continuous_score_generation": "GNN-derived continuous score generation refers to a method where a Graph Neural Network (GNN) is used to generate continuous scores for fibrosis by mapping ordinal grades or stages to bins. This process involves extracting activation layer output logits from the GNN ordinal scoring model and averaging them. The GNN learns inter-bin cutoffs during training, which are used in a piecewise linear mapping from logits to binned continuous scores."},
{"gnn_derived_continuous_score_generation__produces__continuous_mas_and_crn_fibrosis_scores": "The predicate \"produces\" in this context signifies the action of generating or creating. Specifically, it describes the process by which the GNN-derived continuous score generation method results in the creation of continuous fibrosis scores. This is achieved through a series of steps including mapping ordinal scores to bins, averaging logits, and applying piecewise linear mapping based on learned cutoffs."},
{"continuous_mas_and_crn_fibrosis_scores": "Continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores are the output of the GNN-derived continuous score generation process. These scores represent a continuous range of fibrosis severity, derived from mapping ordinal grades to bins. The scores are produced by averaging logits extracted from the GNN model and applying a piecewise linear mapping, ensuring a uniform distribution across the training data.|Here is another description: Continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores are numerical representations of the severity of liver fibrosis, specifically for the MASH CRN and MAS components, generated through a specialized scoring system."},
{"continuous_mas_and_crn_fibrosis_scores__are_produced_by__mapping_gnn_derived_ordinal_grades_stages_to_bins": "The process of producing continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores involves a specific method where ordinal grades or stages derived from a Graph Neural Network (GNN) are mapped to bins. This mapping spreads the ordinal scores over a continuous range, providing a more nuanced representation of fibrosis severity."},
{"mapping_gnn_derived_ordinal_grades_stages_to_bins": "Mapping GNN-derived ordinal grades/stages to bins is a technique used to convert discrete ordinal scores, generated by a Graph Neural Network, into continuous scores. This is achieved by spreading the ordinal scores over a continuous range, with the help of logit-valued cutoffs that separate the bins, ensuring a balanced and uniform distribution of scores across the severity continuum.|Here is another description: Mapping GNN-derived ordinal grades/stages to bins is a process where Graph Neural Network (GNN) outputs, which are ordinal grades or stages, are converted into discrete bins. This method helps in standardizing and categorizing continuous data into manageable and interpretable segments."},
{"ordinal_scores__are_spread_over__a_continuous_range_spanning_a_unit_distance_of_1": "The phrase \"are spread over\" indicates the distribution or mapping of ordinal scores across a specified range. In this context, it describes how the ordinal scores generated by the GNN are mapped to a continuous range."},
{"a_continuous_range_spanning_a_unit_distance_of_1": "A continuous range spanning a unit distance of 1 represents the full spectrum over which the ordinal scores are distributed. This range ensures that the scores are evenly spread out, allowing for a more nuanced and detailed assessment of disease severity."},
{"activation_layer_output_logits": "The activation layer output logits represent the raw, unprocessed outputs from a neural network's activation layer, which are crucial for further processing and interpretation in machine learning models.|Here is another description: The activation layer output logits refer to the raw, unnormalized scores outputted by the activation layer of a neural network, specifically within the context of a Graph Neural Network (GNN) used for ordinal scoring of histologic features like fibrosis."},
{"activation_layer_output_logits__are_extracted_from__the_gnn_ordinal_scoring_model_pipeline": "The phrase \"are extracted from\" indicates the process by which the activation layer output logits are obtained or derived. This extraction is a critical step in the model's pipeline, allowing these logits to be used for subsequent calculations and transformations."},
{"the_gnn_ordinal_scoring_model_pipeline": "The GNN ordinal scoring model pipeline is a sequence of operations and transformations applied to input data within a Graph Neural Network (GNN) to produce ordinal scores. This pipeline includes various layers and processes designed to map complex input data into a structured ordinal output, which is then used for further analysis or decision-making."},
{"activation_layer_output_logits__are__averaged": "The predicate \"are\" indicates a state of being or a process that has been applied to the subject. In this context, it signifies that a specific operation\u2014averaging\u2014has been performed on the activation layer output logits."},
{"averaged": "The object \"averaged\" describes the result of applying an averaging process to the activation layer output logits. This means that the raw logit values have been combined into a single value by calculating their mean, which is a common preprocessing step in machine learning models to normalize data."},
{"gnn__learned__inter_bin_cutoffs_during_training": "The predicate \"learned\" indicates that the GNN has been trained to acquire specific knowledge or parameters. In this case, it has learned to determine certain thresholds or boundaries during its training process."},
{"inter_bin_cutoffs_during_training": "\"Inter-bin cutoffs during training\" refers to the specific thresholds that the GNN has learned to distinguish between different bins or categories of ordinal scores. These cutoffs are crucial for mapping the ordinal scores to continuous values in a piecewise linear fashion."},
{"piecewise_linear_mapping": "Piecewise linear mapping is a method used to convert discrete ordinal data into a continuous scale by applying linear transformations within defined intervals or bins."},
{"piecewise_linear_mapping__is_performed__per_logit_ordinal_bin_from_the_logits_to_binned_continuous_scores_using_the_logit_valued_cutoffs_to_separate_bins": "The predicate \"is performed\" indicates the action of executing or carrying out the piecewise linear mapping process. This involves applying the mapping technique to transform ordinal data into continuous scores."},
{"per_logit_ordinal_bin_from_the_logits_to_binned_continuous_scores_using_the_logit_valued_cutoffs_to_separate_bins": "The object describes the specific application of piecewise linear mapping, which is done per logit ordinal bin. This means that for each bin of logit values derived from the activation layer output, the mapping is applied to convert these logits into binned continuous scores. The logit-valued cutoffs are used to separate these bins, ensuring a smooth transition between them."},
{"bins_on_either_end_of_the_disease_severity_continuum_per_histologic_feature": "Bins on either end of the disease severity continuum per histologic feature refer to the extreme ends of the spectrum of disease severity as categorized by specific histologic features. These bins represent the most severe and least severe cases within the continuum of the disease."},
{"bins_on_either_end_of_the_disease_severity_continuum_per_histologic_feature__have__long_tailed_distributions_that_are_not_penalized_during_training": "The predicate \"have\" indicates possession or association. In this context, it signifies that the bins at the ends of the disease severity continuum are characterized by or associated with certain properties or features."},
{"long_tailed_distributions_that_are_not_penalized_during_training": "Long-tailed distributions that are not penalized during training refer to data distributions where the majority of the data points are concentrated at one end, with a few extreme values extending the tail of the distribution. In the context of machine learning training, these distributions are not adjusted or altered to fit a standard model, meaning the model does not apply penalties or corrections to these extreme values during the training process."},
{"logit_values_in_the_first_and_last_bins": "The logit values in the first and last bins refer to the extreme ends of the ordinal bins used in the GNN-derived continuous score generation process. These bins represent the minimum and maximum logit values, respectively, and are crucial for ensuring the balanced linear mapping of the outer bins in the disease severity continuum."},
{"logit_values_in_the_first_and_last_bins__were_restricted_to__minimum_and_maximum_values_respectively_during_a_post_processing_step": "The predicate \"were restricted to\" indicates a limitation or constraint applied to the logit values in the first and last bins. This restriction is necessary to maintain the uniformity of logit value distributions across the training data, ensuring that the outer bins are mapped linearly and balanced."},
{"minimum_and_maximum_values_respectively_during_a_post_processing_step": "The minimum and maximum values, respectively, during a post-processing step, refer to the outer-edge cutoffs chosen to maximize the uniformity of logit value distributions across the training data. These values are applied to the first and last bins to ensure that the logit values are constrained within a defined range, facilitating a balanced and linear mapping of the bins."},
{"these_values": "The values in question refer to the minimum and maximum logit values applied to the first and last bins during a post-processing step in the generation of continuous fibrosis scores using a Graph Neural Network (GNN)."},
{"these_values__were_defined_by__outer_edge_cutoffs_chosen_to_maximize_the_uniformity_of_logit_value_distributions_across_training_data": "The phrase \"were defined by\" indicates that the subject values were established or set according to a specific method or criteria, in this case, the outer-edge cutoffs."},
{"outer_edge_cutoffs_chosen_to_maximize_the_uniformity_of_logit_value_distributions_across_training_data": "The outer-edge cutoffs are specific values chosen during the training of a GNN model. Their purpose is to maximize the uniformity of logit value distributions across the training data, ensuring a balanced linear mapping of the outer bins in the disease severity continuum."},
{"gnn_continuous_feature_training_and_ordinal_mapping": "The process of GNN continuous feature training and ordinal mapping involves using a Graph Neural Network (GNN) to generate continuous scores for fibrosis by mapping ordinal grades to bins. This process includes extracting activation layer output logits, averaging them, and performing piecewise linear mapping from logits to binned continuous scores."},
{"gnn_continuous_feature_training_and_ordinal_mapping__were_performed_for__each_mash_crn_and_mas_component_fibrosis_separately": "The phrase \"were performed for\" indicates that the GNN continuous feature training and ordinal mapping process was specifically carried out in relation to each MASH CRN and MAS component fibrosis. This suggests a targeted application of the GNN methodology to these particular fibrosis components."},
{"each_mash_crn_and_mas_component_fibrosis_separately": "Each MASH CRN (Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis Clinical Research Network) and MAS (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) component fibrosis refers to specific, distinct elements of fibrosis that are assessed within the context of metabolic dysfunction-associated liver diseases. These components are evaluated separately to ensure accurate and detailed scoring and mapping using the GNN methodology."},
{"quality_control_measures": "Quality control measures refer to the systematic actions and procedures put in place to maintain and enhance the quality of a product or process. In the context of model learning, these measures are crucial to ensure that the data used for training is accurate, reliable, and of high quality."},
{"quality_control_measures__were_implemented__to_ensure_model_learning_from_high_quality_data": "The predicate \"were implemented\" indicates that specific actions or procedures were put into effect. In this case, it signifies that quality control measures were actively introduced and applied to the process of model learning."},
{"to_ensure_model_learning_from_high_quality_data": "The object \"to ensure model learning from high-quality data\" describes the purpose or goal of the quality control measures. It highlights the importance of using accurate and reliable data to train models, ensuring that the learning process is effective and the resulting models are robust and dependable."},
{"pathai_liver_pathologists": "PathAI liver pathologists are specialized medical professionals employed by PathAI, a company that focuses on applying artificial intelligence to pathology. These pathologists have expertise in liver-related diseases and are responsible for ensuring the accuracy and quality of annotations and scoring in pathology projects."},
{"pathai_liver_pathologists__evaluated__all_annotators_for_annotation_scoring_performance_at_project": "The predicate \"evaluated\" signifies that the PathAI liver pathologists conducted a thorough assessment. This evaluation aimed to measure the performance and accuracy of the annotators involved in the project, ensuring that the annotations and scorings met the required standards for high-quality data."},
{"all_annotators_for_annotation_scoring_performance_at_project": "All annotators for annotation/scoring performance at the project refers to the group of individuals responsible for creating annotations and performing scorings as part of the pathology project. Their performance was assessed to ensure consistency, accuracy, and adherence to the project's quality standards."},
{"pathai_pathologists": "PathAI pathologists are medical professionals specialized in pathology, employed by PathAI, a company that leverages artificial intelligence to enhance pathology practices. They are responsible for reviewing and ensuring the quality of medical data used in machine learning model training.|Here is another description: PathAI pathologists are medical professionals specialized in pathology, employed by PathAI, a company that leverages artificial intelligence to enhance pathology practices. They are responsible for reviewing and ensuring the quality of annotations and model performance in the context of AI-driven pathology solutions.|Here is another description: PathAI pathologists are medical professionals employed by PathAI who specialize in the interpretation of medical images, particularly in the context of pathology. They are responsible for reviewing and assessing the quality of annotations and the performance of machine learning models in medical imaging tasks."},
{"pathai_pathologists__performed__quality_control_review_on_all_annotations_collected_throughout_model_training": "The predicate \"performed\" indicates that the PathAI pathologists actively carried out a specific task. In this context, it means they executed a thorough examination and evaluation process."},
{"quality_control_review_on_all_annotations_collected_throughout_model_training": "The quality control review on all annotations collected throughout model training refers to a meticulous process where PathAI pathologists assessed the accuracy and reliability of annotations gathered during the development of a machine learning model. This review aimed to ensure that only high-quality annotations were utilized for training the model, thereby enhancing its performance and reliability."},
{"annotations_deemed_to_be_of_high_quality": "Annotations deemed to be of high quality refer to the specific markings or labels that were reviewed and approved by PathAI pathologists as meeting the necessary standards for use in the model development process."},
{"annotations_deemed_to_be_of_high_quality__were_used_for__model_training": "The phrase \"were used for\" indicates that the high-quality annotations served as input or data for a subsequent process, in this case, the training of a machine learning model."},
{"all_other_annotations": "All other annotations refer to the data points or markings that were not deemed to be of high quality by PathAI pathologists during the quality control review process."},
{"all_other_annotations__were_excluded_from__model_development": "Were excluded from indicates the action taken where certain annotations were not included in the subsequent stages of the process due to not meeting the quality standards set by the PathAI pathologists."},
{"pathai_pathologists__performed__slide_level_review_of_the_model_s_performance_after_every_iteration_of_model_training": "The predicate \"performed\" indicates that the PathAI pathologists actively carried out a specific task. In this context, it refers to the action of conducting a thorough examination and evaluation of the model's performance at the slide level."},
{"slide_level_review_of_the_model_s_performance_after_every_iteration_of_model_training": "The slide-level review of the model\u2019s performance after every iteration of model training is a critical quality control step. It involves PathAI pathologists assessing how well the AI model is performing on individual slides, providing feedback on its strengths and weaknesses, and ensuring that the model is learning effectively from the training data."},
{"pathai_pathologists__provided__specific_qualitative_feedback_on_areas_of_strength_weakness_after_each_iteration": "The predicate \"provided\" indicates that PathAI pathologists offered or supplied something. In this context, it means that after each iteration of model training, the pathologists gave their insights and observations."},
{"specific_qualitative_feedback_on_areas_of_strength_weakness_after_each_iteration": "Specific qualitative feedback on areas of strength/weakness refers to detailed comments and observations provided by PathAI pathologists. This feedback highlights the model's performance, pointing out what it does well (strengths) and where it may need improvement (weaknesses) after each training iteration."},
{"model_performance": "Model performance refers to the effectiveness and accuracy of a machine learning model in completing its designated tasks. In this context, it specifically pertains to how well the model trained by PathAI pathologists can identify and classify features within medical images.|Here is another description: The model performance refers to the effectiveness and accuracy of a machine learning model in analyzing and interpreting medical images, specifically in the context of pathology."},
{"model_performance__was_characterized_at__the_patch_and_slide_levels_in_an_internal_held_out_test_set": "\"Was characterized at\" indicates the process of evaluating and describing the model's performance metrics at specific levels. This involves assessing how well the model functions under different conditions or at different scales, such as individual image patches or entire slides."},
{"the_patch_and_slide_levels_in_an_internal_held_out_test_set": "The patch and slide levels in an internal (held-out) test set represent the two scales at which the model's performance was evaluated. The patch level involves small, localized areas within an image, while the slide level encompasses the entire image. The internal held-out test set is a reserved dataset used specifically for testing the model, separate from the data it was trained on."},
{"model_performance__was_compared_against__pathologist_consensus_scoring_in_an_entirely_held_out_test_set": "The phrase \"was compared against\" indicates an evaluation process where the model's results were assessed by comparing them to a standard or benchmark, in this case, the consensus scoring by pathologists."},
{"pathologist_consensus_scoring_in_an_entirely_held_out_test_set": "Pathologist consensus scoring in an entirely held-out test set represents the collective evaluation and scoring of medical images by a group of expert pathologists, used as a benchmark to assess the model's performance on images that were not part of the model's training data."},
{"entirely_held_out_test_set": "The entirely held-out test set is a dataset that was completely separate from the data used during the model's development and training phases. It was specifically reserved to evaluate the model's performance under conditions that it had not encountered during its development."},
{"entirely_held_out_test_set__contained__images_that_were_out_of_distribution_relative_to_images_from_which_the_model_had_learned_during_development": "The predicate \"contained\" indicates that the entirely held-out test set included or had within it certain types of images. This relation highlights the composition of the test set in terms of the data it encompasses."},
{"images_that_were_out_of_distribution_relative_to_images_from_which_the_model_had_learned_during_development": "The images that were out of distribution relative to images from which the model had learned during development are those that differ significantly from the training data. These images present new, unseen scenarios or variations that the model must handle, testing its generalization capabilities."},
{"ai_based_scoring": "AI-based scoring refers to the use of artificial intelligence algorithms to evaluate and assign scores to various data inputs. In this context, it involves the application of AI algorithms to assess certain criteria."},
{"ai_based_scoring__assessed_for__repeatability": "Assessed for indicates the process of evaluating or examining a particular characteristic or quality. Here, it signifies the examination of the AI-based scoring's capability to produce consistent results."},
{"repeatability": "Repeatability in this context refers to the consistency or reliability of the AI-based scoring when applied multiple times to the same dataset. It measures the intra-method variability, which is the degree to which the AI algorithms produce the same results across different runs.|Here is another description: Repeatability refers to the consistency of results obtained from the same method under the same conditions. In the context of AI-based scoring, it measures the intra-method variability of the AI algorithms."},
{"repeatability__assessed_by__deploying_ai_algorithms_ten_times": "Assessed by indicates the method or process used to evaluate or measure a particular characteristic or performance. In this case, it describes how the repeatability of AI-based scoring is evaluated."},
{"deploying_ai_algorithms_ten_times": "Deploying AI algorithms ten times involves running the same AI algorithms on the same test set multiple times to observe the variability in the results. This process helps in computing the percentage positive agreement across the ten reads by the model, thereby assessing the repeatability of the AI-based scoring."},
{"ai_algorithms": "AI algorithms refer to the computational methods and processes designed to enable machines to perform tasks that typically require human intelligence. In this context, they are specifically tailored for scoring and analysis purposes."},
{"ai_algorithms__deployed_on__held_out_analytic_performance_test_set": "The phrase \"deployed on\" indicates the action of implementing or applying the AI algorithms to a specific dataset or environment. It suggests that the algorithms are being used to process and analyze the data contained within the held-out analytic performance test set."},
{"held_out_analytic_performance_test_set": "The held-out analytic performance test set is a reserved dataset used to evaluate the performance and reliability of AI algorithms. It serves as an independent benchmark to assess how well the algorithms can generalize and produce consistent results when applied to new, unseen data."},
{"percentage_positive_agreement": "Percentage positive agreement refers to the metric used to evaluate the consistency and reliability of AI-based scoring across multiple iterations."},
{"percentage_positive_agreement__computed_across__ten_reads_by_the_model": "The term 'computed across' indicates the process of calculating or determining the percentage positive agreement by analyzing data from multiple instances or iterations."},
{"ten_reads_by_the_model": "Ten reads by the model represent the number of times the AI algorithms were deployed on the same test set to assess the repeatability and intra-method variability of the AI-based scoring system."},
{"model_derived_predictions__compared_with__median_consensus_grades_stages": "The phrase 'compared with' indicates an evaluation process where the model's predictions are assessed against a standard or benchmark to determine their accuracy and reliability."},
{"median_consensus_grades_stages": "Median consensus grades/stages are the average assessments provided by a group of expert pathologists who have independently evaluated the same set of MASH biopsies. These grades serve as a benchmark for comparing the model's performance."},
{"panel_of_three_expert_pathologists": "A panel of three expert pathologists is a group of highly skilled medical professionals specialized in the study of diseases, particularly through the examination of tissue samples. In this context, they are responsible for evaluating and providing consensus grades for various histologic features in MASH biopsies."},
{"panel_of_three_expert_pathologists__evaluated__mash_biopsies": "The predicate \"evaluated\" signifies the action taken by the panel of expert pathologists to assess and analyze MASH biopsies. This process involves examining the tissue samples to determine the severity of different histologic features such as steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and fibrosis, and then providing consensus grades based on their expert judgment."},
{"mash_biopsies": "MASH biopsies refer to tissue samples taken from patients with Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH), a type of liver disease. These biopsies are crucial for diagnosing and assessing the severity of the disease. In this context, the biopsies were evaluated by a panel of expert pathologists to provide consensus grades for various histologic features, which were then used to verify the performance accuracy of a model."},
{"clinical_trial": "The clinical trial mentioned is a recently completed phase 2b trial focused on MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis), where biopsies were evaluated by a panel of expert pathologists. This trial provided the data used for comparing model-derived predictions with pathologist consensus grades."},
{"clinical_trial__not_included_in__model_training": "The phrase \"not included in\" indicates that the images from the clinical trial were deliberately excluded from the process of training the model. This exclusion ensures that the model's performance can be objectively evaluated on new, unseen data."},
{"clinical_trial_images": "The clinical trial images refer to the set of images obtained from a recently completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial, which were used for evaluating model performance in grading histologic features such as steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and fibrosis."},
{"clinical_trial_images__served_as__external_held_out_test_set": "The predicate \"served as\" indicates the role or function that the clinical trial images played in the context of model performance evaluation. Specifically, these images were utilized as a means to test and verify the accuracy and reliability of the model's predictions against expert pathologist consensus."},
{"external_held_out_test_set": "The external, held-out test set refers to a set of data that is not included in the training process of the model but is used to evaluate the model's performance. In this context, the clinical trial images functioned as this external test set, providing an unbiased assessment of the model's ability to predict histologic grades accurately."},
{"alignment_between_model_predictions_and_pathologist_consensus": "The alignment between model predictions and pathologist consensus refers to the degree to which the predictions made by a machine learning model match the consensus grades/stages provided by a panel of expert pathologists. This alignment is crucial for validating the model's performance in medical image analysis, particularly in the context of evaluating histologic features in MASH biopsies."},
{"alignment_between_model_predictions_and_pathologist_consensus__measured_via__agreement_rates": "The predicate \"measured via\" indicates the method or metric used to quantify the alignment between model predictions and pathologist consensus. In this context, it specifies that the alignment is assessed using a specific approach, which in this case is the calculation of agreement rates."},
{"agreement_rates": "Agreement rates represent the proportion of positive agreements between the model's predictions and the consensus grades/stages provided by the pathologists. These rates serve as a quantitative measure of how well the model's predictions align with the expert consensus, providing an indication of the model's accuracy and reliability in medical image analysis.|Here is another description: Agreement rates are metrics used to evaluate the performance of a model by comparing its predictions to a consensus derived from expert evaluations. In the context of medical image analysis, these rates help assess how well a model aligns with the assessments of human experts."},
{"agreement_rates__reflect__proportion_of_positive_agreements_between_the_model_and_consensus": "Reflecting in this context means that the agreement rates serve as an indicator or measure of the alignment between the model's predictions and the consensus grades provided by expert pathologists. It quantifies the degree to which the model's outputs match the expert consensus."},
{"proportion_of_positive_agreements_between_the_model_and_consensus": "The proportion of positive agreements between the model and consensus refers to the percentage of instances where the model's predictions match the consensus grades given by the panel of expert pathologists. This metric is crucial for evaluating the accuracy and reliability of the model in a clinical setting."},
{"performance_of_each_expert_reader": "The performance of each expert reader refers to the individual assessment and grading of histologic features in MASH biopsies by expert pathologists, which is a critical component in evaluating the accuracy and reliability of their diagnostic capabilities."},
{"performance_of_each_expert_reader__evaluated_against__consensus": "Evaluated against signifies the process of comparing the individual assessments and gradings of each expert reader to a standard or benchmark, in this case, the consensus opinion of a panel of expert pathologists. This comparison helps in assessing the consistency and accuracy of each reader's performance."},
{"mloo_analysis": "MLOO analysis refers to a method used to evaluate model performance by treating the model as an additional reader in a consensus process involving expert pathologists. It is a technique to assess how well the model's predictions align with the consensus of human experts."},
{"mloo_analysis__model_considered_as__fourth_reader": "The predicate \"model considered as\" indicates that in the context of MLOO analysis, the model is treated or regarded in the same manner as an additional expert reader. This means the model's predictions are evaluated alongside those of human pathologists to determine its performance and reliability."},
{"fourth_reader": "The object \"fourth \u2018reader\u2019\" describes the role assigned to the model in the MLOO analysis. By being considered as a fourth reader, the model's predictions are compared with those of three expert pathologists to evaluate its accuracy and effectiveness in grading histologic features."},
{"consensus__determined_from__model_derived_score_and_that_of_two_pathologists": "The predicate \"determined from\" indicates the process by which the consensus was reached. It involves evaluating and comparing the model-derived scores with the assessments of the pathologists to achieve an agreement."},
{"model_derived_score_and_that_of_two_pathologists": "The object \"model-derived score and that of two pathologists\" describes the sources used to determine the consensus. It includes the scores generated by the machine learning model and the evaluations provided by two of the three expert pathologists involved in the study."},
{"performance_of_the_third_pathologist": "The performance of the third pathologist refers to the accuracy and reliability of the assessments made by one of the three expert pathologists involved in evaluating MASH biopsies. This performance is crucial for establishing a benchmark against which the model's predictions can be compared."},
{"performance_of_the_third_pathologist__evaluated_using__consensus": "Evaluated using indicates the method or standard by which the performance of the third pathologist is assessed. In this context, it means that the pathologist's evaluations are compared against a consensus to determine their accuracy and alignment with the agreed-upon grades/stages."},
{"average_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_rate": "The average individual pathologist versus consensus agreement rate refers to the mean rate at which individual pathologists' assessments align with the consensus opinion of a panel of experts. This metric is used as a benchmark to evaluate the performance of models or other pathologists in grading histologic features."},
{"average_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_rate__computed_per__histologic_feature": "The phrase \"computed per\" indicates that the average agreement rate is calculated separately for each distinct histologic feature. This allows for a detailed comparison of how well individual pathologists agree with the consensus on different aspects of histology."},
{"confidence_intervals": "Confidence intervals are statistical measures that provide a range of values within which the true value of a parameter is expected to lie with a certain level of confidence. They are crucial in assessing the reliability and precision of statistical estimates."},
{"confidence_intervals__computed_using__bootstrapping": "The phrase \"computed using\" indicates the method or process employed to derive or calculate the subject. In this context, it specifies the technique used to generate the confidence intervals."},
{"bootstrapping": "Bootstrapping is a statistical method for estimating the distribution of a statistic by sampling with replacement from the data. It is often used to calculate confidence intervals when theoretical distributions are not available or when the sample size is small."},
{"concordance": "Concordance refers to the agreement or alignment between different assessments or evaluations. In the context of the provided information, it specifically pertains to the agreement between model-derived predictions and the consensus grades/stages provided by expert pathologists for various histologic features in MASH biopsies.|Here is another description: Concordance refers to the degree of agreement or similarity between different assessments or measurements. In the context of this study, it specifically pertains to the agreement between the AI's assessments and the consensus determinations made by expert pathologists regarding clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints."},
{"concordance__assessed_for_scoring_of__steatosis_lobular_inflammation_hepatocellular_ballooning_and_fibrosis": "Assessed for scoring of indicates the process of evaluating and assigning scores to specific histologic features based on predefined criteria. In this context, it involves the comparison of model predictions with pathologist consensus scores for various liver conditions."},
{"steatosis_lobular_inflammation_hepatocellular_ballooning_and_fibrosis": "Steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis are histologic features evaluated in MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) biopsies. Steatosis refers to the accumulation of fat in liver cells, lobular inflammation indicates inflammation within the liver lobules, hepatocellular ballooning describes the swelling of liver cells, and fibrosis signifies the formation of excess fibrous connective tissue in the liver. These features are scored using the MASH CRN (Clinical Research Network) system to assess the severity of liver disease."},
{"analytic_performance_test_set": "The analytic performance test set is a dataset used in the evaluation of AI's performance in medical assessments, specifically for clinical trials. It includes data from baseline and end-of-treatment (EOT) biopsies across different treatment arms."},
{"analytic_performance_test_set__was_leveraged_to_assess__ai_s_ability_to_recapitulate_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria_and_efficacy_endpoints": "The phrase \"was leveraged to assess\" indicates that the analytic performance test set was utilized or employed as a tool to evaluate or measure something. In this context, it was used to gauge the effectiveness or accuracy of an AI system."},
{"ai_s_ability_to_recapitulate_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria_and_efficacy_endpoints": "The AI\u2019s ability to recapitulate MASH clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints refers to the AI's capability to accurately replicate or simulate the specific conditions and outcomes defined by the MASH clinical trial. This includes both the criteria used to enroll patients in the trial and the endpoints that measure the trial's success or efficacy."},
{"baseline_and_eot_biopsies": "Baseline and EOT biopsies refer to tissue samples taken at the beginning (baseline) and end of treatment (EOT) from patients participating in a clinical trial."},
{"baseline_and_eot_biopsies__were_grouped__across_treatment_arms": "The predicate \"were grouped\" indicates that the baseline and EOT biopsies were organized or categorized together for analysis, likely to compare changes over the course of treatment."},
{"across_treatment_arms": "\"Across treatment arms\" refers to the different groups or conditions under which patients were treated in the clinical trial, such as different dosages or types of treatment, including a placebo group."},
{"efficacy_endpoints": "Efficacy endpoints refer to the specific outcomes or measurements used to evaluate the effectiveness of a treatment or intervention in a clinical trial. These endpoints are critical for determining whether the treatment has achieved its intended therapeutic goals."},
{"efficacy_endpoints__were_computed_using__each_study_patient_s_paired_baseline_and_eot_biopsies": "The phrase \"were computed using\" indicates the method or process by which the efficacy endpoints were determined. In this context, it suggests that a specific computational approach was applied to derive these endpoints from the available data."},
{"each_study_patient_s_paired_baseline_and_eot_biopsies": "Each study patient\u2019s paired baseline and EOT (End of Treatment) biopsies represent the biological samples taken from patients at two different time points: before the treatment began (baseline) and after the treatment concluded (EOT). These biopsies are essential for assessing changes in the patient\u2019s condition in response to the treatment."},
{"statistical_method": "The statistical method refers to the techniques and procedures used to analyze data and draw conclusions in a scientific study. In the context of clinical trials, statistical methods are essential for evaluating the efficacy and safety of treatments by comparing outcomes between different groups, such as those receiving a treatment versus a placebo."},
{"statistical_method__used_to_compare_treatment_with_placebo__cochran_mantel_haenszel_test": "The predicate \"used to compare treatment with placebo\" describes the specific application of a statistical method in a clinical trial setting. This involves analyzing data to determine if there is a significant difference in outcomes between patients who received the treatment and those who received a placebo, which helps in assessing the treatment's effectiveness."},
{"cochran_mantel_haenszel_test": "The Cochran\u2013Mantel\u2013Haenszel test is a statistical method used to compare categorical data across different strata or groups. It is particularly useful in clinical trials for analyzing the association between treatment and outcome while controlling for confounding variables, such as diabetes status and cirrhosis at baseline in this context."},
{"p_values": "P values are statistical measures used to determine the significance of results in hypothesis testing, indicating the probability that the observed data would occur under the null hypothesis."},
{"p_values__were_based_on__response_stratified_by_diabetes_status_and_cirrhosis_at_baseline": "The phrase \"were based on\" indicates that the P values were calculated or derived from a specific set of conditions or data, in this case, the response stratified by diabetes status and cirrhosis at baseline."},
{"response_stratified_by_diabetes_status_and_cirrhosis_at_baseline": "The response stratified by diabetes status and cirrhosis at baseline refers to the categorization of patient responses in a clinical trial according to whether they have diabetes and the presence of cirrhosis at the start of the trial. This stratification is used to analyze the data more accurately by accounting for these specific health conditions."},
{"concordance__was_assessed_with__\u03ba_statistics": "\"Was assessed with\" indicates the method or metric used to evaluate the level of agreement or similarity. In this study, it describes how the concordance between the AI's assessments and the expert pathologists' consensus determinations was quantitatively evaluated."},
{"accuracy": "Accuracy in this context refers to the measure of how well the AI system can correctly identify and evaluate clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints, as compared to the consensus determinations made by expert pathologists."},
{"accuracy__was_evaluated_by_computing__f1_scores": "The phrase \"was evaluated by computing\" indicates the method used to assess the accuracy of the AI system. It suggests that a specific computational process was employed to quantify the AI's performance in terms of correctly identifying and evaluating clinical trial criteria and endpoints."},
{"f1_scores": "F1 scores are a statistical measure used to evaluate the accuracy of a test by considering both precision (the proportion of true positive results among all positive results) and recall (the proportion of true positive results among all actual positives). In this context, F1 scores were computed to evaluate the accuracy of the AI system in assessing clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints."},
{"consensus_determination": "The consensus determination refers to the collective decision made by a panel of three expert pathologists regarding clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints. This determination is used as a benchmark in evaluating other assessments."},
{"consensus_determination__served_as_a_reference_for_evaluating__ai_concordance_and_accuracy": "The phrase \"served as a reference for evaluating\" indicates that the consensus determination was utilized as a standard or baseline against which the performance of AI in terms of concordance and accuracy was measured."},
{"ai_concordance_and_accuracy": "AI concordance and accuracy refer to the degree to which the AI\u2019s assessments align with the consensus determinations made by the expert pathologists, and the overall correctness of the AI\u2019s evaluations, respectively. These metrics are crucial for validating the AI\u2019s performance in clinical assessments."},
{"ai": "AI in this context refers to an artificial intelligence system designed to assess clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints in medical research, specifically for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) clinical trials."},
{"ai__was_treated_as__an_independent_fourth_reader": "The phrase \"was treated as\" indicates that the AI system was considered or evaluated in a specific manner within the study. In this case, it was assessed alongside human pathologists to determine its performance and reliability in evaluating clinical trial data."},
{"an_independent_fourth_reader": "An \"independent, fourth \u2018reader\u2019\" refers to the role assigned to the AI system within the study. It was considered as an additional, unbiased evaluator, similar to how human pathologists would review and assess clinical trial data, thereby providing a comparative measure of its performance against human experts."},
{"consensus_determinations": "Consensus determinations refer to the collective decisions made by a group of expert pathologists in evaluating clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints. These determinations serve as a reference standard for assessing the performance of individual pathologists and AI systems."},
{"consensus_determinations__were_composed_of__the_aim_and_two_pathologists_for_evaluating_the_third_pathologist_not_included_in_the_consensus": "The phrase \"were composed of\" indicates the makeup or constituents of the consensus determinations. It specifies that the consensus was formed by including certain elements\u2014in this case, the AIM and two pathologists\u2014to evaluate a third pathologist."},
{"the_aim_and_two_pathologists_for_evaluating_the_third_pathologist_not_included_in_the_consensus": "The AIM and two pathologists for evaluating the third pathologist not included in the consensus represent the specific components that made up the consensus determinations. This configuration was used to assess the performance of the third pathologist, employing a \"leave-one-out\" approach."},
{"mloo_approach": "The MLOO (Modified Leave-One-Out) approach is a method used in the evaluation process where one element is systematically excluded from the consensus to assess the performance of the remaining elements."},
{"mloo_approach__was_followed_to_evaluate__the_performance_of_each_pathologist_against_a_consensus_determination": "The phrase \"was followed to evaluate\" indicates that the MLOO approach was systematically applied or adhered to in order to assess or measure something, in this case, the performance of pathologists."},
{"the_performance_of_each_pathologist_against_a_consensus_determination": "The performance of each pathologist against a consensus determination refers to how well individual pathologists' assessments align with a collective decision made by a group of expert pathologists, serving as a benchmark for evaluation."},
{"continuous_scoring_system__demonstrates__interpretability": "The predicate \"demonstrates\" indicates that the continuous scoring system is being shown to have a certain quality or capability. In this context, it is being used to show that the system can provide interpretable results, meaning the scores it generates can be understood and correlated with human evaluations."},
{"interpretability": "Interpretability in this context refers to the ability of the continuous scoring system's outputs to be understood and correlated with human evaluations. It means that the system's scores should reflect the same directional bias as the mean pathologist scores, allowing for a meaningful comparison and validation of the system's performance."},
{"mash_crn_continuous_scores": "The MASH CRN continuous scores are numerical values generated to quantify the severity of histologic features in liver tissue images, specifically within the context of a clinical trial for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"mash_crn_continuous_scores__were_generated_in__wsis_from_a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The phrase \"were generated in\" indicates the process by which the MASH CRN continuous scores were produced. It suggests that these scores were calculated or derived from specific data or images within a defined context or dataset."},
{"wsis_from_a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "WSIs from a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial refer to Whole Slide Images that were part of a clinical study evaluating treatments for MASH. These images were used as the basis for generating the MASH CRN continuous scores, providing a visual and analytical resource for assessing liver histology."},
{"continuous_scores__were_compared_with__mean_pathologist_scores_from_three_study_central_readers": "The phrase \"were compared with\" indicates an analytical process where two sets of data are evaluated against each other to determine similarities, differences, or correlations. In this context, it signifies the comparison of continuous scores with mean pathologist scores to assess interpretability and directional bias."},
{"mean_pathologist_scores_from_three_study_central_readers": "Mean pathologist scores from three study central readers represent the average evaluations provided by a panel of expert pathologists who reviewed and scored histologic features in medical images. These scores serve as a benchmark to validate the accuracy and reliability of the AI-derived continuous scoring system."},
{"comparison_method": "The comparison method refers to the process of evaluating the continuous scores generated by the MASH CRN system against the mean pathologist scores from a panel of three study central readers."},
{"comparison_method__used__kendall_rank_correlation": "The predicate 'used' indicates that the Kendall rank correlation was employed as the statistical method to perform the comparison between the continuous scores and the mean pathologist scores."},
{"kendall_rank_correlation": "The Kendall rank correlation is a non-parametric statistical method used to measure the correspondence between two ranked variables. In this context, it was used to compare the continuous scores from the MASH CRN system with the mean pathologist scores, assessing whether the AI-derived scores reflected the same directional bias as the pathologists."},
{"goal_of_measuring_mean_pathologist_score": "The goal of measuring the mean pathologist score refers to the objective of assessing the average rating given by a panel of pathologists for specific histologic features in medical images. This measurement is crucial for establishing a baseline for comparison with AI-derived continuous scores."},
{"goal_of_measuring_mean_pathologist_score__was_to__capture_the_directional_bias_of_the_panel_per_feature": "The predicate \"was to\" indicates the purpose or intention behind the action of measuring the mean pathologist score. In this context, it signifies that the measurement was undertaken with a specific aim in mind, which is to achieve a particular outcome or gain certain insights."},
{"capture_the_directional_bias_of_the_panel_per_feature": "Capturing the directional bias of the panel per feature involves identifying and quantifying the consistent tendencies or preferences of the pathologists when evaluating specific histologic features. This directional bias is important for understanding how the panel's assessments align with the AI-derived continuous scores and for ensuring the interpretability and reliability of the scoring system."},
{"ai_derived_continuous_score": "The AI-derived continuous score is a numerical value generated by an artificial intelligence system to quantify specific histologic features in medical images. It is designed to provide a consistent and objective measure that can be compared across different images and features."},
{"ai_derived_continuous_score__reflected__the_same_directional_bias": "The predicate \"reflected\" indicates that the AI-derived continuous score accurately represents or mirrors a particular characteristic or trend. In this context, it signifies that the score aligns with the directional bias observed by pathologists."},
{"the_same_directional_bias": "The same directional bias refers to the consistent trend or inclination observed by a panel of pathologists when evaluating histologic features in medical images. This bias represents the general agreement or consensus among the pathologists regarding the severity or presence of specific features."},
{"reporting_summary": "The Reporting summary is a document or section within a research article that aims to give readers a concise overview of the research methodology and design employed in the study."},
{"reporting_summary__provides__further_information_on_research_design": "The predicate \"provides\" indicates that the Reporting summary serves as a source or means through which additional details or insights about the research design can be accessed or understood by the reader."},
{"further_information_on_research_design": "Further information on research design refers to detailed explanations, methodologies, and specific approaches used in conducting the research, which are elaborated beyond the main text of the article, typically in a supplementary document like the Nature Portfolio Reporting Summary."},
{"further_information": "Further information refers to additional details or data that supplement the primary content of the article, providing deeper insights into the research design and methodology."},
{"further_information__is_available_in__the_nature_portfolio_reporting_summary": "The phrase 'is available in' indicates that the subject, further information, can be found or accessed within a specified location or document, in this case, the Nature Portfolio Reporting Summary."},
{"the_nature_portfolio_reporting_summary": "The Nature Portfolio Reporting Summary is a document linked to the article that contains detailed information about the research design, methods, and other relevant data, serving as a comprehensive resource for readers seeking more in-depth knowledge."},
{"nature_portfolio_reporting_summary": "The Nature Portfolio Reporting Summary is a document that provides further information on the research design of a study. It is part of the Nature Portfolio's efforts to enhance transparency and reproducibility in scientific research."},
{"nature_portfolio_reporting_summary__is_linked_to__this_article": "The phrase \"is linked to\" indicates a direct connection or association between the Nature Portfolio Reporting Summary and the article in question. This link allows readers to access additional details about the research methodology and design."},
{"histopathology_data": "The histopathology data refers to the collection of microscopic images and related information gathered for a specific study, which is crucial for analyzing tissue samples and understanding disease processes at a cellular level.|Here is another description: Histopathology data refers to the detailed microscopic examination of tissue samples, which is crucial for diagnosing diseases and understanding their progression. In this context, it is specifically the data collected for a particular study and maintained by PathAI."},
{"histopathology_data__maintained_by__pathai": "The phrase \"maintained by\" indicates the entity responsible for the upkeep, security, and management of the histopathology data, ensuring its integrity, confidentiality, and accessibility according to specified conditions."},
{"histopathology_data__purpose__preserve_patient_confidentiality_and_proprietary_image_analysis": "The purpose of maintaining histopathology data by PathAI is to ensure that patient confidentiality is preserved and that the proprietary image analysis techniques developed by the company remain protected. This involves controlling access to the data and ensuring that it is used appropriately."},
{"preserve_patient_confidentiality_and_proprietary_image_analysis": "Preserving patient confidentiality involves protecting sensitive medical information from unauthorized access or disclosure. Proprietary image analysis refers to the unique methods and algorithms developed by PathAI for analyzing histopathology images, which are considered valuable intellectual property."},
