{"aim_mash_performance": "The AIM-MASH performance refers to the evaluation metrics and results of the AIM-MASH system, which is designed to grade and stage MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) based on histological images.|Here is another description: The AIM-MASH performance refers to the effectiveness and accuracy of the AIM-MASH model in grading and staging histologic features related to MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). It is evaluated based on its ability to provide consistent and reproducible assessments that align with expert pathologists' consensus."},
{"aim_mash_performance__tested_on__external_held_out_dataset": "The predicate \"tested on\" indicates that the AIM-MASH performance was evaluated or assessed using a specific dataset to determine its effectiveness and reliability in grading and staging MASH."},
{"external_held_out_dataset": "The external, held-out dataset is a collection of 640 H&E (Hematoxylin and Eosin) and 634 trichrome Whole Slide Images (WSIs) from the EMMINENCE phase 2b MASH clinical trial. This dataset was used to test the AIM-MASH performance independently from the data it was trained on, ensuring an unbiased evaluation.|Here is another description: The external, held-out dataset refers to a set of data that was not used during the training phase of a model and is used to evaluate the model's performance. In this context, it is a collection of images used to test the AIM-MASH performance."},
{"external_held_out_dataset__comprises__640_h_e_and_634_trichrome_wsis": "The predicate \"comprises\" indicates that the external, held-out dataset is made up of or includes specific elements. In this case, it specifies the types and number of images that make up the dataset."},
{"640_h_e_and_634_trichrome_wsis": "The dataset comprises 640 Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) and 634 trichrome Whole Slide Images (WSIs), which are used for detailed histological analysis in medical research. H&E staining highlights general tissue structures, while trichrome staining emphasizes connective tissues. These images are crucial for training and validating machine learning models to accurately segment and grade/stage histologic features in pathology."},
{"640_h_e_and_634_trichrome_wsis__from__emminence_a_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"from\" indicates the origin or source of the dataset. It specifies that the dataset is derived from a specific clinical trial, providing context about where the data was collected and under what conditions."},
{"emminence_a_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The object \"EMMINENCE, a phase 2b MASH clinical trial\" describes the source of the dataset. EMMINENCE is a clinical trial focused on MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis), indicating that the WSIs were collected as part of this trial to evaluate the performance of AIM-MASH in grading and staging liver disease."},
{"agreement_rates_for_aim_mash_grades_stages": "The agreement rates for AIM-MASH grades/stages refer to the level of concordance between the automated AIM-MASH system's assessments of MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) severity and the evaluations made by expert pathologists. These rates are crucial for validating the reliability and accuracy of the AIM-MASH system in clinical settings.|Here is another description: The agreement rates for AIM-MASH grades/stages refer to the level of concordance between the assessments made by the AIM-MASH system and a consensus of three expert pathologists on MASH clinical trial data.|Here is another description: The agreement rates for AIM-MASH grades/stages refer to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH system's assessments and a consensus of three expert pathologists on MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) grading and staging."},
{"agreement_rates_for_aim_mash_grades_stages__versus__consensus_of_three_expert_pathologists": "The predicate \"versus\" indicates a comparison between two entities. In this context, it signifies the evaluation of how the agreement rates of the AIM-MASH system compare to the consensus reached by three expert pathologists. This comparison helps determine the effectiveness and reliability of the AIM-MASH system relative to human expert assessments."},
{"consensus_of_three_expert_pathologists": "The consensus of three expert pathologists represents the collective judgment and agreement reached by a group of highly skilled and experienced pathologists regarding the grading and staging of MASH. This consensus serves as a benchmark for evaluating the performance of the AIM-MASH system, providing a standard against which the system's accuracy and reliability can be measured."},
{"agreement_rates_for_aim_mash_grades_stages__were_superior_to__mean_agreement_between_any_individual_pathologist_and_a_panel_comprising_the_other_two_pathologists_and_the_model": "The phrase \"were superior to\" indicates that the agreement rates for AIM-MASH grades/stages exceeded another measure of agreement in the context of evaluating MASH clinical trial data."},
{"mean_agreement_between_any_individual_pathologist_and_a_panel_comprising_the_other_two_pathologists_and_the_model": "The mean agreement between any individual pathologist and a panel comprising the other two pathologists and the model represents a benchmark for comparison, highlighting the performance of individual pathologists versus a collective assessment in the context of MASH diagnosis."},
{"agreement_rates_for_aim_mash_grades_stages__were_superior_to__any_mean_pairwise_pathologist_agreement": "The predicate \"were superior to\" indicates that the agreement rates achieved by the AIM-MASH system outperformed another measure of agreement in the context of MASH grading and staging."},
{"any_mean_pairwise_pathologist_agreement": "Any mean pairwise pathologist agreement refers to the average level of agreement between any two individual pathologists when assessing MASH grades and stages, serving as a benchmark for comparison with the AIM-MASH system's performance.|Here is another description: Any mean pairwise pathologist agreement represents the average level of concordance between individual pathologists' assessments when compared in pairs for the same histologic features."},
{"\u03ba_statistics": "_\u03ba_ statistics refer to a measure of agreement or reliability used in statistical analysis, particularly in the context of comparing ratings or classifications made by different observers or methods.|Here is another description: _\u03ba_ statistics refer to a measure of agreement or reliability, often used in medical research to assess the consistency of ratings or diagnoses among different observers or methods.|Here is another description: _\u03ba_ statistics refer to a measure of agreement or reliability used in statistical analysis, particularly in the context of comparing different observers or methods in medical diagnostics.|Here is another description: \u03ba statistics, or Kappa statistics, are a measure used to assess the level of agreement between two raters, taking into account the possibility of agreement occurring by chance. In this context, \u03ba statistics were employed to evaluate the concordance between the AI's assessments and those of expert pathologists."},
{"\u03ba_statistics__from__kleiner_and_colleagues_13": "The predicate \"from\" indicates the source or origin of the _\u03ba_ statistics, specifying that these statistics are derived from or based on the work of Kleiner and colleagues as documented in reference [13]."},
{"kleiner_and_colleagues_13": "Kleiner and colleagues [13] represent a group of researchers or authors who have conducted a study or series of studies, the results of which are referenced in the context. Their work, specifically the _\u03ba_ statistics, is used as a benchmark or comparison in the current study."},
{"\u03ba_statistics__are_reported_in__the_rightmost_column": "The phrase \"are reported in\" indicates that the _\u03ba_ statistics have been documented or presented within a specific section or location of a report or study."},
{"the_rightmost_column": "The rightmost column in this context refers to the final column in a table or dataset where additional information, such as _\u03ba_ statistics for comparison, is presented alongside other data."},
{"\u03ba_statistics__as_a_comparison_with__recently_published_results_from_the_pathology_committee_of_the_mash_crn": "The phrase \"as a comparison with\" indicates that the _\u03ba_ statistics are being used to benchmark or evaluate the performance or reliability of AIM-MASH against another set of results."},
{"recently_published_results_from_the_pathology_committee_of_the_mash_crn": "The recently published results from the pathology committee of the MASH CRN represent a set of data or findings that have been released by the pathology committee associated with the MASH Clinical Research Network, serving as a reference point for comparison."},
{"gnn": "GNN refers to a Graph Neural Network, which is a type of machine learning model designed to operate on graph-structured data. In this context, it is specifically trained to analyze and interpret medical images, particularly for assessing liver conditions.|Here is another description: GNN refers to a Graph Neural Network, which is a type of machine learning model designed to operate directly on graph structures. In this context, it is specifically trained to analyze and interpret medical images.|Here is another description: GNN refers to a Graph Neural Network, a type of machine learning model designed to operate directly on graph structures. In this context, it is specifically trained to analyze and interpret medical images.|Here is another description: GNN refers to a Graph Neural Network, which is a type of machine learning model designed to process data represented in a graph structure. In this context, the GNN is specifically trained to analyze and interpret medical images.|Here is another description: GNN refers to a Graph Neural Network, which is a type of neural network designed to work with graph-structured data. In this context, the GNN is used for ordinal scoring of fibrosis in liver histology images, mapping ordinal grades to continuous scores.|Here is another description: GNN is an abbreviation used in the context of machine learning and artificial intelligence, specifically referring to a type of neural network designed to work with graph-structured data."},
{"gnn__predicts__mash_crn_steatosis_grade": "The predicate 'predicts' indicates the action performed by the GNN, which involves using its trained algorithms to forecast or estimate specific medical outcomes or grades based on the input data it processes."},
{"mash_crn_steatosis_grade": "MASH CRN steatosis grade is a medical grading system used to quantify the severity of steatosis, a condition characterized by the accumulation of fat in liver cells. The GNN is trained to predict these grades from histological images, providing a valuable tool for diagnosing and monitoring liver conditions."},
{"gnn__predicts__lobular_inflammation_grade": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates that the GNN model is used to forecast or estimate specific medical conditions or grades based on the input data, which in this case are Whole Slide Images (WSIs) stained with certain dyes."},
{"lobular_inflammation_grade": "Lobular inflammation grade is a medical assessment that quantifies the degree of inflammation present in the lobules of the liver. This grade is part of the MASH CRN scoring system used to evaluate liver conditions from histological images."},
{"gnn__predicts__hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates the action performed by the GNN, which involves estimating or forecasting specific medical grades or stages based on the input data, such as histological images."},
{"hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "Hepatocellular ballooning grade is a medical assessment criterion used to evaluate the severity of liver cell swelling in conditions like steatohepatitis. It is one of the parameters that the GNN is trained to predict from histological images."},
{"gnn__predicts__fibrosis_stage": "The predicate \"predicts\" indicates the action performed by the GNN. It signifies that the GNN is capable of forecasting or estimating a specific outcome based on the input data it processes, which in this case are medical images stained with certain dyes."},
{"fibrosis_stage": "\"Fibrosis stage\" is the outcome or the target variable that the GNN is trained to predict. Fibrosis refers to the thickening and scarring of connective tissue, usually as a result of injury. In medical imaging, determining the stage of fibrosis is crucial for diagnosing and monitoring the progression of certain diseases."},
{"h_e_stained_wsis": "H&E-stained WSIs refer to whole slide images that have been stained using the Hematoxylin and Eosin method, a common staining technique in histopathology that highlights cellular and tissue structures for detailed examination."},
{"h_e_stained_wsis__used_for__predicting_mash_crn_steatosis_grade_lobular_inflammation_grade_and_hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "The predicate \"used for\" indicates the purpose or application of the subject, in this case, the H&E-stained WSIs, which are utilized in a specific process or function, such as predicting various grades of liver conditions."},
{"predicting_mash_crn_steatosis_grade_lobular_inflammation_grade_and_hepatocellular_ballooning_grade": "Predicting MASH CRN steatosis grade, lobular inflammation grade, and hepatocellular ballooning grade involves using the H&E-stained WSIs to estimate the severity levels of these specific liver conditions, which are part of the MASH CRN (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis Clinical Research Network) grading system."},
{"mt_stained_wsis": "MT-stained WSIs refer to whole slide images that have been stained using a Masson's Trichrome (MT) technique, which is commonly used to highlight fibrous tissue in histological samples."},
{"mt_stained_wsis__used_for__predicting_fibrosis_stage": "The phrase \"used for\" indicates the purpose or application of the subject in a specific context, in this case, the utilization of MT-stained WSIs."},
{"predicting_fibrosis_stage": "Predicting fibrosis stage involves assessing the extent of fibrous tissue accumulation in liver tissue, which is a critical factor in diagnosing and staging liver diseases such as MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"gnn_predicted_scores": "GNN-predicted scores refer to the continuous numerical values generated by a Graph Neural Network (GNN) for predicting various grades and stages of liver conditions such as steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis."},
{"gnn_predicted_scores__mapped_to__bins_equivalent_to_one_grade_or_stage": "The predicate \"mapped to\" describes the process of converting continuous GNN-predicted scores into discrete bins. This mapping allows for the interpretation of these scores in terms of specific grades or stages, making the results more clinically relevant and easier to understand."},
{"bins_equivalent_to_one_grade_or_stage": "Bins equivalent to one grade or stage represent the discrete categories into which continuous GNN-predicted scores are converted. Each bin corresponds to a specific grade (0-3 for steatosis, lobular inflammation, and hepatocellular ballooning) or stage (for fibrosis) as defined by the MASH CRN criteria."},
{"aim_mash_algorithm_scoring": "The AIM-MASH algorithm scoring is a method used for evaluating histologic features in medical images, specifically designed to provide consistent and accurate assessments."},
{"aim_mash_algorithm_scoring__was__perfectly_repeatable": "The predicate \"was\" indicates a state of being or condition that the subject, AIM-MASH algorithm scoring, exhibited during the initial model performance testing."},
{"perfectly_repeatable": "The object \"perfectly repeatable\" describes the characteristic of the AIM-MASH algorithm scoring, indicating that it consistently produced the same results when tested multiple times under the same conditions."},
{"model_versus_model_agreement_rate": "The model versus model agreement rate refers to the percentage of agreement between multiple independent runs or instances of the AIM-MASH algorithm when scoring the same whole slide images (WSIs) for histologic features."},
{"model_versus_model_agreement_rate__was__100": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the state or result of the model versus model agreement rate during the initial performance testing of the AIM-MASH algorithm."},
{"100": "The object \"100%\" represents the perfect agreement rate achieved by the AIM-MASH algorithm when comparing ten independent reads per WSI for each of the four cardinal histologic features, indicating no variability in the model's scoring."},
{"intra_pathologist_agreement_using_conventional_approaches": "The intra-pathologist agreement using conventional approaches refers to the consistency in diagnoses or assessments made by a single pathologist when evaluating the same histologic features multiple times using traditional methods."},
{"intra_pathologist_agreement_using_conventional_approaches__ranged_from__37_to_74": "The phrase \"ranged from\" indicates the variability or spectrum of values observed in the intra-pathologist agreement rates when using conventional approaches for histologic feature assessments."},
{"37_to_74": "The object \"37% to 74%\" represents the minimum and maximum percentages of agreement observed among repeated reads by pathologists using conventional methods, highlighting the variability in their assessments across different histologic features."},
{"aim_mash_performance_accuracy": "The AIM-MASH performance accuracy refers to the effectiveness and precision of the AIM-MASH algorithm in scoring and predicting histologic features compared to a pathologist-based consensus."},
{"aim_mash_performance_accuracy__was_assessed_using__a_mixed_leave_one_out_mloo_approach": "The assessment of AIM-MASH performance accuracy using a mixed leave-one-out (MLOO) approach indicates the method employed to evaluate how well the AIM-MASH algorithm's predictions align with established histologic feature evaluations by pathologists."},
{"a_mixed_leave_one_out_mloo_approach": "A mixed leave-one-out (MLOO) approach is a method used to evaluate model performance by iteratively leaving out one sample at a time, training the model on the remaining data, and testing it on the left-out sample. This process ensures a robust assessment of the model's generalizability and accuracy."},
{"model_versus_consensus_agreement_rates": "The subject refers to the agreement rates between the model and a consensus, specifically in the context of assessing histologic features using the AIM-MASH algorithm. This involves comparing the model's predictions with a pathologist-based consensus for features like steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and fibrosis."},
{"model_versus_consensus_agreement_rates__fell_within_the_range_of__previously_reported_rates_of_inter_pathologist_agreement": "The predicate indicates that the model versus consensus agreement rates are being evaluated in terms of their position within a specific range. This evaluation is crucial for understanding how the model's performance compares to established benchmarks in the field."},
{"previously_reported_rates_of_inter_pathologist_agreement": "The object describes the previously reported rates of inter-pathologist agreement, which serve as a benchmark for evaluating the model's performance. These rates reflect the variability and consistency in pathologists' assessments of histologic features, providing a context for interpreting the model's agreement rates."},
{"agreement_rate_for_steatosis": "The agreement rate for steatosis refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of steatosis in liver tissue images."},
{"agreement_rate_for_steatosis__was__\u03ba_0_74_95_ci_0_71_0_77": "The predicate \"was\" indicates that the agreement rate for steatosis has been measured and recorded as a specific value, reflecting the performance of the AIM-MASH algorithm in comparison to pathologist consensus."},
{"\u03ba_0_74_95_ci_0_71_0_77": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.74, 95% CI 0.71\u20130.77\" represents the kappa statistic value of 0.74 with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.71 to 0.77, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for steatosis."},
{"agreement_rate_for_ballooning": "The agreement rate for ballooning refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of ballooning in liver tissue."},
{"agreement_rate_for_ballooning__was__\u03ba_0_70_95_ci_0_66_0_73": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the measured or observed value of the agreement rate for ballooning, reflecting the performance of the AIM-MASH algorithm in comparison to the consensus of pathologists."},
{"\u03ba_0_70_95_ci_0_66_0_73": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.70, 95% CI 0.66\u20130.73\" represents the kappa statistic value and its 95% confidence interval, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for the ballooning histologic feature."},
{"agreement_rate_for_lobular_inflammation": "The agreement rate for lobular inflammation refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus on the histologic feature of lobular inflammation in liver tissue samples."},
{"agreement_rate_for_lobular_inflammation__was__\u03ba_0_67_95_ci_0_64_0_71": "The predicate \"was\" indicates that the agreement rate for lobular inflammation has been measured and recorded as a specific value, reflecting the performance of the AIM-MASH algorithm in comparison to pathologist consensus."},
{"\u03ba_0_67_95_ci_0_64_0_71": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.67, 95% CI 0.64\u20130.71\" represents the kappa statistic value and its 95% confidence interval, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for lobular inflammation, indicating a substantial level of agreement."},
{"agreement_rate_for_fibrosis": "The agreement rate for fibrosis refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring and a pathologist-based consensus for the histologic feature of fibrosis in liver tissue samples."},
{"agreement_rate_for_fibrosis__was__\u03ba_0_62_95_ci_0_58_0_65": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the measured or observed value of the agreement rate for fibrosis in the context of the AIM-MASH algorithm's performance testing."},
{"\u03ba_0_62_95_ci_0_58_0_65": "The object \"\u03ba = 0.62, 95% CI 0.58\u20130.65\" represents the kappa statistic value and its 95% confidence interval, which quantifies the agreement rate between the AIM-MASH algorithm and the pathologist-based consensus for fibrosis."},
{"agreement_between_the_model_and_consensus": "The agreement between the model and consensus refers to the level of concordance observed when comparing the scoring predictions of the AIM-MASH algorithm with a pathologist-based consensus for histologic features.|Here is another description: The agreement between the model and consensus refers to the level of concordance between the AIM-MASH algorithm's scoring predictions and the pathologist-based consensus for the four cardinal histologic features in MASH diagnosis."},
{"agreement_between_the_model_and_consensus__was_greater_than__agreement_for_any_individual_pathologist_against_the_other_three_reads": "The phrase \"was greater than\" indicates a comparative relationship where the level of agreement between the model and the consensus exceeded another specified measure of agreement."},
{"agreement_for_any_individual_pathologist_against_the_other_three_reads": "The agreement for any individual pathologist against the other three reads represents the level of concordance when comparing the scoring of one pathologist with the combined scoring of the other three pathologists for the same histologic features."},
{"agreement_between_the_model_and_consensus__was_greater_than__any_mean_pairwise_pathologist_agreement": "The phrase \"was greater than\" indicates a comparative relationship where the agreement level between the model and the consensus exceeded another specified measure of agreement."},
{"fda__has_proposed__criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment": "The predicate \"has proposed\" indicates that the FDA has put forward or suggested specific criteria. This action signifies the FDA's intention to establish standards or requirements that must be met for MASH clinical trial enrollment, reflecting their regulatory oversight and commitment to ensuring the safety and efficacy of clinical trials."},
{"criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment": "The criteria for MASH clinical trial enrollment refer to the specific standards or requirements that patients must meet to be eligible to participate in a clinical trial for MASH. These criteria are essential for ensuring that the trial participants are appropriate for the study and that the trial can yield meaningful and reliable results. The FDA's proposed criteria aim to standardize and improve the enrollment process for MASH clinical trials."},
{"aim_mash__was_deployed_on__wsis_from_two_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trials": "The phrase \"was deployed on\" indicates that AIM-MASH was applied or utilized in a specific context, in this case, on whole slide images (WSIs) from clinical trials to assess their histologic criteria and generate relevant scores."},
{"wsis_from_two_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trials": "WSIs from two completed phase 2b MASH clinical trials refer to the whole slide images that were used as the dataset on which AIM-MASH was deployed. These images were part of clinical trials aimed at evaluating patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis."},
{"aim_mash__generated_scores_based_on__histologic_criteria": "The predicate \"generated scores based on\" indicates that AIM-MASH produces evaluations or ratings derived from specific standards or benchmarks. In this context, it refers to the process by which AIM-MASH creates scores using established histologic criteria."},
{"aim_mash__identified__patients_eligible_for_enrollment": "The predicate \"identified\" in this context refers to the action performed by AIM-MASH in recognizing and selecting patients who meet the specific criteria for enrollment in MASH clinical trials. This involves assessing histologic features and comparing them against established criteria."},
{"patients_eligible_for_enrollment": "Patients eligible for enrollment are those individuals who, after being evaluated by AIM-MASH, are found to meet the necessary histologic criteria for participation in MASH clinical trials. These criteria include specific MASH CRN scores and fibrosis levels as determined by the model\u2019s predictions."},
{"ai_derived_predictions": "AI-derived predictions refer to the scores and assessments generated by an artificial intelligence model, specifically AIM-MASH, based on histologic criteria from whole slide images (WSIs) of patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis.|Here is another description: AI-derived predictions refer to the assessments and gradings made by artificial intelligence models, specifically in the context of analyzing histologic features in medical images for conditions like MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"ai_derived_predictions__were_compared_with__each_trial_s_central_pathologist_cp_scores": "The phrase \"were compared with\" indicates an evaluation process where the AI-derived predictions are assessed against a standard or benchmark. In this context, it means that the AI model's outputs are being measured for their accuracy and reliability by comparing them to established scores."},
{"each_trial_s_central_pathologist_cp_scores": "Each trial\u2019s central pathologist (CP) scores represent the assessments and evaluations made by a designated expert pathologist who reviews and scores the histologic criteria for each cohort in the clinical trials. These scores serve as the benchmark against which the AI-derived predictions are compared."},
{"model_derived_histologic_predictions": "Model-derived histologic predictions refer to the scores and assessments generated by an AI model, specifically AIM-MASH, based on the analysis of whole slide images (WSIs) from patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. These predictions are used to evaluate histologic criteria and determine patient eligibility for clinical trials.|Here is another description: Model-derived histologic predictions refer to the assessments generated by an AI model, specifically AIM-MASH, which evaluates histologic criteria from whole slide images (WSIs) to determine patient eligibility for clinical trials."},
{"model_derived_histologic_predictions__were_used_to_calculate__mash_crn_scores": "The phrase \"were used to calculate\" indicates that the model-derived histologic predictions served as the basis for computing specific scores. In this context, it means that the AI-generated predictions were applied to determine the MASH CRN scores, which are critical for assessing patient eligibility in clinical trials."},
{"mash_crn_scores": "MASH CRN scores are quantitative assessments used in the evaluation of patients with nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (MASH). These scores are derived from histologic criteria and are essential for determining whether a patient meets the enrollment criteria for MASH clinical trials. Specifically, they help distinguish between patients with a MASH Activity Score (MAS) of \u2265 4 and those with a MAS < 4."},
{"model_derived_histologic_predictions__distinguished__mas_4_from_mas_4": "The predicate \"distinguished\" indicates that the model-derived histologic predictions were able to differentiate between two categories based on specific histologic criteria, showcasing the model's capability in making relevant clinical distinctions."},
{"mas_4_from_mas_4": "MAS \u2265 4 from MAS < 4 refers to the Model for Assessing Steatohepatitis (MAS) scores, where MAS \u2265 4 indicates a higher severity of steatohepatitis compared to MAS < 4, which indicates a lower severity. This distinction is critical for determining patient eligibility for clinical trials."},
{"aim_mash_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement": "The AIM-MASH versus consensus percentage agreement refers to the level of agreement between the AI-based model AIM-MASH and the consensus scores provided by pathologists in evaluating histologic criteria for MASH clinical trial enrollment."},
{"aim_mash_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement__was__0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_85": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the measured level of agreement between AIM-MASH and the consensus scores, reflecting how closely the AI model's predictions aligned with the pathologists' assessments."},
{"0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_85": "The object \"0.82 (95% CI 0.79\u20130.85)\" represents the percentage agreement between AIM-MASH and the consensus scores, with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.79 to 0.85, indicating a high level of consistency between the AI model and the pathologists."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement": "The average pathologist versus consensus percentage agreement refers to the level of agreement between the average assessments made by individual pathologists and the consensus scores provided by a group of pathologists with expertise in MASH histology."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_percentage_agreement__was__0_81_95_ci_0_78_0_83": "The predicate \"was\" indicates that the average pathologist versus consensus percentage agreement had a specific value at a given point in time, reflecting the degree of concordance between individual pathologists' assessments and the consensus scores."},
{"0_81_95_ci_0_78_0_83": "The object \"0.81 (95% CI 0.78\u20130.83)\" represents the percentage agreement between the average assessments of individual pathologists and the consensus scores, with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.78 to 0.83, indicating a high level of agreement."},
{"similar_result": "The subject \"Similar result\" refers to the comparable outcome observed in the context of AI-derived predictions for histologic criteria in MASH clinical trials, specifically in relation to the assessment of fibrosis."},
{"similar_result__was_observed_for__fibrosis": "The predicate \"was observed for\" indicates that the subject, a similar result, was noted or found in the context of evaluating fibrosis in the clinical trials. It suggests a comparison or observation made during the assessment process."},
{"fibrosis_stages_1_3_f1_f3_versus_f4": "Fibrosis stages 1\u20133 (F1\u2013F3) versus F4 refers to a comparison between the earlier stages of liver fibrosis (F1 to F3) and the more advanced stage (F4), which is often associated with cirrhosis."},
{"fibrosis_stages_1_3_f1_f3_versus_f4__model_versus_consensus_agreement__0_97_95_ci_0_95_0_98": "The model versus consensus agreement indicates the level of agreement between a predictive model's assessment of liver fibrosis stages and the consensus agreement among pathologists."},
{"0_97_95_ci_0_95_0_98": "0.97 (95% CI 0.95\u20130.98) represents a high level of agreement, with a confidence interval that suggests a very narrow range of possible values, indicating strong reliability in the model's predictions compared to the consensus agreement."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement": "The average pathologist versus consensus agreement refers to the level of agreement between the average performance of pathologists and a consensus agreement in assessing fibrosis stages."},
{"average_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement__agreement__0_96_95_ci_0_95_0_97": "The predicate \"agreement\" indicates the degree of concordance or similarity between the average pathologist's assessment and the consensus agreement in the context of fibrosis stage evaluation."},
{"0_96_95_ci_0_95_0_97": "The object \"0.96 (95% CI 0.95\u20130.97)\" represents the quantitative measure of agreement, with a value of 0.96 and a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.95 to 0.97, reflecting the reliability and precision of the agreement between the average pathologist and the consensus."},
{"fda__has_proposed__criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_endpoint_assessment": "The predicate \"has proposed\" indicates that the FDA has put forward or suggested specific guidelines or criteria for a particular process or evaluation, in this case, for MASH clinical trial endpoint assessment."},
{"criteria_for_mash_clinical_trial_endpoint_assessment": "The criteria for MASH clinical trial endpoint assessment refer to the specific standards or requirements proposed by the FDA for evaluating the outcomes of clinical trials related to MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis), focusing on histologic endpoints such as MASH resolution or fibrosis improvement."},
{"recommended_mash_trial_endpoints": "The recommended MASH trial endpoints refer to the specific outcomes that are suggested for clinical trials focused on noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. These endpoints are crucial for evaluating the efficacy of treatments in such trials."},
{"recommended_mash_trial_endpoints__include__evidence_of_efficacy_using_a_histologic_endpoint_of_mash_resolution_or_fibrosis_improvement": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the recommended MASH trial endpoints consist of or encompass certain criteria. It indicates that the endpoints are not exclusive but rather comprise multiple elements that are essential for assessing treatment efficacy."},
{"evidence_of_efficacy_using_a_histologic_endpoint_of_mash_resolution_or_fibrosis_improvement": "Evidence of efficacy using a histologic endpoint of MASH resolution or fibrosis improvement represents the specific criteria included in the recommended MASH trial endpoints. This object describes the desired outcomes in clinical trials, focusing on the histological changes indicating the resolution of MASH or improvement in fibrosis, which are critical for evaluating the effectiveness of treatments."},
{"aim_mash_predictions__were_used_to__determine_component_scores_and_evaluate_composite_endpoints_in_an_exploratory_retrospective_analysis": "The phrase \"were used to\" indicates the application or utilization of AIM-MASH predictions in a specific context. In this case, it describes how the predictions were employed as a tool or method to achieve a particular analytical goal within the clinical trial framework."},
{"determine_component_scores_and_evaluate_composite_endpoints_in_an_exploratory_retrospective_analysis": "Determining component scores and evaluating composite endpoints in an exploratory retrospective analysis involves assessing various individual and combined measures of treatment efficacy in a clinical trial. This process helps in understanding the effectiveness of treatments by analyzing both singular and aggregated outcomes from past data."},
{"aim_mash_derived_histologic_changes": "AIM-MASH-derived histologic changes refer to the alterations in tissue structure and cellular composition identified by the AIM-MASH model in patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. These changes are critical for evaluating the efficacy of treatments in clinical trials."},
{"aim_mash_derived_histologic_changes__were_compared_with__a_consensus_determination_of_the_endpoints_by_three_expert_pathologists": "The phrase \"were compared with\" indicates a direct evaluation and comparison of the histologic changes identified by AIM-MASH against a standard or benchmark. In this context, it signifies that the model's assessments were juxtaposed with expert pathologists' consensus to determine accuracy and reliability."},
{"a_consensus_determination_of_the_endpoints_by_three_expert_pathologists": "A consensus determination of the endpoints by three expert pathologists represents the gold standard for histologic assessment in MASH clinical trials. This consensus is achieved through the collective evaluation and agreement of three experienced pathologists on the histologic changes and endpoints, serving as a benchmark for comparing the AIM-MASH model's predictions."},
{"aim_mash_based_grading_and_staging": "AIM-MASH-based grading and staging refers to the evaluation method using the AIM-MASH model for assessing histologic changes in patients with noncirrhotic MASH and fibrosis. This method is used to determine component scores and evaluate composite endpoints in clinical trials."},
{"aim_mash_based_grading_and_staging__were_comparable_to__those_of_mean_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus": "The phrase \"were comparable to\" indicates that the results or performance of AIM-MASH-based grading and staging were similar in effectiveness or accuracy to another method, in this case, the consensus determination by expert pathologists."},
{"those_of_mean_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus": "\"Those of mean individual pathologist versus consensus\" refers to the average assessments made by individual pathologists compared to a consensus decision reached by a group of expert pathologists. This serves as a benchmark for evaluating the performance of the AIM-MASH model."},
{"fibrosis_improvement_without_worsening_of_mash": "Fibrosis improvement without worsening of MASH refers to a clinical scenario where there is an enhancement in the condition of liver fibrosis, without any deterioration in the severity of Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). This is a critical endpoint in clinical trials assessing the efficacy of treatments for noncirrhotic MASH."},
{"fibrosis_improvement_without_worsening_of_mash__had__percentage_agreement_rates_of_0_80_95_ci_0_76_0_84_for_aim_mash_versus_consensus_and_0_80_95_ci_0_77_0_83_for_pathologist_versus_consensus": "The predicate \"had\" in this context signifies the occurrence or presence of a specific rate of agreement in the evaluation of clinical trial endpoints. It indicates that the subject, fibrosis improvement without worsening of MASH, was associated with certain percentage agreement rates when assessed by AIM-MASH and pathologists."},
{"percentage_agreement_rates_of_0_80_95_ci_0_76_0_84_for_aim_mash_versus_consensus_and_0_80_95_ci_0_77_0_83_for_pathologist_versus_consensus": "The object describes the percentage agreement rates of 0.80 (with 95% confidence intervals of 0.76\u20130.84 for AIM-MASH versus consensus and 0.77\u20130.83 for pathologist versus consensus). These rates represent the level of agreement between the AIM-MASH model and a consensus of expert pathologists, as well as between individual pathologists and the consensus, in assessing the histologic endpoint of fibrosis improvement without worsening of MASH."},
{"mash_resolution_without_worsening_of_fibrosis": "MASH resolution without worsening of fibrosis refers to a clinical trial endpoint where patients with noncirrhotic MASH show resolution of the disease without any deterioration in their fibrosis levels. This is a critical endpoint for assessing the efficacy of treatments in late phase 2b and phase 3 trials."},
{"mash_resolution_without_worsening_of_fibrosis__had__model_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_86_95_ci_0_82_0_89_and_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_86": "The predicate \"had\" indicates the presence or occurrence of a specific condition or agreement rate in the context of clinical trial endpoints. It signifies that the subject (MASH resolution without worsening of fibrosis) was associated with a certain level of agreement between different evaluation methods."},
{"model_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_86_95_ci_0_82_0_89_and_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_82_95_ci_0_79_0_86": "The object describes the agreement rates between different evaluation methods for the endpoint of MASH resolution without worsening of fibrosis. Specifically, it mentions a model versus consensus agreement of 0.86 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.82\u20130.89, and a pathologist versus consensus agreement of 0.82 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.79\u20130.86. These rates indicate the level of concordance between automated model predictions and expert pathologist assessments."},
{"2_point_reduction_in_mas": "A \u22652-point reduction in MAS refers to a significant decrease in the Metabolic Activity Score, which is a measure used in evaluating the severity of metabolic activity in a clinical context, particularly in the assessment of MASH (Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis) and its related histologic changes."},
{"2_point_reduction_in_mas__had__model_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_79_95_ci_0_74_0_83_and_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_77_95_ci_0_74_0_81": "The predicate 'had' in this context indicates the presence or occurrence of a specific agreement rate between the model's predictions and the consensus determination by expert pathologists regarding the histologic changes assessed."},
{"model_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_79_95_ci_0_74_0_83_and_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_of_0_77_95_ci_0_74_0_81": "The object describes the agreement rates between the model's predictions and the consensus agreement of expert pathologists. Specifically, it mentions a model versus consensus agreement of 0.79 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.74\u20130.83, and a pathologist versus consensus agreement of 0.77 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.74\u20130.81, indicating the level of concordance in assessing a \u22652-point reduction in MAS."},
{"aim_mash__ability_to_measure__histologic_response_to_a_therapeutic": "The ability to measure refers to AIM-MASH's capability to quantify and evaluate the histologic changes in liver tissue in response to therapeutic interventions. This involves generating model predictions for MASH CRN grades/stages and comparing them with clinical pathologist (CP) measurements."},
{"histologic_response_to_a_therapeutic": "Histologic response to a therapeutic pertains to the changes observed in the liver tissue's histology following the administration of a drug or treatment. In the context of the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial, this involves assessing improvements in fibrosis stages and MASH resolution in patients treated with drugs like cilofexor and firsocostat."},
{"retrospective_analysis": "A retrospective analysis is a type of study that looks back at existing data to evaluate the effectiveness or outcomes of a treatment or intervention. In this context, it was used to assess drug efficacy."},
{"retrospective_analysis__performed_in__atlas_phase_2b_clinical_trial_nct03449446": "The phrase \"performed in\" indicates that the retrospective analysis was conducted within the framework or setting of the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial. This means the data from the trial was used to carry out the analysis."},
{"atlas_phase_2b_clinical_trial_nct03449446": "The ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial (NCT03449446) is a study designed to evaluate the efficacy of two drugs, cilofexor and firsocostat, both as monotherapies and in combination, in patients with advanced fibrosis. The trial aimed to measure improvements in histology relative to a placebo."},
{"atlas": "ATLAS is a phase 2b clinical trial designed to assess the efficacy of certain drugs in treating patients with advanced fibrosis. It specifically focuses on evaluating the effectiveness of these drugs in improving histologic outcomes in patients with F3\u2013F4 fibrosis."},
{"atlas__evaluated_the_efficacy_of__cilofexor_cilo_and_firsocostat_fir": "The predicate \"evaluated the efficacy of\" refers to the process by which the ATLAS trial assessed how well the drugs cilofexor and firsocostat worked in treating the targeted condition. This involved analyzing the outcomes and improvements observed in patients who received these drugs."},
{"cilofexor_cilo_and_firsocostat_fir": "Cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR) are two drugs that were tested in the ATLAS trial. They were evaluated both as monotherapies and in combination to determine their effectiveness in improving histologic response in patients with advanced fibrosis.|Here is another description: Cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR) are two drugs that were tested in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial for their efficacy in treating patients with advanced fibrosis."},
{"cilofexor_cilo_and_firsocostat_fir__evaluated_as__monotherapies_and_in_combination_cilo_fir": "The predicate \"evaluated as\" indicates that the efficacy of cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR) was assessed in the clinical trial."},
{"monotherapies_and_in_combination_cilo_fir": "Monotherapies and in combination (CILO + FIR) refer to the different treatment regimens tested in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial, where the drugs were administered either individually or together."},
{"patients": "The patients in this context are participants in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial, specifically those who were administered either cilofexor, firsocostat, or a combination of both as part of the study to evaluate drug efficacy.|Here is another description: The patients referred to in the context are individuals with advanced MASH fibrosis who participated in a clinical trial. They provided baseline and week 48 biopsies that were analyzed using AIM-MASH models to assess drug efficacy.|Here is another description: The subject, patients, refers to adult individuals diagnosed with MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) who were involved in clinical trials. These patients provided informed consent for their anonymized liver tissue samples and digitized whole slide images (WSIs) of H&E- and trichrome-stained liver biopsies to be used in future research.|Here is another description: The patients in this context are individuals who have participated in various randomized controlled trials for MASH therapeutics. They are the primary subjects from whom anonymized liver tissue samples and digitized whole slide images (WSIs) of liver biopsies were obtained."},
{"patients__had__advanced_f3_f4_fibrosis": "The predicate \"had\" indicates the condition or state of the patients at the time of the clinical trial, specifically referring to the presence of advanced fibrosis."},
{"advanced_f3_f4_fibrosis": "Advanced (F3\u2013F4) fibrosis refers to a severe stage of liver fibrosis, where the liver tissue has significant scarring. In the context of the ATLAS trial, this condition was present in the patients who were part of the study evaluating the efficacy of the drugs cilofexor and firsocostat."},
{"no_treatment_arm": "The \"no treatment arm\" refers to a control group in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial that did not receive any of the tested drugs (cilofexor, firsocostat, or their combination) and was used as a baseline to compare the efficacy of the treatments."},
{"no_treatment_arm__achieved__statistical_significance_for_the_primary_endpoint": "The predicate \"achieved\" indicates that the control group in the trial reached a certain level of outcome or result in terms of the primary endpoint, which in this case, was the assessment of treatment response in patients with advanced fibrosis."},
{"statistical_significance_for_the_primary_endpoint": "\"Statistical significance for the primary endpoint\" refers to the level of outcome that would indicate a meaningful difference between the treatment and control groups in the clinical trial. In this context, it means that the control group did not show a statistically significant improvement in histology compared to the placebo, as measured by the primary endpoint of the trial."},
{"cohort_that_received_the_combination_of_cilo_fir": "The cohort refers to a group of patients with advanced (F3\u2013F4) fibrosis who participated in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial and were administered a combination therapy of cilofexor (CILO) and firsocostat (FIR)."},
{"cohort_that_received_the_combination_of_cilo_fir__showed__the_greatest_improvement_in_histology_relative_to_placebo": "The predicate \"showed\" indicates that the cohort demonstrated a measurable outcome or result in response to the treatment they received. In this context, it signifies that the cohort exhibited a specific level of improvement in histology."},
{"the_greatest_improvement_in_histology_relative_to_placebo": "The greatest improvement in histology relative to placebo signifies that among all the treatment arms in the ATLAS trial, the cohort receiving the combination of CILO + FIR had the most significant positive change in their histologic condition compared to those who received a placebo."},
{"aim_mash_models": "AIM-MASH models are AI-based tools designed to assess treatment response in clinical trials for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). These models are specifically developed to measure histologic response to therapeutic interventions.|Here is another description: AIM-MASH models are advanced computational models designed to assess and predict the efficacy of drug treatments for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) based on imaging data.|Here is another description: AIM-MASH models are advanced computational tools designed to assess drug efficacy in medical trials, specifically for patients with advanced MASH fibrosis."},
{"aim_mash_models__deployed_on__digitized_wsis_n_99_from_baseline_and_week_48_biopsies": "The phrase \"deployed on\" indicates the application or implementation of AIM-MASH models on a specific dataset. In this context, it means that the models were utilized to analyze and generate predictions from the provided set of digitized whole slide images."},
{"digitized_wsis_n_99_from_baseline_and_week_48_biopsies": "Digitized WSIs (whole slide images) from baseline and week 48 biopsies represent the dataset on which the AIM-MASH models were applied. These images, totaling 99 in number, were used to evaluate the histologic changes in patients over the course of the clinical trial, specifically looking at the effects of the treatments administered."},
{"model_predictions": "Model predictions refer to the outcomes generated by the AIM-MASH models when applied to digitized whole slide images (WSIs) from clinical trial biopsies. These predictions are used to assess treatment response in patients.|Here is another description: Model predictions refer to the outcomes generated by the AIM-MASH models when applied to digitized whole slide images (WSIs) from baseline and week 48 biopsies in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial. These predictions are aimed at assessing histologic response to therapeutic interventions."},
{"model_predictions__generated_for__ordinal_mash_crn_grades_stages": "The phrase \"generated for\" indicates that the model predictions were specifically created to evaluate and provide data on the ordinal MASH CRN grades/stages. This process involves analyzing the histologic changes in the biopsies to determine the effectiveness of the treatment."},
{"ordinal_mash_crn_grades_stages": "Ordinal MASH CRN grades/stages are a standardized system used to classify the severity of metabolic associated steatohepatitis (MASH) based on histologic features observed in liver biopsies. These grades/stages help in assessing the progression or improvement of the disease in response to treatment."},
{"model_predictions__compared_with__cp_measurements_of_grades_stages_for_the_trial_s_primary_and_two_exploratory_endpoints": "The phrase \"compared with\" indicates a direct evaluation or analysis between two sets of data. In this context, it signifies the comparison of model predictions with CP measurements to assess the accuracy and effectiveness of the AIM-MASH models in detecting treatment response."},
{"cp_measurements_of_grades_stages_for_the_trial_s_primary_and_two_exploratory_endpoints": "CP measurements of grades/stages for the trial\u2019s primary and two exploratory endpoints refer to the conventional pathologist (CP) assessments of histologic changes in liver biopsies. These measurements are used as a benchmark to evaluate the performance of the AIM-MASH model predictions in the ATLAS trial."},
{"aim_mash__detected__a_greater_proportion_of_treatment_responders_in_the_cilo_fir_group_for_all_three_endpoints_measured_compared_with_the_cp": "The predicate \"detected\" refers to AIM-MASH's capability to identify and quantify treatment responses in clinical trial data. It involves analyzing changes in histologic features over time and comparing these changes across different treatment groups."},
{"a_greater_proportion_of_treatment_responders_in_the_cilo_fir_group_for_all_three_endpoints_measured_compared_with_the_cp": "The object describes a significant finding where AIM-MASH identified a greater proportion of treatment responders in the CILO + FIR group across three measured endpoints compared to the CP (central pathology). This indicates that the combination therapy of cilofexor and firsocostat showed improved histologic outcomes in patients with advanced fibrosis."},
