{"model_derived_scores": "Model-derived scores refer to the numerical values generated by an artificial intelligence model, specifically AIM-MASH, to evaluate and grade medical conditions based on biopsy samples."},
{"model_derived_scores__distinguished__fibrosis_stages_f1_f3_versus_f4_and_mas_4": "The predicate \"distinguished\" indicates that the model-derived scores were able to differentiate or identify distinct categories or stages of a medical condition, in this case, fibrosis and MAS grades, with a high degree of accuracy."},
{"fibrosis_stages_f1_f3_versus_f4_and_mas_4": "Fibrosis stages F1\u2013F3 versus F4 and MAS \u2265 4 represent specific categories of liver condition severity. Fibrosis stages F1\u2013F3 indicate earlier or less severe stages of liver fibrosis, while F4 indicates a more advanced stage. MAS \u2265 4 signifies a higher grade of steatohepatitis activity, with each component grade being at least 1, compared to MAS < 4 which indicates lower activity."},
{"aim_mash_agreement": "The AIM-MASH agreement refers to the level of agreement or consistency achieved by the AIM-MASH model in grading or staging medical conditions, specifically in the context of clinical trials for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis).|Here is another description: The AIM-MASH agreement refers to the level of consensus achieved by an AI model in grading and staging medical conditions, specifically in the context of clinical trials for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). It is used to evaluate enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints."},
{"aim_mash_agreement__was_comparable_to__consensus_and_each_pathologist": "The phrase \"was comparable to\" indicates that the level of agreement or performance of the AIM-MASH model was on par with, or similar to, another standard or benchmark. In this context, it suggests that the AIM-MASH model's agreement with consensus was as good as that of individual pathologists."},
{"consensus_and_each_pathologist": "The object \"consensus and each pathologist\" refers to the collective agreement reached by a group of experts (consensus) and the individual assessments made by each pathologist. In the context of medical grading or staging, this represents the standard against which the AIM-MASH model's performance is being compared."},
{"bar_plots": "Bar plots are graphical representations used to display categorical data with rectangular bars proportional to the values they depict. In the context of clinical trials, they are utilized to visually summarize the outcomes or measurements of different criteria or endpoints."},
{"bar_plots__represent__the_point_estimate_of_each_enrollment_criteria_endpoint": "The verb \"represent\" in this context signifies that the bar plots serve as a visual depiction or illustration of the point estimates for each enrollment criteria endpoint. They provide a clear and concise way to convey the quantitative results of the criteria assessed in the clinical trials."},
{"the_point_estimate_of_each_enrollment_criteria_endpoint": "The point estimate of each enrollment criteria endpoint refers to the specific value or measurement that represents the central tendency or typical outcome for a given criterion in the clinical trial. These point estimates are crucial for evaluating the performance and agreement of the AIM-MASH model with consensus and pathologist assessments."},
{"whiskers": "Whiskers in this context refer to the visual elements in a bar plot that indicate the range of the 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for the data being represented.|Here is another description: Whiskers in this context refer to the lines extending from the ends of the box in a box plot, which are used to represent the variability outside the upper and lower quartiles."},
{"whiskers__represent__the_95_cis_estimated_using_10_000_bootstrap_samples": "The predicate'represent' indicates that the whiskers are used to visually depict or symbolize the 95% CIs in the bar plots."},
{"the_95_cis_estimated_using_10_000_bootstrap_samples": "The 95% CIs estimated using 10,000 bootstrap samples are statistical measures that provide a range within which the true value of a parameter is expected to lie with 95% confidence. These CIs are calculated through a resampling technique called bootstrapping, which involves repeatedly sampling from the data to estimate the variability and uncertainty in the measurements."},
{"aim_mash_agreement__was_comparable_to__that_of_an_average_pathologist": "The predicate \"was comparable to\" indicates that the performance or agreement level of the AIM-MASH model was on par with another standard, in this case, the performance of an average pathologist. This suggests a similar level of accuracy or reliability in the assessments made."},
{"that_of_an_average_pathologist": "The object \"that of an average pathologist\" refers to the typical level of agreement or performance expected from a human pathologist when grading and staging medical conditions. It serves as a benchmark for comparing the effectiveness of the AIM-MASH model."},
{"assessment": "The assessment refers to the evaluation process conducted using the AIM-MASH model to determine agreement with consensus on efficacy endpoints in a clinical trial. This process involves analyzing biopsy samples to grade and stage various clinical criteria."},
{"assessment__was_performed_on__an_external_held_out_validation_dataset_from_a_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"was performed on\" indicates that the assessment was carried out using a specific dataset. This action signifies the application of the AIM-MASH model to evaluate the given endpoints on the provided dataset to measure its performance and agreement with consensus."},
{"an_external_held_out_validation_dataset_from_a_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The object, an external held-out validation dataset from a phase 2b MASH clinical trial, represents a set of biopsy samples used to validate the AIM-MASH model. This dataset includes samples from patients meeting specific clinical endpoints, such as fibrosis improvement and MASH resolution, and is separate from the training data to ensure an unbiased evaluation of the model\u2019s performance."},
{"biopsies": "Biopsies refer to the tissue samples obtained from patients in clinical trials, specifically from the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trials, used to assess various medical conditions and endpoints."},
{"biopsies__were_of_patients_meeting__the_following_endpoints_fibrosis_improvement_without_mash_worsening_n_279_mash_resolution_without_fibrosis_worsening_n_279_and_mas_reduction_2_n_326": "The phrase \"were of patients meeting\" indicates that the biopsies were taken from patients who satisfied specific clinical criteria or endpoints during the trials."},
{"the_following_endpoints_fibrosis_improvement_without_mash_worsening_n_279_mash_resolution_without_fibrosis_worsening_n_279_and_mas_reduction_2_n_326": "The following endpoints represent specific clinical outcomes assessed in the trials: fibrosis improvement without worsening of Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH), resolution of MASH without worsening of fibrosis, and a reduction in Metabolic Associated Steatosis (MAS) score by at least 2 points. These endpoints were evaluated in a specified number of patients (n=279 for the first two, n=326 for the third)."},
{"5": "The subject refers to a 5% threshold or condition within the context of a study or experiment, likely indicating a specific percentage of participants or a particular condition met by a subset of the study population.|Here is another description: The object \"5\" likely represents a specific affiliation or institution that Arun J. Sanyal is associated with, as part of the collaborative effort detailed in the context."},
{"5__2_point_reduction_in_mas__60_versus_35": "The predicate describes a condition where there is a reduction of at least 2 points in the Modified Ashworth Scale (MAS), which is a common measure used to assess muscle spasticity in clinical settings."},
{"60_versus_35": "The object indicates a comparison of two percentages, 60% and 35%, which likely represent the proportion of patients achieving the \u22652-point reduction in MAS in two different groups, possibly a treatment group versus a placebo group."},
