{"ai_based_continuous_scores": "AI-based continuous scores refer to the continuous numerical values generated by artificial intelligence algorithms to assess and quantify certain medical conditions or parameters, in this case, MASH CRN scores."},
{"ai_based_continuous_scores__correlate_with__mean_scores_across_three_pathologists_from_emminence": "The phrase \"correlate with\" indicates a statistical relationship where changes in one variable are associated with changes in another. In this context, it means that the AI-based continuous scores show a statistical association with the mean scores provided by pathologists."},
{"mean_scores_across_three_pathologists_from_emminence": "Mean scores across three pathologists from EMMINENCE represent the average scores given by three expert pathologists who evaluated the same medical condition or parameter as part of the EMMINENCE study. These scores serve as a benchmark for comparing the performance of AI-based continuous scoring methods."},
{"results__derived_from__kendall_s_tau_\u03c4_rank_correlation_analysis": "The predicate \"derived from\" indicates the method or analysis used to obtain the results. In this context, it signifies that the results were obtained through the application of Kendall\u2019s tau (\u03c4) rank correlation analysis."},
{"kendall_s_tau_\u03c4_rank_correlation_analysis": "Kendall\u2019s tau (\u03c4) rank correlation analysis is a non-parametric statistical method used to measure the correlation between two ranked variables. In this context, it was employed to analyze the correlation between AI-based continuous scores and mean scores from pathologists."},
{"fdr_correction_of_p_values": "FDR correction of P values refers to the adjustment of P values to control the false discovery rate, which is the expected proportion of false discoveries amongst the total number of statistical tests conducted."},
{"fdr_correction_of_p_values__performed_using__benjamini_hochberg_procedure": "Performed using indicates the method or procedure applied to carry out a specific task or analysis, in this case, the adjustment of P values."},
{"benjamini_hochberg_procedure": "The Benjamini\u2013Hochberg procedure is a commonly used method for controlling the false discovery rate in multiple hypothesis testing. It adjusts the P values to account for the number of tests performed, thereby reducing the likelihood of false positives."},
{"filled_circles": "Filled circles in the context refer to specific data points or markers used in a graph or chart to represent certain values or outcomes. In this case, they are used to visually indicate results that meet a particular statistical threshold."},
{"filled_circles__indicate__statistical_significance_fdr_corrected_p_0_05": "The predicate \"indicate\" in this context means to show or signal the presence of a specific condition or result. Here, it is used to describe how filled circles on a graph serve as a visual cue for data points that have achieved statistical significance after FDR correction."},
{"statistical_significance_fdr_corrected_p_0_05": "Statistical significance, specifically with FDR-corrected P < 0.05, refers to a result that is unlikely to have occurred by chance, as determined by a statistical test. The False Discovery Rate (FDR) correction is a method used to control the expected proportion of incorrectly rejected null hypotheses (type I errors) in a list of rejected hypotheses when conducting multiple comparisons. A P-value less than 0.05 after FDR correction suggests strong evidence against the null hypothesis, indicating that the observed effect is statistically significant."},
{"cfib_versus_cpa_measurements": "The subject refers to the comparison of cFib (continuous fibrosis score) and CPA (computer-assisted proportionate area) measurements. These measurements are used to assess the extent of fibrosis in patients."},
{"cfib_versus_cpa_measurements__in__primary_endpoint_responders_in_the_atlas_clinical_trial": "The predicate indicates that the cFib and CPA measurements are being analyzed within a specific group of patients. Specifically, these measurements are being evaluated in the context of their response to a primary endpoint in a clinical trial."},
{"primary_endpoint_responders_in_the_atlas_clinical_trial": "The object describes the group of patients being studied, namely the primary endpoint responders in the ATLAS clinical trial. These are patients who showed a positive response to the treatment being tested in the trial, as determined by predefined criteria for the primary endpoint."},
{"cfib_and_cpa": "cFib and CPA are metrics used to measure continuous fibrosis scores and proportionate area fibrosis, respectively, in the context of medical imaging and pathology assessments."},
{"cfib_and_cpa__compared_between__patients_receiving_treatment_and_placebo_using_two_sided_mann_whitney_u_tests": "The comparison between cFib and CPA measurements was conducted using two-sided Mann\u2013Whitney U tests, a non-parametric statistical test used to determine if there are differences between two independent samples."},
{"patients_receiving_treatment_and_placebo_using_two_sided_mann_whitney_u_tests": "Patients receiving treatment and those receiving a placebo represent the two groups being compared in the study to evaluate the effectiveness of the treatment on fibrosis scores."},
{"continuous_fibrosis_scores": "Continuous fibrosis scores refer to the quantitative measurements of fibrosis levels in patients, derived using AI-based methods. These scores provide a detailed and nuanced assessment of the extent of fibrosis, which is crucial for evaluating treatment efficacy in clinical trials."},
{"continuous_fibrosis_scores__significantly_reduced_in__treated_patients_n_17_versus_placebo_patients_n_6_mann_whitney_u_20_0_p_0_02": "The phrase \"significantly reduced in\" indicates a statistically meaningful decrease in continuous fibrosis scores when comparing two groups. This reduction is assessed using the Mann\u2013Whitney U test, which is a nonparametric test used to determine if there are differences between two independent groups."},
{"treated_patients_n_17_versus_placebo_patients_n_6_mann_whitney_u_20_0_p_0_02": "Treated patients (n = 17) versus placebo patients (n = 6; Mann\u2013Whitney U = 20.0, P = 0.02) refers to the two groups being compared in the study. The treated group consists of 17 patients who received the experimental treatment, while the placebo group includes 6 patients who received a placebo. The Mann\u2013Whitney U test result (U = 20.0, P = 0.02) indicates a statistically significant difference in continuous fibrosis scores between these two groups."},
{"proportionate_area_fibrosis_measurements": "Proportionate area fibrosis measurements refer to the quantitative assessment of the extent of fibrosis in a given area, typically expressed as a percentage or ratio. These measurements are crucial in evaluating the severity and progression of fibrotic diseases, providing a detailed view of tissue damage and the effectiveness of treatments."},
{"proportionate_area_fibrosis_measurements__not_significantly_reduced__mann_whitney_u_39_0_p_0_21": "The phrase \"not significantly reduced\" indicates that the proportionate area fibrosis measurements did not show a statistically significant decrease following a specific treatment or intervention. This suggests that the treatment did not lead to a notable improvement in reducing fibrosis compared to a control or placebo group."},
{"mann_whitney_u_39_0_p_0_21": "Mann\u2013Whitney U = 39.0, P = 0.21 represents the statistical outcome of a Mann\u2013Whitney U test, a nonparametric test used to compare differences between two independent groups. In this context, the U value of 39.0 and the P-value of 0.21 indicate that there was no significant difference in proportionate area fibrosis measurements between the treatment and placebo groups, as the P-value is greater than the conventional significance level of 0.05."},
{"cfib_and_cpa_values": "The cFib and CPA values refer to continuous fibrosis scores and proportionate area fibrosis measurements, respectively, which are used to assess the extent of fibrosis in patients."},
{"cfib_and_cpa_values__for_patients_classified_as__nonresponders_n_76_in_the_treatment_n_45_or_placebo_n_31_group": "The phrase \"for patients classified as\" indicates a categorization or grouping of patients based on their response to treatment, specifically whether they are responders or nonresponders."},
{"nonresponders_n_76_in_the_treatment_n_45_or_placebo_n_31_group": "Nonresponders (n = 76) in the treatment (n = 45) or placebo (n = 31) group refers to patients who did not show a positive response to the treatment, divided into those who received the actual treatment and those who received a placebo."},
{"boxes": "Boxes in the context refer to the visual elements in a box plot used to represent statistical data, specifically in the comparison of cFib and CPA measurements between treated and placebo patients in the ATLAS clinical trial."},
{"boxes__represent__the_25th_percentile": "The predicate'represent' indicates that the boxes in the box plot are used to visually depict or symbolize a specific statistical measure, in this case, the 25th percentile of the data."},
{"the_25th_percentile": "The 25th percentile, also known as the first quartile, is a statistical measure that indicates the value below which 25% of the data points fall. In the context, it is used to show the lower boundary of the interquartile range in the box plot, helping to visualize the distribution of cFib and CPA measurements among patients."},
{"whiskers__extend_to__points_that_lie_within_1_5_fold_of_the_inter_quartile_range_of_the_25th_and_75th_percentiles": "The predicate \"extend to\" describes the action of the whiskers reaching out to specific data points within a defined statistical range."},
{"points_that_lie_within_1_5_fold_of_the_inter_quartile_range_of_the_25th_and_75th_percentiles": "The object \"points that lie within 1.5-fold of the inter-quartile range of the 25th and 75th percentiles\" refers to the specific data points that the whiskers reach, which are determined by a statistical calculation involving the inter-quartile range and the 25th and 75th percentiles of the data set."},
{"stratification_of_patients": "The stratification of patients refers to the process of categorizing patients into different groups based on specific criteria. In this context, it involves dividing patients with BL F3 or F4 fibrosis from the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts into rapid and slow progressors."},
{"stratification_of_patients__based_on__continuous_score_cutoffs_of_3_6_and_4_6": "The stratification of patients is based on continuous score cutoffs, which are specific numerical thresholds used to divide patients into different groups. These cutoffs are derived from a continuous scoring system that assesses the progression of fibrosis in patients."},
{"continuous_score_cutoffs_of_3_6_and_4_6": "The continuous score cutoffs of 3.6 and 4.6 are specific numerical thresholds used to stratify patients with BL F3 or F4 fibrosis into rapid and slow progressors. These cutoffs were chosen to maximize the hazards and provide a clear distinction between the two groups."},
{"f3": "F3 refers to a stage of liver fibrosis, specifically stage 3, which indicates significant scarring of the liver tissue. In the context of the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts, F3 patients are those who have been diagnosed with this level of fibrosis at baseline.|Here is another description: F3 refers to a stage of liver fibrosis, specifically indicating a moderate level of scarring in the liver tissue. In the context of the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trials, F3 fibrosis is one of the conditions being studied in relation to disease progression and treatment outcomes."},
{"f3__log_rank_statistic__31_0": "The log-rank statistic is a statistical measure used in survival analysis to compare the survival distributions of two or more groups. It assesses whether the difference in survival times between groups is statistically significant."},
{"31_0": "The value 31.0 represents the log-rank statistic calculated for the F3 fibrosis group in the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts. This statistic indicates a significant difference in survival or progression rates between the rapid and slow progressors within the F3 group."},
{"f3__p_value__2_6_10_8": "The P-value is a statistical measure that helps determine the significance of results in hypothesis testing. In this context, the P-value is used to assess the likelihood that the observed differences in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors with F3 fibrosis are due to chance."},
{"2_6_10_8": "The P-value of 2.6 \u00d7 10^-8 indicates an extremely low probability that the observed differences in progression rates between rapid and slow progressors with F3 fibrosis occurred by random chance. This suggests a highly significant statistical difference, supporting the hypothesis that the stratification into rapid and slow progressors is meaningful."},
{"f4": "F4 refers to a stage of liver fibrosis, specifically the fourth stage, indicating significant scarring of the liver tissue. In the context of the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts, F4 fibrosis is used to stratify patients based on the severity of their liver condition.|Here is another description: F4 refers to a stage of liver fibrosis, specifically the fourth stage, indicating severe scarring of the liver. In the context of the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts, F4 fibrosis is used to categorize patients based on the severity of their liver condition."},
{"f4__log_rank_statistic__4_8": "The log-rank statistic is a measure used in survival analysis to compare the survival distributions of two or more groups. In this context, it is used to evaluate the difference in progression rates between patients with F4 fibrosis who are classified as rapid or slow progressors based on continuous score cutoffs."},
{"4_8": "The value 4.8 represents the log-rank statistic calculated for patients with F4 fibrosis in the STELLAR-3 and STELLAR-4 trial cohorts. This statistic indicates the degree of difference in survival distributions between the rapid and slow progressor groups, with a p-value of 0.028 suggesting a statistically significant difference."},
{"f4__p_value__0_028": "The P-value is a statistical measure that helps determine the significance of results in a hypothesis test. In this context, the P-value is used to assess the likelihood that the observed differences in patient progression between F3 and F4 fibrosis stages are due to chance."},
{"0_028": "The P-value of 0.028 indicates a statistically significant result at a common threshold of 0.05. This suggests that the difference in progression rates between patients with F4 fibrosis and those with F3 fibrosis is unlikely to have occurred by random chance, supporting the hypothesis that F4 patients have a distinct progression pattern."},
{"ai_derived_continuous_scores": "AI-derived continuous scores are numerical values generated by artificial intelligence algorithms to assess the likelihood of disease progression. These scores are based on continuous data analysis and are used to stratify patients into different risk categories."},
{"ai_derived_continuous_scores__discriminatory_accuracy__greater_than_ordinal_scores_to_predict_progression_to_cirrhosis_and_lre": "Discriminatory accuracy refers to the ability of a scoring system to correctly distinguish between patients who will progress to a more severe disease state and those who will not. It is a measure of the effectiveness of the scoring system in predicting outcomes."},
{"greater_than_ordinal_scores_to_predict_progression_to_cirrhosis_and_lre": "Greater than ordinal scores to predict progression to cirrhosis and LRE indicates that the AI-derived continuous scores have a higher discriminatory accuracy compared to traditional ordinal scores. This means that the continuous scores are more effective in predicting which patients will progress to cirrhosis or experience a liver-related event (LRE)."},
{"progression_to_cirrhosis": "Progression to cirrhosis refers to the advancement of liver disease to a stage where the liver tissue becomes scarred and its function is significantly impaired. This condition is a critical stage in the spectrum of liver diseases, often resulting from chronic liver damage.|Here is another description: Progression to cirrhosis refers to the advancement of liver disease to a stage where significant scarring (fibrosis) has developed, leading to cirrhosis. This condition is a critical stage in liver disease progression and is associated with increased risk of liver-related events and complications."},
{"progression_to_cirrhosis__continuous_auc__0_66_95_ci_0_60_0_71": "The continuous AUC (Area Under the Curve) is a statistical measure used to evaluate the performance of a diagnostic test or model. In this context, it assesses how well the AI-derived continuous scores can predict the progression to cirrhosis compared to ordinal scores."},
{"0_66_95_ci_0_60_0_71": "The object \"0.66 (95% CI 0.60\u20130.71)\" represents the continuous AUC value, indicating the discriminatory accuracy of the AI-derived continuous scores in predicting progression to cirrhosis. The 95% confidence interval (CI) of 0.60\u20130.71 provides a range within which the true AUC is likely to fall, offering a measure of the reliability and precision of the AUC estimate."},
{"progression_to_cirrhosis__ordinal_auc__0_59_95_ci_0_55_0_60": "The ordinal AUC (Area Under the Curve) is a statistical measure used to evaluate the performance of a binary classifier system as its discrimination threshold is varied. In this context, it is used to assess the accuracy of AI-derived scores in predicting the progression to cirrhosis."},
{"0_59_95_ci_0_55_0_60": "The object \"0.59 (95% CI 0.55\u20130.60)\" represents the ordinal AUC value with a 95% confidence interval, indicating the discriminatory accuracy of the ordinal scores in predicting progression to cirrhosis. This value suggests moderate predictive accuracy, though it is lower than the continuous AUC, which was 0.66 (95% CI 0.60\u20130.71)."},
{"progression_to_lre": "Progression to LRE refers to the advancement of liver disease to a stage where liver-related events (LRE) occur. This is a critical endpoint in studies evaluating the efficacy of treatments for liver conditions, as it indicates the effectiveness of interventions in preventing severe liver outcomes.|Here is another description: Progression to LRE refers to the advancement towards liver-related events, which are significant outcomes in liver disease progression, often used as endpoints in clinical trials evaluating treatments for liver conditions."},
{"progression_to_lre__continuous_auc__0_61_95_ci_0_51_0_71": "The continuous AUC (Area Under the Curve) is a statistical measure used in receiver operating characteristic (ROC) analysis to evaluate the performance of a diagnostic test or model. In this context, it assesses how well the AI-derived continuous scores can discriminate between patients who will progress to LRE and those who will not."},
{"0_61_95_ci_0_51_0_71": "The object \"0.61 (95% CI 0.51\u20130.71)\" represents the continuous AUC value with its 95% confidence interval. This value indicates the discriminatory accuracy of the AI-derived continuous scores in predicting progression to LRE, suggesting moderate predictive performance."},
{"progression_to_lre__ordinal_auc__0_54_95_ci_0_47_59": "The ordinal AUC (Area Under the Curve) is a statistical measure used to evaluate the performance of a binary classifier system, such as a diagnostic test or a predictive model, as its discrimination threshold is varied. In this context, it assesses how well the AI-derived ordinal scores can distinguish between patients who will progress to LRE and those who will not."},
{"0_54_95_ci_0_47_59": "The object \"0.54 (95% CI 0.47\u201359)\" represents the ordinal AUC value with its 95% confidence interval, indicating the accuracy of the AI-derived ordinal scores in predicting progression to LRE. An AUC of 0.54 suggests moderate predictive accuracy, slightly better than random chance (AUC of 0.5), with the confidence interval providing a range within which the true AUC is likely to fall."},
{"nature_medicine__specific_page__2920": "The specific_page predicate indicates the exact page number within the journal where a particular piece of content, such as an article or section, can be found. This helps readers locate specific information within the publication."},
{"2920": "Page 2920 in the context of Nature Medicine refers to a specific page within the journal's October 2024 issue, Volume 30. This page is part of a range of pages (2914\u20132923) that likely contains detailed research or discussion relevant to the medical field."},
{"patient_enrollment_and_endpoint_measurement": "Patient enrollment and endpoint measurement refer to the processes involved in recruiting participants for a clinical trial and assessing the outcomes or endpoints of the trial. These processes are critical for evaluating the efficacy and safety of a treatment or intervention."},
{"patient_enrollment_and_endpoint_measurement__were__100_reproducible": "The predicate \"were\" indicates a state of being or existence. In this context, it describes the condition or status of patient enrollment and endpoint measurement in a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial."},
{"100_reproducible": "100% reproducible signifies that the processes of patient enrollment and endpoint measurement were consistent and reliable when the analysis was repeated on the same images. This high level of reproducibility suggests that the approach used could enable consistent measurement of disease severity within and across different timepoints and clinical trials."},
{"analysis": "The analysis refers to the process of examining and interpreting data, in this case, likely involving the assessment of histologic features in medical images related to MASH (Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis) clinical trials."},
{"analysis__was_repeated_on__the_same_images": "The predicate \"was repeated on\" indicates that the analysis process was conducted again, emphasizing the importance of reproducibility and consistency in the results obtained from the examination of the images."},
{"the_same_images": "The object \"the same images\" refers to the specific set of medical images that were used in the initial analysis and were subsequently re-examined to ensure the reproducibility of the results. These images are crucial for validating the accuracy and reliability of the analysis method."},
{"this_approach": "This approach refers to a method or system, likely involving artificial intelligence (AI), used for analyzing medical images to assess disease severity in clinical trials."},
{"this_approach__could_enable__consistent_measurement_of_disease_severity_within_and_across_timepoints_and_clinical_trials": "The phrase \"could enable\" suggests a potential capability or advantage offered by the approach, indicating that it has the potential to facilitate or make possible a certain outcome or process."},
{"consistent_measurement_of_disease_severity_within_and_across_timepoints_and_clinical_trials": "Consistent measurement of disease severity within and across timepoints and clinical trials refers to the ability to accurately and reliably assess the progression or regression of a disease at different points in time and in various clinical trial settings, ensuring that the measurements are uniform and comparable."},
{"further_analytical_validation": "Further analytical validation refers to additional testing and verification processes designed to ensure the reliability and accuracy of a method or system. In this context, it involves confirming the consistency and robustness of the AIM-MASH system under various conditions."},
{"further_analytical_validation__will_assess__reproducibility_with_various_pre_analytic_factors": "The phrase \"will assess\" indicates a future action where the analytical validation process will evaluate specific aspects of the AIM-MASH system. This assessment aims to determine how well the system performs under different pre-analytic factors, ensuring its effectiveness and reliability."},
{"reproducibility_with_various_pre_analytic_factors": "Reproducibility with various pre-analytic factors involves testing the AIM-MASH system's ability to produce consistent results under different conditions. These factors include different scanners, drug candidates, screening and enrolled populations, stain quality, biopsy, and section quality. This ensures that the system can reliably measure disease severity across diverse scenarios."},
{"the_accuracy_and_reproducibility_of_aim_mash": "The accuracy and reproducibility of AIM-MASH refers to the consistent and reliable performance of an artificial intelligence model designed to assess and grade histopathological features in medical images, specifically for the condition known as MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"the_accuracy_and_reproducibility_of_aim_mash__may_prove_especially_valuable_in__assisting_pathologists_to_achieve_reproducible_results_for_borderline_cases": "May prove especially valuable in indicates a potential significant benefit or utility that the subject (AIM-MASH's accuracy and reproducibility) could offer in a specific application or context, which in this case is the assistance to pathologists in achieving consistent results."},
{"assisting_pathologists_to_achieve_reproducible_results_for_borderline_cases": "Assisting pathologists to achieve reproducible results for borderline cases describes the specific application or context where the subject's (AIM-MASH's accuracy and reproducibility) value is highlighted. Borderline cases refer to instances where the histopathology is ambiguous or falls between two grades/stages, leading to common discordance among pathologists."},
{"aim_mash_grading_and_staging": "AIM-MASH grading and staging is an AI-based system designed to assess and measure the severity of histologic features in MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) with high accuracy and reproducibility. It aims to assist pathologists in achieving consistent results, especially in borderline cases where traditional methods may show discordance."},
{"aim_mash_grading_and_staging__can_detect__response_to_drug_treatment_with_comparable_accuracy_to_pathologists": "The predicate \"can detect\" refers to the capability of AIM-MASH to identify and measure specific responses in patients undergoing drug treatment. This detection is performed with a level of accuracy that is comparable to that of expert pathologists, highlighting the system's effectiveness in clinical settings."},
{"response_to_drug_treatment_with_comparable_accuracy_to_pathologists": "The response to drug treatment with comparable accuracy to pathologists indicates the effectiveness of AIM-MASH in identifying changes in disease severity in patients undergoing treatment. This capability is crucial for evaluating the efficacy of drug treatments in clinical trials and for making informed decisions about patient care."},
{"retrospective_assessment_of_primary_and_exploratory_endpoints": "The retrospective assessment of primary and exploratory endpoints refers to a detailed review and analysis of the outcomes and secondary measures from previous clinical trials, specifically the EMMINENCE and phase 2b ATLAS trials, to evaluate the effectiveness and reliability of a new method or tool."},
{"retrospective_assessment_of_primary_and_exploratory_endpoints__showed_that__aim_mash_achieved_a_high_level_of_scoring_accuracy_and_superior_reproducibility_compared_with_pathologists": "The phrase \"showed that\" indicates the result or outcome of the retrospective assessment. It suggests that the analysis provided clear evidence or data supporting a particular conclusion about the performance of the method or tool being evaluated."},
{"aim_mash_achieved_a_high_level_of_scoring_accuracy_and_superior_reproducibility_compared_with_pathologists": "AIM-MASH is an advanced imaging and measurement system designed to assess and score histopathological features in medical images, particularly in the context of liver diseases like MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). This system achieved a high level of scoring accuracy, meaning its assessments closely matched those of expert pathologists, and superior reproducibility, indicating consistent results across different assessments, outperforming traditional pathologist evaluations."},
{"aim_mash__detected__a_greater_proportion_of_responders_in_treated_patients_than_manual_scoring": "The predicate 'detected' in this context refers to AIM-MASH's capability to identify and quantify a specific outcome\u2014namely, the proportion of responders in treated patients\u2014with greater accuracy and reproducibility than traditional manual scoring methods."},
{"a_greater_proportion_of_responders_in_treated_patients_than_manual_scoring": "A greater proportion of responders in treated patients than manual scoring refers to the enhanced ability of AIM-MASH to accurately identify patients who show a positive response to treatment in clinical trials, outperforming the results obtained through manual pathologist scoring."},
{"validation_studies": "Validation studies refer to the ongoing evaluations and assessments designed to confirm the accuracy, reliability, and effectiveness of a particular method or system. In this context, they are specifically aimed at verifying the performance of the AIM-MASH application."},
{"validation_studies__are_underway__to_support_aim_mash_application_to_prospective_clinical_trial_workflows": "The phrase \"are underway\" indicates that the validation studies are currently being conducted or are in progress. This suggests an active phase where data is being collected, analyzed, and reviewed to ensure the AIM-MASH application meets the necessary standards for clinical use."},
{"to_support_aim_mash_application_to_prospective_clinical_trial_workflows": "The object \"to support AIM-MASH application to prospective clinical trial workflows\" describes the goal of the validation studies. It involves ensuring that the AIM-MASH application can be effectively integrated into future clinical trials, providing reliable and consistent results that can be trusted by researchers and medical professionals."},
{"achieving_the_surrogate_biopsy_based_endpoints": "Achieving the surrogate biopsy-based endpoints refers to the process of meeting the criteria set by regulatory bodies for evaluating the effectiveness of treatments in clinical trials for Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). These endpoints are crucial for determining the success of new therapies."},
{"achieving_the_surrogate_biopsy_based_endpoints__has_been_exceedingly_difficult__owing_to_the_slow_rate_at_which_mash_progresses_and_regresses": "The phrase \"has been exceedingly difficult\" indicates the significant challenges faced in meeting these endpoints. This difficulty is attributed to various factors, primarily the nature of MASH itself."},
{"owing_to_the_slow_rate_at_which_mash_progresses_and_regresses": "The slow rate at which MASH progresses and regresses is a key factor making it difficult to achieve the surrogate biopsy-based endpoints. This slow progression complicates the assessment of treatment efficacy within the timeframe of clinical trials."},
{"ai_models": "AI models refer to artificial intelligence systems designed to analyze and interpret complex data, in this context specifically used for medical image analysis and histopathological assessments."},
{"ai_models__were_used_to_investigate__non_crn_histological_features_associated_with_clinical_outcomes": "The phrase \"were used to investigate\" indicates that the AI models were employed as a tool or method to explore and analyze certain characteristics or features within medical data, aiming to uncover associations or patterns that could be clinically relevant."},
{"non_crn_histological_features_associated_with_clinical_outcomes": "Non-CRN histological features associated with clinical outcomes refer to specific characteristics or markers within tissue samples that are not part of the standard CRN (Clinical Research Network) scoring system but have been found to correlate with patient health outcomes. These features are analyzed to better understand disease progression and to potentially identify new biomarkers for clinical assessment."},
{"the_area_of_portal_inflammation": "The area of portal inflammation refers to the extent or size of inflammation present in the portal tracts of the liver, which are areas surrounding the branches of the portal vein. This inflammation is a histologic feature that can be measured and analyzed, particularly in the context of liver diseases such as MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"the_area_of_portal_inflammation__was_predictive_of__risk_of_disease_progression_lres_and_cirrhosis": "The phrase \"was predictive of\" indicates a relationship where the extent of portal inflammation has been shown to be a significant indicator or marker. In this context, it suggests that a greater area of portal inflammation is associated with an increased likelihood of certain outcomes related to liver disease."},
{"risk_of_disease_progression_lres_and_cirrhosis": "The risk of disease progression, LREs (liver-related events), and cirrhosis represents the potential adverse outcomes associated with liver disease. Disease progression refers to the worsening of the disease state, LREs encompass various serious liver-related complications, and cirrhosis is the advanced stage of scarring (fibrosis) of the liver, which can lead to liver failure."},
{"the_ratio_of_steatosis_to_hepatocellular_ballooning": "The ratio of steatosis to hepatocellular ballooning is a specific measurement used in assessing liver histology, particularly in the context of metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH). It reflects the proportion of fat accumulation (steatosis) relative to the swelling of liver cells (hepatocellular ballooning)."},
{"the_ratio_of_steatosis_to_hepatocellular_ballooning__was_identified_as__a_notable_human_interpretable_feature_using_ai_based_measurements": "The phrase \"was identified as\" indicates that through a process of analysis or evaluation, a particular characteristic or feature was recognized and designated as having a specific role or significance. In this context, it suggests that the ratio of steatosis to hepatocellular ballooning was determined to be an important factor in understanding or assessing a condition."},
{"a_notable_human_interpretable_feature_using_ai_based_measurements": "A notable human interpretable feature using AI-based measurements refers to a characteristic or attribute that can be understood and interpreted by humans, derived from data analyzed by artificial intelligence. In the context of medical imaging and pathology, this could involve AI algorithms identifying patterns or ratios in tissue samples that correlate with disease states or outcomes, providing valuable insights for diagnosis or prognosis."},
{"patients_with_higher_hepatocellular_ballooning_to_steatosis": "Patients with higher hepatocellular ballooning to steatosis are individuals who have a greater ratio of hepatocellular ballooning compared to steatosis in their liver histology, as measured by AI-based analysis."},
{"patients_with_higher_hepatocellular_ballooning_to_steatosis__were_more_likely_to__experience_clinical_events": "The predicate \"were more likely to\" indicates a higher probability or increased risk associated with the subject's condition, specifically in relation to experiencing clinical events."},
{"experience_clinical_events": "Clinical events in this context refer to significant medical occurrences or outcomes, such as liver-related events or disease progression, that patients may experience based on their histologic features as analyzed by AI."},
{"several_measurement_systems": "Several measurement systems have been developed and suggested for use in medical and scientific contexts to improve the detection and analysis of histologic changes."},
{"several_measurement_systems__have_been_proposed__to_detect_subordinal_levels_of_histologic_change": "The phrase \"have been proposed\" indicates that these measurement systems were suggested or put forward as potential solutions or methods for detecting subordinal levels of histologic change."},
{"to_detect_subordinal_levels_of_histologic_change": "The object \"to detect subordinal levels of histologic change\" refers to the goal or purpose of the proposed measurement systems, which is to identify and measure very small or subtle changes in histology, the study of the microscopic structure of tissues."},
{"ai_based_continuous_measures_of_fibrosis": "AI-based continuous measures of fibrosis refer to the use of artificial intelligence algorithms to quantify the extent of fibrosis in a continuous, rather than ordinal, manner. This approach aims to provide a more sensitive and precise assessment of fibrosis levels in medical images, such as liver biopsies."},
{"ai_based_continuous_measures_of_fibrosis__are_useful_for__detecting_subtle_yet_statistically_significant_changes_in_fibrosis_in_response_to_treatment": "The phrase \"are useful for\" indicates that the AI-based continuous measures of fibrosis serve a practical purpose. Specifically, they are employed to enhance the detection capabilities in medical assessments, offering a more nuanced understanding of changes in fibrosis."},
{"detecting_subtle_yet_statistically_significant_changes_in_fibrosis_in_response_to_treatment": "Detecting subtle, yet statistically significant, changes in fibrosis in response to treatment refers to the capability of identifying minor variations in the degree of fibrosis that are meaningful and can be measured with statistical confidence. This is crucial in evaluating the effectiveness of treatments for conditions like liver disease, where changes in fibrosis can indicate disease progression or regression."},
{"the_continuous_scoring_system": "The continuous scoring system is a method designed to provide a more nuanced and detailed assessment of disease severity in MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) by offering a finer scale of measurement compared to traditional ordinal scoring systems."},
{"the_continuous_scoring_system__maps_each_mash_crn_grade_stage_to__a_bin_derived_from_the_ordinal_scoring_system": "The predicate \"maps each MASH CRN grade/stage to\" describes the process by which the continuous scoring system correlates or translates the discrete grades and stages assigned by the MASH CRN (Clinical Research Network) into a continuous scale, enhancing the granularity of disease assessment."},
{"a_bin_derived_from_the_ordinal_scoring_system": "A bin derived from the ordinal scoring system represents a range or interval on the continuous scale that corresponds to each specific grade or stage in the ordinal system, allowing for a direct comparison and integration of continuous and ordinal scoring methods in assessing MASH severity."},
{"the_ai_derived_continuous_mash_crn_scores": "The AI-derived continuous MASH CRN scores are a set of measurements generated by an artificial intelligence system designed to assess the severity of Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH) based on continuous scoring rather than traditional ordinal grading. These scores are intended to provide a more nuanced and sensitive evaluation of disease severity."},
{"the_ai_derived_continuous_mash_crn_scores__strongly_correlated_with__mean_scores_derived_by_a_panel_of_expert_pathologists": "Strongly correlated with indicates a robust and significant statistical relationship between the AI-derived continuous MASH CRN scores and the mean scores provided by expert pathologists. This correlation suggests that the AI system's assessments are reliable and closely align with expert evaluations."},
{"mean_scores_derived_by_a_panel_of_expert_pathologists": "Mean scores derived by a panel of expert pathologists represent the average assessments of disease severity made by a group of highly trained and experienced medical professionals specializing in histopathology. These scores are based on traditional ordinal grading systems and serve as a benchmark for evaluating the accuracy and reliability of new measurement methods, such as those derived from AI."},
{"aim_mash_based_continuous_mash_crn_fibrosis_score": "The AIM-MASH-based continuous MASH CRN fibrosis score is a method used to assess the severity of liver fibrosis in patients with metabolic associated steatohepatitis (MASH). It provides a continuous scale for evaluating the extent of fibrosis, which is crucial for monitoring disease progression and the effectiveness of treatments.|Here is another description: The AIM-MASH-based continuous MASH CRN fibrosis score is a scoring system designed to evaluate the severity of liver fibrosis in patients with MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). It provides a continuous measure of fibrosis, which is more sensitive to treatment-induced changes compared to traditional ordinal grading systems."},
{"aim_mash_based_continuous_mash_crn_fibrosis_score__is_more_sensitive_to__treatment_induced_changes_in_fibrosis": "The phrase \"is more sensitive to\" indicates that the AIM-MASH-based continuous MASH CRN fibrosis score has a greater ability to detect and measure subtle changes in liver fibrosis compared to other methods. This sensitivity is important for accurately assessing the impact of treatments on fibrosis."},
{"treatment_induced_changes_in_fibrosis": "Treatment-induced changes in fibrosis refer to the alterations in the degree of liver fibrosis that occur as a result of medical interventions aimed at reducing fibrosis. These changes are critical for evaluating the efficacy of treatments and for predicting patient outcomes in clinical trials."},
{"aim_mash_based_continuous_mash_crn_fibrosis_score__is_more_strongly_predictive_of__progression_to_cirrhosis_and_liver_related_complications": "The phrase \"is more strongly predictive of\" indicates a relationship where the AIM-MASH-based continuous MASH CRN fibrosis score has a greater ability to forecast or indicate the likelihood of certain outcomes, specifically the progression to cirrhosis and liver-related complications, compared to other scoring methods."},
{"progression_to_cirrhosis_and_liver_related_complications": "Progression to cirrhosis and liver-related complications refers to the advancement of liver disease to a state of severe scarring (cirrhosis) and the development of associated health issues such as liver failure, portal hypertension, and other complications that arise from chronic liver damage."},
{"continuous_fibrosis_score": "The continuous fibrosis score is a method used to quantify the severity of fibrosis in patients with MASH, providing a more sensitive measure of treatment-induced changes compared to traditional methods."},
{"continuous_fibrosis_score__enabled_the_definition_of__cutoffs_that_stratified_patients_with_mash_with_stage_3_f3_or_stage_4_f4_fibrosis_into_slow_versus_rapid_progressors": "The phrase \"enabled the definition of\" indicates that the continuous fibrosis score facilitated the establishment of specific criteria or thresholds."},
{"cutoffs_that_stratified_patients_with_mash_with_stage_3_f3_or_stage_4_f4_fibrosis_into_slow_versus_rapid_progressors": "Cutoffs that stratified patients with MASH with stage 3 (F3) or stage 4 (F4) fibrosis into slow versus rapid progressors are specific thresholds defined using the continuous fibrosis score to categorize patients based on the rate of their disease progression."},
{"continuous_scoring__may_offer_a_means_to_measure__subtle_changes_in_mash_that_are_more_realistically_achievable_on_the_timescale_of_clinical_trials": "The phrase \"may offer a means to measure\" indicates a potential capability or application of continuous scoring. It suggests that continuous scoring could be used as a tool or method for quantifying or assessing something, in this case, subtle changes in MASH."},
{"subtle_changes_in_mash_that_are_more_realistically_achievable_on_the_timescale_of_clinical_trials": "Subtle changes in MASH that are more realistically achievable on the timescale of clinical trials refer to minor, gradual alterations in the histologic features of MASH that can be observed and measured within the timeframe of a clinical study. These changes are considered more feasible to detect compared to significant, rapid progressions or regressions of the disease."},
{"continuous_scoring__presents_disease_progression_and_regression_on__a_linear_scale": "The phrase \"presents disease progression and regression on\" describes how continuous scoring represents changes in disease severity over time. It indicates that the scoring system is designed to show both the worsening (progression) and improvement (regression) of the disease."},
{"a_linear_scale": "A linear scale is a method of measurement that uses a straight line to represent values, with equal intervals between each value. In the context of continuous scoring for MASH, a linear scale is used to quantify the severity of fibrosis and other histologic features, allowing for precise and incremental changes in disease severity to be recorded.|Here is another description: A linear scale is a method of measurement where values increase or decrease uniformly along a straight line. In the context of medical assessments, it is often used to quantify the severity or progression of a disease."},
{"a_linear_scale__is_inconsistent_with__how_mash_progresses_and_regresses": "Being inconsistent with something means that there is a lack of harmony or agreement between two concepts or systems. In this case, the linear scale does not align well with the natural progression and regression patterns of MASH, a liver disease."},
{"how_mash_progresses_and_regresses": "The progression and regression of MASH, a liver disease, refers to the natural course of the disease as it worsens or improves over time. Unlike a linear scale, MASH does not progress or regress in a uniform, straight-line fashion, making direct comparisons using a linear scale potentially misleading."},
{"clinically_meaningful_thresholds_of_continuous_scoring": "Clinically meaningful thresholds of continuous scoring refer to specific, significant values on a continuous scale that indicate important changes or stages in a medical condition, such as the progression or regression of a disease. These thresholds are crucial for interpreting the results of continuous scoring systems in a clinical context."},
{"clinically_meaningful_thresholds_of_continuous_scoring__are_not_yet__known": "The phrase \"are not yet\" indicates a current state of uncertainty or lack of established knowledge regarding the clinically meaningful thresholds of continuous scoring. It suggests that while continuous scoring systems are being used or developed, the exact thresholds that signify clinically relevant changes have not been definitively determined or agreed upon."},
{"known": "The object \"known\" in this context refers to the established, recognized, or scientifically validated information about clinically meaningful thresholds of continuous scoring. It implies that there is a lack of definitive knowledge or consensus in the medical or scientific community about what these thresholds should be."},
{"changes_in_the_continuous_fibrosis_score": "The subject refers to the variations observed in the continuous fibrosis score, which is a metric used to quantify the extent of fibrosis in liver tissue, particularly in the context of MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"changes_in_the_continuous_fibrosis_score__must_be__defined_and_characterized": "The predicate indicates a necessity for the subject to undergo a process of definition and characterization. This means that the changes in the continuous fibrosis score need to be clearly described and understood in terms of what they represent and how they correlate with clinical outcomes."},
{"defined_and_characterized": "The object pertains to the process of defining and characterizing the changes in the continuous fibrosis score. This involves establishing what constitutes a significant change in the score, understanding the implications of these changes, and determining how they relate to patient outcomes and disease progression."},
{"a_sub_integer_reduction_in_fibrosis_score": "A sub-integer reduction in fibrosis score refers to a minor decrease in the numerical value assigned to the degree of liver fibrosis, as measured by a continuous scoring system. This slight reduction indicates a potential improvement in the fibrotic condition of the liver."},
{"a_sub_integer_reduction_in_fibrosis_score__is_associated_with__improved_clinical_outcome": "The phrase \"is associated with\" indicates a correlation or relationship between a sub-integer reduction in fibrosis score and an improved clinical outcome. This suggests that as the fibrosis score decreases slightly, there is a corresponding positive change in the patient's health status or disease progression."},
{"improved_clinical_outcome": "Improved clinical outcome refers to a positive change in the patient's health status or disease progression as a result of treatment or intervention. In the context of liver fibrosis, this could mean a reduction in symptoms, slower disease progression, or a decreased risk of complications such as cirrhosis or liver-related events."},
{"collaboration_between_ai_developers_and_pathologists_with_expertise_in_mash": "The collaboration between AI developers and pathologists with expertise in MASH represents a multidisciplinary effort aimed at improving the assessment and understanding of MASH through advanced technology and medical knowledge."},
{"collaboration_between_ai_developers_and_pathologists_with_expertise_in_mash__can_make__consequential_steps_toward_solving_the_problems_inherent_to_mash_histologic_assessment": "The phrase \"can make\" in this context signifies the potential and capability of the collaboration to achieve significant advancements. It implies that through combined efforts and expertise, meaningful progress can be realized in addressing complex medical challenges."},
{"consequential_steps_toward_solving_the_problems_inherent_to_mash_histologic_assessment": "Consequential steps toward solving the problems inherent to MASH histologic assessment refer to significant advancements or actions that directly contribute to overcoming the challenges and limitations associated with the evaluation and understanding of MASH through histological methods."},
{"aim_mash__is_being_evaluated_by__the_fda_and_the_ema_for_qualification_as_a_drug_discovery_tool_for_use_in_clinical_trials": "The phrase \"is being evaluated by\" indicates that AIM-MASH is currently under review and assessment by regulatory authorities to determine its suitability and effectiveness as a tool in drug discovery and clinical trials."},
{"the_fda_and_the_ema_for_qualification_as_a_drug_discovery_tool_for_use_in_clinical_trials": "The FDA (Food and Drug Administration) and the EMA (European Medicines Agency) are regulatory bodies responsible for the approval and oversight of medical products, including drugs and diagnostic tools. They are evaluating AIM-MASH to qualify it as a drug discovery tool for use in clinical trials, ensuring it meets the necessary standards for safety, efficacy, and reliability."},
{"aim_mash__is_well_poised_to_improve__the_accuracy_and_reproducibility_of_pathologists_evaluation_of_liver_biopsies_within_scalable_workflows": "The phrase \"is well poised to improve\" indicates that AIM-MASH has the potential to enhance or make better a specific aspect related to its application. In this context, it suggests that AIM-MASH is in a favorable position to bring about positive changes in the evaluation process of liver biopsies."},
{"the_accuracy_and_reproducibility_of_pathologists_evaluation_of_liver_biopsies_within_scalable_workflows": "The accuracy and reproducibility of pathologists\u2019 evaluation of liver biopsies within scalable workflows refers to the precision and consistency with which pathologists can assess liver biopsies, especially when using systems that can handle increased demand efficiently. This includes the ability to reliably detect and quantify changes in liver conditions, which is crucial for clinical trials and patient outcomes."},
{"aim_mash__has_potential_as__a_research_use_only_tool_to_investigate_histological_features_such_as_portal_inflammation_as_new_biomarkers_or_scoring_systems": "The phrase \"has potential as\" indicates that AIM-MASH is being considered or evaluated for a specific role or application. In this context, it suggests that AIM-MASH is being assessed for its capability to serve a particular function in research or clinical settings."},
{"a_research_use_only_tool_to_investigate_histological_features_such_as_portal_inflammation_as_new_biomarkers_or_scoring_systems": "A research use only tool to investigate histological features such as portal inflammation as new biomarkers or scoring systems refers to a tool intended exclusively for research purposes. This tool would be used to study specific histological characteristics, like portal inflammation, with the goal of identifying new biomarkers or developing novel scoring systems for assessing disease states or progression."},
{"online_content": "Online content encompasses a wide range of digital materials and resources available on the internet, including articles, videos, datasets, and supplementary information. It is designed for consumption or utilization through various online platforms and devices, often for educational, informational, or entertainment purposes. In specific contexts, such as academic or scientific publications, online content may include supplementary materials like methods, references, and additional data."},
{"online_content__includes__methods": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the subject, online content, comprises or contains certain elements. In this case, it indicates that online content is not limited to the main text of a publication but also encompasses additional resources and materials."},
{"methods": "Methods, in this context, refer to the detailed procedures and techniques used in the research or study being published. They are part of the supplementary information that is made available online to provide readers with a comprehensive understanding of the research process."},
{"online_content__includes__additional_references": "The term \"includes\" in this context signifies that online content is comprised of or contains certain elements. It indicates a relationship where the subject (online content) is made up of or features the object (additional references) as part of its composition."},
{"additional_references": "Additional references in the context of online content are supplementary materials or sources that provide further information or support for the primary content. These can include citations, links to other resources, or detailed explanations that enhance the understanding or credibility of the main content."},
{"online_content__includes__nature_portfolio_reporting_summaries": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the subject, online content, comprises or contains the object, Nature Portfolio reporting summaries, as part of its components. This relationship indicates that Nature Portfolio reporting summaries are one of the elements that make up the online content."},
{"nature_portfolio_reporting_summaries": "Nature Portfolio reporting summaries are concise documents that provide a summary of the key findings, methodologies, and significance of research articles published under the Nature Portfolio. These summaries aim to offer readers a quick overview of the research, enhancing accessibility and understanding of the scientific content."},
{"online_content__includes__source_data": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the subject, online content, comprises or contains certain elements within it. In this case, it indicates that online content is made up of various components, one of which is source data."},
{"source_data": "Source data, as an object in this triple, represents the original data collected or generated during the course of a research study. It is a critical component of online content for scientific publications, as it allows for the verification and reproducibility of the research findings."},
{"online_content__includes__extended_data": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the subject encompasses or contains the object within its scope. In this case, it indicates that online content comprises various elements, one of which is extended data."},
{"extended_data": "Extended data refers to additional datasets or information that supplement the primary content of a publication. It provides further detail and supports the research findings, often including raw data, additional analyses, or other relevant information not included in the main text.|Here is another description: Extended data refers to supplementary information or additional datasets that complement the main content of a research paper. It is often provided to offer further insights, detailed methodologies, or additional results that support the paper's conclusions."},
{"online_content__includes__supplementary_information": "The term \"includes\" in this context signifies that the online content is not limited to primary materials but also encompasses additional components that enhance the primary content. This can involve providing further details, explanations, or resources that support the main content."},
{"supplementary_information": "Supplementary information refers to additional data, explanations, or resources that are provided alongside the primary content to offer a more comprehensive understanding. This can include detailed methodologies, extended datasets, or further references that support the main content but are not essential to its core message."},
{"online_content__includes__acknowledgements": "The term \"includes\" in this context signifies that the online content is comprised of various elements. It indicates a comprehensive aggregation of different components that together form the complete online material available for users."},
{"acknowledgements": "Acknowledgements in online content are sections where the creators or contributors recognize and thank the individuals, organizations, or funding bodies that have supported or contributed to the work. This is a common practice in academic and research publications to give credit where it is due."},
{"online_content__includes__peer_review_information": "The term \"includes\" in this context signifies that the online content is comprised of various elements. It indicates a comprehensive aggregation of different types of information and resources that are part of the online content."},
{"peer_review_information": "Peer review information is a critical component of scholarly and scientific publications. It involves the evaluation of work by one or more experts with similar competences as the producers of the work. This process ensures the quality, validity, and reliability of the content before it is published."},
{"online_content__includes__details_of_author_contributions": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the subject encompasses or contains the specified object within its scope. In this case, it indicates that the online content comprises various elements, among which are the details of author contributions."},
{"details_of_author_contributions": "Details of author contributions refer to the specific information about the roles and involvement of each author in the creation and development of the published work. This can include tasks such as research design, data collection, analysis, writing, and revision, providing transparency and accountability in scholarly publications."},
{"online_content__includes__competing_interests": "The predicate \"includes\" signifies that the subject, online content, comprises or contains certain elements within its scope. In this context, it indicates that online content is not limited to a single type of material but encompasses a variety of components."},
{"competing_interests": "Competing interests refer to situations where individuals or entities involved in the creation, publication, or dissemination of online content have potential conflicts of interest. These conflicts may arise from financial, personal, or professional relationships that could influence the objectivity, integrity, or perceived credibility of the content."},
{"online_content__includes__statements_of_data_and_code_availability": "The term 'includes' in this context signifies that the online content encompasses or contains certain elements as part of its composition. It indicates that these elements are integral to the overall content being offered."},
{"statements_of_data_and_code_availability": "Statements of data and code availability are declarations or notices provided by authors or creators to inform users about the accessibility and usage of the data and code related to a particular work or publication. These statements are crucial for transparency, reproducibility, and further research."},
{"online_content__available_at__https_doi_org_10_1038_s41591_024_03172_7": "The predicate \"available at\" indicates the specific location or URL where the online content can be accessed. It serves as a direct link to the digital resources, allowing users to retrieve the information with ease."},
{"zhai_m_et_al": "Zhai, M. et al. refers to a group of researchers led by M. Zhai who conducted a study. Their work is part of a larger body of research focused on liver diseases, particularly those related to nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH).|Here is another description: Zhai, M. et al. refers to a group of researchers led by Zhai, who conducted a study on the incidence trends of liver cirrhosis caused by nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) using data from the Global Burden of Disease (GBD) study 2017.|Here is another description: Zhai, M. et al. refers to a group of authors led by M. Zhai who conducted a study on the incidence trends of liver cirrhosis caused by nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) using data from the Global Burden of Disease (GBD) study in 2017."},
