{"ml_model_development": "ML model development refers to the process of creating machine learning models designed to segment and grade histologic features of MASH, using datasets from various clinical trials."},
{"ml_model_development__uses__8_747_h_e_and_7_660_mt_wsis": "The predicate \"uses\" indicates the utilization of specific datasets in the development of ML models. In this context, it highlights the resources employed to train and validate the machine learning algorithms."},
{"8_747_h_e_and_7_660_mt_wsis": "The object \"8,747 H&E and 7,660 MT WSIs\" represents the whole slide images (WSIs) used in the training of ML models. These images, stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) and Masson's Trichrome (MT), were collected from six completed phase 2b and phase 3 MASH clinical trials."},
{"ml_models": "ML models refer to machine learning models developed for medical image analysis, specifically designed to process and interpret whole slide images (WSIs) of liver biopsies.|Here is another description: ML models refer to machine learning models that were developed and utilized in the context of analyzing Whole Slide Images (WSIs) for medical diagnostics. These models are designed to process and interpret complex visual data from histological slides.|Here is another description: ML models refer to machine learning models that were developed and utilized in the context of analyzing Whole Slide Images (WSIs) for medical purposes, specifically for detecting and quantifying features relevant to MASH histology and distinguishing between image/tissue artifacts and foreground versus background.|Here is another description: ML models refer to machine learning models that were developed and utilized in the context of analyzing Whole Slide Images (WSIs) for medical diagnostics. These models are designed to process and interpret complex visual data from histological slides."},
{"ml_models__are_for__segmenting_and_grading_staging_mash_histologic_features": "The phrase \"are for\" indicates the purpose or function of the ML models, which in this context is to perform specific tasks related to the analysis of liver biopsy images."},
{"segmenting_and_grading_staging_mash_histologic_features": "Segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features involve the identification, delineation, and assessment of the severity of histologic characteristics associated with Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH) in liver tissue images."},
{"samples": "Samples refer to the biological specimens, specifically liver biopsy whole-slide images (WSIs), collected from patients participating in various phase 2b and phase 3 MASH clinical trials. These samples are crucial for the development and validation of machine learning models aimed at segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features.|Here is another description: Samples refer to the tissue specimens collected from patients as part of various clinical trials focused on MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). These samples were processed according to the specific protocols of their respective trials."},
{"samples__were_collected_and_processed_according_to__the_protocols_of_their_respective_trials": "The phrase \"were collected and processed according to\" indicates the standardized procedures and protocols followed in the handling and preparation of the samples. This ensures consistency and reliability in the data used for machine learning model development and validation."},
{"the_protocols_of_their_respective_trials": "The protocols of their respective trials refer to the specific guidelines and procedures established for each clinical trial. These protocols dictate how samples are collected, stored, and processed to maintain data integrity and comparability across different trials and datasets."},
{"samples__were_scanned_on__leica_aperio_at2_or_scanscope_v1_scanners": "The predicate \"were scanned on\" indicates the process by which the collected samples were digitized. This scanning is a critical step in converting physical tissue samples into digital whole slide images (WSIs) for further analysis."},
{"leica_aperio_at2_or_scanscope_v1_scanners": "Leica Aperio AT2 and Scanscope V1 scanners are high-resolution digital pathology scanners used to capture images of tissue samples at various magnifications, such as \u00d720 or \u00d740. These scanners are essential tools in modern pathology for creating digital records of histological slides."},
{"model_performance_repeatability_assessments": "Model performance repeatability assessments refer to the evaluations conducted to ensure that the machine learning models used for segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features consistently produce reliable and reproducible results across different datasets and conditions."},
{"model_performance_repeatability_assessments__were_conducted_in__an_external_held_out_validation_dataset": "The phrase \"were conducted in\" indicates the location or dataset where the model performance repeatability assessments took place. It signifies that these assessments were performed within a specific, separate dataset to validate the model's performance."},
{"an_external_held_out_validation_dataset": "An external, held-out validation dataset is a separate set of data that was not used during the model training phase. It serves as a robust and unbiased means to test the model's performance, ensuring that the results are generalizable and not overfitted to the training data."},
{"external_validation_dataset": "The external validation dataset is a collection of whole slide images (WSIs) used to test the performance of machine learning models. It is separate from the dataset used for model training, ensuring an unbiased evaluation of the model's accuracy and repeatability.|Here is another description: The external validation dataset is a collection of whole slide images (WSIs) from liver biopsies used to test the performance of machine learning models. It is separate from the datasets used for model training, ensuring an unbiased evaluation of the model's accuracy and repeatability.|Here is another description: The external validation dataset is a collection of whole slide images (WSIs) from a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial, used specifically for assessing model performance repeatability and accuracy verification. It includes baseline and end-of-treatment biopsies reviewed by clinical pathologists for CRN grading and staging.|Here is another description: The external validation dataset is a collection of whole slide images (WSIs) from a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial, used specifically for assessing the performance and accuracy of machine learning models developed for segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features."},
{"external_validation_dataset__comprises__wsis_of_baseline_and_end_of_treatment_biopsies": "The predicate \"comprises\" indicates that the external validation dataset is made up of or includes specific elements. In this context, it signifies that the dataset is constituted by a particular set of whole slide images."},
{"wsis_of_baseline_and_end_of_treatment_biopsies": "The whole slide images (WSIs) of baseline and end-of-treatment biopsies are digital images of liver tissue samples taken at the beginning and end of a treatment period in a clinical trial. These images are used to assess changes in histologic features over the course of treatment."},
{"external_validation_dataset__is_from__a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The phrase \"is from\" indicates the origin or source of the external validation dataset. In this context, it specifies that the dataset used for validating the machine learning models is derived from a specific clinical trial."},
{"a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "A completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial is a specific study designed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of a drug or treatment in patients with metabolic associated steatohepatitis (MASH). This trial has been fully executed, and its data is utilized for external validation of machine learning models in the context of MASH histology."},
{"clinical_trial_methodology_and_results": "The clinical trial methodology and results refer to the detailed processes and outcomes of medical research studies designed to evaluate the safety and efficacy of new treatments or interventions for a specific condition, in this case, MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"clinical_trial_methodology_and_results__have_been_described_in__previous_publication_24": "The phrase \"have been described in\" indicates that the details of the clinical trial methodology and results were previously documented and presented in a specific publication, providing readers with a reference to obtain more comprehensive information."},
{"previous_publication_24": "The previous publication [24] is a scholarly article or report that contains a detailed account of the clinical trial's design, execution, and findings. This publication serves as a resource for those seeking in-depth knowledge about the trial's procedures and outcomes."},
{"digitized_wsis": "Digitized WSIs refer to whole slide images that have been converted into digital format. These images are high-resolution scans of histological slides, which are essential for detailed examination and analysis in medical research and diagnostics."},
{"digitized_wsis__were_reviewed_for__crn_grading_and_staging": "The phrase \"were reviewed for\" indicates that the digitized whole slide images underwent a detailed examination. This review was conducted to assess specific histopathological features and to assign appropriate grades and stages based on established medical criteria."},
{"crn_grading_and_staging": "CRN grading and staging are systematic methods used in histopathology to evaluate the severity and extent of liver disease. CRN, or the Clinical Research Network, provides a standardized framework for grading (assessing the severity of disease activity) and staging (evaluating the extent of fibrosis) in liver biopsies."},
{"clinical_pathologists": "Clinical pathologists are medical professionals specialized in the diagnosis of diseases through the examination of tissue samples and bodily fluids. They play a crucial role in interpreting histologic features and providing accurate diagnoses based on microscopic analysis."},
{"clinical_pathologists__have__extensive_experience_evaluating_mash_histology": "The predicate \"have\" in this context indicates possession or the acquisition of a particular skill or experience. It signifies that the clinical pathologists possess a certain level of expertise or familiarity with a specific area of histology."},
{"extensive_experience_evaluating_mash_histology": "Extensive experience evaluating MASH histology refers to a deep and broad understanding and practical knowledge in assessing the histologic features associated with Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). This experience is crucial for accurate diagnosis and grading of the disease based on tissue samples."},
{"median_scores_of_three_pathologists": "The median scores of three pathologists refer to the central values of the grading and staging assessments conducted by these experts on liver biopsy whole slide images (WSIs). These pathologists have extensive experience in evaluating MASH histology and their scores serve as a benchmark for assessing the performance of AI models."},
{"median_scores_of_three_pathologists__were_computed_for__all_wsis": "The predicate \"were computed for\" indicates the process by which the median scores from the three pathologists were determined. This computation was essential to establish a consistent and reliable reference standard for evaluating the accuracy and performance of machine learning models in analyzing WSIs."},
{"all_wsis": "All WSIs (whole slide images) represent the comprehensive set of digitized liver biopsy images that were reviewed and scored by the three pathologists. These images were part of various clinical trials and were used to train and validate machine learning models for segmenting and grading histologic features related to MASH."},
{"median_scores": "Median scores refer to the average values calculated from the assessments of three clinical pathologists who evaluated liver biopsy whole slide images (WSIs) for CRN grading and staging in a phase 2b MASH clinical trial."},
{"median_scores__were_used_as__a_reference_for_ai_model_performance": "The predicate \"were used as\" indicates that the median scores served a specific purpose or function in the context of the study. In this case, they were utilized as a benchmark or standard against which the performance of an AI model was measured."},
{"a_reference_for_ai_model_performance": "A reference for AI model performance signifies that the median scores of the three pathologists were employed as a comparative measure to assess how well the AI model performed in grading and staging MASH histologic features. This external validation dataset ensured that the model's accuracy could be fairly evaluated."},
{"external_validation_dataset__was_not_used_for__model_development": "The predicate \"was not used for\" indicates that the external validation dataset did not contribute to or influence the process of developing the machine learning models. It was kept separate to ensure an unbiased evaluation of the models' performance."},
{"model_development": "Model development refers to the process of creating and training machine learning models using a designated training dataset. In this context, the models were developed using a different set of data, distinct from the external validation dataset.|Here is another description: Model development refers to the phase in which the machine learning model is trained and refined using high-quality annotations, with the goal of improving its performance and accuracy."},
{"external_validation_dataset__served_as__a_robust_external_validation_dataset": "The phrase \"served as\" indicates the role or function that the external validation dataset played in the context of machine learning model development and testing. It highlights the dataset's purpose as a means to fairly evaluate the performance of the models without being part of the model's training process."},
{"a_robust_external_validation_dataset": "A robust external validation dataset refers to a dataset that is separate from the training data and is used to objectively evaluate the performance of machine learning models. It ensures that the models are tested on unseen data, providing a fair assessment of their accuracy and generalizability."},
{"clinical_utility_of_model_derived_features": "The clinical utility of model-derived features refers to the effectiveness and practical value of features extracted from machine learning models in a clinical setting, specifically in the context of assessing and managing MASH (metabolic associated steatohepatitis) histologic features."},
{"clinical_utility_of_model_derived_features__was_assessed_by__generated_ordinal_and_continuous_ml_features": "The phrase \"was assessed by\" indicates the method or means through which the clinical utility of model-derived features was evaluated. In this context, it suggests that the effectiveness of these features was measured or judged using a specific approach or set of criteria."},
{"generated_ordinal_and_continuous_ml_features": "Generated ordinal and continuous ML features refer to the types of machine learning-derived data used to evaluate the clinical utility. Ordinal features likely represent ranked or ordered data, while continuous features are numerical data that can take any value within a range, both of which were produced by machine learning algorithms applied to whole slide images (WSIs) from MASH clinical trials."},
{"wsis__are_from__four_completed_mash_clinical_trials": "The phrase \"are from\" indicates the origin or source of the WSIs, specifying that they are derived from specific clinical trials focused on MASH."},
{"four_completed_mash_clinical_trials": "Four completed MASH clinical trials refer to phase 2b and phase 3 trials that have been concluded, providing datasets for the development and validation of machine learning models in the study of MASH histology."},
{"atlas_phase_2b_clinical_trial": "The ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial is a completed medical study focused on evaluating the efficacy and safety of a treatment for a specific condition, involving the collection and analysis of whole slide images (WSIs) from liver biopsies."},
{"atlas_phase_2b_clinical_trial__enrolled__395_patients": "The predicate \"enrolled\" indicates the action of participants being registered and included in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial to receive the treatment and have their data collected for analysis."},
{"395_patients": "The 395 patients represent the total number of individuals who were officially registered and participated in the ATLAS phase 2b clinical trial, contributing their baseline and end-of-treatment WSIs for the study."},
{"stellar_3_clinical_trial": "The STELLAR-3 clinical trial is a completed phase 2b clinical trial focused on evaluating treatments for MASH (metabolic associated steatohepatitis). It is one of several trials used to train and validate machine learning models for segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features."},
{"stellar_3_clinical_trial__enrolled__725_patients": "The predicate \"enrolled\" indicates the action of participants being registered and included in the STELLAR-3 clinical trial. This process involves patients meeting specific criteria and agreeing to participate in the study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of the treatments being tested."},
{"725_patients": "The 725 patients represent the total number of individuals who were enrolled in the STELLAR-3 clinical trial. These patients provided baseline whole slide images (WSIs) that were used in the development and validation of machine learning models aimed at assessing MASH histologic features."},
{"stellar_4_clinical_trial": "The STELLAR-4 clinical trial is a completed phase 2b or phase 3 clinical trial focused on MASH (metabolic associated steatohepatitis), involving the collection and analysis of liver biopsy whole slide images (WSIs) to assess histologic features and disease severity."},
{"stellar_4_clinical_trial__enrolled__794_patients": "The predicate \"enrolled\" indicates the action of including or registering participants into the STELLAR-4 clinical trial. This process involves selecting patients based on specific criteria to participate in the trial and contribute to the dataset used for model training and performance assessment."},
{"794_patients": "The 794 patients represent the number of individuals who were enrolled in the STELLAR-4 clinical trial. These patients contributed their liver biopsy WSIs, which were used in the development and validation of machine learning models for segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features."},
{"eminence_trial": "The EMINENCE trial is a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial that provided a dataset of digitized whole slide images (WSIs) for model performance accuracy analysis and clinical utility assessment of machine learning models in segmenting and grading/staging MASH histologic features."},
{"eminence_trial__has__640_h_e_and_634_trichrome_wsis": "The predicate \"has\" in this context indicates the possession or inclusion of specific resources within the EMINENCE trial dataset, specifically a set number of H&E and trichrome WSIs that were used for machine learning model development and validation."},
{"board_certified_pathologists": "Board-certified pathologists are medical professionals who have completed specialized training and certification in pathology, the study of diseases and their causes. They are experts in diagnosing diseases by examining tissues, cells, and bodily fluids.|Here is another description: Board-certified pathologists are medical professionals who have completed specialized training and certification in pathology, the study of diseases. They possess expertise in evaluating histology, which involves examining tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases."},
{"board_certified_pathologists__have__experience_in_evaluating_mash_histology": "The predicate \"have\" in this context indicates possession or the acquisition of a specific skill or expertise. It signifies that the subject, board-certified pathologists, possess a particular capability or knowledge."},
{"experience_in_evaluating_mash_histology": "Experience in evaluating MASH histology refers to the practical knowledge and skill that pathologists have gained in assessing and interpreting the microscopic features of liver tissue affected by Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). This experience is crucial for accurate diagnosis and understanding of the disease."},
{"board_certified_pathologists__assisted_in__the_development_of_the_present_mash_ai": "The phrase \"assisted in\" indicates that the board-certified pathologists provided support and contributed their expertise to a collaborative effort. In this context, their involvement was crucial in guiding and informing the process."},
{"the_development_of_the_present_mash_ai": "The development of the present MASH AI refers to the creation and refinement of an artificial intelligence system designed to assist in the diagnosis and evaluation of MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis), a liver condition. This AI system likely incorporates machine learning algorithms and data analysis to provide accurate assessments."},
{"algorithms": "Algorithms in this context refer to computational methods or procedures designed to perform specific tasks, particularly in the analysis and interpretation of histologic images. These algorithms are integral to automating and enhancing the accuracy of medical image analysis.|Here is another description: Algorithms in this context refer to computational methods or models designed to analyze and interpret histologic features in medical images, specifically for the assessment of liver conditions."},
{"algorithms__provide__hand_drawn_annotations_of_key_histologic_features": "The predicate \"provide\" indicates the action performed by the subject (algorithms) in supplying or offering something\u2014in this case, hand-drawn annotations. This action is crucial for training and developing machine learning models, ensuring they have the necessary data to learn and make accurate predictions."},
{"hand_drawn_annotations_of_key_histologic_features": "Hand-drawn annotations of key histologic features are detailed markings or labels made by pathologists on histologic images to highlight important characteristics or areas of interest. These annotations serve as ground truth data for training image segmentation models, enabling the algorithms to recognize and differentiate various histologic structures and pathologies."},
{"algorithms__provide__slide_level_mash_crn_steatosis_grades_ballooning_grades_lobular_inflammation_grades_and_fibrosis_stages": "The predicate 'provide' indicates the action of supplying or furnishing necessary data or annotations that are essential for the training and development of image segmentation models and AI scoring models in the field of histopathology."},
{"slide_level_mash_crn_steatosis_grades_ballooning_grades_lobular_inflammation_grades_and_fibrosis_stages": "Slide-level MASH CRN steatosis grades, ballooning grades, lobular inflammation grades, and fibrosis stages are specific quantitative assessments of liver histology. These grades and stages are critical for diagnosing and evaluating the severity of liver diseases, such as nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH), and are used to train AI models to automate and standardize the grading process."},
{"pathologists__required_to_pass__a_proficiency_examination": "The requirement to pass a proficiency examination signifies a formal assessment process that pathologists must undergo to demonstrate their competency and accuracy in grading and staging specific medical conditions, ensuring they meet the necessary standards for contributing to model development."},
{"a_proficiency_examination": "A proficiency examination in this context is a test designed to evaluate the skills and knowledge of pathologists in accurately grading and staging medical conditions, such as MASH CRN grades/stages, to ensure their contributions are reliable and consistent with established standards."},
{"proficiency_examination": "The proficiency examination is a test required for pathologists to pass in order to provide slide-level MASH CRN grades/stages for model development. It ensures that the pathologists have the necessary skills and knowledge to accurately assess MASH cases."},
{"proficiency_examination__ask_to_provide__mash_crn_grades_stages_for_20_mash_cases": "The predicate \"ask to provide\" indicates that during the proficiency examination, pathologists are required to supply specific information or assessments. In this context, it means that pathologists must deliver MASH CRN grades/stages for a set number of MASH cases as part of the evaluation process."},
{"mash_crn_grades_stages_for_20_mash_cases": "The MASH CRN grades/stages for 20 MASH cases represent the specific assessments that pathologists are required to provide during the proficiency examination. These grades/stages are critical for training AI scoring models and ensuring the accuracy and reliability of the model development process."},
{"scores": "The scores refer to the MASH CRN grades/stages provided by pathologists for model development, which include steatosis grades, ballooning grades, lobular inflammation grades, and fibrosis stages."},
{"scores__compared_with__a_consensus_median_provided_by_three_mash_crn_pathologists": "The comparison involves evaluating the agreement between the scores provided by individual pathologists and a consensus median. This process helps in assessing the proficiency and consistency of the pathologists' grading."},
{"a_consensus_median_provided_by_three_mash_crn_pathologists": "The consensus median is a collective grading derived from the evaluations of three MASH CRN pathologists. It serves as a benchmark for comparing individual pathologists' scores to ensure accuracy and consistency in the grading process."},
{"agreement_statistics": "Agreement statistics refer to the measures of how closely the scores or grades provided by different pathologists align with a consensus median. These statistics are crucial for evaluating the consistency and reliability of pathologists' assessments.|Here is another description: Agreement statistics refer to the measures of how closely the pathologists' MASH CRN grades/stages align with a consensus median provided by three MASH CRN pathologists. These statistics are crucial for evaluating the proficiency and reliability of pathologists in grading MASH cases."},
{"agreement_statistics__reviewed_by__a_pathai_pathologist_with_expertise_in_mash": "The phrase \"reviewed by\" indicates that the agreement statistics underwent an evaluation process. This process involved a detailed examination to ensure the accuracy and reliability of the statistics before they were used to select pathologists for model development."},
{"a_pathai_pathologist_with_expertise_in_mash": "A PathAI pathologist with expertise in MASH is a specialist who has in-depth knowledge and experience in the field of MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis). This expert was responsible for reviewing the agreement statistics to ensure they met the necessary standards for pathologist selection in model development."},
{"agreement_statistics__leveraged_to__select_pathologists_for_assisting_in_model_development": "Leveraged to indicates that the agreement statistics were utilized as a criterion or tool to make informed decisions. In this context, the statistics were used to assess and select pathologists based on their performance in providing accurate MASH CRN grades/stages."},
{"select_pathologists_for_assisting_in_model_development": "Select pathologists for assisting in model development refers to the process of choosing qualified pathologists to contribute to the training and improvement of AI scoring models. These pathologists were selected based on their performance in a proficiency examination, as indicated by their agreement statistics."},
{"59_pathologists": "A group of 59 pathologists who were involved in the process of developing algorithms for medical image analysis, specifically for the assessment of histologic features in MASH cases."},
{"59_pathologists__provided__feature_annotations_for_model_training": "The action of supplying or furnishing, in this case, the pathologists provided essential data in the form of annotations which were critical for the training of image segmentation models and AI scoring models."},
{"feature_annotations_for_model_training": "Feature annotations are detailed markings or labels applied to specific characteristics or elements within an image. In the context of model training, these annotations serve as ground truth data that machine learning algorithms use to learn and improve their accuracy in identifying and categorizing histologic features in medical images."},
{"five_pathologists": "Five pathologists who were selected after passing a proficiency examination and demonstrating agreement with a consensus median provided by three MASH CRN pathologists."},
{"five_pathologists__provided__slide_level_mash_crn_grades_stages": "The action of supplying or furnishing something needed or requested, in this case, the provision of slide-level MASH CRN grades/stages by the pathologists for the development of AI scoring models."},
{"pathologists__provided__pixel_level_annotations_on_wsis": "The action 'provided' in this context refers to the contribution or supply of detailed, pixel-level annotations by pathologists. These annotations are essential for training machine learning models to recognize and quantify various histological features in digital images."},
{"pixel_level_annotations_on_wsis": "Pixel-level annotations on WSIs are detailed markings made by pathologists on digital images of tissue slides. These annotations highlight specific areas or features at the pixel level, providing precise data for machine learning algorithms to learn from and improve their accuracy in detecting and quantifying histological features."},
{"pathologists__used__a_proprietary_digital_wsi_viewer_interface": "The predicate 'used' indicates the action performed by the subject (pathologists) in relation to the object. Here, it describes how pathologists utilized a specific tool or interface to carry out their task of annotating WSIs."},
{"a_proprietary_digital_wsi_viewer_interface": "A proprietary digital WSI viewer interface is a specialized software tool designed for viewing and interacting with whole slide images. In this context, it is a digital platform that pathologists used to annotate and analyze WSIs, providing pixel-level annotations for various histologic features."},
{"pathologists__were_instructed_to__draw_or_annotate_over_the_h_e_and_mt_wsis": "The instruction given to pathologists to draw or annotate over the H&E and MT WSIs signifies a directive to mark specific areas or features on the images. This process is crucial for collecting examples of substances relevant to medical conditions, as well as distinguishing between artifacts and background, which are essential for the accurate training of machine learning models."},
{"draw_or_annotate_over_the_h_e_and_mt_wsis": "The H&E (Hematoxylin and Eosin) and MT (Masson's Trichrome) WSIs are types of stained tissue slides used in histology to visualize different tissue components and structures. Annotating these images involves marking areas that represent various histologic substances, artifacts, and background, which are critical for the detection and quantification of features relevant to medical diagnoses and research."},
{"pathologists__collected__examples_of_substances_relevant_to_mash": "The action 'collected' refers to the process where pathologists gathered and annotated examples of various substances on WSIs. This collection is crucial for training machine learning models to recognize and quantify features relevant to medical conditions like MASH."},
{"examples_of_substances_relevant_to_mash": "Examples of substances relevant to MASH include various histologic features and artifacts observed in tissue samples. These examples are essential for the machine learning models to learn and differentiate between different tissue characteristics and conditions."},
{"pathologists__collected__examples_of_artifact_and_background": "The predicate \"collected\" indicates the action performed by the pathologists, which involves gathering and compiling data. Specifically, they collected examples of various substances and features relevant to medical analysis, including artifacts and background elements from the WSIs."},
{"examples_of_artifact_and_background": "Examples of artifact and background refer to the various non-diagnostic elements present in whole slide images (WSIs). Artifacts can include staining irregularities or imaging defects, while background typically consists of non-relevant tissue areas or empty spaces. These examples are crucial for training machine learning models to distinguish between relevant histologic features and non-diagnostic elements."},
{"instructions": "Instructions refer to the guidelines or directions provided to pathologists for annotating whole slide images (WSIs) with pixel-level precision. These instructions are crucial for ensuring consistency and accuracy in the annotations, which are used to train machine learning models."},
{"instructions__are_included_in__supplementary_table_4_refs_33_36": "The phrase \"are included in\" indicates that the instructions are part of a larger document or resource. This relationship shows that the instructions can be found within a specific section or table of the referenced material."},
{"supplementary_table_4_refs_33_36": "Supplementary Table 4 (refs. 33\u201336) is a table within the document that contains references to additional information or sources. This table specifically includes the instructions given to pathologists for annotating WSIs, along with other relevant details and citations."},
{"total_feature_annotations_collected": "The total feature annotations collected refer to the comprehensive set of pixel-level annotations provided by pathologists on whole slide images (WSIs) using a proprietary digital viewer interface. These annotations were gathered to serve as training data for machine learning models aimed at detecting and quantifying various features relevant to image/tissue artifact, foreground versus background separation, and MASH histology."},
{"total_feature_annotations_collected__were__103_579": "The predicate \"were\" indicates the existence or occurrence of the total feature annotations collected. It signifies that the number of annotations has been determined and is being reported as a completed action."},
{"103_579": "The object \"103,579\" represents the total number of feature annotations that were collected. This figure quantifies the extensive dataset that pathologists have annotated on whole slide images, which is intended to be used for training machine learning models in the context of MASH histology and related tasks."},
{"103_579_feature_annotations": "The subject refers to the total number of feature annotations, which are detailed, pixel-level markings provided by pathologists on whole slide images (WSIs) to identify various substances and artifacts relevant to MASH histology."},
{"103_579_feature_annotations__were_used_to__train_the_ml_models": "The predicate indicates the action or process that was performed using the subject. In this case, it describes how the collected feature annotations were utilized in a specific manner to develop machine learning models."},
{"train_the_ml_models": "The object represents the machine learning models that were developed. These models were trained using the 103,579 feature annotations to enable them to detect and quantify features relevant to image/tissue artifact, foreground versus background separation, and MASH histology."},
{"ml_models__were_trained_to__detect_and_quantify_features_relevant_to_image_tissue_artifact": "The phrase \"were trained to\" indicates the process by which the ML models underwent training. This training involved learning from a large dataset of pixel-level annotations provided by pathologists, enabling the models to recognize and differentiate various features within the images."},
{"detect_and_quantify_features_relevant_to_image_tissue_artifact": "The object \"detect and quantify features relevant to image/tissue artifact\" describes the specific task the ML models were trained to perform. This involves identifying and measuring artifacts within tissue images, which are important for distinguishing between actual histologic features and distortions or errors in the imaging process."},
{"ml_models__were_trained_to__separate_foreground_versus_background": "The phrase 'were trained to' indicates the process by which the ML models underwent training. This training involved using a large dataset of pixel-level annotations provided by pathologists to teach the models how to recognize and differentiate between various elements within the WSIs, such as histologic substances, artifacts, and the separation of foreground from background."},
{"separate_foreground_versus_background": "The object 'separate foreground versus background' refers to the specific task that the ML models were trained to perform. This task is crucial in the analysis of WSIs as it involves distinguishing the areas of the image that contain relevant tissue information (foreground) from those that do not (background), which is essential for accurate histologic assessment and quantification."},
{"ml_models__were_trained_to__detect_mash_histology": "The phrase \"were trained to\" indicates the process by which the ML models underwent training. This training involved learning from a large dataset of pixel-level annotations provided by pathologists, enabling the models to recognize and differentiate various histological features and artifacts."},
{"detect_mash_histology": "Detecting MASH histology involves the identification and quantification of specific histological features associated with MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) within the tissue samples. This detection is crucial for diagnosing and understanding the severity of the condition through the analysis of WSIs."},
{"pathologists__evaluated__histologic_features": "The action of evaluating involves a thorough examination and assessment of specific characteristics or features. In this case, the pathologists are tasked with assessing particular aspects of liver tissue to determine the severity of a condition."},
{"histologic_features__evaluated_according_to__mas_and_crn_fibrosis_staging_rubrics": "Evaluated according to signifies that the assessment or analysis of histologic features is conducted based on specific criteria or standards, in this case, the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics."},
{"mas_and_crn_fibrosis_staging_rubrics": "MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics are standardized systems developed by Kleiner et al. for grading and staging the severity of liver fibrosis, providing a framework for pathologists to consistently evaluate tissue samples.|Here is another description: The MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics are standardized systems used by pathologists to evaluate and grade the severity of liver fibrosis in histologic slides."},
{"mas_and_crn_fibrosis_staging_rubrics__developed_by__kleiner_et_al": "The phrase \"developed by\" indicates the origin or creators of the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics, highlighting the individuals or group responsible for establishing these grading systems."},
{"kleiner_et_al": "Kleiner et al. refers to a group of researchers, led by Dr. Kleiner, who are credited with creating and publishing the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics, providing a structured approach for pathologists to assess liver fibrosis."},
{"cases": "The cases refer to the histologic features evaluated by pathologists according to the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics."},
{"cases__reviewed_and_scored_using__wsi_viewer": "The phrase'reviewed and scored using' indicates the process by which the cases were examined and assessed based on specific criteria provided by the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics."},
{"wsi_viewer": "The WSI viewer is a tool used by pathologists to review and score the cases according to the MAS and CRN fibrosis staging rubrics, facilitating the evaluation of histologic features."},
{"model_development_dataset": "The model development dataset is a collection of data used specifically for the purpose of developing a model. This dataset includes various Whole Slide Images (WSIs) from patients, which are critical for training and evaluating the model."},
{"model_development_dataset__was_split_into__training_70_validation_15_and_held_out_test_15_sets": "The predicate \"was split into\" indicates the process by which the model development dataset was divided into different subsets. This division is crucial for the model's training, validation, and testing phases, ensuring that the model is both trained effectively and evaluated accurately."},
{"training_70_validation_15_and_held_out_test_15_sets": "The object \"training (~70%), validation (~15%) and held-out test (\u223c15%) sets\" refers to the three distinct subsets into which the model development dataset was divided. The training set, comprising approximately 70% of the data, is used to teach the model. The validation set, about 15%, is used to tune the model's parameters. The held-out test set, also around 15%, is used to evaluate the model's performance on unseen data, ensuring its generalizability."},
{"dataset": "The dataset refers to the model development dataset that was divided into training, validation, and held-out test sets. This dataset is crucial for training and evaluating the performance of the model."},
{"dataset__was_split_at__the_patient_level": "The phrase \"was split at\" indicates the method or level at which the dataset was divided. In this context, it specifies that the division of the dataset was conducted according to a particular criterion, ensuring that all data from the same source are kept together during the split."},
{"the_patient_level": "The patient level refers to the criterion used for splitting the dataset. This means that all whole slide images (WSIs) from the same patient were allocated to the same development set, ensuring that data from individual patients were not mixed across different sets."},
{"all_wsis_from_the_same_patient": "All WSIs (Whole Slide Images) from the same patient refer to the collection of digital pathology images that are derived from a single individual's tissue samples. These images are crucial for detailed analysis and diagnosis in medical research."},
{"all_wsis_from_the_same_patient__were_allocated_to__the_same_development_set": "The phrase \"were allocated to\" indicates the process of assigning or distributing the WSIs to a specific category or group. In this context, it means that all WSIs from a single patient were grouped together during the dataset splitting process."},
{"the_same_development_set": "The same development set refers to a predefined subset of the model development dataset. This subset is used for either training, validation, or testing the machine learning model, ensuring that data from the same patient remains consistent across these phases."},
{"sets": "The subject refers to the model development dataset split into training, validation, and held-out test sets. These sets were created at the patient level, ensuring that all whole slide images (WSIs) from the same patient were allocated to the same development set."},
{"sets__were_balanced_for__key_mash_disease_severity_metrics": "The predicate indicates that the sets were carefully balanced to ensure that certain critical metrics were evenly distributed across them. This balancing was done to the greatest extent possible, despite the challenges posed by the clinical trial enrollment criteria."},
{"key_mash_disease_severity_metrics": "The object describes the key MASH disease severity metrics that were balanced across the sets. These metrics include MASH CRN steatosis grade, ballooning grade, lobular inflammation grade, and fibrosis stage, which are essential for assessing the severity of MASH disease."},
{"balancing_step": "The balancing step refers to the process of ensuring that the model development dataset is evenly distributed across different severity metrics of MASH disease, such as steatosis grade, ballooning grade, lobular inflammation grade, and fibrosis stage."},
{"balancing_step__was_occasionally__challenging": "The phrase \"was occasionally\" indicates that the balancing step did not always proceed smoothly and faced difficulties at times during the process of dataset splitting for model development."},
{"challenging": "The term \"challenging\" describes the difficulty encountered during the balancing step, which was influenced by the MASH clinical trial enrollment criteria that limited the patient population to specific ranges within the disease severity spectrum."},
{"challenge": "The challenge refers to the difficulty encountered during the balancing step of splitting the model development dataset into training, validation, and held-out test sets. This step aimed to ensure that the sets were balanced for key MASH disease severity metrics."},
{"challenge__was_due_to__mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria": "The phrase \"was due to\" indicates the cause-and-effect relationship between the challenge faced during the balancing step and the restrictive nature of the MASH clinical trial enrollment criteria. It highlights that the challenge arose as a direct result of these criteria."},
{"mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria": "The MASH clinical trial enrollment criteria are the specific requirements that patients must meet to be included in the MASH clinical trial. These criteria restricted the patient population to those fitting within certain ranges of the disease severity spectrum, which in turn made the balancing of the dataset more challenging."},
{"enrollment_criteria": "The enrollment criteria refer to the specific conditions or requirements that patients must meet to be included in a clinical trial or study. These criteria are designed to ensure that the study population is appropriate for the research objectives and that the results will be meaningful and applicable."},
{"enrollment_criteria__restricted__the_patient_population_to_those_fitting_within_specific_ranges_of_the_disease_severity_spectrum": "The predicate \"restricted\" indicates that the enrollment criteria have placed limitations or boundaries on the patient population that can participate in the study. This means that only patients who meet certain predefined conditions related to the severity of their disease are allowed to enroll."},
{"the_patient_population_to_those_fitting_within_specific_ranges_of_the_disease_severity_spectrum": "The patient population refers to the group of individuals who are eligible to participate in a clinical trial or study. In this context, the patient population is limited to those who fit within specific ranges of the disease severity spectrum, meaning that only patients with certain levels of disease severity, as measured by metrics like steatosis grade, ballooning grade, lobular inflammation grade, and fibrosis stage, are included in the study."},
{"held_out_test_set": "The held-out test set is a portion of the model development dataset, specifically allocated to evaluate the final performance of the model. It comprises approximately 15% of the total dataset and is kept separate from the training and validation sets to provide an unbiased assessment of the model's capabilities.|Here is another description: The held-out test set is a reserved subset of data that was not used during the model's training phase. It is utilized to evaluate the model's performance and generalization capabilities after the training process is complete."},
{"held_out_test_set__contains__a_dataset_from_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"contains\" signifies that the held-out test set includes or holds within it a specific dataset. This inclusion is crucial for ensuring that the model is tested on data it has not encountered during training or validation, thereby providing a true measure of its generalizability and performance."},
{"a_dataset_from_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The object, a dataset from an independent clinical trial, represents a collection of data gathered from a separate clinical study not involved in the initial model training or validation processes. This dataset is critical for assessing the model's performance in a real-world, unseen scenario, ensuring that the model's predictions are reliable and accurate when applied to new, independent data."},
{"algorithm_performance": "Algorithm performance refers to the effectiveness and efficiency of a developed model in processing and analyzing data, particularly in the context of medical imaging and diagnosis. In this scenario, it involves the model's ability to accurately assess MASH disease severity metrics across different patient datasets."},
{"algorithm_performance__is_meeting__acceptance_criteria_on_a_completely_held_out_patient_cohort_in_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The predicate \"is meeting\" indicates that the algorithm's performance is being evaluated against specific standards or criteria. It suggests a comparison between the observed performance of the algorithm and the expected or required performance levels, particularly in a controlled and independent setting."},
{"acceptance_criteria_on_a_completely_held_out_patient_cohort_in_an_independent_clinical_trial": "Acceptance criteria on a completely held-out patient cohort in an independent clinical trial represent the predefined standards or thresholds that the algorithm must achieve to be considered effective and reliable. This involves testing the algorithm on a new, unseen patient dataset from a separate clinical trial, ensuring that the results are unbiased and that the algorithm performs well in real-world conditions."},
{"test_data_leakage": "Test data leakage refers to a scenario where information from the test set inadvertently influences the model during the training phase, leading to overly optimistic performance estimates. In the context of medical model development, avoiding test data leakage is crucial to ensure the reliability and generalizability of the model's predictions."},
{"test_data_leakage__is_being_avoided__by_using_a_completely_held_out_patient_cohort_in_an_independent_clinical_trial": "The phrase \"is being avoided\" indicates a deliberate action taken to prevent test data leakage. In the context of dataset management for model development, this involves implementing strategies to ensure that the test set remains completely independent from the training and validation sets."},
{"by_using_a_completely_held_out_patient_cohort_in_an_independent_clinical_trial": "Using a completely held-out patient cohort in an independent clinical trial means that a separate group of patients, not included in the training or validation phases, is used to test the model. This approach ensures that the model\u2019s performance is evaluated on truly unseen data, thereby providing an accurate assessment of its generalizability and avoiding any form of test data leakage."},
{"ai_mash_algorithms": "The AI MASH algorithms refer to a set of artificial intelligence methodologies developed for medical image analysis, specifically within the context of convolutional neural networks (CNNs). These algorithms are designed to process and interpret complex medical images, such as whole slide images (WSIs), with high spatial precision."},
{"ai_mash_algorithms__were_trained_using__three_categories_of_tissue_compartment_segmentation_models": "The phrase \"were trained using\" indicates the process through which the AI MASH algorithms acquired their capabilities. This training involved exposure to specific datasets or models that allowed the algorithms to learn and improve their performance in segmenting tissue compartments within medical images."},
{"three_categories_of_tissue_compartment_segmentation_models": "The three categories of tissue compartment segmentation models represent distinct approaches or frameworks used to segment different types of tissue within medical images. These models are detailed in Supplementary Tables 7\u20139, providing insights into their objectives, inputs, outputs, and training parameters, which were essential for the training of the AI MASH algorithms."},
{"summaries_of_each_model": "The summaries of each model refer to concise descriptions or overviews of the various tissue compartment segmentation models utilized in the AI MASH algorithms. These summaries encapsulate the objectives and key features of each model."},
{"summaries_of_each_model__are_included_in__supplementary_table_6": "The phrase \"are included in\" indicates that the summaries of each model are part of a larger document or table. This inclusion provides readers with a comprehensive view of the models by placing the summaries within a specific section of the document."},
{"supplementary_table_6": "Supplementary Table 6 is a document or table that contains additional information supporting the main text. In this context, it includes summaries of each model and their respective objectives, offering readers a detailed yet concise overview of the models discussed in the main text."},
{"detailed_descriptions_of_each_model_s_purpose_input_and_output_as_well_as_training_parameters": "Detailed descriptions of each model\u2019s purpose, input and output, as well as training parameters, refer to comprehensive information about the specific goals, data requirements, results produced, and settings used for training various AI models in the context of tissue compartment segmentation."},
{"detailed_descriptions_of_each_model_s_purpose_input_and_output_as_well_as_training_parameters__can_be_found_in__supplementary_tables_7_9": "The phrase \"can be found in\" indicates the location where detailed information or data is stored or documented. In this context, it points to the specific supplementary tables where one can locate the comprehensive details about the models."},
{"supplementary_tables_7_9": "Supplementary Tables 7\u20139 are additional tables provided in the document that contain detailed information about the models\u2019 purpose, input and output, and training parameters. These tables offer a deeper insight into the technical aspects and configurations of the AI models used for tissue compartment segmentation."},
{"cnns": "CNNs, or Convolutional Neural Networks, are a category of deep learning models used in the AI MASH algorithms for tissue compartment segmentation. They are designed to process input data and produce segmented outputs with high spatial precision.|Here is another description: CNNs, or Convolutional Neural Networks, are a class of deep learning models particularly effective in image recognition and processing tasks. In the context of medical imaging, CNNs are utilized for their ability to learn and extract complex patterns from visual data, making them suitable for tasks like segmenting various tissue structures in histological images."},
{"cnns__allowed__massively_parallel_patch_wise_inference": "The predicate \"allowed\" signifies that the use of cloud-computing infrastructure enabled or made possible a specific capability or process within the CNNs' operation."},
{"massively_parallel_patch_wise_inference": "Massively parallel patch-wise inference refers to the efficient and exhaustive processing technique where every tissue-containing region of a Whole Slide Image (WSI) is analyzed in parallel, with a high level of spatial precision ranging from 4 to 8 pixels."},
{"cloud_computing_infrastructure": "Cloud-computing infrastructure refers to the hardware and software resources provided by cloud service providers that enable the execution of complex computational tasks. In the context of AI MASH algorithms, it facilitates the processing of large-scale data by distributing the workload across multiple servers."},
{"cloud_computing_infrastructure__allowed__efficiently_and_exhaustively_performed_on_every_tissue_containing_region_of_a_wsi": "The predicate \"allowed\" signifies that the cloud-computing infrastructure made it possible to perform certain operations. Specifically, it enabled the efficient and thorough execution of tasks on a comprehensive scale, ensuring that no part of the data was overlooked."},
{"efficiently_and_exhaustively_performed_on_every_tissue_containing_region_of_a_wsi": "Efficiently and exhaustively performing on every tissue-containing region of a WSI means that the cloud-computing infrastructure supported the detailed analysis of whole slide images (WSIs) at a high level of precision. This involved examining each tissue-containing area within the WSI to ensure accurate and comprehensive results."},
{"spatial_precision": "Spatial precision refers to the accuracy with which a system can determine the location of an object or feature within a given space. In the context of convolutional neural networks (CNNs) used for tissue compartment segmentation, spatial precision is crucial for ensuring that the segmentation is accurate and detailed."},
{"spatial_precision__is__4_8_pixels": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the specific measurement or value assigned to the spatial precision of the CNNs. It indicates that the spatial precision has been quantified and is being reported as a specific range."},
{"4_8_pixels": "The object \"4\u20138 pixels\" represents the range of spatial precision achieved by the CNNs in the tissue compartment segmentation process. This means that the CNNs can accurately segment tissue compartments within a margin of error of 4 to 8 pixels, which is a measure of the system's ability to localize features within the tissue images."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_differentiate__evaluable_liver_tissue_from_wsi_background": "The phrase \"was trained to differentiate\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process to learn how to distinguish between different types of image content. This training enables the CNN to accurately identify and separate specific elements within the images it analyzes."},
{"evaluable_liver_tissue_from_wsi_background": "\"Evaluable liver tissue from WSI background\" refers to the CNN's ability to distinguish between actual liver tissue that can be assessed for medical purposes and the background of Whole Slide Images (WSI). This differentiation is crucial for accurate medical analysis and diagnosis."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_differentiate__evaluable_tissue_from_artifacts_introduced_via_tissue_preparation_or_slide_scanning": "The phrase \"was trained to differentiate\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process to learn how to distinguish between different types of elements within the medical images it analyzes. This training enables the CNN to accurately identify and segment various components based on their visual characteristics."},
{"evaluable_tissue_from_artifacts_introduced_via_tissue_preparation_or_slide_scanning": "The objects the CNN was trained to differentiate include \"evaluable tissue\" and \"artifacts introduced via tissue preparation or slide scanning.\" Evaluable tissue refers to the actual liver tissue that can be assessed for diagnostic purposes, while artifacts are unwanted elements such as tissue folds or out-of-focus regions that can obscure or complicate the analysis of the tissue."},
{"cnn__was_developed_for__both_h_e_and_mt_stains": "The phrase \"was developed for\" indicates the purpose or specific application for which the CNN was designed and trained. It highlights the intended use case or functionality of the CNN within the given context."},
{"both_h_e_and_mt_stains": "Both H&E and MT stains refer to two different types of histological staining techniques commonly used in medical imaging. H&E stands for Hematoxylin and Eosin stain, while MT stands for Masson's Trichrome stain. These stains are used to highlight different tissue structures and components in histological samples."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_segment__cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features": "The phrase \"was trained to segment\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process to learn how to identify and delineate specific features within H&E stained histological images. This involves recognizing patterns and structures within the images that correspond to various histological features."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features": "Cardinal MASH H&E histologic features refer to key histological characteristics observed in liver tissue stained with H&E. These features include macrovesicular steatosis, hepatocellular ballooning, and lobular inflammation, which are important indicators of liver health and disease status.|Here is another description: Cardinal MASH H&E histologic features refer to the key microscopic characteristics observed in liver tissue stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) that are indicative of Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH). These features are critical for diagnosing and assessing the severity of liver conditions related to MASH.|Here is another description: Cardinal MASH H&E histologic features refer to key microscopic characteristics observed in liver tissue stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) as part of the MASH (Model for Assessing Steatohepatitis) scoring system. These features are critical for diagnosing and assessing the severity of liver conditions such as steatohepatitis.|Here is another description: Cardinal MASH H&E histologic features refer to key microscopic characteristics observed in liver tissue stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) as part of the MASH (Model for Assessing Steatohepatitis) scoring system. These features are critical for diagnosing and assessing the severity of liver conditions such as steatohepatitis."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features__include__macrovesicular_steatosis": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, cardinal MASH H&E histologic features, comprises or encompasses certain elements. In this context, it means that macrovesicular steatosis is one of the features that are part of the cardinal MASH H&E histologic features."},
{"macrovesicular_steatosis": "Macrovesicular steatosis is a histologic feature characterized by the presence of large fat vacuoles within hepatocytes, which displace the cell nucleus to the periphery. It is a significant indicator of liver steatosis and is one of the key features observed in the context of MASH."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features__include__hepatocellular_ballooning": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, cardinal MASH H&E histologic features, comprises or contains the object, hepatocellular ballooning, as one of its components. This relationship indicates that hepatocellular ballooning is a specific characteristic within the broader set of histologic features evaluated in the MASH system."},
{"hepatocellular_ballooning": "Hepatocellular ballooning is a histologic feature characterized by the swelling of liver cells (hepatocytes) due to the accumulation of fat or other substances. This condition is often associated with liver diseases such as non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) and is one of the key features assessed in the MASH scoring system for liver histology."},
{"cardinal_mash_h_e_histologic_features__include__lobular_inflammation": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject, cardinal MASH H&E histologic features, comprises or contains the object, lobular inflammation, as one of its components. This relationship indicates that lobular inflammation is a recognized and integral part of the histologic features assessed in the MASH scoring system."},
{"cnn__was_trained_to_segment__other_relevant_features": "The phrase \"was trained to segment\" indicates that the CNN underwent a training process to learn how to identify and delineate specific features within H&E stained whole slide images (WSIs). This training allows the CNN to accurately segment or separate different histological features from the background and from each other."},
{"other_relevant_features": "\"Other relevant features\" in this context refers to additional histological characteristics beyond the primary MASH H&E histologic features. These include portal inflammation, microvesicular steatosis, interface hepatitis, and normal hepatocytes. These features are important for a comprehensive analysis of liver histology in the context of conditions like steatohepatitis.|Here is another description: Other relevant features refer to additional histological characteristics beyond the primary MASH H&E histologic features that are important for a comprehensive analysis of liver tissue in H&E stained whole slide images (WSIs).|Here is another description: Other relevant features refer to additional histological characteristics observed in liver tissue samples stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E). These features are important for a comprehensive assessment of liver histology beyond the primary MASH H&E histologic features.|Here is another description: Other relevant features refer to additional histological characteristics observed in liver tissue samples stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E). These features are important for a comprehensive assessment of liver histology beyond the cardinal MASH H&E histologic features.|Here is another description: Other relevant features refer to additional histological characteristics observed in H&E stained whole slide images (WSIs) beyond the primary MASH features. These features provide a more comprehensive view of the tissue condition."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__portal_inflammation": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various histological characteristics, among which portal inflammation is specifically mentioned."},
{"portal_inflammation": "Portal inflammation is a histological feature characterized by the presence of inflammatory cells within the portal tracts of the liver, which can be an indicator of various liver conditions and is one of the features segmented by the CNN in H&E WSIs."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__microvesicular_steatosis": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various histological characteristics. It indicates that these features are part of a broader category of observations made during the histological examination of liver tissue."},
{"microvesicular_steatosis": "Microvesicular steatosis is a histological feature characterized by the presence of small fat droplets within hepatocytes. Unlike macrovesicular steatosis, where larger fat droplets displace the nucleus, microvesicular steatosis involves numerous small fat droplets that do not displace the nucleus, often indicating a different pathological process."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__interface_hepatitis": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various histological characteristics. It indicates that these features are part of a broader category of observations made in liver tissue samples."},
{"interface_hepatitis": "Interface hepatitis is a specific histological feature observed in liver tissue. It is characterized by inflammation at the interface between the portal tracts and the lobules of the liver, often associated with autoimmune hepatitis and other chronic liver diseases."},
{"other_relevant_features__include__normal_hepatocytes": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the set of other relevant features comprises various histological elements. It indicates a relationship where the subject encompasses a range of specific items or characteristics."},
{"normal_hepatocytes": "Normal hepatocytes are liver cells that do not exhibit signs of steatosis (fat accumulation) or ballooning (swelling due to injury). They represent the healthy, unaffected portion of the liver tissue in histological examinations.|Here is another description: Normal hepatocytes refer to liver cells that are functioning properly and do not show signs of damage or disease. In the context of histologic analysis, these are the hepatocytes that are not affected by conditions like steatosis or ballooning."},
{"normal_hepatocytes__are__hepatocytes_not_exhibiting_steatosis_or_ballooning": "The predicate \"are\" in this context signifies the state or condition of the subject, indicating that normal hepatocytes are a specific subset of hepatocytes that do not exhibit certain pathological features."},
{"hepatocytes_not_exhibiting_steatosis_or_ballooning": "Hepatocytes not exhibiting steatosis or ballooning are liver cells that remain unaffected by the accumulation of fat (steatosis) or the swelling (ballooning) typically associated with liver damage. These cells are considered normal in the context of histologic evaluation."},
{"cnns__were_trained_to_segment__large_intrahepatic_septal_and_subcapsular_regions_pathologic_fibrosis_bile_ducts_and_blood_vessels": "The phrase \"were trained to segment\" indicates that the CNNs underwent a training process specifically aimed at developing their capability to distinguish and delineate different components within medical images. This training involves learning from annotated examples provided by experts, allowing the CNNs to recognize and segment specific structures accurately."},
{"large_intrahepatic_septal_and_subcapsular_regions_pathologic_fibrosis_bile_ducts_and_blood_vessels": "The objects of segmentation in this context include \"large intrahepatic septal and subcapsular regions,\" which are areas within the liver that may contain both nonpathologic and pathologic fibrosis. Additionally, the CNNs were trained to segment \"bile ducts and blood vessels,\" which are critical structures within liver histology that need to be accurately identified for proper diagnosis and research."},
{"training_set_of_wsis": "The training set of Whole Slide Images (WSIs) consists of digital pathology images used for training machine learning models. These images are critical for developing algorithms that can segment and analyze different tissue structures and pathologies."},
{"training_set_of_wsis__was_shared_with__a_select_team_of_pathologists": "The phrase \"was shared with\" indicates the action of distributing the training set of WSIs to a specific group. This sharing is essential for collaborative annotation and model development, ensuring that expert input is incorporated into the machine learning process."},
{"a_select_team_of_pathologists": "A select team of pathologists refers to a group of medical professionals specialized in the study of diseases through the examination of tissues and cells. These experts were chosen for their expertise in assessing MASH histology, making their annotations crucial for the accurate training of segmentation models."},
{"pathologists__were_instructed_to_annotate__over_the_h_e_and_mt_wsis": "The predicate \"were instructed to annotate\" indicates that the pathologists were given specific instructions to mark or label certain features or areas within the whole slide images (WSIs). This annotation process is essential for training machine learning models to recognize and segment different histological structures accurately."},
{"over_the_h_e_and_mt_wsis": "The objects \"over the H&E and MT WSIs\" refer to the whole slide images stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) and Masson's Trichrome (MT). These staining techniques are commonly used in histopathology to highlight different tissue components and structures, aiding in the diagnosis and analysis of various conditions."},
{"primary_annotations": "Primary annotations refer to the initial set of annotations made by a select team of pathologists with expertise in the assessment of MASH histology. These annotations were made over H&E and MT WSIs as part of the iterative model development process for training segmentation models."},
{"primary_annotations__were_reviewed_by__internal_pathologists": "The predicate \"were reviewed by\" indicates a process where the primary annotations underwent a review. This review was essential to ensure the quality and accuracy of the annotations before they were used to train the segmentation models."},
{"internal_pathologists": "Internal pathologists are medical professionals within the organization who possess the expertise to review and validate the annotations made by the initial team of pathologists. Their role was crucial in removing any inappropriate or misunderstood annotations, thereby ensuring the integrity of the data used for model training.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are a specialized team within an organization who possess expertise in pathology. They are responsible for reviewing and validating annotations and data related to histology and medical imaging.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are medical professionals within an organization who specialize in the diagnosis of diseases by examining tissues and cells under a microscope. They play a crucial role in ensuring the accuracy and reliability of histopathological assessments.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are specialized medical professionals within an organization who review and analyze medical images, such as whole slide images (WSIs), to provide expert assessments and annotations. They play a crucial role in ensuring the accuracy and quality of annotations used for training machine learning models.|Here is another description: Internal pathologists are medical professionals within an organization who specialize in the diagnosis of diseases by examining tissues and cells under a microscope. They play a crucial role in ensuring the accuracy and reliability of histopathological assessments."},
{"internal_pathologists__removed_annotations_from__pathologists_who_had_misunderstood_instructions_or_provided_inappropriate_annotations": "The predicate \"removed annotations from\" indicates an action taken by the internal pathologists to eliminate or discard certain annotations. This action is based on the internal pathologists' assessment that the annotations were either made in error due to misunderstanding instructions or were deemed inappropriate for the intended use."},
{"pathologists_who_had_misunderstood_instructions_or_provided_inappropriate_annotations": "The object \"pathologists who had misunderstood instructions or provided inappropriate annotations\" refers to a subset of pathologists whose annotations were not suitable for use in the training and development of segmentation models. These annotations were either incorrect due to a misunderstanding of the instructions provided or were otherwise not appropriate for the specific requirements of the model training process."},
{"final_subset_of_primary_annotations": "The final subset of primary annotations refers to a curated selection of annotations made by pathologists on whole slide images (WSIs). These annotations were reviewed and refined to ensure accuracy and relevance, forming a high-quality dataset used for training machine learning models."},
{"final_subset_of_primary_annotations__was_used_to_train__the_first_iteration_of_all_three_segmentation_models": "The phrase \"was used to train\" indicates that the final subset of primary annotations served as the foundational dataset for the initial training phase of machine learning models. This process involved feeding the annotations into the models to enable them to learn and recognize patterns within the data."},
{"the_first_iteration_of_all_three_segmentation_models": "The first iteration of all three segmentation models refers to the initial versions of machine learning models designed to segment different regions within whole slide images, such as nonpathologic fibrosis, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels. These models were trained using the refined annotations to achieve an initial level of performance."},
{"internal_pathologists__reviewed__model_derived_segmentation_overlays": "The predicate \"reviewed\" indicates that the internal pathologists examined and assessed the model-derived segmentation overlays to evaluate their accuracy and identify any areas of failure or improvement."},
{"model_derived_segmentation_overlays": "Model-derived segmentation overlays are visual representations generated by machine learning models that highlight specific regions or features within medical images, such as histopathological slides. These overlays are used to assist pathologists in identifying and analyzing various tissue structures and pathologies."},
{"internal_pathologists__identified__areas_of_model_failure": "The predicate \"identified\" signifies that the internal pathologists examined the model-derived segmentation overlays and pinpointed specific regions where the model's performance was inadequate. This process involved a detailed review to determine where the model failed to accurately segment the histologic features."},
{"areas_of_model_failure": "Areas of model failure refer to specific regions within the whole slide images where the convolutional neural network (CNN) models did not perform as expected. These regions may include misclassifications or inaccuracies in segmenting pathologic and nonpathologic features, such as fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"internal_pathologists__requested__correction_annotations_for_substances_for_which_the_model_was_performing_poorly": "The predicate \"requested\" indicates that the internal pathologists made a formal or informal demand for something they deemed necessary to improve the performance of the segmentation models. This action suggests a proactive approach to enhancing model accuracy and reliability."},
{"correction_annotations_for_substances_for_which_the_model_was_performing_poorly": "Correction annotations for substances refer to additional, refined markings or labels provided by expert pathologists to correct or improve the initial annotations. These annotations are specifically targeted at areas where the model was not performing well, aiming to provide more accurate examples of histologic features to enhance model training."},
{"trained_cnn_models": "Trained CNN models refer to convolutional neural networks that have undergone a training process to learn how to segment specific regions in medical images, such as large intrahepatic septal and subcapsular regions, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"trained_cnn_models__were_deployed_on__the_validation_set_of_images": "The phrase \"were deployed on\" indicates that the trained CNN models were applied or utilized on a specific dataset to perform their trained task, which in this context is the segmentation of various histologic features in images."},
{"the_validation_set_of_images": "The validation set of images is a collection of medical images that were not used during the training phase of the CNN models. These images are used to evaluate the performance and generalizability of the trained models, providing a quantitative measure of how well the models can segment the desired features in unseen data."},
{"correction_annotations": "Correction annotations refer to the additional annotations gathered to improve the performance of machine learning models, specifically convolutional neural networks (CNNs), in segmenting histologic features in medical images. These annotations are crucial for refining the model's accuracy in identifying and categorizing different tissue types and pathologies."},
{"correction_annotations__were_collected_from__expert_pathologists": "The phrase \"were collected from\" indicates the process by which correction annotations were obtained. This involved expert pathologists reviewing the initial segmentation results produced by the CNN models and providing additional, more accurate annotations where the model had performed poorly."},
{"model_training": "Model training refers to the process of training convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to segment various regions in whole slide images (WSIs), such as nonpathologic fibrosis, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels. This training is part of an iterative development process aimed at improving the accuracy and reliability of the segmentation models.|Here is another description: Model training is the process where a machine learning model learns from data to improve its performance. In this context, it involved using the high-quality annotations to teach the model how to recognize and classify different elements within the data.|Here is another description: Model training refers to the process of using data to teach a machine learning model to make predictions or decisions. In this context, the model was trained using a dataset separate from the images obtained in the clinical trial, ensuring that the model\u2019s performance could be objectively evaluated on new, unseen data."},
{"model_training__was_monitored_and_hyperparameters_were_adjusted_based_on__the_model_s_performance_on_pathologist_annotations_from_the_held_out_validation_set": "The predicate \"was monitored, and hyperparameters were adjusted based on\" indicates that during the model training process, the performance of the CNNs was closely observed. Adjustments to the hyperparameters were made in response to how well the models performed on annotations provided by pathologists, ensuring continuous improvement and optimization of the models."},
{"the_model_s_performance_on_pathologist_annotations_from_the_held_out_validation_set": "The object \"the model\u2019s performance on pathologist annotations from the held-out validation set\" refers to the evaluation metric used to assess the effectiveness of the CNN models. Pathologist annotations serve as ground truth, and the validation set provides an unbiased assessment of the model's ability to generalize and perform accurately on unseen data."},
{"artifact_h_e_tissue_and_mt_tissue_cnns": "The subject refers to convolutional neural networks (CNNs) specifically designed for analyzing H&E (Hematoxylin and Eosin) stained tissue and MT (Masson's Trichrome) stained tissue. These CNNs are specialized tools used in medical imaging to segment and analyze different tissue types and structures within whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"artifact_h_e_tissue_and_mt_tissue_cnns__were_trained_using__pathologist_annotations_comprising_8_12_blocks_of_compound_layers_with_a_topology_inspired_by_residual_networks_and_inception_networks_with_a_softmax_loss": "The predicate indicates the process by which the CNNs were developed and refined. It involves training these networks using annotations provided by pathologists. This training process is iterative, involving multiple stages of annotation review, model training, and performance evaluation to ensure the CNNs accurately segment and analyze tissue images."},
{"pathologist_annotations_comprising_8_12_blocks_of_compound_layers_with_a_topology_inspired_by_residual_networks_and_inception_networks_with_a_softmax_loss": "The object describes the detailed methodology used for training the CNNs. It includes pathologist annotations that consist of 8\u201312 blocks of compound layers. These layers are structured with a topology inspired by residual networks and inception networks, and the training utilizes a softmax loss function to optimize the model's performance."},
{"a_pipeline_of_image_augmentations": "A pipeline of image augmentations refers to a series of preprocessing steps applied to images to enhance the robustness and generalization of machine learning models. These augmentations typically include transformations such as random cropping, rotation, color perturbations, and noise addition, which help create a more diverse training dataset."},
{"a_pipeline_of_image_augmentations__was_used_during_training_for__all_cnn_segmentation_models": "The phrase \"was used during training for\" indicates that the pipeline of image augmentations was an integral part of the training process for the CNN segmentation models. This means that the augmentations were applied to the training images to improve the models' ability to generalize and perform well on unseen data."},
{"all_cnn_segmentation_models": "All CNN segmentation models in this context refer to a set of convolutional neural network models designed to segment different regions within medical images, specifically Whole Slide Images (WSIs) stained with Masson's Trichrome (MT). These models were trained to identify and delineate areas such as nonpathologic fibrosis, pathologic fibrosis, bile ducts, and blood vessels."},
{"cnn_models_learning": "CNN models\u2019 learning refers to the process by which convolutional neural networks are trained to recognize and segment specific features within medical images, such as those used in the assessment of MASH histology."},
{"cnn_models_learning__was_augmented_using__distributionally_robust_optimization_to_achieve_model_generalization_across_multiple_clinical_and_research_contexts_and_augmentations": "The phrase \"was augmented using\" indicates that additional techniques or methods were incorporated into the training process of the CNN models to enhance their performance and robustness."},
{"distributionally_robust_optimization_to_achieve_model_generalization_across_multiple_clinical_and_research_contexts_and_augmentations": "Distributionally robust optimization is a method used to improve the generalization capabilities of machine learning models across various contexts and data augmentations, ensuring that the models perform well under different conditions and with varied input data."},
{"augmentations": "Augmentations refer to a set of techniques applied to training data to improve the performance and generalization of machine learning models. In the context of convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for medical image segmentation, these augmentations help the model learn more robust features by exposing it to varied and perturbed versions of the input data."},
{"augmentations__included__random_crops_random_rotation_color_perturbations_and_random_noise_addition": "The predicate \"included\" signifies that the specified augmentations were part of the training pipeline for the CNN models. These augmentations were systematically applied to the input patches to create a diverse set of training examples, thereby enhancing the model's ability to generalize across different clinical and research contexts."},
{"random_crops_random_rotation_color_perturbations_and_random_noise_addition": "The object \"random crops, random rotation, color perturbations and random noise addition\" describes specific types of augmentations applied during the training of CNN models. Random crops involve extracting smaller regions from the images, random rotation changes the orientation of the images, color perturbations alter the hue, saturation, and brightness, and random noise addition introduces Gaussian or binary-uniform noise to the images. These techniques help the model become more resilient to variations in the input data."},
{"input_and_feature_level_mix_up": "Input- and feature-level mix-up refers to a technique used in machine learning, particularly in the training of convolutional neural networks (CNNs), to enhance the robustness and generalization capabilities of the model. It involves combining inputs and features in a way that creates new training examples, which helps the model learn more effectively from the data."},
{"input_and_feature_level_mix_up__was_employed__as_a_regularization_technique_to_further_increase_model_robustness": "The phrase \"was employed\" indicates that input- and feature-level mix-up was utilized or applied as part of the training process for the CNN models. This employment suggests that the technique was integrated into the model's learning pipeline to improve its performance."},
{"as_a_regularization_technique_to_further_increase_model_robustness": "As a regularization technique, input- and feature-level mix-up was used to further increase model robustness. Regularization techniques are methods used in machine learning to prevent overfitting, where a model performs well on training data but poorly on unseen data. By increasing model robustness, the technique helps ensure that the model generalizes better to new, unseen data."},
{"images__were__zero_mean_normalized": "The predicate \"were\" indicates a past action or state. In this context, it describes the process that the images underwent as part of the preprocessing steps before being used to train the CNN models. Specifically, it denotes that the images have been subjected to a certain transformation."},
{"zero_mean_normalized": "Zero-mean normalization is a preprocessing technique applied to images to standardize their pixel values. In this context, it involves reordering the color channels of the RGB images to BGR and then subtracting 128 from each pixel value, resulting in a range of [-128, 127]. This normalization helps in reducing the effect of varying illumination and enhances the model's ability to generalize across different images."},
{"cnn_model_predictions": "CNN model predictions refer to the outputs generated by a Convolutional Neural Network, which in this context are used to identify and quantify histologic features in medical images."},
{"cnn_model_predictions__were_used_in_combination_with__mash_crn_scores_from_eight_pathologists": "The phrase \"were used in combination with\" indicates that the CNN model predictions were integrated or merged with another set of data or scores to enhance the overall analysis or prediction process."},
{"mash_crn_scores_from_eight_pathologists": "MASH CRN scores from eight pathologists represent the assessments made by a group of medical experts on the severity of liver conditions, specifically using the MASH Clinical Research Network scoring system, which evaluates aspects like steatosis, lobular inflammation, ballooning, and fibrosis.|Here is another description: MASH CRN scores from eight pathologists represent the dataset used to train the GNNs. These scores are evaluations of histologic features related to MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis) provided by experienced pathologists. Using scores from multiple pathologists helps mitigate individual biases and provides a more robust training dataset for the GNNs."},
{"gnns__were_trained_to_predict__ordinal_mash_crn_grades_for_steatosis_lobular_inflammation_ballooning_and_fibrosis": "The phrase 'were trained to predict' indicates that the GNNs underwent a training process where they learned to make predictions based on input data. This training likely involved adjusting the network's parameters to minimize prediction errors."},
{"ordinal_mash_crn_grades_for_steatosis_lobular_inflammation_ballooning_and_fibrosis": "Ordinal MASH CRN grades for steatosis, lobular inflammation, ballooning, and fibrosis are specific diagnostic categories used in pathology to assess the severity of liver conditions. These grades are ordinal, meaning they represent a ranked scale of severity rather than absolute measurements."},
{"gnn_methodology": "GNN methodology refers to the use of Graph Neural Networks, a type of machine learning model designed to work with data that can be represented as graphs. This methodology is particularly effective for analyzing complex structures like human tissues, where relationships and patterns can be better understood through graph representations.|Here is another description: GNN methodology refers to the use of Graph Neural Networks, a type of machine learning model designed to work with data that has a graph-like structure. It is particularly effective in scenarios where relationships and connections between data points are crucial."},
{"gnn_methodology__was_leveraged_for__the_present_development_effort": "The phrase \"was leveraged for\" indicates that GNN methodology was utilized or applied as a tool or technique in the process of the present development effort. This suggests that the unique capabilities of GNNs were specifically chosen to address the challenges or requirements of the development task at hand."},
{"the_present_development_effort": "The present development effort refers to the ongoing project or initiative aimed at creating or improving a system, product, or process. In this context, it involves the application of GNNs to predict ordinal MASH CRN grades for various histologic features in human tissues, leveraging the structural topologies of these tissues."},
{"gnn_methodology__is_well_suited_to__data_types_that_can_be_modeled_by_a_graph_structure": "The phrase \"is well suited to\" indicates that GNN methodology is particularly effective or appropriate for handling certain types of data. This suitability arises from the inherent design of GNNs to process and analyze data with complex, interconnected structures."},
{"data_types_that_can_be_modeled_by_a_graph_structure": "Data types that can be modeled by a graph structure include information that is naturally organized into nodes and edges, representing entities and their relationships. Examples include social networks, molecular structures, and, as mentioned in the context, human tissues with organized structural topologies like fibrosis architecture."},
{"human_tissues": "Human tissues refer to the various types of cells and structures that make up the human body. These tissues are complex and diverse, each serving specific functions necessary for the body's overall health and operation."},
{"human_tissues__are_organized_into__structural_topologies_including_fibrosis_architecture": "The phrase \"are organized into\" describes the systematic arrangement or structuring of human tissues. This organization is crucial for the proper functioning of tissues, as it ensures that each type of tissue is positioned and interacts in a way that supports the body's needs."},
{"structural_topologies_including_fibrosis_architecture": "Structural topologies, including fibrosis architecture, refer to the specific patterns and arrangements of tissues within the body. Fibrosis architecture, in particular, involves the formation of excess fibrous connective tissue in an organ or tissue, often as a reparative or reactive process."},
{"superpixels": "Superpixels are small, perceptually meaningful atomic regions in an image that are used to simplify complex images by grouping pixels with similar characteristics. In the context of image processing and computer vision, superpixels help in reducing the complexity of an image by clustering similar pixels together."},
{"superpixels__construct__nodes_in_the_graph": "The predicate \"construct\" in this context refers to the process of building or forming the nodes in a graph using superpixels. This involves converting pixel-level data into a higher-level representation where each node represents a cluster of superpixels."},
{"nodes_in_the_graph": "Nodes in the graph represent individual superpixel clusters that have been formed from the original pixel-level predictions. Each node is characterized by a set of features derived from CNN predictions, including spatial, topological, and logit-related features, which are used to describe the biological and clinical relevance of the clusters."},
{"hundreds_of_thousands_of_pixel_level_predictions": "Hundreds of thousands of pixel-level predictions refer to the detailed, granular data points generated by a convolutional neural network (CNN) when analyzing whole slide images (WSIs). Each prediction corresponds to a specific pixel within the image, indicating the presence or absence of certain histologic features."},
{"hundreds_of_thousands_of_pixel_level_predictions__reduce_into__thousands_of_superpixel_clusters": "The process of reducing involves consolidating and simplifying the vast amount of pixel-level data into a more manageable and interpretable form. This reduction helps in creating a higher-level representation of the image data, making it easier to analyze and understand."},
{"thousands_of_superpixel_clusters": "Thousands of superpixel clusters are the result of aggregating similar pixel-level predictions into larger, coherent groups. These clusters represent regions within the WSIs that share common histologic features, thus providing a more organized and structured view of the image data."},
{"wsi_regions_predicted_as_background_or_artifact": "WSI regions predicted as background or artifact refer to specific areas within Whole Slide Images (WSIs) that have been identified by a predictive model as either irrelevant background or as artifacts, which are unwanted distortions or anomalies in the image."},
{"wsi_regions_predicted_as_background_or_artifact__excluded_during__clustering": "The predicate \"excluded during\" indicates that these identified WSI regions, deemed as background or artifacts, were intentionally left out or not included in a subsequent process. In this context, it means these regions were not considered in the clustering phase of the analysis."},
{"clustering": "Clustering in this context refers to a process where similar data points are grouped together based on certain features or characteristics. In the analysis of WSIs, clustering is used to organize superpixels into coherent groups, which can then be analyzed to extract meaningful patterns or features relevant to the study of histologic features."},
{"directed_edges": "Directed edges in the context of graph theory and machine learning refer to connections that have a specific direction, indicating a one-way relationship between nodes. In this scenario, directed edges are utilized to link nodes in a graph constructed from superpixel clusters derived from whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"directed_edges__placed_between__each_node_and_its_five_nearest_neighboring_nodes": "The predicate \"placed between\" describes the action of establishing directed edges as connections. These edges are specifically positioned to link each node with its five nearest neighboring nodes, based on spatial proximity determined by the _k_-nearest neighbor algorithm."},
{"each_node_and_its_five_nearest_neighboring_nodes": "Each node in the graph represents a superpixel cluster, and the object \"each node and its five nearest neighboring nodes\" refers to the specific set of nodes that are directly connected to a given node. These connections are established to reflect the spatial relationships and proximity between superpixel clusters in the WSIs."},
{"k_nearest_neighbor_algorithm": "The _k_-nearest neighbor algorithm is a method used in machine learning to classify data points based on the majority class of their nearest neighbors. In the context of graph construction, it is utilized to determine the most similar nodes to a given node."},
{"k_nearest_neighbor_algorithm__used_for__placing_directed_edges": "The predicate \"used for\" indicates the purpose or application of the _k_-nearest neighbor algorithm in this context. It specifies that the algorithm is employed to establish connections or relationships between nodes in a graph."},
{"placing_directed_edges": "Placing directed edges refers to the process of creating connections between nodes in a graph where the direction of the edge is significant. In this context, directed edges are placed between each node and its five nearest neighboring nodes as determined by the _k_-nearest neighbor algorithm."},
{"each_graph_node": "Each graph node in the context refers to individual superpixel clusters within a whole slide image (WSI), which are derived from pixel-level predictions. These nodes are essential components of the graph structure used for analyzing and interpreting the WSI."},
{"each_graph_node__represented_by__three_classes_of_features": "The predicate \"represented by\" indicates that each graph node is characterized or defined by a specific set of attributes or features. In this context, it means that the nodes are described using certain classes of features extracted from the data."},
{"three_classes_of_features": "The three classes of features used to represent each graph node include spatial features (mean and standard deviation of coordinates), topological features (area, perimeter, and convexity), and logit-related features (mean and standard deviation of logits for CNN-generated overlays). These features are generated from previously trained convolutional neural network (CNN) predictions and are predefined as biologically relevant classes with known clinical significance.|Here is another description: The three classes of features refer to distinct categories of data attributes derived from the analysis of superpixel clusters in whole slide images (WSIs). These features are crucial for representing the characteristics of each graph node in a machine learning model designed to assess histologic features."},
{"three_classes_of_features__generated_from__previously_trained_cnn_predictions": "The predicate \"generated from\" indicates the process by which the three classes of features were created. In this context, it signifies that these features were produced using the outputs of a previously trained Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), which had made predictions on the WSIs."},
{"previously_trained_cnn_predictions": "Previously trained CNN predictions are the outputs from a Convolutional Neural Network that has been trained beforehand to recognize and classify various histologic features within whole slide images. These predictions serve as the basis for generating the three classes of features used in the graph nodes."},
{"cnn_predictions": "CNN predictions refer to the outputs generated by Convolutional Neural Networks, which are a class of deep learning algorithms designed to analyze visual imagery. In this context, CNN predictions are used to identify and classify various histologic features within whole slide images (WSIs)."},
{"cnn_predictions__predefined_as__biological_classes_of_known_clinical_relevance": "The predicate \"predefined as\" indicates that the CNN predictions have been categorized or labeled according to a set of pre-established criteria. These criteria are specifically tailored to align with biological classes that have known clinical relevance, ensuring that the predictions are meaningful and applicable in a medical context."},
{"biological_classes_of_known_clinical_relevance": "Biological classes of known clinical relevance are categories or groups that have been determined to have significant implications for patient diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis. In this context, these classes are used to organize and interpret the CNN predictions, ensuring that the identified histologic features are clinically meaningful and can inform medical decisions."},
{"spatial_features": "Spatial features refer to the characteristics that describe the arrangement and distribution of elements within a defined space. In the context of image analysis and machine learning, spatial features are critical for understanding the positional relationships between different components of an image."},
{"spatial_features__included__mean_and_standard_deviation_of_x_y_coordinates": "The predicate \"included\" signifies that certain elements or characteristics are part of a larger set or category. In this context, it indicates that specific statistical measures are incorporated as part of the spatial features used to represent graph nodes."},
{"mean_and_standard_deviation_of_x_y_coordinates": "The mean and standard deviation of (_x_, _y_) coordinates are statistical measures that describe the central tendency and dispersion of the coordinates within a spatial dataset. These measures help in quantifying the spread and average position of elements within the spatial features, providing valuable information for analysis."},
{"topological_features": "Topological features refer to the geometric and structural properties of a cluster, which are essential for understanding the spatial arrangement and shape characteristics within a dataset."},
{"topological_features__included__area_perimeter_and_convexity_of_the_cluster": "The term 'included' indicates that the topological features are part of a broader set of characteristics used to describe and analyze the clusters in the dataset."},
{"area_perimeter_and_convexity_of_the_cluster": "Area, perimeter, and convexity of the cluster are specific measurements that quantify the size, boundary length, and degree of curvature or indentation of the cluster, respectively. These metrics are crucial for assessing the morphological attributes of the clusters."},
{"logit_related_features": "Logit-related features refer to specific characteristics derived from the output of a logistic regression model, which in this context are generated from previously trained CNN predictions predefined as biological classes of known clinical relevance."},
{"logit_related_features__included__mean_and_standard_deviation_of_logits_for_each_of_the_classes_of_cnn_generated_overlays": "The predicate \"included\" signifies that the logit-related features are part of the set of features used to represent each graph node in the model. These features are essential for capturing the nuanced predictions made by the CNN."},
{"mean_and_standard_deviation_of_logits_for_each_of_the_classes_of_cnn_generated_overlays": "The mean and standard deviation of logits for each of the classes of CNN-generated overlays represent statistical measures of the logit values produced by the CNN for different classes. These measures help in understanding the distribution and variability of the CNN's predictions across various biological classes."},
{"scores_from_multiple_pathologists": "Scores from multiple pathologists refer to the individual assessments and ratings provided by different medical professionals who specialize in pathology. These scores are crucial in evaluating and diagnosing medical conditions based on histological features observed in tissue samples."},
{"scores_from_multiple_pathologists__used_independently_during__training": "The phrase \"used independently during\" indicates that the scores from multiple pathologists were utilized separately and without aggregation or consensus during a specific process, in this case, the training phase of a machine learning model. This approach helps mitigate individual biases and variabilities in scoring."},
{"training": "Training in this context refers to the phase in machine learning where a model learns from a dataset to make predictions or decisions. During training, the model adjusts its parameters based on the input data to minimize errors and improve accuracy in future predictions.|Here is another description: Training in this context refers to the process by which the GNN ordinal scoring model learns to map ordinal grades/stages to continuous scores. During this training phase, the model adjusts its parameters to minimize the difference between predicted and actual scores, without applying any penalties to the long-tailed distributions in the outer bins."},
{"consensus_scores_n_3": "Consensus scores, specifically from three pathologists (_n_ = 3), are utilized in the context of evaluating the performance of a model. These scores are derived from independent evaluations by multiple pathologists, aiming to mitigate individual scoring variability and biases."},
{"consensus_scores_n_3__used_for__evaluating_model_performance_on_validation_data": "The predicate \"used for\" indicates the purpose or application of the consensus scores. In this context, it specifies that the consensus scores are employed as a means to assess or judge the effectiveness and accuracy of the model when applied to validation data."},
{"evaluating_model_performance_on_validation_data": "Evaluating model performance on validation data refers to the process of assessing how well a machine learning model performs on a set of data that it has not encountered during training. This validation data serves as a benchmark to ensure the model's generalization capabilities and reliability in real-world applications."},
{"leveraging_scores_from_multiple_pathologists": "Leveraging scores from multiple pathologists involves using the assessments and evaluations provided by several medical professionals who specialize in pathology. This approach aims to enhance the accuracy and reliability of medical diagnoses by incorporating diverse expert opinions."},
{"leveraging_scores_from_multiple_pathologists__reduced__the_potential_impact_of_scoring_variability_and_bias_associated_with_a_single_reader": "The act of reducing in this context refers to minimizing or lessening the influence of certain factors. Specifically, it aims to decrease the effects of scoring variability and bias that can arise when diagnoses are made by a single reader or pathologist."},
{"the_potential_impact_of_scoring_variability_and_bias_associated_with_a_single_reader": "The potential impact of scoring variability and bias associated with a single reader refers to the risks and errors that can occur when medical diagnoses are based solely on the judgments of one pathologist. This can include inconsistencies in scoring and subjective biases that may affect the accuracy of the diagnosis."},
{"gnn_model": "The GNN model is a type of machine learning model that utilizes graph neural networks to process and analyze data represented in a graph structure. In this context, it is designed to handle complex, multi-dimensional data by leveraging the relationships between different data points."},
{"gnn_model__specified_as__mixed_effects_model": "The phrase \"specified as\" indicates that the GNN model has been configured or defined in a particular way to incorporate certain characteristics or functionalities. In this case, it has been tailored to include a specific type of statistical modeling approach."},
{"mixed_effects_model": "The \u2018mixed effects\u2019 model is a statistical model that includes both fixed effects and random effects. It is used to account for variability and bias in data, particularly when there are multiple sources of variation. In this context, it helps to mitigate the impact of individual pathologists' scoring biases on the model's predictions."},
{"each_pathologist_s_policy": "Each pathologist\u2019s policy refers to the individual approach or method used by different pathologists in evaluating and scoring medical images, specifically in the context of MASH histology."},
{"each_pathologist_s_policy__specified_in_the_model_by__a_set_of_bias_parameters_learned_during_training": "Specified in the model by indicates how each pathologist\u2019s unique scoring tendencies or biases are incorporated into the Graph Neural Network (GNN) model during the training phase."},
{"a_set_of_bias_parameters_learned_during_training": "A set of bias parameters learned during training represents the specific adjustments or corrections that the GNN model learns to apply to account for the consistent overestimation or underestimation of patient disease severity by individual pathologists. These parameters are discarded at test time to ensure unbiased predictions."},
{"bias_parameters": "Bias parameters are specific values learned during the training of a model to account for the individual scoring tendencies of different pathologists. These parameters help adjust the model's predictions to mitigate the impact of variability and bias introduced by human raters."},
{"bias_parameters__discarded_at__test_time": "The phrase \"discarded at\" indicates that the bias parameters, which were utilized during the training phase to adjust for individual pathologists' scoring biases, are not used when the model is applied to new, unseen data during the test phase."},
{"test_time": "Test time refers to the phase in which a trained model is applied to new, previously unseen data to make predictions. During this phase, the model operates without the bias parameters that were used to adjust for individual pathologists' scoring tendencies during training."},
{"to_learn_these_biases": "The subject in this context refers to the process of learning biases within a model, specifically in the context of a Graph Neural Network (GNN) used for medical image analysis. This involves understanding and accounting for the variability and potential biases introduced by different pathologists when scoring medical images."},
{"to_learn_these_biases__trained_the_model_on__all_unique_label_graph_pairs": "The predicate describes the action taken to mitigate these biases. In this case, the model was trained on specific data pairs to learn and incorporate the biases of individual pathologists, which were then used to adjust the model's predictions during the training phase."},
{"all_unique_label_graph_pairs": "The object of the triple refers to the dataset used for training the model to recognize and adjust for biases. This dataset consists of all unique label\u2013graph pairs, where each label is associated with a score and an identifier for the pathologist who provided the score. These pairs are essential for the model to learn the specific biases of each pathologist."},
{"label": "The label in this context refers to a specific designation or classification assigned to a whole slide image (WSI) based on the evaluation of histologic features by pathologists. It serves as a critical component in the training and evaluation of graph neural network (GNN) models designed to predict patient disease states from WSIs."},
{"label__represented_by__a_score_and_a_variable_that_indicated_which_pathologist_in_the_training_set_generated_this_score": "The predicate \"represented by\" indicates the manner in which the label is encoded or symbolized within the GNN model. It specifies that the label is not a simple categorical or numerical value but is instead characterized by a more complex representation that includes both a score and additional information about the pathologist who provided the score."},
{"a_score_and_a_variable_that_indicated_which_pathologist_in_the_training_set_generated_this_score": "The object \"a score and a variable that indicated which pathologist in the training set generated this score\" describes a dual-component representation used for labels in the GNN model. The score likely reflects the pathologist's assessment of the histologic features, while the variable identifies the specific pathologist, allowing the model to account for individual biases and variabilities in scoring among different pathologists."},
{"the_model__selected__the_specified_pathologist_bias_parameter_and_added_it_to_the_unbiased_estimate_of_the_patient_s_disease_state": "The predicate \"selected\" refers to the action performed by the GNN model during its training phase. It involves choosing specific bias parameters associated with individual pathologists to adjust the model's predictions, thereby accounting for inter-pathologist variability and systemic bias in disease state estimations."},
{"the_specified_pathologist_bias_parameter_and_added_it_to_the_unbiased_estimate_of_the_patient_s_disease_state": "The specified pathologist bias parameter represents an adjustment factor learned by the GNN model for each pathologist involved in the training dataset. This parameter is added to the unbiased estimate of a patient\u2019s disease state to correct for any consistent overestimation or underestimation of disease severity by that pathologist. These bias parameters are utilized during training but discarded when the model is deployed for actual predictions."},
{"these_biases": "These biases refer to the individual pathologist's policy parameters within the mixed effects model, which are learned during the training phase to account for systemic bias in scoring."},
{"these_biases__updated_via__backpropagation_only_on_wsis_scored_by_the_corresponding_pathologists": "Updated via indicates the method by which the biases are refined during the training process, specifically through the use of backpropagation."},
{"backpropagation_only_on_wsis_scored_by_the_corresponding_pathologists": "Backpropagation only on WSIs scored by the corresponding pathologists describes the specific condition under which the biases are updated, ensuring that each pathologist's biases are adjusted based solely on the whole slide images (WSIs) they have scored."},
{"gnns__deployed_using__only_the_unbiased_estimate": "The phrase \"deployed using\" indicates the manner in which the GNNs are implemented or utilized in practical applications. Specifically, it refers to the operational strategy or configuration adopted when the GNNs are put into use for tasks like evaluating patient disease states from WSIs."},
{"only_the_unbiased_estimate": "\"Only the unbiased estimate\" refers to a specific method used when deploying the GNNs. Instead of incorporating any pathologist-specific biases learned during training, the model relies solely on an unbiased estimate of the patient\u2019s disease state. This approach aims to produce more consistent and fair evaluations across different pathologists' scorings."},
{"gnns_in_this_study": "GNNs in this study refer to Graph Neural Networks utilized for analyzing and interpreting medical images, specifically Whole Slide Images (WSIs) in the context of histopathology. These networks are designed to process complex image data by transforming pixel-level predictions into superpixel clusters, which are then used to construct graph nodes and edges."},
{"gnns_in_this_study__trained_using__mash_crn_scores_from_eight_pathologists": "The predicate \"trained using\" indicates the method by which the GNNs were prepared for their task. In this case, the GNNs were trained using a specific dataset and approach, which involved incorporating scores from multiple pathologists to reduce scoring variability and bias."},
{"pathologists__had_experience_in_evaluating__mash_histology_on_a_subset_of_the_data_used_for_image_segmentation_model_training": "The phrase \"had experience in evaluating\" indicates that the pathologists involved in the study had prior knowledge and practical skills in assessing specific types of histology. This experience is critical for providing reliable and consistent scoring, which is essential for training and validating machine learning models in medical imaging."},
{"mash_histology_on_a_subset_of_the_data_used_for_image_segmentation_model_training": "MASH histology refers to the microscopic examination of tissue samples to diagnose Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis, a liver condition. In this context, it involves analyzing a subset of data used for training an image segmentation model, which aims to accurately identify and quantify histologic features relevant to the disease."},
{"the_gnn_nodes_and_edges": "The GNN nodes and edges refer to the components of a Graph Neural Network (GNN) used in the context of analyzing Whole Slide Images (WSIs) for medical diagnosis. Nodes represent clusters of superpixels, which are groups of pixels with similar characteristics, while edges connect each node to its nearest neighbors, forming a graph structure that captures spatial relationships."},
{"the_gnn_nodes_and_edges__built_from__cnn_predictions_of_relevant_histologic_features_in_the_first_model_training_stage": "The predicate \"built from\" indicates the process by which the GNN nodes and edges are constructed. In this context, it means that the structure and features of the GNN nodes and edges are derived from or based on the outputs of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). Specifically, the CNN provides predictions of relevant histologic features that are then used to form the GNN."},
{"cnn_predictions_of_relevant_histologic_features_in_the_first_model_training_stage": "CNN predictions of relevant histologic features in the first model training stage refer to the outputs generated by a Convolutional Neural Network during its initial training phase. These predictions include various biological classes of known clinical relevance, spatial, topological, and logit-related features that are essential for constructing the GNN nodes and edges. These features help in representing the histologic characteristics of the tissue samples being analyzed."},
{"this_tiered_approach": "The tiered approach refers to a method that integrates multiple stages or layers of processing to achieve a specific outcome. In this context, it involves using superpixels and graph neural networks (GNNs) to enhance the analysis of whole slide images (WSIs) in medical diagnostics."},
{"this_tiered_approach__improved_upon__previous_work": "The phrase \"improved upon\" indicates that the tiered approach has enhanced or made advancements compared to earlier methods. This improvement is demonstrated through more efficient processing, better integration of data from multiple sources, and potentially more accurate results."},
{"previous_work": "The \"previous work\" refers to earlier methodologies or models that were used for analyzing WSIs, specifically those that involved training models on scores from a single pathologist and using separate models for different tasks like slide-level scoring and histologic feature quantification.|Here is another description: Previous work refers to earlier research efforts conducted prior to the current study. These efforts involved the development and application of machine learning models for analyzing medical images, specifically in the context of histopathology."},
{"previous_work__trained_separate_models_for__slide_level_scoring_and_histologic_feature_quantification": "The phrase \"trained separate models for\" indicates that in the previous work, distinct machine learning models were developed and trained independently. Each model was designed to perform a specific task related to the analysis of histopathological images."},
{"slide_level_scoring_and_histologic_feature_quantification": "Slide-level scoring and histologic feature quantification are two separate tasks in histopathology image analysis. Slide-level scoring involves assessing the overall severity or grade of a disease on a whole slide image, while histologic feature quantification involves measuring specific features or characteristics within the tissue, such as the size, shape, and distribution of cells or structures."},
{"ordinal_scores": "Ordinal scores refer to the numerical values assigned to different levels of severity or categories in a medical context, specifically in the evaluation of histologic features in whole slide images (WSIs).|Here is another description: Ordinal scores refer to the grades or stages derived from the Graph Neural Network (GNN) model, which are used to assess the severity of histologic features such as fibrosis in medical imaging.|Here is another description: Ordinal scores refer to the grades or stages derived from a Graph Neural Network (GNN) used in assessing liver conditions such as steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and CRN fibrosis."},
{"ordinal_scores__constructed_directly_from__cnn_labeled_wsis": "Constructed directly from indicates that the ordinal scores are derived or generated immediately from the data provided by the CNN-labeled WSIs, without intermediate steps or additional processing."},
{"cnn_labeled_wsis": "CNN-labeled WSIs are whole slide images that have been processed and annotated by a convolutional neural network (CNN) to identify and classify various histologic features relevant to the medical evaluation, such as biological classes of known clinical relevance."},
{"gnn_derived_continuous_score_generation": "GNN-derived continuous score generation refers to a method where a Graph Neural Network (GNN) is used to generate continuous scores for fibrosis by mapping ordinal grades or stages to bins. This process involves extracting activation layer output logits from the GNN ordinal scoring model pipeline and averaging them. The GNN learns inter-bin cutoffs during training, which are then used in a piecewise linear mapping from logits to binned continuous scores."},
{"gnn_derived_continuous_score_generation__produces__continuous_mas_and_crn_fibrosis_scores": "The predicate \"produces\" in this context signifies the action of generating or creating continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores through a specific method. This involves the transformation of ordinal grades or stages into continuous scores by utilizing a GNN model, which maps these grades to bins and applies piecewise linear mapping based on learned cutoffs."},
{"continuous_mas_and_crn_fibrosis_scores": "Continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores are the output of the GNN-derived continuous score generation process. These scores represent a continuous range of values that correspond to the severity of fibrosis, as determined by mapping ordinal grades to bins and applying linear mapping techniques. The scores are designed to provide a more nuanced and detailed assessment of fibrosis severity compared to traditional ordinal grading systems.|Here is another description: Continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores are numerical values that represent the severity of liver fibrosis according to the MAS and CRN scoring systems. These scores are generated to provide a more detailed and nuanced assessment of fibrosis compared to traditional ordinal grading.|Here is another description: Continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores refer to the quantitative assessments of liver conditions, specifically measuring the severity of steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and fibrosis. These scores are crucial for evaluating the progression and severity of liver diseases."},
{"continuous_mas_and_crn_fibrosis_scores__are_produced_by__mapping_gnn_derived_ordinal_grades_stages_to_bins": "The process of producing continuous MAS and CRN fibrosis scores involves a specific method where ordinal grades or stages derived from a Graph Neural Network (GNN) are mapped to continuous bins. This mapping allows for a more granular and precise representation of fibrosis severity."},
{"mapping_gnn_derived_ordinal_grades_stages_to_bins": "Mapping GNN-derived ordinal grades/stages to bins is a technique used to convert discrete ordinal scores produced by a GNN into continuous values. This is achieved by spreading the ordinal scores over a continuous range, allowing for a more detailed analysis of fibrosis severity.|Here is another description: Mapping GNN-derived ordinal grades/stages to bins is a process where ordinal scores generated by a Graph Neural Network (GNN) are converted into continuous scores by assigning them to specific bins. This method allows for a more precise and quantitative assessment of liver conditions."},
{"ordinal_scores__are_spread_over__a_continuous_range_spanning_a_unit_distance_of_1": "The phrase \"are spread over\" describes the process by which ordinal scores are distributed or mapped across a continuous range. This mapping is achieved through a piecewise linear transformation that converts discrete ordinal grades into continuous values."},
{"a_continuous_range_spanning_a_unit_distance_of_1": "A continuous range spanning a unit distance of 1 represents the full spectrum of possible scores after the transformation. This range allows for a more nuanced and detailed assessment of disease severity, providing a finer granularity than the original ordinal scores."},
{"activation_layer_output_logits": "The activation layer output logits are numerical values generated by the activation layer of a neural network, representing the raw, unnormalized predictions before applying a softmax or other activation function.|Here is another description: The activation layer output logits are the raw, unnormalized scores produced by the activation layer of a neural network, specifically within the context of a Graph Neural Network (GNN) used for ordinal scoring of histologic features like fibrosis."},
{"activation_layer_output_logits__are_extracted_from__the_gnn_ordinal_scoring_model_pipeline": "The phrase \"are extracted from\" indicates the process of obtaining or deriving the activation layer output logits from a larger system or model, specifically highlighting the source of these logits."},
{"the_gnn_ordinal_scoring_model_pipeline": "The GNN ordinal scoring model pipeline is a machine learning system that uses Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) to produce ordinal scores. This pipeline processes input data through several layers to output logits, which are then used to determine continuous scores through a mapping process."},
{"activation_layer_output_logits__are_averaged__during_the_process": "The process of averaging the activation layer output logits involves taking the mean of these raw scores. This step is crucial in the pipeline of the GNN ordinal scoring model to produce a more stable and representative value for each ordinal bin, which is then used in the piecewise linear mapping to continuous scores."},
{"during_the_process": "During the process refers to the specific stage in the GNN ordinal scoring model pipeline where the averaging of activation layer output logits occurs. This stage is part of a series of operations that transform ordinal grades/stages into continuous scores, facilitating a more nuanced assessment of disease severity."},
{"gnn__learned__inter_bin_cutoffs_during_training": "The predicate \"learned\" indicates that the GNN has been trained to acquire specific knowledge or parameters. In this case, the GNN has learned to determine the inter-bin cutoffs, which are critical for mapping ordinal scores to continuous values during the training process."},
{"inter_bin_cutoffs_during_training": "Inter-bin cutoffs during training refer to the thresholds that the GNN has learned to distinguish between different bins of ordinal scores. These cutoffs are essential for converting the ordinal scores produced by the GNN into continuous scores, allowing for a more nuanced representation of fibrosis severity."},
{"piecewise_linear_mapping": "Piecewise linear mapping is a method used to convert ordinal data into continuous scores by applying linear transformations between defined intervals or bins."},
{"piecewise_linear_mapping__is_performed__per_logit_ordinal_bin_from_the_logits_to_binned_continuous_scores": "The action \"is performed\" indicates that the piecewise linear mapping is executed as a process within the GNN ordinal scoring model pipeline to transform logit ordinal bins into continuous scores."},
{"per_logit_ordinal_bin_from_the_logits_to_binned_continuous_scores": "Per logit ordinal bin from the logits to binned continuous scores refers to the specific transformation applied to each bin of logit values, converting them into continuous scores based on learned inter-bin cutoffs."},
{"logit_valued_cutoffs": "Logit-valued cutoffs are specific threshold values derived from the activation layer output logits of a Graph Neural Network (GNN) used in the ordinal scoring model pipeline. These cutoffs are learned during the training process to help map ordinal grades or stages to continuous scores."},
{"logit_valued_cutoffs__are_used_to__separate_bins": "The phrase \"are used to\" indicates the functional relationship where logit-valued cutoffs serve as a means or method. In this context, they are employed to facilitate the piecewise linear mapping from logits to binned continuous scores, ensuring that ordinal scores are appropriately distributed over a continuous range."},
{"separate_bins": "Separate bins refer to the distinct intervals or categories into which continuous scores are divided. These bins represent different levels of disease severity and are created by applying the logit-valued cutoffs to the GNN-derived ordinal scores. The separation of bins allows for a more granular and balanced representation of the disease severity continuum."},
{"bins_on_either_end_of_the_disease_severity_continuum_per_histologic_feature": "Bins on either end of the disease severity continuum per histologic feature refer to the extreme ends of the spectrum of disease severity as categorized by specific histologic features. These bins are part of a continuous scoring system used to assess the severity of conditions like MAS and CRN fibrosis."},
{"bins_on_either_end_of_the_disease_severity_continuum_per_histologic_feature__have__long_tailed_distributions": "The predicate \"have\" in this context indicates the possession or characteristic association of the subject (bins) with a specific attribute (long-tailed distributions). It suggests that these bins are inherently characterized by the described distributions."},
{"long_tailed_distributions": "Long-tailed distributions refer to a type of probability distribution where most of the data points are clustered at one end of the scale, with a few extreme values at the other end. In the context of disease severity bins, this means that most cases fall into the less severe categories, with only a few cases at the extreme end of severity.|Here is another description: Long-tailed distributions refer to the uneven distribution of data points where a few values occur very frequently, while the majority of the values occur very rarely. In the context of GNN-derived continuous score generation, these distributions are observed in the bins on either end of the disease severity continuum per histologic feature."},
{"long_tailed_distributions__are_not_penalized_during__training": "The phrase \"are not penalized during\" indicates that the long-tailed distributions do not incur any negative consequences or adjustments in the training process of the GNN ordinal scoring model. This means that the model does not apply any specific corrections or modifications to these distributions while learning."},
{"logit_values_in_the_first_and_last_bins": "The logit values in the first and last bins refer to the extreme ends of the continuous range of scores generated by the GNN ordinal scoring model. These values are crucial for mapping ordinal grades to a continuous score, especially for bins that have long-tailed distributions."},
{"logit_values_in_the_first_and_last_bins__are_restricted_to__minimum_and_maximum_values_respectively_during_a_post_processing_step": "The restriction of logit values in the first and last bins to minimum and maximum values, respectively, is a post-processing step aimed at ensuring a balanced linear mapping of these outer bins. This step is necessary because the bins on either end of the disease severity continuum have distributions that are not penalized during training, potentially leading to imbalances in the mapping process."},
{"minimum_and_maximum_values_respectively_during_a_post_processing_step": "The minimum and maximum values to which the logit values in the first and last bins are restricted are defined by outer-edge cutoffs. These cutoffs are chosen to maximize the uniformity of logit value distributions across the training data, ensuring that the mapping of ordinal scores to continuous scores is as balanced and accurate as possible."},
{"these_values": "These values refer to the minimum and maximum logit values applied to the first and last bins during a post-processing step in the GNN ordinal scoring model pipeline. They are crucial for ensuring a balanced linear mapping of the outer bins in the disease severity continuum."},
{"these_values__are_defined_by__outer_edge_cutoffs_chosen_to_maximize_the_uniformity_of_logit_value_distributions_across_training_data": "The phrase \"are defined by\" indicates that the subject values are determined or set according to a specific criterion. In this context, it means that the minimum and maximum logit values are established based on certain outer-edge cutoffs."},
{"outer_edge_cutoffs_chosen_to_maximize_the_uniformity_of_logit_value_distributions_across_training_data": "Outer-edge cutoffs are specific threshold values chosen during the training of the GNN ordinal scoring model. These cutoffs are selected to maximize the uniformity of logit value distributions across the training data, ensuring that the logit values in the first and last bins are appropriately constrained."},
{"gnn_continuous_feature_training_and_ordinal_mapping": "GNN continuous feature training and ordinal mapping refers to the process of training a Graph Neural Network (GNN) to produce continuous scores for fibrosis by mapping ordinal grades to bins. This involves extracting activation layer output logits, averaging them, and performing piecewise linear mapping from logits to binned continuous scores."},
{"gnn_continuous_feature_training_and_ordinal_mapping__are_performed_for__each_mash_crn_and_mas_component_fibrosis_separately": "The phrase \"are performed for\" indicates that the actions of GNN continuous feature training and ordinal mapping are carried out specifically targeting certain entities. In this context, it means that these processes are applied to each MASH CRN and MAS component fibrosis as separate entities."},
{"each_mash_crn_and_mas_component_fibrosis_separately": "Each MASH CRN and MAS component fibrosis refers to distinct histologic features related to liver fibrosis that are evaluated separately. MASH CRN (Metavir Activity Score for Hepatitis C) and MAS (Metavir Activity Score) are scoring systems used to assess the severity of liver fibrosis, and each component is analyzed individually in the context of GNN-derived continuous score generation."},
{"quality_control_measures": "Quality control measures refer to the systematic actions and procedures put in place to maintain and enhance the quality of a product or process. In the context of model learning, these measures are crucial to ensure that the data used for training is accurate, reliable, and free from errors."},
{"quality_control_measures__were_implemented__to_ensure_model_learning_from_high_quality_data": "The predicate \"were implemented\" indicates that specific actions or procedures were put into effect. In this case, it signifies that quality control measures were actively introduced and applied to the process of model learning from data."},
{"to_ensure_model_learning_from_high_quality_data": "The object \"to ensure model learning from high-quality data\" describes the purpose or goal of the quality control measures. It highlights the importance of using accurate and reliable data for the effective training and performance of machine learning models."},
{"pathai_liver_pathologists": "PathAI liver pathologists are specialized medical professionals employed by PathAI, a company that focuses on applying artificial intelligence to pathology. These experts are specifically trained in liver pathology and are responsible for assessing and ensuring the quality of data related to liver conditions."},
{"pathai_liver_pathologists__evaluated__all_annotators_for_annotation_scoring_performance_at_project": "The predicate \"evaluated\" signifies that the PathAI liver pathologists conducted a thorough assessment or review. In this context, it means they critically examined and judged the performance of other individuals involved in the annotation and scoring processes related to a specific project."},
{"all_annotators_for_annotation_scoring_performance_at_project": "All annotators for annotation/scoring performance at the project refers to the group of individuals who were responsible for labeling and scoring data as part of a specific project. Their performance was subject to evaluation by the PathAI liver pathologists to ensure the quality and accuracy of the annotations and scores provided."},
{"pathai_pathologists": "PathAI pathologists are medical professionals specialized in pathology, employed by PathAI, a company that leverages artificial intelligence to enhance pathology practices. They are responsible for reviewing and ensuring the quality of medical data used in machine learning model training.|Here is another description: PathAI pathologists are medical professionals specialized in pathology, employed by PathAI, a company that leverages artificial intelligence to enhance pathology practices. They are responsible for reviewing and ensuring the quality of annotations and the performance of AI models in pathology.|Here is another description: PathAI pathologists are medical professionals employed by PathAI who specialize in the interpretation of medical images, particularly in the context of pathology. They are involved in the quality control and review processes of medical image annotations and model performance."},
{"pathai_pathologists__performed__quality_control_review_on_all_annotations_collected_throughout_model_training": "The predicate \"performed\" indicates that the PathAI pathologists actively carried out a specific task. In this context, it means they executed a detailed examination and evaluation process."},
{"quality_control_review_on_all_annotations_collected_throughout_model_training": "The quality control review on all annotations collected throughout model training refers to a thorough examination process where PathAI pathologists assessed the accuracy and reliability of annotations gathered during the development of a machine learning model. This review aimed to ensure that only high-quality annotations were used for training the model."},
{"annotations_deemed_to_be_of_high_quality": "Annotations deemed to be of high quality refer to the carefully reviewed and approved annotations by PathAI pathologists, which met the necessary standards for use in further processes."},
{"annotations_deemed_to_be_of_high_quality__were_used_for__model_training": "The phrase \"were used for\" indicates that the high-quality annotations served as essential inputs or resources in a subsequent process, specifically in this case, model training."},
{"all_other_annotations": "All other annotations refer to the data points or markings that were not deemed to be of high quality by PathAI pathologists during the quality control review process."},
{"all_other_annotations__were_excluded_from__model_development": "Were excluded from indicates that certain annotations were not included or considered in the subsequent stages of the process, specifically in this case, the model development phase."},
{"pathai_pathologists__performed__slide_level_review_of_the_model_s_performance_after_every_iteration_of_model_training": "The predicate \"performed\" indicates that the PathAI pathologists actively carried out a specific task or series of actions. In this context, it refers to the execution of a slide-level review process by the pathologists after each iteration of model training."},
{"slide_level_review_of_the_model_s_performance_after_every_iteration_of_model_training": "The slide-level review of the model\u2019s performance after every iteration of model training refers to a detailed evaluation process conducted by PathAI pathologists. This process involves assessing the AI model's performance on individual slides, providing qualitative feedback on its strengths and weaknesses, and ensuring that the model is improving with each training iteration."},
{"pathai_pathologists__provided__specific_qualitative_feedback_on_areas_of_strength_weakness_after_each_iteration": "The action of providing involves the delivery of information or feedback. In this context, it refers to the act of PathAI pathologists giving detailed insights and observations after reviewing the model's performance."},
{"specific_qualitative_feedback_on_areas_of_strength_weakness_after_each_iteration": "Specific qualitative feedback on areas of strength/weakness after each iteration refers to the detailed comments and observations provided by PathAI pathologists regarding the performance of a model. This feedback highlights both the positive aspects (strengths) and the areas needing improvement (weaknesses) after each round of model training."},
{"model_performance": "Model performance refers to the effectiveness and accuracy of a machine learning model in completing its designated tasks, which in this context involves analyzing and interpreting pathological data.|Here is another description: Model performance refers to the effectiveness and accuracy of a machine learning model in making predictions or classifications. In this context, it specifically pertains to the performance of a model trained to analyze and interpret medical images, particularly in the field of pathology."},
{"model_performance__was_characterized_at__the_patch_and_slide_levels_in_an_internal_held_out_test_set": "The phrase \"was characterized at\" indicates the process of evaluating and defining the attributes or qualities of the model performance under specific conditions or levels, providing insights into how well the model functions in practical applications."},
{"the_patch_and_slide_levels_in_an_internal_held_out_test_set": "The patch and slide levels in an internal (held-out) test set represent the different granularities at which the model's performance was assessed. The patch level involves small, localized areas within a slide, while the slide level encompasses the entire specimen. An internal held-out test set is a reserved subset of data not used during the model's training phase, used to objectively evaluate its performance."},
{"model_performance__was_compared_against__pathologist_consensus_scoring_in_an_entirely_held_out_test_set": "The phrase \"was compared against\" indicates a process where the model's performance metrics were evaluated in relation to a standard or benchmark. This comparison helps in assessing how well the model performs relative to established methods or expert opinions."},
{"pathologist_consensus_scoring_in_an_entirely_held_out_test_set": "Pathologist consensus scoring in an entirely held-out test set represents a benchmark where multiple pathologists independently review and score the same set of medical images. This scoring is used as a gold standard to evaluate the model's performance, ensuring that the model's predictions are aligned with expert human judgments."},
{"held_out_test_set__contained__images_that_were_out_of_distribution_relative_to_images_from_which_the_model_had_learned_during_development": "The predicate \"contained\" indicates that the held-out test set included or had within it specific types of images. This relation highlights the composition of the test set in terms of the data it encompasses."},
{"images_that_were_out_of_distribution_relative_to_images_from_which_the_model_had_learned_during_development": "The images that were out of distribution relative to images from which the model had learned during development are those that differ significantly from the training data. These images present new, unseen variations or features that the model has not encountered during its learning phase."},
{"ai_based_scoring": "AI-based scoring refers to the application of artificial intelligence algorithms to evaluate or grade certain tasks or data. In this context, it involves using AI to assess a specific test set multiple times."},
{"ai_based_scoring__assessed_for__repeatability": "Assessed for indicates the evaluation or measurement of a particular characteristic or quality. Here, it signifies the process of examining the repeatability of AI-based scoring by conducting multiple assessments."},
{"repeatability": "Repeatability in this context refers to the consistency or reliability of the AI-based scoring when applied to the same test set multiple times. It is a measure of how reproducible the results are under the same conditions.|Here is another description: Repeatability refers to the consistency of results obtained from the same method under the same conditions. In the context of AI-based scoring, it measures the intra-method variability of the AI algorithms when applied to the same test set multiple times."},
{"repeatability__assessed_by__deploying_ai_algorithms_ten_times": "Assessed by indicates the method or process used to evaluate or measure a particular characteristic or performance. In this case, it describes how the repeatability of AI-based scoring is evaluated through a specific procedure involving multiple deployments of AI algorithms."},
{"deploying_ai_algorithms_ten_times": "Deploying AI algorithms ten times involves running the same AI algorithms on the same held-out analytic performance test set multiple times to gather data on their performance consistency. This process helps in computing the percentage positive agreement across the ten reads by the model, thereby assessing the repeatability of the AI-based scoring."},
{"ai_algorithms": "AI algorithms refer to the computational methods and processes designed to enable machines to perform tasks that typically require human intelligence, such as learning, reasoning, and problem-solving. In the context provided, these algorithms are specifically tailored for AI-based scoring, which involves analyzing data and making predictions or decisions based on that analysis."},
{"ai_algorithms__deployed_on__held_out_analytic_performance_test_set": "The phrase \"deployed on\" indicates the action of implementing or utilizing the AI algorithms in a specific context or environment. In this case, it means that the AI algorithms were applied or run on a particular dataset to perform their intended function, which is to score or analyze the data."},
{"held_out_analytic_performance_test_set": "The held-out analytic performance test set is a dataset that is reserved for evaluating the performance of the AI algorithms. It is called \"held-out\" because it is not used during the training phase of the algorithms but is instead kept separate to assess how well the algorithms generalize to new, unseen data. The performance of the AI algorithms on this test set provides an indication of their effectiveness and reliability."},
{"percentage_positive_agreement": "Percentage positive agreement refers to the metric used to evaluate the consistency and reliability of AI-based scoring across multiple iterations. It indicates the proportion of positive outcomes that are consistently identified by the model."},
{"percentage_positive_agreement__computed_across__ten_reads_by_the_model": "The predicate \"computed across\" signifies the process of calculating or determining the percentage positive agreement by analyzing the results from multiple iterations or reads performed by the AI model."},
{"ten_reads_by_the_model": "Ten reads by the model represent the number of times the AI algorithms were deployed on the same held-out analytic performance test set to assess the repeatability of AI-based scoring. These multiple reads provide data points for computing the percentage positive agreement."},
{"model_derived_predictions__compared_with__median_consensus_grades_stages": "The comparison of model-derived predictions with median consensus grades/stages involves evaluating how closely the model's predictions align with the average assessments provided by a panel of expert pathologists."},
{"median_consensus_grades_stages": "Median consensus grades/stages are the average histologic assessments given by a panel of three expert pathologists for features like steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and fibrosis in MASH biopsies, serving as a benchmark for evaluating the accuracy of model-derived predictions."},
{"panel_of_three_expert_pathologists": "A panel of three expert pathologists is a group of highly skilled medical professionals specialized in the study of diseases, particularly through the examination of tissues and cells. In this context, they are responsible for evaluating and providing consensus grades for various histologic features in MASH biopsies."},
{"panel_of_three_expert_pathologists__evaluated__mash_biopsies": "The predicate \"evaluated\" signifies the action taken by the panel of pathologists to assess and analyze the MASH biopsies. This process involves examining the tissue samples to determine the severity and characteristics of the disease, such as the degree of steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis."},
{"mash_biopsies": "MASH biopsies refer to tissue samples taken from patients with Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis (MASH), a liver condition characterized by fat accumulation and inflammation. These biopsies are crucial for diagnosing and assessing the severity of MASH, providing valuable information for treatment and management of the disease."},
{"images_from_clinical_trial": "The images from the clinical trial refer to a set of medical images obtained during a recently completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial. These images were used by a panel of expert pathologists to evaluate MASH biopsies.|Here is another description: The images from the clinical trial are a set of medical images obtained during a phase 2b MASH clinical trial, which were used for evaluating model performance in predicting various histologic features of liver biopsies."},
{"images_from_clinical_trial__not_included_in__model_training": "The phrase \"not included in\" indicates that the images from the clinical trial were deliberately excluded from the process of model training. This exclusion ensures that the model's performance can be evaluated on data it has not seen before, providing an unbiased assessment of its accuracy."},
{"images_from_clinical_trial__served_as__external_held_out_test_set": "The predicate \"served as\" indicates that the images from the clinical trial were utilized in a specific role or capacity, which in this case was to function as an external, held-out test set for evaluating the accuracy and performance of a model."},
{"external_held_out_test_set": "The external, held-out test set refers to a dataset that is kept separate from the training data and is used to assess the generalizability and performance of a model. In this context, it consists of images from the clinical trial that were not included in the model's training process."},
{"alignment_between_model_predictions_and_pathologist_consensus": "The alignment between model predictions and pathologist consensus refers to the degree to which the predictions made by a machine learning model match the consensus grades or stages determined by a panel of expert pathologists. This alignment is crucial for validating the model's performance in a clinical setting."},
{"alignment_between_model_predictions_and_pathologist_consensus__measured_via__agreement_rates": "Measured via indicates the method or metric used to quantify the alignment between model predictions and pathologist consensus. In this context, it specifies that the alignment is assessed using agreement rates."},
{"agreement_rates": "Agreement rates represent the proportion of instances where the model's predictions align positively with the consensus grades or stages provided by the pathologists. This metric is used to reflect the model's accuracy and reliability in mimicking expert human judgments.|Here is another description: Agreement rates refer to the metrics used to measure the alignment between model predictions and the consensus grades provided by expert pathologists. These rates are crucial for evaluating the accuracy and reliability of the model in predicting histologic features in MASH biopsies."},
{"agreement_rates__reflecting__proportion_of_positive_agreements_between_the_model_and_consensus": "Reflecting indicates that the agreement rates serve as a direct indicator or measure of the proportion of positive agreements. This means that higher agreement rates suggest a greater alignment between the model's predictions and the consensus opinions of the pathologists."},
{"proportion_of_positive_agreements_between_the_model_and_consensus": "The proportion of positive agreements between the model and consensus represents the percentage of instances where the model's predictions matched the consensus grades provided by the panel of expert pathologists. This metric is essential for assessing the model's performance in a clinical setting."},
{"performance_of_each_expert_reader": "The performance of each expert reader refers to the individual assessment accuracy of expert pathologists in grading histologic features such as steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis in MASH biopsies."},
{"performance_of_each_expert_reader__evaluated_against__consensus": "Evaluated against signifies the process of comparing the individual assessments of expert pathologists to a standard or benchmark, in this case, the consensus opinion of a panel of pathologists."},
{"model": "The model in question is an algorithm designed to predict ordinal grades of various histologic features in MASH biopsies, such as steatosis, ballooning, lobular inflammation, and fibrosis. It was trained on a dataset separate from the clinical trial images used for evaluation."},
{"model__considered_as__fourth_reader": "The phrase \"considered as\" indicates that the model is being evaluated in the same manner as human expert readers. This means its predictions are compared against a consensus derived from the evaluations of expert pathologists, serving as a benchmark for its performance."},
{"fourth_reader": "The term \"fourth \u2018reader\u2019\" refers to the model being treated as an additional expert in the evaluation process. In this context, the model's predictions are combined with those of two pathologists to form a consensus, against which the performance of a third pathologist is measured."},
{"consensus__determined_from__model_derived_score_and_that_of_two_pathologists": "The predicate \"determined from\" indicates the process by which the consensus was reached. It involved combining the model-derived score with the assessments of two pathologists to form a unified decision."},
{"model_derived_score_and_that_of_two_pathologists": "The object \"model-derived score and that of two pathologists\" describes the sources used to determine the consensus. It includes the quantitative output from a machine learning model and the qualitative assessments from two expert pathologists, which were integrated to reach a consensus grade or stage for histologic features."},
{"performance_of_the_third_pathologist": "The performance of the third pathologist refers to the evaluation of how well the third expert pathologist's assessments align with a consensus in grading MASH biopsies. This evaluation is part of a larger process to benchmark the performance of a machine learning model against human expert assessments."},
{"performance_of_the_third_pathologist__evaluated_using__consensus": "Evaluated using indicates the method by which the performance of the third pathologist is assessed. In this context, it means that the pathologist's individual gradings are compared against a consensus derived from the combined assessments of the other two pathologists and potentially a machine learning model."},
{"average_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_rate": "The average individual pathologist versus consensus agreement rate refers to the mean rate at which individual pathologists' assessments align with the consensus opinion of a panel of experts. This metric is used as a benchmark to evaluate the performance of individual pathologists and models in grading histologic features."},
{"average_individual_pathologist_versus_consensus_agreement_rate__computed_per__histologic_feature": "The phrase \"computed per\" indicates that the average individual pathologist versus consensus agreement rate is calculated separately for each distinct histologic feature. This allows for a detailed comparison of agreement rates across different features, providing insights into areas where pathologists may have more or less consensus."},
{"confidence_intervals": "Confidence intervals are statistical measures used to indicate the reliability of an estimate. In the context of model performance evaluation, they provide a range within which the true value of a parameter is expected to lie with a certain level of confidence."},
{"confidence_intervals__computed_using__bootstrapping": "The phrase \"computed using\" indicates the method or process by which the subject (confidence intervals) was determined. In this case, it specifies that bootstrapping, a statistical method, was employed to calculate the confidence intervals."},
{"bootstrapping": "Bootstrapping is a resampling technique used to estimate statistics on a population by sampling a dataset with replacement. It is often used to measure the accuracy of a model by generating multiple samples from the original dataset and computing the statistic of interest for each sample."},
{"concordance": "Concordance refers to the agreement or alignment between different assessments or evaluations. In the context of the study, it specifically pertains to the agreement between model-derived predictions and the consensus grades/stages provided by expert pathologists for various histologic features in MASH biopsies.|Here is another description: Concordance refers to the degree of agreement or similarity between different assessments or evaluations. In the context of clinical trials, it is crucial to ensure that the AI's assessments align closely with those of expert pathologists to validate the AI's performance."},
{"concordance__assessed_for_scoring_of__steatosis_lobular_inflammation_hepatocellular_ballooning_and_fibrosis_using_the_mash_crn_system": "Assessed for scoring of indicates the process of evaluating and assigning scores to specific histologic features using a standardized system. In this context, it involves the comparison of model predictions with pathologist consensus scores for features like steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis."},
{"steatosis_lobular_inflammation_hepatocellular_ballooning_and_fibrosis_using_the_mash_crn_system": "Steatosis, lobular inflammation, hepatocellular ballooning, and fibrosis are key histologic features evaluated in MASH biopsies. These features are scored using the MASH CRN system, a standardized method for assessing the severity of these conditions. The scoring helps in diagnosing and understanding the progression of MASH."},
{"analytic_performance_test_set": "The analytic performance test set is a dataset used for evaluating the performance of AI in medical assessments, specifically in the context of clinical trials. It includes data from baseline and end-of-treatment (EOT) biopsies across different treatment arms."},
{"analytic_performance_test_set__was_leveraged_to_assess__ai_s_ability_to_recapitulate_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria_and_efficacy_endpoints": "The phrase \"was leveraged to assess\" indicates that the analytic performance test set was utilized or employed as a tool to evaluate or measure something. In this context, it was used to gauge the effectiveness or accuracy of the AI system."},
{"ai_s_ability_to_recapitulate_mash_clinical_trial_enrollment_criteria_and_efficacy_endpoints": "The AI\u2019s ability to recapitulate MASH clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints refers to the AI system's capability to accurately replicate or simulate the criteria used for enrolling patients in MASH clinical trials and to predict or measure the outcomes or endpoints of these trials. This involves assessing how well the AI can match human expert judgments in these areas."},
{"baseline_and_eot_biopsies": "Baseline and EOT biopsies refer to the tissue samples collected at the beginning (baseline) and end of treatment (EOT) from patients participating in a clinical trial. These samples are crucial for evaluating the efficacy of treatments by comparing changes in tissue characteristics over the course of the trial."},
{"baseline_and_eot_biopsies__were_grouped__across_treatment_arms": "The phrase \"were grouped\" indicates that the baseline and EOT biopsies were organized or categorized together. This grouping likely facilitates the comparison of treatment outcomes across different patient groups or treatment arms within the clinical trial."},
{"across_treatment_arms": "\"Across treatment arms\" refers to the different groups of patients in a clinical trial who receive various treatments or interventions. Grouping biopsies across these treatment arms allows for a comprehensive analysis of how different treatments affect patient outcomes, enabling researchers to draw conclusions about the efficacy and safety of each treatment."},
{"efficacy_endpoints": "Efficacy endpoints refer to the specific outcomes or measurements used to evaluate the effectiveness of a treatment or intervention in a clinical trial. These endpoints are critical for determining whether the treatment has the desired therapeutic effect."},
{"efficacy_endpoints__were_computed_using__each_study_patient_s_paired_baseline_and_eot_biopsies": "The phrase \"were computed using\" indicates the method or process by which the efficacy endpoints were determined. In this context, it suggests that a specific computational approach was applied to derive these endpoints from the data collected during the clinical trial."},
{"each_study_patient_s_paired_baseline_and_eot_biopsies": "Each study patient\u2019s paired baseline and EOT (End of Treatment) biopsies represent the biological samples taken from patients at two different time points: before the treatment began (baseline) and after the treatment concluded (EOT). These paired samples are essential for assessing changes in the patient\u2019s condition as a result of the treatment."},
{"statistical_method": "The statistical method refers to the mathematical and analytical techniques used to evaluate and interpret data in a study. In the context of clinical trials, these methods are crucial for determining the efficacy and safety of treatments compared to a control, such as a placebo."},
{"statistical_method__used_to_compare_treatment_with_placebo__cochran_mantel_haenszel_test": "The predicate \"used to compare treatment with placebo\" describes the specific application of a statistical method in a clinical trial setting. This involves analyzing data to determine if there is a significant difference in outcomes between patients receiving the treatment and those receiving a placebo."},
{"cochran_mantel_haenszel_test": "The Cochran\u2013Mantel\u2013Haenszel test is a statistical method used to compare treatment outcomes with a placebo in clinical trials. It is particularly useful for analyzing stratified data, where the response is categorized by different levels of a confounding variable, such as diabetes status or cirrhosis at baseline in this context."},
{"p_values": "P values are statistical measures used to determine the significance of results in hypothesis testing, indicating the probability that the observed data would occur under the null hypothesis."},
{"p_values__were_based_on__response_stratified_by_diabetes_status_and_cirrhosis_at_baseline": "The phrase \"were based on\" indicates that the P values were calculated or derived from a specific set of conditions or data stratifications."},
{"response_stratified_by_diabetes_status_and_cirrhosis_at_baseline": "The response stratified by diabetes status and cirrhosis at baseline refers to the categorization of patient responses in a clinical trial according to their diabetes status and the presence of cirrhosis at the start of the trial. This stratification allows for a more nuanced analysis of treatment effects across different patient subgroups."},
{"concordance__was_assessed_with__\u03ba_statistics": "The phrase \"was assessed with\" indicates the method or metric used to evaluate the subject. In this case, it specifies that the concordance of the AI's assessments was measured using a particular statistical method."},
{"accuracy": "Accuracy in this context refers to the measure of how well the AI system performs in assessing clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints, compared to a consensus determination by expert pathologists."},
{"accuracy__was_evaluated_by__computing_f1_scores": "The predicate \"was evaluated by\" indicates the method used to assess the accuracy of the AI system. It suggests that a specific metric or procedure was applied to determine how accurately the AI performed its tasks."},
{"computing_f1_scores": "Computing F1 scores is the method used to evaluate the accuracy of the AI system. F1 score is a harmonic mean of precision and recall, often used in machine learning to assess the performance of a model, especially in cases of imbalanced datasets."},
{"consensus_determination": "The consensus determination refers to the collective decision made by three expert pathologists regarding clinical trial enrollment criteria and efficacy endpoints. This determination is used as a benchmark in the context of evaluating the performance of an AI system."},
{"consensus_determination__served_as_a_reference_for__evaluating_ai_concordance_and_accuracy": "The phrase \"served as a reference for\" indicates that the consensus determination was utilized as a standard or baseline against which the AI's concordance and accuracy were measured. This predicate highlights the role of the consensus in providing a comparative framework."},
{"evaluating_ai_concordance_and_accuracy": "Evaluating AI concordance and accuracy involves assessing how well the AI system's assessments align with and match the consensus determinations made by the expert pathologists. This process is crucial for validating the AI's performance in a clinical context."},
{"mloo_approach": "The MLOO (Modified Leave-One-Out) approach is a method used in the context of evaluating the performance of pathologists in medical assessments. It involves systematically excluding one pathologist at a time from the consensus determination to assess their individual performance against the consensus."},
{"mloo_approach__was_followed_to_evaluate__the_performance_of_each_pathologist_against_a_consensus_determination": "The predicate \"was followed to evaluate\" indicates that the MLOO approach was systematically applied or adhered to in order to assess or measure something. In this context, it specifically refers to the process of using the MLOO method to evaluate the performance of each pathologist."},
{"the_performance_of_each_pathologist_against_a_consensus_determination": "The performance of each pathologist against a consensus determination refers to how well individual pathologists' assessments align with a collective decision made by a group of expert pathologists. This consensus serves as a benchmark or standard against which the individual pathologists' performances are measured."},
{"continuous_scoring_system__demonstrates__interpretability": "The predicate \"demonstrates\" indicates that the continuous scoring system is being shown to have a certain quality or capability. In this context, it is used to show that the system can exhibit interpretability, meaning its scoring can be understood and correlated with human evaluations."},
{"interpretability": "Interpretability in this context refers to the ability of the continuous scoring system to produce results that can be understood and verified by human pathologists. It involves comparing the system's scores with those from human evaluators to ensure that the AI-derived scores reflect the same directional bias as the human scores."},
{"mash_crn_continuous_scores": "MASH CRN continuous scores refer to a set of numerical values generated to quantify the severity of histologic features in liver tissue images, specifically within the context of a clinical trial for MASH (Metabolic Associated Steatohepatitis)."},
{"mash_crn_continuous_scores__were_generated_in__wsis_from_a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "The phrase \"were generated in\" indicates the process by which the MASH CRN continuous scores were produced. It suggests that these scores were calculated or derived from specific data or images related to the clinical trial."},
{"wsis_from_a_completed_phase_2b_mash_clinical_trial": "WSIs from a completed phase 2b MASH clinical trial refers to Whole Slide Images that were part of a phase 2b clinical trial focused on MASH. These images are digital representations of liver tissue slides used for detailed analysis and scoring of histologic features."},
{"continuous_scores__were_compared_with__mean_pathologist_scores_from_three_study_central_readers": "The predicate \"were compared with\" indicates an analytical process where the continuous scores were evaluated against another set of scores to assess their interpretability and accuracy. This comparison aimed to verify if the AI-derived scores reflected the same directional bias as the reference scores."},
{"mean_pathologist_scores_from_three_study_central_readers": "Mean pathologist scores from three study central readers represent the average scores given by a panel of expert pathologists for the same histologic features in the WSIs. These scores serve as a benchmark to validate the continuous scoring system's ability to capture the directional bias observed by human experts."},
{"comparison_method": "The comparison method refers to the process of evaluating the continuous scores generated by the MASH CRN system against the mean pathologist scores from a panel of three study central readers. This method is crucial for assessing the interpretability and accuracy of the AI-derived continuous scoring system in reflecting the directional bias observed by human pathologists."},
{"comparison_method__used__kendall_rank_correlation": "The predicate 'used' indicates the application of a specific statistical method to perform the comparison. In this context, the Kendall rank correlation was employed to measure the agreement or correlation between the continuous scores produced by the AI system and the mean scores provided by the pathologists, thereby validating the system's performance."},
{"kendall_rank_correlation": "Kendall rank correlation is a non-parametric statistical method used to measure the correspondence between two ranked variables. In this study, it was utilized to compare the rankings of histologic features scored by the AI system with those scored by pathologists, providing a quantitative measure of the agreement between the two scoring methods."},
{"goal_of_measuring_mean_pathologist_score": "The goal of measuring the mean pathologist score refers to the objective of assessing the average score given by a panel of pathologists in evaluating specific histologic features in medical images."},
{"goal_of_measuring_mean_pathologist_score__was_to__capture_the_directional_bias_of_the_panel_per_feature": "The predicate \"was to\" indicates the purpose or intention behind the action of measuring the mean pathologist score, which in this context is to achieve a specific outcome related to the evaluation of histologic features."},
{"capture_the_directional_bias_of_the_panel_per_feature": "The object \"capture the directional bias of the panel per feature\" describes the desired outcome of the measurement, which is to identify and record the consistent tendency or inclination of the pathologist panel in their scoring of each histologic feature."},
{"ai_derived_continuous_score": "The AI-derived continuous score is a numerical value generated by an artificial intelligence system to quantify specific histologic features in medical images, such as those from Whole Slide Images (WSIs). This score is designed to provide a continuous, interpretable measure that can be compared against human expert assessments."},
{"ai_derived_continuous_score__reflected__the_same_directional_bias": "The predicate \"reflected\" indicates that the AI-derived continuous score mirrors or represents a particular characteristic or trend. In this context, it shows that the AI score aligns with or corresponds to the directional bias observed in the assessments made by human pathologists."},
{"the_same_directional_bias": "The same directional bias refers to the consistent trend or inclination observed in the assessments of histologic features by a panel of pathologists. This bias represents the general agreement or consensus among the pathologists regarding the severity or characteristics of the histologic features being evaluated."},
{"reporting_summary": "The Reporting summary is a document or section within a research article that offers additional details and insights into the methodology and design of the study."},
